    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 25-year-old female undergoes a post-coital neurological evaluation as part of a study on sexual satiety. The investigators observe specific neurochemical shifts in her brain during the resolution phase of the sexual response cycle. Which of the following shifts is classically observed?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Increased brain dopaminergic activity and decreased serotonin release",
      "B": "Sudden spike in acetylcholine with a drop in norepinephrine",
      "C": "Selective surge in histamine with total blockage of endorphins",
      "D": "Increased brain serotonergic activity and decreased dopamine release",
      "E": "Elevated progesterone with a drop in prolactin"
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "release \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Physiology of sexual arousal / Passage 1 / 12 \u25cf Explanation: A. Increased brain dopaminergic activity and decreased serotonin release: Incorrect. This is the neurochemical profile of desire and excitement, not resolution. B. Sudden spike in acetylcholine with a drop in norepinephrine: Incorrect. This does not represent the primary CNS shift in the resolution phase. C. Selective surge in histamine with total blockage of endorphins: Incorrect. This option is biochemically inaccurate. D. Increased brain serotonergic activity and decreased dopamine release: Correct. During the resolution phase, brain serotonergic activity increases (mediating sexual satiety/resolution) and dopamine release decreases, returning the HPG and HPA axes to a resting state. E. Elevated progesterone with a drop in prolactin: Incorrect. Prolactin actually peaks during and immediately after orgasm/resolution.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
