    "stem": "A resident is presenting a journal club on the prevalence and relationship impact of female sexual dysfunction in the United States. She quotes data from the National Health and Social Life Survey and the PRESIDE study. Which of the following profiles is correct?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "10% of women report some sexual dysfunction, 1% have diagnosable disorders, and \"good sex\" is the only factor that predicts relationship survival",
      "B": "80% of women report some sexual dysfunction, 50% have diagnosable disorders, and sexual activity is unrelated to relationship satisfaction",
      "C": "40% of women report some sexual dysfunction, 12% have diagnosable disorders, and \"bad sex\" has a much greater impact on ruining a relationship (70%) than \"good sex\" has on improving it (15%)",
      "D": "5% of women report some sexual dysfunction, 5% have diagnosable disorders, and \"bad sex\" is readily compensated for by high household income",
      "E": "25% of women report some sexual dysfunction, 20% have diagnosable disorders, and \"good sex\" improves relationships by 90%"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "diagnosable disorders, and \"bad sex\" has a much greater impact on ruining a relationship (70%) than \"good sex\" has on improving it (15%) \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Female Sexual Dysfunction / Passage 1 / 12 \u25cf Explanation: A. 10% of women report some sexual dysfunction, 1% have diagnosable disorders, and \"good sex\" is the only factor that predicts relationship survival: Incorrect. This significantly underestimates the prevalence and mischaracterizes the relationship impact. B. 80% of women report some sexual dysfunction, 50% have diagnosable disorders, and sexual activity is unrelated to relationship satisfaction: Incorrect. These numbers are overinflated. C. 40% of women report some sexual dysfunction, 12% have diagnosable disorders, and \"bad sex\" has a much greater impact on ruining a relationship (70%) than \"good sex\" has on improving it (15%): Correct. According to major epidemiologic surveys (National Health and 185 Social Life Survey and the PRESIDE study), approximately 40% of US women report some type of sexual dysfunction, with 12% meeting the criteria for a diagnosable disorder. Furthermore, research shows that \"bad sex\" has a profound negative impact on ruining a relationship (up to 70%), whereas \"good sex\" has only a 15% positive impact on improving a relationship. D. 5% of women report some sexual dysfunction, 5% have diagnosable disorders, and \"bad sex\" is readily compensated for by high household income: Incorrect. These statistics are inaccurate. E. 25% of women report some sexual dysfunction, 20% have diagnosable disorders, and \"good sex\" improves relationships by 90%: Incorrect. This is epidemiologically incorrect.",
    "pearl": "",
