    "topic": "Infertility (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 29-year-old G0 female undergoes a transvaginal pelvic ultrasound (TVUS) as part of an initial infertility workup. The scan is performed on cycle day 12 and reveals a 7.5 mm endometrial thickness with a classic \"trilaminar\" (three-layered) pattern. The clinician explains that this is an excellent physiological finding. What does the endometrial \"stripe\" thickness and pattern on TVUS primarily serve as a surrogate for in clinical practice?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The current circulating concentration of follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)",
      "B": "The genetic euploidy of the developing oocyte",
      "C": "Endometrial receptivity for embryo implantation",
      "D": "The patency of the bilateral Fallopian tubes",
      "E": "The total lifetime endowment of primordial follicles"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "\u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Transvaginal Pelvic Ultrasound / Passage 8 / 9 \u25cf Explanation: 119 A. The current circulating concentration of follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH): Incorrect. TVUS does not measure circulating serum FSH. B. The genetic euploidy of the developing oocyte: Incorrect. TVUS cannot determine the genetic composition of an egg. C. Endometrial receptivity for embryo implantation: Correct. Transvaginal pelvic ultrasound (TVUS) is a mainstay of the basic infertility evaluation. It is utilized to assess the endometrial \"stripe\" (both its absolute thickness and its architectural pattern, such as a pre-ovulatory trilaminar pattern versus a homogeneous secretory pattern) as a crucial surrogate marker for endometrial receptivity to embryo implantation . D. The patency of the bilateral Fallopian tubes: Incorrect. Standard TVUS cannot reliably assess tubal patency; this requires specialized imaging like HSG or SIS. E. The total lifetime endowment of primordial follicles: Incorrect. Ovarian reserve is estimated by the Antral Follicle Count (AFC) on TVUS and serum AMH, not the endometrial stripe.",
