    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 31-year-old G0 female is found to have a 1.2 cm echogenic, well-defined lesion projecting into her endometrial cavity during a saline infusion sonogram (SIS). The lesion is confirmed to be an endometrial polyp. The reproductive endocrinologist recommends hysteroscopic polypectomy to optimize her fertility. What is the primary physiological mechanism by which endometrial polyps interfere with fertility?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "They cause systemic anovulation by releasing high levels of progesterone",
      "B": "They act like an endogenous intrauterine device (IUD), interfering with embryo transport and implantation",
      "C": "They secrete toxic enzymes that directly lyse sperm upon entry into the vagina",
      "D": "They physically block the cervix, preventing any semen from entering the uterus",
      "E": "They induce premature menopause by accelerating follicular atresia"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "interfering with embryo transport and implantation \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Hysteroscopic polypectomy / Passage 9 / 10 \u25cf Explanation: A. They cause systemic anovulation by releasing high levels of progesterone: Incorrect. Polyps do not synthesize systemic hormones and do not cause anovulation. B. They act like an endogenous intrauterine device (IUD), interfering with embryo transport and implantation: Correct. Intracavitary uterine lesions, such as endometrial polyps or submucosal fibroids, physically alter the endometrial microenvironment. Clinically, polyps interfere with embryo transport and endometrial receptivity/implantation, acting essentially like an endogenous intrauterine device (IUD) . Their surgical removal via hysteroscopic polypectomy significantly restores implantation rates. C. They secrete toxic enzymes that directly lyse sperm upon entry into the vagina: Incorrect. Polyps are located in the uterine cavity, not the vagina, and do not secrete sperm-lytic enzymes. D. They physically block the cervix, preventing any semen from entering the uterus: Incorrect. While large cervical polyps can block the canal, endometrial polyps are inside the cavity and interfere primarily with implantation and embryo transport rather than blocking initial semen entry. E. They induce premature menopause by accelerating follicular atresia: Incorrect. Polyps are uterine structures and have absolutely no impact on the ovarian follicle count or the timing of menopause.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
