    "topic": "Infertility (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 32-year-old G0 female presents with hot flashes, night sweats, vaginal dryness, and amenorrhea for the past 8 months. Her family history is significant for her mother undergoing menopause at age 35, and a maternal uncle with intellectual disability and tremors. Her laboratory evaluation reveals: LH 42 mIU/mL, FSH 68 mIU/mL (FSH > LH), estradiol 12 pg/mL, progesterone 0.2 ng/mL, and AMH 0.1 ng/mL. Which of the following 113 is the most likely diagnosis, and what genetic mutation is classically associated with this condition?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS); CYP17A1 mutation",
      "B": "Hypothalamic amenorrhea; GnRHR gene mutation",
      "C": "Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (POI); FMR1 gene premutation (Fragile X carrier)",
      "D": "McCune-Albright Syndrome; GNAS gene mutation",
      "E": "Kallmann Syndrome; KAL1 gene deletion"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "(Fragile X carrier) \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Chromosomal Location of Plausible Genes Associated with POI / Slide Labs / Passage 11 / 13 / 14 \u25cf Explanation: A. Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS); CYP17A1 mutation: Incorrect. PCOS presents with normal/high AMH and low FSH, not hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. B. Hypothalamic amenorrhea; GnRHR gene mutation: Incorrect. Hypothalamic amenorrhea presents with low-normal FSH, not markedly elevated FSH. C. Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (POI); FMR1 gene premutation (Fragile X carrier): Correct. This patient has Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (POI), defined as loss of ovarian function before age 40, presenting with hot flashes, amenorrhea, and a hypergonadotropic hypogonadal lab profile (elevated FSH and LH, FSH > LH, low estradiol, and low AMH) . The slides state that 29 of 44 genomic loci associated with POI harbor DNA damage response genes, which includes the FMR1 gene (Fragile X premutation) and the BRCA1 gene . A family history of early menopause (mother at 35) and a maternal uncle with intellectual disability and tremors (Fragile X syndrome / FXTAS) is highly characteristic of an FMR1 premutation. D. McCune-Albright Syndrome; GNAS gene mutation: Incorrect. McCune-Albright presents with precocious puberty, caf\u00e9-au-lait spots, and fibrous dysplasia, not POI. E. Kallmann Syndrome; KAL1 gene deletion: Incorrect. Kallmann syndrome presents with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and anosmia, not POI.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
