    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A forensic pathologist and a legal counsel are reviewing the clinical documentation of a sexual assault case for a court proceeding. The medical record notes that oral penetration by a sex organ occurred without the patient's consent, but there was no evidence of vaginal or anal penetration. Which of the following is correct regarding the FBI 2013 updated legal definition of rape in this scenario?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It does not qualify as rape because the vagina or anus was not penetrated",
      "B": "It is classified only as contact abuse because no foreign object was utilized",
      "C": "It is legally defined as rape, which includes penetration, no matter how slight, of the vagina or anus with any body part or object, or oral penetration by a sex organ of another person, without consent",
      "D": "It is classified as statutory coercion because there was no physical trauma or laceration noted",
      "E": "It does not qualify as rape because attempts or non-completed assaults are legally excluded from the definition"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "how slight, of the vagina or anus with any body part or object, or oral penetration by a sex organ of another person, without consent \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Rape / Passage 3 / 22 \u25cf Explanation: A. It does not qualify as rape because the vagina or anus was not penetrated: Incorrect. The updated FBI definition explicitly includes oral penetration by a sex organ. B. It is classified only as contact abuse because no foreign object was utilized: Incorrect. It is legally defined as rape under the updated definition. C. It is legally defined as rape, which includes penetration, no matter how slight, of the vagina or anus with any body part or object, or oral penetration by a sex organ of another person, without consent: Correct. The FBI's updated 2013 definition of rape is: \"Penetration, no matter how slight, of the vagina or anus with any body part or object, or oral penetration by a sex organ of another person, without the consent of the victim.\" 98 D. It is classified as statutory coercion because there was no physical trauma or laceration noted: Incorrect. Physical trauma is not required to define rape, and statutory rape refers specifically to sex under the age of consent. E. It does not qualify as rape because attempts or non-completed assaults are legally excluded from the definition: Incorrect. Attempts or assaults to commit rape are explicitly included in the definition.",
