    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 26-year-old female presents to her gynecologist with a 3-month history of recurrent nightmares, intrusive flashbacks, a severe phobia of being touched, chronic pelvic pain, and dysmenorrhea. She reveals that she was sexually assaulted 4 months ago. Which of the following phases of the rape-trauma syndrome is most consistent with her current presentation?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Acute phase (disorganization phase)",
      "B": "Delayed phase (organization phase)",
      "C": "Hyper-acute somatization phase",
      "D": "Acute panic phase",
      "E": "Immediate integration phase"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "102 \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Psychological Consequences / Passage 9 / 28 \u25cf Explanation: A. Acute phase (disorganization phase): Incorrect. The acute phase occurs in the immediate days to weeks after the assault. B. Delayed phase (organization phase): Correct. The delayed phase (or organization phase) of rape-trauma syndrome begins weeks to months after the sexual assault. It is characterized by nightmares, intrusive flashbacks, new phobias, and somatic or gynecological symptoms (including chronic pelvic pain, dysmenorrhea, and sexual dysfunction). C. Hyper-acute somatization phase: Incorrect. This is not a clinical term or phase of rape-trauma syndrome. D. Acute panic phase: Incorrect. This is not a term used to describe the delayed, long-term psychological and somatic adaptation phase of rape-trauma syndrome. E. Immediate integration phase: Incorrect. Integration is a long-term goal, not a phase characterized by nightmares, flashbacks, and chronic pain.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
