    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "crossing by diffusion \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Placental transport / Passage 3 / 23 / 33 \u25cf Explanation: A. It is actively transported back into the maternal circulation by ATP-dependent efflux pumps: Incorrect. Insulin is not actively pumped back into maternal circulation. B. It is rapidly degraded by maternal antibodies in the decidua before reaching the villi: Incorrect. While some placental degradation of insulin occurs, the physical barrier size restriction is the primary mechanism preventing transfer. C. It is too lipophilic to dissolve in the lipid bilayer of the trophoblast membrane: Incorrect. Insulin is a large, hydrophilic peptide hormone, not a lipophilic molecule. D. It has a molecular weight larger than 600 Daltons, preventing easy crossing by diffusion: Correct. The placental membrane acts as a selective barrier. A key clinical principle is that \"compounds larger than 600 Daltons cannot easily cross the placenta by diffusion.\" A classic, high-yield example of this is insulin, which is a large polypeptide with a molecular weight of approximately 6,000 Daltons. Because of its massive size, maternal insulin cannot cross the placenta to reach the fetus. Therefore, maternal insulin therapy is safe in pregnancy because it does not cause direct fetal hypoglycemia. E. It lacks the necessary receptor-mediated endocytosis proteins on the villous membrane: Incorrect. While maternal antibodies cross via receptor-mediated endocytosis, insulin does not use this pathway, and its lack of transfer is primarily due to its large size blocking passive diffusion.",
