    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "bone marrow, leading to severe anemia and high-output cardiac failure \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Parvovirus B19 / Slide Checkpoint Question #2 / Slide Framework / Passage 8 / 10 / 20 / 26 / 27 / 29 / 30 / 44 / 45 \u25cf Explanation: A. Cytomegalovirus; direct destruction of the fetal renal parenchyma leading to severe oligohydramnios: Incorrect. CMV does not cause hydrops via primary renal parenchyma destruction or oligohydramnios. B. Toxoplasma gondii; severe chorioretinitis leading to intracranial pressure changes and hydrops: Incorrect. Toxoplasmosis can cause hydrocephalus and chorioretinitis but is not a classic cause of hydrops fetalis via erythroid destruction. C. Parvovirus B19; destruction of fetal erythroid precursors in the bone marrow, leading to severe anemia and high-output cardiac failure: Correct. Parvovirus B19 is a single-stranded DNA virus that causes \"Fifth disease\" (erythema infectiosum) in children, presenting with a \"slapped-cheek\" rash. In pregnant individuals, the virus can cross the placenta and infect the fetus. Parvovirus B19 has a specific tropism for and directly infects and destroys fetal erythroid progenitor cells (erythroblasts) in the liver and bone marrow. This halts red blood cell production, causing severe, acute fetal anemia. To compensate, the fetal heart must pump blood faster, leading to high-output cardiac failure. This heart failure causes a backup of fluid, 78 presenting as hydrops fetalis (characterized by diffuse fluid accumulation in >= 2 fetal compartments, such as ascites, pleural effusion, and skin edema). D. Rubella virus; vascular disruption of the fetal umbilical arteries leading to systemic ischemia: Incorrect. While Rubella causes cataracts, PDA, and deafness, it does not cause hydrops via umbilical artery disruption. E. Syphilis; direct hepatic necrosis leading to a complete loss of oncotic pressure: Incorrect. Congenital syphilis can cause hepatomegaly and ascites, but Parvovirus is the classic \"slapped-cheek\" associated cause of fetal anemia-induced hydrops fetalis.",
