    "rationale": "periventricular calcifications and microcephaly, whereas toxoplasmosis features diffuse calcifications and hydrocephalus \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Congenital Infections / Slide CMV vs Toxoplasma Calcifications / Passage 7 / 9 / 18 / 25 / 29 / 42 \u25cf Explanation: A. Toxoplasma gondii; distinguished because toxoplasmosis features diffuse (not periventricular) calcifications and hydrocephalus (not microcephaly): Incorrect. Toxoplasmosis is the cause of diffuse calcifications and hydrocephalus, but this baby has periventricular calcifications and microcephaly, which points to CMV. B. Cytomegalovirus (CMV); distinguished because CMV features periventricular calcifications and microcephaly, whereas toxoplasmosis features diffuse calcifications and hydrocephalus: Correct. Cytomegalovirus (CMV) and Toxoplasmosis are two major TORCH infections that can present with intracranial calcifications, microcephaly/hydrocephalus, and a \"blueberry muffin\" rash. They are distinguished on imaging: \u25cf  CMV classically presents with periventricular calcifications (lining the lateral ventricles) and microcephaly (small head size due to impaired brain development). \u25cf  Toxoplasmosis classically presents with diffuse calcifications (scattered throughout the brain parenchyma) and obstructive hydrocephalus (enlarged ventricles and head size due to aqueductal stenosis). \u25cf  Both can feature sensorineural hearing loss, though it is highly characteristic of CMV. C. Rubella virus; distinguished because Rubella does not cause any intracranial calcifications: Incorrect. Rubella does not feature periventricular calcifications; its classic triad is PDA, cataracts, and deafness. D. Parvovirus B19; distinguished because Parvovirus causes diffuse skeletal calcifications: Incorrect. Parvovirus causes fetal anemia and hydrops, not intracranial or skeletal calcifications. E. Treponema pallidum; distinguished because Syphilis causes perivascular calcifications in the cerebellum: Incorrect. Congenital syphilis does not typically present with periventricular calcifications. 79",
