    "rationale": "\u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Toxoplasma gondii / Slide CMV vs Toxoplasma / Passage 6 / 7 / 9 / 25 / 29 / 42 \u25cf Explanation: A. Patent ductus arteriosus, cataracts, and sensorineural deafness: Incorrect. This is the classic triad of congenital Rubella syndrome, not toxoplasmosis. B. Hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis, and diffuse cerebral calcifications: Correct. Toxoplasma gondii is a protozoan transmitted to pregnant patients via contact with cat feces (e.g., litter boxes) or ingestion of undercooked/raw meat. A primary maternal infection during pregnancy can lead to vertical transplacental transmission. The classic clinical triad of congenital toxoplasmosis consists of: (1) obstructive hydrocephalus (due to inflammation-induced aqueductal stenosis), (2) chorioretinitis (inflammation of the choroid and retina of the eye), and (3) diffuse intracranial calcifications scattered throughout the brain parenchyma. C. Microcephaly, periventricular calcifications, and sensorineural hearing loss: Incorrect. This triad is characteristic of congenital cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection, not toxoplasmosis. D. Snuffles, Hutchinson teeth, and saddle nose: Incorrect. This constellation of findings is diagnostic of congenital syphilis, not toxoplasmosis. E. Fetal anemia, pleural effusions, and aplastic crisis: Incorrect. This describes the clinical manifestations of congenital Parvovirus B19 infection.",
