    "rationale": "inflammatory effects, meaning adverse fetal outcome does not require direct fetal infection \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Perinatal Infection mechanisms / Slide Listeria / Passage 5 / 10 / 25 / 28 / 29 / 42 \u25cf Explanation: A. Adverse fetal outcomes can only occur if there is direct, widespread infection of the fetal organs: Incorrect. Direct fetal organ infection is not required; severe placental damage alone can cause fetal hypoxia and death. B. Fetal injury and pregnancy loss can reflect placental injury and inflammatory effects, meaning adverse fetal outcome does not require direct fetal infection: Correct. This case of Listeria monocytogenes (a Gram-positive bacillus associated with contaminated unheated deli meats and soft cheeses) demonstrates a major clinical principle: \"adverse fetal outcome does not require direct fetal infection.\" A pathogen can infect the placenta, causing severe deciduitis, placental villitis, and microabscesses. This placental injury and the subsequent maternal/placental inflammatory response can compromise uteroplacental blood flow, leading to chronic fetal hypoxia, growth restriction, or acute fetal death and miscarriage, even in the absence of direct bacterial invasion of fetal tissues. C. Foodborne pathogens like Listeria monocytogenes can only infect the mother during the third trimester: Incorrect. Listeria can infect the mother and cross the placenta during any trimester, leading to first- or second-trimester miscarriage as well as third-trimester stillbirth. D. Maternal IgA antibodies actively transport Listeria bacteria across the villous membrane: Incorrect. Maternal IgG, not IgA, crosses the placenta, and it does not transport bacterial pathogens. Listeria crosses via direct invasion of placental trophoblasts. E. Ascent of vaginal bacteria is the only mechanism by which foodborne pathogens reach the placenta: Incorrect. Foodborne pathogens typically reach the placenta via hematogenous (transplacental) spread through the maternal bloodstream, not via ascending genital tract infection.",
