    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "binds to hemoglobin, decreasing oxygen delivery and causing chronic fetal hypoxia and growth restriction \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Tobacco / Slide Framework / Passage 12 / 21 / 31 / 44 / 45 \u25cf Explanation: A. Nicotine causes direct lysis of fetal red blood cells; Carbon monoxide causes severe maternal renal failure: Incorrect. Nicotine is a vasoconstrictor, and carbon monoxide does not cause maternal renal failure; rather, it binds to hemoglobin to form carboxyhemoglobin, reducing oxygen transport. B. Nicotine causes placental vasoconstriction; Carbon monoxide binds to hemoglobin, decreasing oxygen delivery and causing chronic fetal hypoxia and growth restriction: Correct. Tobacco smoke contains thousands of compounds, but two are primary drivers of fetal injury: \u25cf  Nicotine: is a potent vasoconstrictor. It causes constriction of the placental and uterine blood vessels, reducing blood flow to the placenta and fetus. \u25cf  Carbon monoxide: binds to maternal and fetal hemoglobin with high affinity (forming carboxyhemoglobin), which physically displaces oxygen and shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the left, decreasing oxygen delivery to fetal tissues. Together, these mechanisms lead to chronic fetal hypoxia, resulting in fetal growth restriction (FGR), low birth weight, and an increased risk of preterm labor, placental abruption, and Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS). C. Nicotine stimulates fetal surfactant production prematurely; Carbon monoxide causes premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus: Incorrect. Tobacco smoke does not mature lungs or close the ductus arteriosus (NSAIDs close the ductus). D. Nicotine causes direct destruction of the fetal thyroid gland; Carbon monoxide blocks active transport of amino acids: Incorrect. These are not the mechanisms of tobacco-associated fetal injury. 83 E. Both nicotine and carbon monoxide act as direct mutagens that cause chromosomal translocations during mitosis: Incorrect. The primary teratogenic effects of tobacco smoke are mediated by hypoxia and hypoperfusion, not mitotic chromosomal translocations.",
