    "rationale": "\u25cf Lecture Source: Slide High Yield Teratogens / Slide Thalidomide / Passage 15 / 34 / 35 \u25cf Explanation: A. Transposition of the great arteries and tetralogy of Fallot: Incorrect. While thalidomide can cause cardiac defects, its pathognomonic, classic effect is phocomelia. B. Phocomelia (severe limb reduction/underdevelopment): Correct. Thalidomide is one of the most notorious teratogens in medical history. Developed and marketed in Europe as a safe over-the-counter remedy for insomnia and morning sickness, it caused a global epidemic of severe birth defects. Its classic, highly characteristic teratogenic effect is phocomelia, which is a severe limb reduction deformity where the long bones of the limbs (arms and legs) are extremely underdeveloped, shortened, or completely absent, with the hands or feet attached close to the trunk (resembling seal flippers). C. Renal agenesis and oligohydramnios sequence: Incorrect. This is the classic presentation of ACE inhibitor fetopathy, not thalidomide. D. Anencephaly and spina bifida: Incorrect. These are neural tube defects, classically associated with valproate or folate deficiency, not thalidomide. E. Permanent yellow-brown discoloration of the teeth: Incorrect. This is the classic side effect of tetracycline exposure, not thalidomide.",
