    "rationale": "supplementation prior to conception \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide High Yield Teratogens / Slide Valproic acid / Passage 15 / 34 / 36 / 37 \u25cf Explanation: A. Ebstein anomaly; prevent by starting low-dose aspirin: Incorrect. Ebstein anomaly is caused by lithium, and aspirin does not prevent it. B. Permanent teeth discoloration; prevent by avoiding dairy products: Incorrect. Teeth discoloration is caused by tetracyclines. C. Neural tube defects; prevent by initiating high-dose folate supplementation prior to conception: Correct. Valproic acid is a commonly used anticonvulsant and mood stabilizer. First-trimester exposure is strongly associated with an increased risk of neural tube defects (NTDs) like spina bifida and anencephaly. Valproic acid directly interferes with folate metabolism (antagonizing folate absorption and utilization). To prevent NTDs, patients on valproate who are planning to conceive should be switched to a safer alternative if possible and must be prescribed high-dose folic acid (4 mg or 4000 mcg daily, which is 10 times the standard dose) starting at least 1-3 months prior to conception. D. Phocomelia; prevent by administering intramuscular magnesium sulfate: Incorrect. Phocomelia is caused by thalidomide, and magnesium sulfate does not prevent it. E. Renal injury; prevent by maintaining strict fluid restriction: Incorrect. Renal injury is caused by ACE inhibitors, and fluid restriction is not a preventative or therapeutic measure.",
