    "rationale": "subsequent renal ischemia/damage, leading to decreased fetal urine production, oligohydramnios, and pulmonary hypoplasia \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide High Yield Teratogens / Slide ACE Inhibitors / Slide Checkpoint Question #6 / Passage 15 / 16 / 20 / 21 / 34 / 39 / 43 / 44 / 45 \u25cf Explanation: 87 A. Lisinopril causes fetal hypotension, renal hypoperfusion, and subsequent renal ischemia/damage, leading to decreased fetal urine production, oligohydramnios, and pulmonary hypoplasia: Correct. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, such as lisinopril, are strictly contraindicated in the second and third trimesters of pregnancy. The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) is critical for fetal renal development and hemodynamic stability. Blocking ACE in the fetus causes profound fetal hypotension and renal hypoperfusion. This leads to severe renal ischemia and permanent tubular damage (renal dysgenesis). Consequently, the fetal kidneys fail to produce urine. Because fetal urine is the primary source of amniotic fluid in the second and third trimesters, this results in severe oligohydramnios (low amniotic fluid). The lack of amniotic fluid causes uterine compression, leading to pulmonary hypoplasia, limb contractures, and calvarium maldevelopment (ACE inhibitor fetopathy). B. Lisinopril causes direct chemical destruction of the fetal amniotic membrane, allowing amniotic fluid to leak continuously: Incorrect. Lisinopril has no direct chemical impact on the physical integrity of the amniotic membranes. C. Lisinopril blocks maternal fluid absorption in the gut, leading to maternal and fetal dehydration: Incorrect. Lisinopril does not impair maternal intestinal fluid absorption. D. Lisinopril stimulates premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus, reducing systemic blood flow: Incorrect. Premature closure of the ductus arteriosus is caused by NSAIDs (like indomethacin) in the third trimester, not ACE inhibitors. E. Lisinopril induces direct fetal hepatic necrosis, which impairs fetal clotting factor synthesis: Incorrect. The primary site of injury is the fetal kidneys, not the liver, and it does not directly impair clotting factor synthesis.",
