    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "decreases, while cardiac output increases by 30-50%, with most of this increase occurring in the first trimester. \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Cardiovascular System / Passage 4 / 5 \u25cf Explanation: A. Stroke volume peaks in the first trimester, while cardiac output remains constant until the third trimester: Incorrect. Stroke volume peaks in the second trimester (around 24 weeks) and then decreases, whereas cardiac output starts rising early, with most of its increase occurring in the first trimester. B. Stroke volume peaks around 24 weeks gestation and then decreases, while cardiac output increases by 30-50%, with most of this increase occurring in the first trimester: Correct. During pregnancy, blood volume increases, leading to increased preload and stroke volume. Stroke volume peaks around 24 weeks and then decreases. Cardiac output increases by 30-50%, with the majority of this rise occurring in the first trimester. This is driven by both an increase in stroke volume (first trimester and early second) and heart rate (which increases throughout pregnancy). C. Stroke volume increases continuously until term, while cardiac output peaks in the second trimester and then drops rapidly: Incorrect. Stroke volume does not increase continuously; it peaks at 24 weeks and then declines. Cardiac output remains elevated throughout pregnancy and during labor. D. Both stroke volume and cardiac output increase progressively and linearly throughout all three trimesters, peaking at the time of delivery: Incorrect. Neither variable increases in a simple linear fashion. Cardiac output increases early and levels off, while stroke volume peaks around 24 weeks and then falls. E. Stroke volume remains unchanged throughout pregnancy, and the increase in cardiac output is driven entirely by a 50% increase in maternal heart rate: Incorrect. Stroke volume increases significantly in the first and second trimesters. Maternal heart rate increases by approximately 15-20% (~16 beats per minute), not 50%.",
