    "topic": "Maternal Physiological Changes in (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 28-year-old G1P0 at 22 weeks gestation presents for a routine prenatal checkup. Her blood pressure is 102/60 mmHg. Her pre-pregnancy blood pressure was 118/76 mmHg. She is concerned that her blood pressure is too low. The clinician reassures her that this is a normal physiological response. Which of the following is the primary mechanism responsible for this change in blood pressure, and when does the nadir typically occur?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Increased production of human placental lactogen causing profound venodilation; nadir occurs in the first trimester.",
      "B": "Decreased blood volume leading to reduced venous return; nadir occurs at 36 weeks gestation.",
      "C": "Progesterone-mediated smooth muscle relaxation leading to decreased systemic vascular resistance (SVR); nadir occurs around 24 weeks gestation.",
      "D": "High levels of estrogen causing arterial stiffening; nadir occurs at 12 weeks gestation.",
      "E": "Direct mechanical compression of the aorta by the gravid uterus; nadir occurs at 40 weeks gestation."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "decreased systemic vascular resistance (SVR); nadir occurs around 24 weeks gestation. \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Cardiovascular System / Passage 4 \u25cf Explanation: A. Increased production of human placental lactogen causing profound venodilation; nadir occurs in the first trimester: Incorrect. Human placental lactogen (hPL) has metabolic effects (inducing insulin resistance) but is not the primary mediator of systemic vascular smooth muscle relaxation and decreased blood pressure. 52 B. Decreased blood volume leading to reduced venous return; nadir occurs at 36 weeks gestation: Incorrect. Blood volume increases significantly (not decreases) by 40-50% during pregnancy . Blood pressure reaches its nadir around 24 weeks, not 36 weeks. C. Progesterone-mediated smooth muscle relaxation leading to decreased systemic vascular resistance (SVR); nadir occurs around 24 weeks gestation: Correct. Progesterone acts directly on vascular smooth muscle, causing relaxation and vasodilation. This results in a significant reduction in systemic vascular resistance (SVR) and afterload. Consequently, blood pressure decreases during the first two trimesters, reaching its nadir around 24 weeks gestation before gradually returning to pre-pregnancy baselines by term. D. High levels of estrogen causing arterial stiffening; nadir occurs at 12 weeks gestation: Incorrect. High levels of estrogen do not cause arterial stiffening; instead, vascular compliance is increased in pregnancy. The blood pressure nadir is at 24 weeks, not 12 weeks. E. Direct mechanical compression of the aorta by the gravid uterus; nadir occurs at 40 weeks gestation: Incorrect. Aortic compression can occur when supine, but it causes acute hypoperfusion and hypotension, not the gradual, physiological drop in SVR and diastolic blood pressure that nadirs at 24 weeks.",
    "pearl": "",
