    "topic": "Maternal Physiological Changes in (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 31-year-old G2P1 at 28 weeks gestation is evaluated in the clinic for mild fatigue. On cardiac examination, she has a normal S1 and S2, with a grade 2/6 midsystolic ejection murmur heard best at the left sternal border. No diastolic murmurs or rubs are heard. Her lungs are clear. A screening chest X-ray (performed for a separate indication with appropriate abdominal shielding) shows mild cardiac enlargement and leftward displacement of the heart. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management, and what is the underlying explanation for these findings?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Obtain an urgent echocardiogram; both the murmur and X-ray findings are highly pathologic.",
      "B": "Reassure the patient; both the systolic ejection murmur and the radiographic appearance of cardiomegaly are normal physiological findings in pregnancy.",
      "C": "Initiate low-dose loop diuretic therapy; these findings are early signs of peripartum cardiomyopathy.",
      "D": "Schedule a chest CT scan to evaluate for a mediastinal mass displacing the heart.",
      "E": "Admit the patient for continuous cardiac monitoring; a systolic ejection murmur is a sign of acute aortic stenosis in pregnancy."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "radiographic appearance of cardiomegaly are normal physiological findings in pregnancy. \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Clinical correlates / Passage 5 \u25cf Explanation: A. Obtain an urgent echocardiogram; both the murmur and X-ray findings are highly pathologic: Incorrect. Echocardiography is not routinely indicated for these common, benign physiological adaptations. B. Reassure the patient; both the systolic ejection murmur and the radiographic appearance of cardiomegaly are normal physiological findings in pregnancy: Correct. Due to the massive increase in blood volume (40-50%) and subsequent hyperdynamic state (increased cardiac output), a benign physiologic systolic ejection flow murmur is heard in up to 90% of pregnant patients. Furthermore, as the uterus grows and elevates the diaphragm, the heart is pushed upward and rotated to the left . This displacement changes the cardiac axis, which can give the false impression of cardiomegaly on a standard anterior-posterior chest X-ray . Reassurance is the appropriate next step. C. Initiate low-dose loop diuretic therapy; these findings are early signs of peripartum cardiomyopathy: Incorrect. While peripartum cardiomyopathy is a serious concern, it presents with signs of clinical heart failure (rales, severe dyspnea, edema) and a pathologic diastolic or severe systolic murmur. Normal physiological findings should not be treated with diuretics. D. Schedule a chest CT scan to evaluate for a mediastinal mass displacing the heart: Incorrect. There is no indication of a mediastinal mass. Leftward displacement is due to normal elevation of the diaphragm by the expanding uterus. E. Admit the patient for continuous cardiac monitoring; a systolic ejection murmur is a sign of acute aortic stenosis in pregnancy: Incorrect. Aortic stenosis would present with a harsh, crescendo-decrescendo murmur that radiates to the carotids, often with symptoms of angina, syncope, or severe dyspnea, not a benign 2/6 flow murmur in an asymptomatic patient.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
