    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 32-year-old female at 34 weeks gestation is brought to the emergency department by paramedics after suffering a sudden cardiac arrest from a suspected pulmonary embolism. Advanced cardiovascular life support (ACLS) is initiated immediately. Despite 4 minutes of high-quality cardiopulmonary resuscitation, there is no return of spontaneous circulation. The patient\u2019s fundus is palpated well above the umbilicus. 44 Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in the management of this patient, and what is a primary maternal physiological benefit?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Perform an immediate resuscitative hysterotomy (perimortem cesarean section); it relieves aortocaval compression and autotransfuses up to 500 mL of uteroplacental blood back to the maternal circulation.",
      "B": "Continue standard ACLS for another 15 minutes before considering any surgical intervention.",
      "C": "Administer high-dose tPA immediately and avoid any surgical incisions due to the risk of uncontrollable hemorrhage.",
      "D": "Move the patient to the operating room for a formal, sterile Cesarean delivery under general anesthesia.",
      "E": "Reposition the patient in a prone position to improve chest wall compliance during CPR."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "cesarean section); it relieves aortocaval compression and autotransfuses up to 500 mL of uteroplacental blood back to the maternal circulation. \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Clinical correlates / Slide Resuscitative hysterotomy / Passage 5 / 6 \u25cf Explanation: 54 A. Perform an immediate resuscitative hysterotomy (perimortem cesarean section); it relieves aortocaval compression and autotransfuses up to 500 mL of uteroplacental blood back to the maternal circulation: Correct. In a pregnant patient beyond 20 weeks gestation (fundus at or above the umbilicus) who undergoes cardiac arrest, the gravid uterus causes severe, continuous aortocaval compression that renders chest compressions highly ineffective . If there is no return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC) within 4 minutes of arrest, a resuscitative hysterotomy (perimortem cesarean) must be performed immediately at the bedside . This crucial procedure (1) physically decompresses the IVC and aorta, immediately improving maternal venous return and the efficacy of CPR , and (2) autotransfuses up to 500 mL of blood from the uteroplacental bed back into the maternal central circulation, which can be life-saving for both the pregnant person and the fetus . B. Continue standard ACLS for another 15 minutes before considering any surgical intervention: Incorrect. Delaying hysterotomy beyond 5 minutes in a patient with a fundus above the umbilicus is associated with a near-zero survival rate for both the mother and fetus due to persistent aortocaval obstruction. C. Administer high-dose tPA immediately and avoid any surgical incisions due to the risk of uncontrollable hemorrhage: Incorrect. Bedside hysterotomy is the absolute priority to relieve mechanical vascular obstruction; medical therapies like tPA will fail if venous return is obstructed. D. Move the patient to the operating room for a formal, sterile Cesarean delivery under general anesthesia: Incorrect. The patient must not be moved; the hysterotomy should be performed immediately at the bedside in the emergency department or ICU where the arrest occurred, using whatever instruments are available, without delaying for sterile OR setup. E. Reposition the patient in a prone position to improve chest wall compliance during CPR: Incorrect. Prone positioning is contraindicated during active cardiac arrest resuscitation and would make performing CPR and the necessary hysterotomy impossible.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
