    "rationale": "and decreased residual volume (RV) and expiratory reserve volume (ERV). \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Respiratory System / Slide Lung volumes / Passage 6 \u25cf Explanation: A. Increased total lung capacity (TLC), increased respiratory rate, and decreased tidal volume (TV): Incorrect. TLC actually decreases due to diaphragmatic elevation , and tidal volume increases . The maternal respiratory rate does not change during normal pregnancy . B. Decreased total lung capacity (TLC), increased tidal volume (TV), and decreased residual volume (RV) and expiratory reserve volume (ERV): Correct. The physical expansion of the uterus pushes the diaphragm upward by about 4 cm, which mechanically reduces the vertical diameter of the thoracic cavity. This leads to a decrease in total lung capacity (TLC), functional residual capacity (FRC), residual volume (RV), and expiratory reserve volume (ERV) . However, 55 under the stimulatory influence of progesterone, the pregnant patient breathes more deeply, resulting in a significant increase in tidal volume (TV) . C. Increased residual volume (RV), decreased expiratory reserve volume (ERV), and decreased tidal volume (TV): Incorrect. Residual volume decreases (not increases) , and tidal volume increases significantly . D. Unchanged tidal volume (TV), increased respiratory rate, and increased functional residual capacity (FRC): Incorrect. Tidal volume increases , respiratory rate remains unchanged , and FRC decreases . E. Increased total lung capacity (TLC), decreased tidal volume (TV), and increased residual volume (RV): Incorrect. This reverses all the correct volume changes; TLC and RV decrease, while TV increases .",
