    "topic": "Maternal Physiological Changes in (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 25-year-old G1P0 at 28 weeks gestation presents to her obstetrician complaining of dyspnea. She reports feeling short of breath when walking up a single flight of stairs or performing housework, which did not bother her before pregnancy. She denies chest pain, cough, wheezing, or leg swelling. Her vital signs are: blood pressure 110/70 mmHg, heart rate 82 beats per minute, respiratory rate 16 breaths per minute, oxygen saturation 99% on room air. Her physical examination is entirely normal. Which of the 45 following is the most likely diagnosis, its approximate prevalence, and the primary physiological mediator?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Asthma flare-up; 10% prevalence; mediated by histamine release.",
      "B": "Peripartum cardiomyopathy; 1% prevalence; mediated by prolactin cleavage.",
      "C": "Dyspnea of pregnancy; 70-80% prevalence; mediated by progesterone-stimulated respiratory center hyperventilation.",
      "D": "Pulmonary embolism; 5% prevalence; mediated by increased clotting factors.",
      "E": "Diaphragmatic paralysis; less than 1% prevalence; mediated by mechanical compression of the phrenic nerve."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "progesterone-stimulated respiratory center hyperventilation. \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Respiratory System / Slide Dyspnea of pregnancy / Passage 6 / 7 \u25cf Explanation: A. Asthma flare-up; 10% prevalence; mediated by histamine release: Incorrect. There is no history of wheezing, cough, or chest tightness, and her exam is normal. True asthma flare-ups require pathological bronchoconstriction. B. Peripartum cardiomyopathy; 1% prevalence; mediated by prolactin cleavage: Incorrect. This rare cardiomyopathy occurs in the late third trimester or early postpartum period and is associated with clear clinical pathology (edema, rales, elevated jugular venous pressure, cardiomegaly on echocardiogram), which this patient does not have. C. Dyspnea of pregnancy; 70-80% prevalence; mediated by progesterone-stimulated respiratory center hyperventilation: Correct. Dyspnea of pregnancy is a highly common, benign physiological sensation that affects approximately 70-80% of pregnant individuals, typically beginning in the first or second trimester. It is primarily mediated by progesterone, which directly stimulates the respiratory center in the brain to increase the sensitivity to carbon dioxide, driving a state of chronic hyperventilation (characterized by an increased tidal volume) . The patient experiences this deeper breathing as \"shortness of breath\" despite normal oxygen saturation, respiratory rate, and physical examination . D. Pulmonary embolism; 5% prevalence; mediated by increased clotting factors: Incorrect. While pregnant patients are at increased risk for VTE, her presentation is slowly progressive and accompanied by completely normal vitals and physical exam, making an acute PE highly unlikely. PE is a pathologic diagnosis, not a benign, highly prevalent physiological state. 56 E. Diaphragmatic paralysis; less than 1% prevalence; mediated by mechanical compression of the phrenic nerve: Incorrect. The diaphragm remains fully functional in pregnancy; it is simply elevated mechanically by the expanding uterus .",
