    "topic": "Maternal Physiological Changes in (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 30-year-old G1P0 at 20 weeks gestation presents with persistent heartburn and constipation. She reports a burning sensation in her retrosternal area after meals and having only two hard bowel movements per week. She is not taking any new medications other than prenatal vitamins. The clinician explains that these symptoms are due to the physiological effects of pregnancy hormones on the gastrointestinal tract. Which of the following hormones is primarily responsible for both of these symptoms, and what is its specific physiological mechanism?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Estrogen, causing increased production of hydrochloric acid in the stomach and hyperperistalsis of the colon.",
      "B": "Progesterone, causing smooth muscle relaxation, which decreases lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone and reduces colonic motility.",
      "C": "Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), causing hypersecretion of gastrin and accelerated gastric emptying.",
      "D": "Human placental lactogen (hPL), causing generalized mucosal atrophy and decreased intestinal absorption.",
      "E": "Relaxin, causing hypermobility of the stomach and mechanical obstruction of the duodenum."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "decreases lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone and reduces colonic motility. \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Gastrointestinal System / Passage 9 \u25cf Explanation: A. Estrogen, causing increased production of hydrochloric acid in the stomach and hyperperistalsis of the colon: Incorrect. Estrogen does not cause hyperperistalsis (colonic transit is slowed) or significant hypersecretion of stomach acid. B. Progesterone, causing smooth muscle relaxation, which decreases lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone and reduces colonic motility: Correct. Progesterone is the primary hormone responsible for generalized smooth muscle relaxation throughout the body during pregnancy. In the GI tract, progesterone reduces the tone of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), allowing gastric contents to reflux back into the esophagus, causing heartburn (GERD) . Simultaneously, progesterone decreases the smooth muscle motility of the small and large intestines, leading to delayed colonic transit, increased water reabsorption, and persistent constipation . C. Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), causing hypersecretion of gastrin and accelerated gastric emptying: Incorrect. hCG is implicated in nausea and vomiting but does not cause gastrin hypersecretion or accelerate gastric emptying (gastric emptying is actually delayed in pregnancy). 58 D. Human placental lactogen (hPL), causing generalized mucosal atrophy and decreased intestinal absorption: Incorrect. hPL is a metabolic hormone that induces insulin resistance and lipolysis ; it does not cause mucosal atrophy or impair GI absorption. E. Relaxin, causing hypermobility of the stomach and mechanical obstruction of the duodenum: Incorrect. Relaxin acts primarily on skeletal ligaments and joints (such as the pubic symphysis) , not stomach smooth muscle.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
