    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 28-year-old female at 26 weeks gestation undergoes a renal ultrasound for evaluation of persistent right flank pain. The ultrasound shows bilateral hydroureter and hydronephrosis, which is significantly more pronounced on the right side than on the left. Urinalysis is completely normal, with no nitrites, leukocyte esterase, or bacteria. Which of the following best explains these imaging findings?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Progesterone-mediated relaxation of ureteral smooth muscle paired with mechanical compression of the right ureter by the dextrorotated uterus and enlarged ovarian vessels.",
      "B": "Bilateral ureteral calculi obstructing the urinary tract, which is a common pathologic finding in pregnancy.",
      "C": "Congenital duplicate collecting system, which is typically unmasked during pregnancy.",
      "D": "Unilateral renal agenesis with compensatory hypertrophy of the left kidney.",
      "E": "Acute pyelonephritis causing severe spasm of the ureters."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "paired with mechanical compression of the right ureter by the dextrorotated uterus and enlarged ovarian vessels. \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Renal System / Slide Physiologic hydronephrosis / Passage 8 \u25cf Explanation: A. Progesterone-mediated relaxation of ureteral smooth muscle paired with mechanical compression of the right ureter by the dextrorotated uterus and enlarged ovarian vessels: Correct. Bilateral, mild-to-moderate hydroureter and hydronephrosis are normal, physiological findings in up to 90% of pregnancies . This \"physiologic hydronephrosis\" is caused by two factors: (1) progesterone-mediated relaxation of the ureteral smooth muscle, which reduces peristalsis and causes dilation , and (2) mechanical compression of the ureters at the pelvic brim by the expanding gravid uterus and enlarged ovarian veins . The dilation is characteristically more pronounced on the right side than on the left (R > L) because the uterus is naturally rotated to the right (dextrorotation) due to the presence of the sigmoid colon on the left side of the pelvis . B. Bilateral ureteral calculi obstructing the urinary tract, which is a common pathologic finding in pregnancy: Incorrect. While stones can occur, they are pathologic, typically unilateral, and accompanied by severe renal colic and hematuria. Urinalysis here is completely normal. C. Congenital duplicate collecting system, which is typically unmasked during pregnancy: Incorrect. A duplicate collecting system is a congenital anatomical variant and would not present as a normal, generalized R>L physiologic dilation in pregnancy. D. Unilateral renal agenesis with compensatory hypertrophy of the left kidney: Incorrect. This would show a completely absent right kidney on ultrasound, not bilateral hydronephrosis. E. Acute pyelonephritis causing severe spasm of the ureters: Incorrect. Pyelonephritis would present with fever, costovertebral angle (CVA) tenderness, and an abnormal urinalysis (pyuria, bacteriuria, white blood cell casts), none of which are present in this patient.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
