    "topic": "Maternal Physiological Changes in (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 27-year-old G1P0 at 32 weeks gestation is evaluated during a routine prenatal visit. Her laboratory evaluation reveals a white blood cell count of 12,500/mcL (normal non-pregnant reference range: 4,500-11,000/mcL) and a platelet count of 122,000/mcL (normal non-pregnant reference range: 150,000-450,000/mcL). She is asymptomatic, with no fever, bruising, or bleeding. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and explanation for these findings?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Acute myeloid leukemia and immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP), requiring immediate bone marrow biopsy.",
      "B": "Normal physiological changes of pregnancy (mild neutrophilic leukocytosis and gestational thrombocytopenia).",
      "C": "Sepsis and disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), requiring hospitalization and intravenous antibiotics.",
      "D": "Severe iron and vitamin B12 deficiencies causing bone marrow suppression.",
      "E": "Preeclampsia with HELLP syndrome, requiring immediate induction of labor."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "leukocytosis and gestational thrombocytopenia). 61 \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Labs / Slide Hematologic System / Passage 3 / 10 / 13 \u25cf Explanation: A. Acute myeloid leukemia and immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP), requiring immediate bone marrow biopsy: Incorrect. A bone marrow biopsy is an invasive procedure that is completely unwarranted. These mild lab deviations are standard in healthy, asymptomatic pregnant patients. B. Normal physiological changes of pregnancy (mild neutrophilic leukocytosis and gestational thrombocytopenia): Correct. Normal pregnancy is characterized by major hematologic shifts . A mild leukocytosis (WBC up to 15,000/mcL or higher, driven primarily by neutrophils) is normal and occurs in the absence of infection. Additionally, a mild, asymptomatic decrease in platelet count (typically between 100,000 and 150,000/mcL) is exceedingly common and is known as gestational thrombocytopenia. It is a benign physiological phenomenon requiring no active treatment or diagnostic workup. C. Sepsis and disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), requiring hospitalization and intravenous antibiotics: Incorrect. Sepsis and DIC are severe, acute clinical emergencies. The patient would be highly symptomatic (fever, hypotension, tachycardia, bleeding, bruising), whereas she is entirely asymptomatic. D. Severe iron and vitamin B12 deficiencies causing bone marrow suppression: Incorrect. While iron deficiency is common and causes anemia , isolated mild leukocytosis and gestational thrombocytopenia are not signs of nutritional bone marrow suppression. E. Preeclampsia with HELLP syndrome, requiring immediate induction of labor: Incorrect. HELLP syndrome is a life-threatening complication characterized by severe hemolysis (elevated bilirubin, schistocytes), elevated liver enzymes (AST/ALT), and low platelets (<100,000/mcL), usually accompanied by severe hypertension and proteinuria. This patient is asymptomatic and her platelet drop is mild, typical of benign gestational thrombocytopenia.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
