    "topic": "Maternal Physiological Changes in (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 28-year-old G1P0 at 10 weeks gestation presents for her first prenatal visit. Her laboratory workup shows a thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) level of 0.33 uIU/mL (normal non-pregnant reference range: 0.45-4.50 uIU/mL) and a free thyroxine (free T4) level of 1.2 ng/dL (normal reference range: 0.7-1.9 ng/dL). She has no symptoms of hyperthyroidism or goiter. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for these laboratory values?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Subclinical Graves' disease, requiring immediate therapy with propylthiouracil.",
      "B": "Estrogen-induced downregulation of thyroid hormone receptors.",
      "C": "Normal first-trimester physiological change, where high levels of hCG (sharing an alpha subunit with TSH) stimulate the thyroid gland, leading to transient TSH suppression while free T4 remains normal.",
      "D": "Severe iodine deficiency causing a compensatory decrease in TSH.",
      "E": "Pituitary adenoma secreting hCG and suppressing TSH."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "hCG (sharing an alpha subunit with TSH) stimulate the thyroid gland, leading to transient TSH suppression while free T4 remains normal. \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Thyroid / Passage 11 \u25cf Explanation: A. Subclinical Graves' disease, requiring immediate therapy with propylthiouracil: Incorrect. Propylthiouracil is a hepatotoxic antithyroid drug used for overt hyperthyroidism. This patient has normal free T4 and is completely asymptomatic, indicating she is euthyroid. B. Estrogen-induced downregulation of thyroid hormone receptors: Incorrect. Estrogen actually increases thyroid-binding globulin (TBG) levels, which increases total T3 and T4, but free T4 (the active fraction) remains normal and receptors are not downregulated . C. Normal first-trimester physiological change, where high levels of hCG (sharing an alpha subunit with TSH) stimulate the thyroid gland, leading to transient TSH suppression while free T4 remains normal: Correct. Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) shares a structural glycoprotein alpha subunit with thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), luteinizing hormone (LH), and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) . Because of this structural homology, high circulating levels of hCG during the late first trimester (peaking around 9-10 weeks) directly bind to and stimulate maternal TSH receptors on the thyroid gland . This stimulates transient thyroid hormone release, which through negative feedback, causes a physiological suppression of maternal serum TSH . Free thyroxine (FT4) remains within the normal reference range, and the patient remains clinically euthyroid . D. Severe iodine deficiency causing a compensatory decrease in TSH: Incorrect. Iodine deficiency would cause a decrease in thyroid hormone production, which would trigger a compensatory  increase  in TSH (hypothyroidism), not  a decrease. 63 E. Pituitary adenoma secreting hCG and suppressing TSH: Incorrect. While a choriocarcinoma can secrete massive amounts of hCG, a pituitary adenoma is not a source of hCG, and this patient's pregnancy is the obvious and normal source of her hCG.",
