    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology & Teratogenicity:** Preconception maternal hyperglycemia (diabetic embryopathy) during organogenesis (weeks 3\u20138 of gestation) has severe teratogenic effects on the developing embryo. The most characteristic and specific anomaly associated with maternal pregestational diabetes is **caudal regression syndrome** (sacral agenesis leading to lower extremity hypoplasia, flaccid paralysis, urinary/fecal incontinence). Maternal diabetes increases the risk of caudal regression by >200-fold compared to the general population.\n- **Other Associated Anomalies:** Congenital heart defects (most commonly **transposition of the great arteries**, ventricular septal defects, and asymmetric septal hypertrophy/hypertrophic cardiomyopathy), neural tube defects (anencephaly, spina bifida), and neonatal complications (macrosomia, transient hypoglycemia secondary to fetal pancreatic beta-cell hyperplasia, hypocalcemia, polycythemia, respiratory distress syndrome due to insulin inhibition of surfactant).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Duodenal atresia:** Classically associated with Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome), presenting with the \"double bubble\" sign on abdominal radiography and polyhydramnios.\n  - **(C) Holoprosencephaly:** Failure of embryonic forebrain division into cerebral hemispheres, most commonly linked to Trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome), sonic hedgehog (*SHH*) gene mutations, or maternal alcohol use.\n  - **(D) Bilateral renal agenesis:** Results from failure of the ureteric buds to develop; leads to oligohydramnios and Potter sequence (flattened facies, clubbed feet, pulmonary hypoplasia).\n  - **(E) Hirschsprung disease:** Congenital megacolon caused by failure of neural crest cells to migrate into the myenteric (Auerbach) and submucosal (Meissner) plexuses of the distal rectosigmoid colon; highly associated with Down syndrome, not maternal diabetes.",
    "pearl": "**Caudal regression syndrome** (sacral agenesis, lower extremity weakness/clubfeet) is the most specific malformation of **maternal pregestational diabetes**. Cardiac defects (especially **transposition of the great arteries**) are the most common congenital heart malformations. Neonatal hypoglycemia occurs due to maternal hyperglycemia -fetal hyperinsulinemia.",
