    "rationale": "- **Folic Acid Requirements:** Folic acid (vitamin B9) is required for DNA synthesis and normal closure of the neural tube by embryonic day 28 (week 4 of gestation). \n- **Dosing Stratification:**\n  - **Standard Risk (General Population):** All women of childbearing age planning pregnancy should take **0.4 mg (400 mcg)** of folic acid daily starting at least 1 month prior to conception.\n  - **High Risk:** Women with a **prior pregnancy affected by a neural tube defect (NTD)** or women taking **anticonvulsants that antagonize folate metabolism (valproate, carbamazepine, phenytoin)** should take **4.0 mg (4000 mcg)** of folic acid daily (a 10-fold increase).\n- **Anticonvulsant Mechanism:** Valproate and carbamazepine inhibit intestinal folate absorption, impair folate metabolism, and directly increase the risk of spina bifida and myelomeningocele (risk ~1\u20132% with valproate).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) 0.04 mg:** Subtherapeutic and ineffective.\n  - **(C) 0.4 mg:** Standard preconception dose for women at average risk without prior NTDs or folate-antagonizing medications.\n  - **(D) 0.8 mg & (E) 1.0 mg:** Sometimes contained in over-the-counter prenatal vitamins, but insufficient for high-risk patients on valproate who require 4.0 mg/day.",
    "pearl": "Folic acid supplementation: **0.4 mg/day** for average-risk women; **4.0 mg/day** for high-risk women (prior child with NTD, family history, or taking folate antagonists like valproate/carbamazepine). Closure of neural tube occurs by the end of **week 4** of embryogenesis.",
