    "rationale": "- **Environmental Toxicology in Pregnancy:** **Methylmercury** is an organic mercury compound that biomagnifies up the aquatic food chain, accumulating in large, long-lived predatory marine species (shark, swordfish, king mackerel, tilefish).\n- **Placental Transfer & Fetal Neurotoxicity:** Methylmercury readily crosses the placenta and the fetal blood-brain barrier. The developing fetal central nervous system is exceptionally vulnerable because methylmercury disrupts neuronal migration, microtubule assembly, and protein synthesis. Severe prenatal exposure causes congenital Minamata disease, characterized by **microcephaly, blindness, sensorineural deafness, severe intellectual disability, cerebral palsy, and choreoathetosis**.\n- **Clinical Guidelines:** The FDA and ACOG recommend that pregnant and lactating women completely avoid apex predatory fish with high mercury levels (shark, swordfish, king mackerel, bigeye tuna) and choose low-mercury alternatives (canned light tuna, salmon, shrimp, tilapia) limited to 8\u201312 ounces per week.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) PCBs:** Chlorinated hydrocarbons found in industrial coolants and contaminated freshwater fish; prenatal exposure causes low birth weight, skin hyperpigmentation, and subtle cognitive deficits, but not the severe focal microcephaly/spasticity syndrome of methylmercury.\n  - **(B) Inorganic arsenic:** Found in contaminated well water; associated with spontaneous abortion, stillbirth, and infant mortality.\n  - **(D) Lead:** Crosses the placenta and accumulates in fetal bone and brain; causes spontaneous abortion, gestational hypertension, neurobehavioral deficits, and impaired IQ, but is classically acquired via old paint, lead pipes, or occupational exposure rather than predatory marine fish.\n  - **(E) Cadmium:** Heavy metal linked to smoking and industrial waste; causes renal tubular dysfunction and placental insufficiency, but does not bioaccumulate in apex marine fish like mercury.",
    "pearl": "**Methylmercury** bioaccumulates in apex predator fish (swordfish, shark, mackerel). Crosses the blood-brain barrier -**fetal neurotoxicity** (microcephaly, ataxia, deafness, intellectual disability, Minamata disease).",
