    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "- **First-Trimester Combined Screening:** Performed between weeks 11 and 13 6/7. Combines:\n  1. **Nuchal Translucency (NT):** Ultrasound measurement of fluid behind the fetal neck. Increased NT (>= 3.0 mm) is seen in chromosomal aneuploidies and congenital heart disease.\n  2. **Serum PAPP-A:** Produced by the trophoblast.\n  3. **Serum free beta-hCG:** Produced by syncytiotrophoblasts.\n- **Down Syndrome (Trisomy 21) Pattern:**\n  - **Nuchal translucency:** Increased (thickened)\n  - **Free beta-hCG:** Elevated (typically > 2.0 MoM)\n  - **PAPP-A:** Significantly decreased (typically < 0.4 MoM)\n- **Contrast with Other Trisomies:**\n  - **Trisomy 18 (Edwards syndrome):** Both PAPP-A and beta-hCG are markedly **decreased**.\n  - **Trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome):** Both PAPP-A and beta-hCG are markedly **decreased**.\n  - **Turner syndrome (45,X):** May present with marked cystic hygroma (extreme NT thickening), but biochemical screening typically shows decreased PAPP-A with normal or low hCG.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Turner syndrome:** Characterized by cystic hygroma, aortic coarctation, and streak ovaries, but does not classically produce the elevated beta-hCG pattern of Trisomy 21.\n  - **(B) Edwards syndrome & (C) Patau syndrome:** Both show **decreased** beta-hCG and decreased PAPP-A.\n  - **(E) Klinefelter syndrome:** Sex chromosome aneuploidy typically not identified by serum biochemical screening.",
    "pearl": "First-trimester screening for **Trisomy 21**:\n- **Nuchal Translucency:** Increased\n- **beta-hCG:** Increased (HIgh -Down syndrome)\n- **PAPP-A:** Decreased\nIn **Trisomy 18 and 13**, both beta-hCG and PAPP-A are decreased (Everything is low).",
