    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 39-year-old primigravida at 10 weeks gestation requests noninvasive prenatal screening (NIPS) using cell-free fetal DNA (cfDNA) analysis. The genetic counselor explains the biological origin and limitations of the genetic material evaluated in maternal blood. Cell-free fetal DNA circulating in maternal plasma predominantly originates from which of the following tissue sources?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Fetal nucleated red blood cells undergoing apoptosis in maternal spleen",
      "B": "Apoptotic syncytiotrophoblast and cytotrophoblast cells of the placenta",
      "C": "Fetal skin squames shed into amniotic fluid diffusing across membranes",
      "D": "Fetal renal tubular epithelial cells excreted into maternal microvasculature",
      "E": "Maternal decidual lymphocytes undergoing epigenetic reprogramming"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Biology of Cell-Free Fetal DNA (cfDNA):**\n  - Circulating \"fetal\" cell-free DNA makes up approximately 3\u201313% of the total cell-free DNA in maternal plasma starting around 10 weeks gestation.\n  - **Origin:** cfDNA does **NOT** originate directly from fetal cells or organs. Instead, it is released during apoptosis of **placental trophoblast cells (syncytiotrophoblasts and cytotrophoblasts)**.\n  - **Clinical Implication (Confined Placental Mosaicism):** Because cfDNA reflects the genetic makeup of the placenta rather than the fetus itself, a discrepancy called **confined placental mosaicism** can occur (e.g., abnormal placental karyotype with a normal fetus, or vice versa), leading to false-positive or false-negative results.\n  - **Diagnostic Confirmation:** cfDNA is a *screening* test with high sensitivity and specificity (> 99% for Trisomy 21). Any positive cfDNA result must be confirmed with definitive invasive diagnostic testing: **Chorionic Villus Sampling (CVS)** at 10\u201313 weeks or **Amniocentesis** at 15\u201320 weeks.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Fetal nucleated RBCs:** Fetal cells do cross into maternal blood, but they are extremely rare (1 in 1,000,000) and are not the primary source of circulating cfDNA fragments.\n  - **(C) Skin squames & (D) Renal cells:** Amniotic fluid cells do not directly contribute to the maternal plasma cfDNA pool in significant quantities.\n  - **(E) Decidual lymphocytes:** Maternal in origin; maternal cfDNA comprises the remaining ~90% of total plasma cfDNA, but does not represent the fetal fraction.",
    "pearl": "**Cell-free fetal DNA (cfDNA)** in maternal circulation originates from **apoptotic placental trophoblasts** (not the fetus directly). Because of this, **confined placental mosaicism** can produce false-positive or false-negative screening results. Must confirm with **amniocentesis or CVS**.",
