    "topic": "Lecture 1.1: Pregnancy, Parturition and the Puerperium",
    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 32-year-old woman (G3P2) at 32 weeks gestation presents with abdominal fullness, rapid abdominal enlargement over the past 3 weeks, and progressive shortness of breath when lying flat. Fundal height measures 38 cm (significantly advanced for gestational age). Ultrasound reveals an amniotic fluid index (AFI) of 32 cm (normal: 5\u201324 cm) and a deepest vertical pocket of 12 cm, establishing a diagnosis of polyhydramnios. Detailed anatomical survey of the fetal gastrointestinal tract shows fluid distension of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum with no distal bowel gas (\"double bubble\" sign). Which of the following fetal conditions is the most likely etiology of this patient's polyhydramnios?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Renal tubular dysgenesis",
      "B": "Congenital diaphragmatic hernia",
      "C": "Duodenal atresia",
      "D": "Posterior urethral valves",
      "E": "Sacrococcygeal teratoma"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Amniotic Fluid Dynamics:** Amniotic fluid volume is a dynamic balance between fluid production (**fetal urination**, lung fluid secretion) and fluid removal (**fetal swallowing**, intramembranous absorption across placenta).\n- **Polyhydramnios (AFI > 24 cm or DVP >= 8 cm):** Results from either **decreased fetal swallowing/absorption** or **increased fetal urination/cardiac output**:\n  1. **Decreased Swallowing/GI Obstruction:** **Duodenal atresia** (failure of recanalization of the duodenal lumen at week 8\u201310; strongly associated with Down syndrome), **esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula**, micrognathia, or cleft palate.\n  2. **Neurologic/Neuromuscular Disorders:** **Anencephaly** (absence of forebrain/swallowing centers), Werdnig-Hoffmann disease (spinal muscular atrophy), myotonic dystrophy.\n  3. **High Fetal Cardiac Output / Polyuria:** Maternal diabetes mellitus (fetal hyperglycemia -> osmotic polyuria), fetal anemia (parvovirus B19, fetomaternal hemorrhage), twin-twin transfusion syndrome (recipient twin).\n- **Imaging Finding:** The **\"double bubble\" sign** represents air/fluid distending the stomach and the proximal blinded duodenum, separated by the pyloric sphincter\u2014pathognomonic for **duodenal atresia**.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Renal tubular dysgenesis & (D) Posterior urethral valves:** Cause decreased or absent fetal urine output, leading to **oligohydramnios**, not polyhydramnios.\n  - **(B) Congenital diaphragmatic hernia:** Associated with polyhydramnios if gastric compression impairs swallowing, but typically presents with bowel loops in the hemithorax and mediastinal shift, not the classic \"double bubble\" sign.\n  - **(E) Sacrococcygeal teratoma:** Can cause high-output cardiac failure and polyhydramnios, but does not present with the duodenal \"double bubble\" sign.",
    "pearl": "**Polyhydramnios** (AFI 24 cm): Impaired swallowing (**esophageal/duodenal atresia**, **anencephaly**) or fetal polyuria (**maternal diabetes**). Duodenal atresia = failure to recanalize duodenal lumen, **\"double bubble\" sign**, associated with **Down syndrome**.",
