    "stem": "A 24-year-old primigravida at 39 weeks gestation is admitted to the labor and delivery unit in active labor. At 08:00, her cervix is 6 cm dilated, 90% effaced, and the fetal vertex is at 0 station; membranes are artificially ruptured. Over the next four hours, an external tocodynamometer records painful contractions occurring every 2 to 3 minutes, and an intrauterine pressure catheter (IUPC) reveals adequate uterine contractions generating 240 Montevideo units (adequate defined as > 200 MVUs). At 12:00, repeat sterile vaginal examination reveals the cervix remains 6 cm dilated, 90% effaced, and the vertex remains at 0 station. Fetal heart rate tracing remains Category I (normal baseline, moderate variability, positive accelerations, no decelerations). What is the diagnosis and the most appropriate next step in management?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Normal latent phase of labor; reassure and re-examine in 4 hours",
      "B": "Protracted active phase of labor; initiate intravenous oxytocin augmentation",
      "C": "Active phase arrest of labor; proceed with Cesarean delivery",
      "D": "Secondary arrest of descent; perform operative vaginal vacuum delivery",
      "E": "Hypertonic uterine dysfunction; administer subcutaneous terbutaline"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Stages of Labor & Active Phase Dynamics:**\n  - **Stage 1:** From onset of regular contractions to complete cervical dilatation (10 cm).\n    - *Latent Phase:* 0 to 5 cm dilatation (slow, variable progress).\n    - *Active Phase:* Begins at **>= 6 cm dilatation** (rapid cervical change, typically >= 1 cm/hr in nulliparas).\n- **ACOG/SMFM Definitions of Labor Abnormalities:**\n  - **Active Phase Arrest:** Defined as cervical dilatation **>= 6 cm** with ruptured membranes and **no cervical change after:**\n    1. **>= 4 hours of adequate uterine contractions** (> 200 Montevideo units via IUPC), OR\n    2. **>= 6 hours of inadequate contractions** despite oxytocin augmentation.\n- **Management:** This patient has reached 6 cm, has ruptured membranes, and has had **zero cervical change over 4 hours despite demonstrably adequate contractions (240 MVUs)**. This meets the criteria for **active phase arrest**. The recommended management is **Cesarean delivery**.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Latent phase:** Latent phase ends at 6 cm; the patient is in the active phase.\n  - **(B) Protraction & oxytocin:** Protraction is slow progress (< 1 cm/hr), whereas arrest is complete cessation of progress. Because contractions are already adequate (> 200 MVUs), additional oxytocin will not overcome cephalopelvic disproportion and increases the risk of uterine rupture or fetal distress.\n  - **(D) Arrest of descent:** Pertains to the **second stage of labor** (after full 10 cm dilation). Operative vaginal delivery (vacuum/forceps) cannot be performed through a 6 cm cervix.\n  - **(E) Terbutaline:** A tocolytic used to stop contractions in acute uterine tachysystole; not indicated here.",
    "pearl": "**Active phase of labor** begins at **>= 6 cm** dilation. **Active phase arrest** = >= 6 cm with ruptured membranes AND **no cervical change for >= 4 hours with adequate contractions** (200 MVUs) or **>= 6 hours with oxytocin**. Management: **Cesarean delivery**.",
