    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 33-year-old woman with gestational diabetes mellitus delivers a vigorous male infant at 40 weeks gestation. After the fetal head delivers easily over the perineum, the head suddenly retracts tightly against the maternal perineum (positive \"turtle sign\"). Gentle downward traction fails to deliver the anterior shoulder. The obstetric team recognizes shoulder dystocia. Which of the following initial maneuvers should be immediately performed to resolve this obstetric emergency?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Hyperflexion of the maternal thighs against the abdomen (McRoberts maneuver) combined with suprapubic pressure",
      "B": "Vigorous manual fundal pressure directed toward the pelvic inlet",
      "C": "Immediate cephalic replacement of the head followed by emergency Cesarean delivery (Zavanelli maneuver)",
      "D": "Forceful outward rotational traction on the fetal head and neck",
      "E": "Intentional bilateral surgical clavicular osteotomy"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Shoulder Dystocia Pathophysiology:**\n  - An obstetric emergency occurring when the anterior fetal shoulder impacts behind the maternal **pubic symphysis** (or less commonly, the posterior shoulder impacts against the sacral promontory) after delivery of the head.\n  - **Risk Factors:** Fetal macrosomia (birth weight > 4000\u20134500 g, especially in maternal diabetes), maternal obesity, prior shoulder dystocia, excessive gestational weight gain, post-term pregnancy, protracted second stage.\n  - **Sign:** **\"Turtle sign\"** (retraction of the delivered fetal head against the maternal perineum).\n- **First-Line Management:**\n  1. **Call for Help & Stop Maternal Pushing.**\n  2. **McRoberts Maneuver:** Hyperflexion and abduction of the maternal hips against her abdomen. This flattens the lumbar lordosis, rotates the pubic symphysis cephalad, and widens the anteroposterior pelvic diameter.\n  3. **Suprapubic Pressure:** An assistant applies downward and lateral pressure on the maternal suprapubic region (over the anterior fetal shoulder) to adduct the shoulder and dislodge it under the pubic symphysis.\n- **Dangers of Prohibited Actions:**\n  - **Fundal Pressure is STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED:** Exerting fundal pressure further impacts the shoulder into the pubic bone, drastically increasing the risk of **fetal brachial plexus injury (Erb-Duchenne palsy, C5-C6)** and maternal **uterine rupture**.\n- **Secondary Maneuvers (if McRoberts fails):** Delivery of the posterior arm, rotational maneuvers (Woods corkscrew, Rubin), episiotomy. The **Zavanelli maneuver** (pushing the head back into the vagina for Cesarean) is a catastrophic last resort.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Fundal pressure:** Absolute board trap; strictly contraindicated due to risk of uterine rupture and brachial plexus avulsion.\n  - **(C) Zavanelli maneuver:** Desperate last-line maneuver, only used after all internal and rotational maneuvers have completely failed.\n  - **(D) Forceful traction:** Causes traction injury to roots of C5\u2013C6, resulting in Erb palsy (\"waiter's tip\" deformity).\n  - **(E) Surgical clavicular osteotomy:** Morbid last-resort intervention.",
    "pearl": "**Shoulder Dystocia:** First-line actions are **McRoberts maneuver** (maternal hip hyperflexion) + **suprapubic pressure**. **FUNDAL PRESSURE IS CONTRAINDICATED** (causes uterine rupture and brachial plexus injury / **Erb palsy C5-C6**).",
