    "stem": "A 36-year-old woman (G2P1) at 36 weeks gestation presents for a routine prenatal visit. Leopold maneuvers and bedside ultrasound confirm a singleton fetus in breech presentation with the fetal thighs flexed at the hips and the lower legs extended straight upward over the anterior trunk and face. Which specific type of breech presentation is described, and what is the standard management option at 37 weeks gestation?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Complete breech; immediate Cesarean delivery at 36 weeks",
      "B": "Frank breech; external cephalic version (ECV) offered at >= 37 weeks gestation",
      "C": "Incomplete (footling) breech; trial of labor with operative forceps extraction",
      "D": "Kneeling breech; internal podalic version under general anesthesia",
      "E": "Frank breech; vacuum-assisted vaginal delivery at onset of labor"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Classification of Breech Presentations:**\n  1. **Frank Breech (50\u201370%):** Hips are **flexed**, and knees are **extended** (feet are up by the ears/face). This is the most common breech presentation.\n  2. **Complete Breech (5\u201310%):** Hips are **flexed**, and knees are **flexed** (cannonball/cross-legged position).\n  3. **Incomplete / Footling Breech (10\u201330%):** One or both hips are extended, with one or both feet or knees presenting below the buttocks into the birth canal (highest risk of **umbilical cord prolapse**).\n- **Management:**\n  - Most fetuses in breech presentation spontaneously rotate to cephalic before 36\u201337 weeks.\n  - At **>= 37 weeks gestation**, if breech persists and there are no contraindications (e.g., placenta previa, prior classical Cesarean, non-reassuring fetal status), patients should be offered an **External Cephalic Version (ECV)**.\n  - **ECV:** Manual transabdominal manipulation of the fetus into a cephalic presentation under tocolysis and ultrasound guidance to permit a trial of vaginal delivery. If ECV fails or is declined, planned Cesarean delivery at 39 weeks is standard.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Complete breech:** Knees are flexed in complete breech, not extended. Furthermore, immediate delivery at 36 weeks is premature.\n  - **(C) Incomplete breech & trial of labor:** Footling breech has a high risk of cord prolapse and head entrapment; vaginal trial is avoided.\n  - **(D) Internal podalic version:** Only performed for delivery of a non-cephalic *second* twin in a twin gestation, never for a singleton vertex/breech.\n  - **(E) Vacuum delivery:** Vacuum extractors must **NEVER** be applied to a non-vertex presentation (cannot be applied to buttocks or face).",
    "pearl": "**Breech Presentations:** **Frank** = hips flexed, knees extended (feet at ears, most common). **Complete** = hips & knees flexed. **Footling** = feet presenting (high cord prolapse risk). Offer **External Cephalic Version (ECV)** at **>= 37 weeks**.",
