    "stem": "A 32-year-old primipara (G1P1) delivers a 4,200 g male infant vaginally after a prolonged second stage of labor complicated by mild shoulder dystocia. Ten minutes after uncomplicated delivery of the placenta, the nurse notes continuous, heavy vaginal bleeding saturating multiple abdominal pads. Physical examination reveals an enlarged, soft, poorly contracted, \"boggy\" uterine fundus palpable 3 cm above the umbilicus. Vital signs demonstrate a heart rate of 115/min and blood pressure of 92/56 mm Hg. What is the most likely diagnosis and the first-line medical intervention?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Retained placental fragments; immediate surgical dilatation and curettage",
      "B": "Uterine atony; bimanual uterine massage and intravenous oxytocin",
      "C": "Cervical laceration; immediate speculum inspection and suture repair",
      "D": "Uterine inversion; manual replacement of the uterine fundus",
      "E": "Coagulopathy from amniotic fluid embolism; infusion of cryoprecipitate"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Postpartum Hemorrhage (PPH) Definition:** Cumulative blood loss >= 1000 mL or bleeding accompanied by signs/symptoms of hypovolemia within 24 hours of delivery.\n- **Etiology \u2014 The \"4 Ts\" of PPH:**\n  1. **Tone (Uterine Atony):** Accounts for **70\u201380% of all PPH cases** (by far the most common cause).\n  2. **Trauma:** Lacerations of cervix, vagina, perineum, or uterine rupture.\n  3. **Tissue:** Retained placenta or membranes, placenta accreta.\n  4. **Thrombin:** Coagulopathy (DIC, von Willebrand disease).\n- **Pathophysiology of Uterine Atony:** Normally, after placental detachment, rhythmic contraction of interlacing myometrial smooth muscle fibers constricts spiral arterioles (the \"physiologic ligatures\" or \"living ligatures\"). When the myometrium fails to contract, spiral arterioles bleed profusely into the uterine cavity.\n- **Risk Factors:** Uterine overdistension (macrosomia, polyhydramnios, multiple gestation), labor fatigue (prolonged labor), chorioamnionitis, multiparity, tocolytic exposure.\n- **Physical Exam Findings:** Soft, poorly contracted, enlarged, **boggy uterus** palpable above the umbilicus.\n- **First-Line Therapy:**\n  1. **Bimanual uterine massage** to physically stimulate mechanical contraction.\n  2. **Intravenous Oxytocin:** First-line uterotonic agent that activates Gq-protein coupled receptors on myometrial cells, increasing intracellular calcium and driving tonic myometrial contraction.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Retained placenta:** Usually presents with a firm uterus or persistent bleeding after incomplete placental delivery on inspection; initial management of atony does not start with D&C.\n  - **(C) Laceration:** Characterized by persistent vaginal bleeding despite a **firm, well-contracted uterus**.\n  - **(D) Uterine inversion:** Presents with severe neurogenic shock and a smooth round mass visible in the vagina, with the uterine fundus absent from the abdomen.\n  - **(E) Amniotic fluid embolism:** Characterized by sudden cardiogenic shock, hypoxemic respiratory failure, and consumptive coagulopathy/DIC during labor or immediately postpartum.",
    "pearl": "**Postpartum Hemorrhage (PPH)**: **Uterine atony is the #1 cause (~80%)**. Presents with a **soft, boggy, enlarged uterus**. First-line management: **Bimanual uterine massage + IV Oxytocin**.",
