    "topic": "Lecture 1.1: Pregnancy, Parturition and the Puerperium",
    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 34-year-old woman (G4P3) with a history of two prior low transverse Cesarean deliveries and a current pregnancy complicated by complete placenta previa delivers a vigorous male infant via repeat Cesarean delivery at 36 weeks gestation. Following delivery of the infant, the placenta fails to separate spontaneously after 30 minutes. Gentle cord traction meets firm resistance, and manual extraction attempts result in severe, brisk hemorrhage without identifying a cleavage plane between the placenta and the uterine wall. Histopathologic examination of the uterine specimen following emergent hysterectomy demonstrates complete absence of the decidua basalis with chorionic villi directly attaching to the underlying myometrium. What is the diagnosis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Placenta accreta",
      "B": "Placenta increta",
      "C": "Placenta percreta",
      "D": "Placental abruption",
      "E": "Choriocarcinoma"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Placenta Accreta Spectrum (PAS):** Characterized by abnormal adherence and invasion of placental trophoblasts into the uterine wall due to a **deficiency or absence of the intervening decidua basalis** and the Nitabuch layer, typically at the site of a prior uterine scar.\n- **Spectrum Classification by Depth of Invasion:**\n  1. **Placenta Accreta (~75\u201380% of PAS):** Chorionic villi **attach directly to the myometrium** without invading into it (absence of intervening decidua).\n  2. **Placenta Increta (~15% of PAS):** Chorionic villi **invade deep into the myometrium**.\n  3. **Placenta Percreta (~5% of PAS):** Chorionic villi **invade completely through the myometrium, penetrating the uterine serosa**; can invade adjacent pelvic structures (most commonly the **posterior bladder wall**, leading to gross hematuria).\n- **Major Risk Factors:**\n  - **Prior Cesarean delivery** combined with current **placenta previa**. A patient with placenta previa and 1 prior Cesarean has a ~10\u201325% risk of accreta; with 2 prior Cesareans, the risk rises to ~40%; with >= 3 prior Cesareans, the risk exceeds 60%.\n  - Other risk factors: prior uterine curettage, myomectomy, maternal age > 35.\n- **Clinical Presentation:** **Retained placenta (> 30 minutes)** after delivery; attempts at manual placental separation fail because no natural cleavage plane exists, causing massive, life-threatening postpartum hemorrhage.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Placenta increta:** Villi invade *into* the myometrium, rather than simply attaching to its surface.\n  - **(C) Placenta percreta:** Villi invade *through* the myometrial serosa into surrounding organs (e.g., bladder).\n  - **(D) Placental abruption:** Premature separation of a normally implanted placenta prior to delivery, causing painful bleeding and retroplacental hematoma.\n  - **(E) Choriocarcinoma:** Malignant gestational trophoblastic neoplasm without chorionic villi, composed of sheets of anaplastic cytotrophoblasts and syncytiotrophoblasts with high beta-hCG.",
    "pearl": "**Placenta Accreta Spectrum:**\n- **Accreta:** Villi **attach** directly to myometrium (defective decidua basalis).\n- **Increta:** Villi **invade into** myometrium.\n- **Percreta:** Villi **penetrate through** myometrium/serosa into bladder.\nGreatest risk factor: **Prior Cesarean section + Placenta previa**.",
