    "stem": "A 26-year-old primipara who is 3 weeks postpartum presents with a 24-hour history of severe right breast pain, chills, and fever. She is exclusively breastfeeding her newborn but reports that nursing on the right side has been excruciating due to a painful crack on her nipple. Her temperature is 38.6\u00b0C (101.5\u00b0F), pulse is 100/min, and blood pressure is 114/70 mm Hg. Physical examination of the right breast reveals a well-demarcated, wedge-shaped area of erythema, induration, and intense tenderness in the upper outer quadrant. There is no fluctuant mass or purulent nipple discharge. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism, and what is the essential clinical management recommendation regarding breastfeeding?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "*Streptococcus pyogenes*; discontinue breastfeeding immediately and discard all pumped milk",
      "B": "*Staphylococcus aureus*; continue breastfeeding or pumping from both breasts while taking oral antibiotics",
      "C": "*Candida albicans*; cease nursing on the affected breast until topical nystatin therapy is complete",
      "D": "*Escherichia coli*; perform immediate incision and drainage in the emergency department",
      "E": "*Staphylococcus epidermidis*; administer intravenous vancomycin and suppress lactation with bromocriptine"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Lactational Mastitis:**\n  - **Pathogenesis:** Occurs when milk stasis (clogged duct, engorgement, incomplete emptying) provides a rich culture medium, and skin breakdown (cracked nipple) allows entry of bacteria from the infant's nasopharynx or maternal skin into the breast ducts and periductal connective tissue.\n  - **Microbiology:** **Staphylococcus aureus** is the most common pathogen (> 80% of cases, including MSSA and increasingly community-acquired MRSA). Other pathogens include coagulase-negative staphylococci and streptococci.\n  - **Clinical Presentation:** Unilateral, localized, wedge-shaped sector of erythema, heat, tenderness, and edema, accompanied by systemic fever (> 38.3\u00b0C), chills, and myalgias.\n- **Management Principles:**\n  1. **Antibiotic Therapy:** Empiric treatment targeting penicillinase-producing *S. aureus*: oral **dicloxacillin** or **cephalexin** (or clindamycin/trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole if MRSA is suspected).\n  2. **CONTINUE BREASTFEEDING / PUMPING:** **The single most important non-pharmacologic intervention is frequent, complete emptying of the breast.** Mothers MUST be instructed to **continue breastfeeding or pumping frequently (every 2\u20133 hours)**, starting on the affected breast if tolerated. Breast milk is completely safe for the healthy term infant. Stopping breastfeeding promotes further milk stasis, dramatically increasing the risk of progression to a **breast abscess** (which presents as a fluctuant mass requiring ultrasound-guided aspiration or incision and drainage).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Ceasing breastfeeding:** Stopping nursing is the classic wrong answer; it causes severe engorgement and promotes abscess formation.\n  - **(C) *Candida albicans*:** Presents with shiny, flaky nipple skin and deep shooting breast pain without focal erythema or fever; does not cause wedge-shaped acute mastitis.\n  - **(D) *E. coli* & Incision/drainage:** *E. coli* is rare; incision and drainage is indicated only for a fluctuant breast *abscess*, not uncomplicated mastitis.\n  - **(E) Bromocriptine:** Suppressing lactation with dopamine agonists is obsolete and dangerous (associated with stroke and seizures).",
    "pearl": "**Lactational Mastitis:** Caused by **Staphylococcus aureus** entering cracked nipples with milk stasis. Treat with oral **dicloxacillin or cephalexin** and **CONTINUE BREASTFEEDING/PUMPING** (emptying the breast prevents progression to a fluctuant **breast abscess**).",
