    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 30-year-old woman (G1P1) who underwent an urgent Cesarean delivery for active phase arrest 48 hours ago develops a temperature of 39.1\u00b0C (102.4\u00b0F), lower abdominal pain, and chills. Physical examination reveals a tachycardic patient with exquisite uterine fundal tenderness on bimanual examination. Her Cesarean Pfannenstiel incision is clean, dry, and intact with no erythema or induration. Pelvic examination reveals copious foul-smelling lochia. Complete blood count shows a leukocyte count of 22,000/uL with 14% band forms. What is the single greatest risk factor for this condition, and what is the first-line intravenous antimicrobial regimen?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Prolonged rupture of membranes; IV ampicillin monotherapy",
      "B": "Cesarean delivery; IV clindamycin plus gentamicin",
      "C": "Gestational diabetes; IV vancomycin plus metronidazole",
      "D": "Internal fetal heart monitoring; IV ciprofloxacin plus doxycycline",
      "E": "Multiple vaginal examinations; IV ceftriaxone plus azithromycin"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Postpartum Endometritis:**\n  - Infection of the uterine decidua and myometrium, typically developing 2 to 3 days postpartum.\n  - **Major Risk Factors:** **Cesarean delivery is by far the single most significant risk factor** (20- to 30-fold increased risk compared to vaginal delivery). Additional risk factors include prolonged rupture of membranes, chorioamnionitis, prolonged labor, multiple digital vaginal examinations, and internal fetal monitoring.\n  - **Microbiology:** **Polymicrobial infection** involving endogenous cervicovaginal flora that ascend into the uterine cavity:\n    - Anaerobes (*Bacteroides*, *Peptostreptococcus*, *Prevotella*)\n    - Aerobic gram-positive cocci (Group B *Streptococcus*, enterococci, *Staphylococcus*)\n    - Aerobic gram-negative bacilli (*E. coli*, *Klebsiella*)\n  - **Clinical Presentation:** Fever (> 38.0\u00b0C), tachycardia, **uterine fundal tenderness**, and **foul-smelling, purulent lochia**.\n- **First-Line Antimicrobial Regimen:**\n  - **Intravenous Clindamycin plus Gentamicin:**\n    - **Clindamycin:** Provides excellent coverage against **gram-positive organisms and anaerobes** (including *Bacteroides fragilis*).\n    - **Gentamicin:** Provides potent coverage against **aerobic gram-negative bacilli** (*E. coli*, *Proteus*, *Klebsiella*).\n    - (Ampicillin is added if enterococcal infection is suspected or fever fails to resolve after 48 hours).\n  - Therapy is continued until the patient is afebrile for >= 24\u201348 hours and symptom-free.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Ampicillin monotherapy:** Lacks coverage against resistant gram-negative rods and anaerobes.\n  - **(C) Vancomycin + Metronidazole:** Lacks broad aerobic gram-negative coverage (e.g., *E. coli*).\n  - **(D) Ciprofloxacin + Doxycycline & (E) Ceftriaxone + Azithromycin:** Used for outpatient pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), completely inadequate for polymicrobial inpatient postpartum endometritis.",
    "pearl": "**Postpartum Endometritis:** Most common complication after **Cesarean delivery** (single biggest risk factor). Presents with **fever, uterine tenderness, and foul-smelling lochia**. Gold-standard treatment: **IV Clindamycin** (covers anaerobes/gram +) + **Gentamicin** (covers gram - bacilli).",
