    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 29-year-old primipara presents to her obstetrician 4 weeks postpartum for a routine visit. She is accompanied by her husband, who expresses concern that she is constantly crying, irritable, and struggling to care for their baby. The patient admits that for the past 3 weeks, she has experienced pervasive sadness, feelings of overwhelming inadequacy as a mother, severe fatigue, insomnia even when the infant is sleeping, loss of appetite, and an inability to feel joy or bond with her newborn. She denies auditory or visual hallucinations and denies thoughts of harming herself or her child. Which of the following conditions is the most accurate diagnosis, and what is the first-line pharmacotherapy?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Postpartum blues; reassurance and supportive outpatient follow-up",
      "B": "Postpartum depression; sertraline",
      "C": "Postpartum psychosis; emergent inpatient hospitalization and lithium",
      "D": "Sheehan syndrome; oral levothyroxine and hydrocortisone replacement",
      "E": "Generalized anxiety disorder; buspirone monotherapy"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Differential Diagnosis of Postpartum Mood Disorders:**\n  1. **Postpartum \"Baby Blues\":**\n     - *Prevalence & Onset:* Extremely common (50\u201385% of women); begins within **2 to 3 days postpartum**, peaks around day 4\u20135.\n     - *Course:* **Resolves spontaneously within 14 days (2 weeks).**\n     - *Symptoms:* Mild tearfulness, emotional lability, anxiety, irritability, insomnia; mother maintains full ability to bond with and care for the newborn.\n     - *Management:* **Reassurance and supportive care.**\n  2. **Postpartum Depression (PPD):**\n     - *Prevalence & Onset:* Affects 10\u201315% of women; onset typically within **4 to 6 weeks** (can occur anytime within the first 12 months postpartum).\n     - *Diagnostic Criteria:* Meets standard DSM-5 criteria for **Major Depressive Episode**: >= 5 of 9 SIGECAPS symptoms present for **>= 2 consecutive weeks**, including depressed mood or anhedonia. Characterized by feelings of worthlessness, guilt regarding maternal capability, impaired bonding, fatigue, insomnia, and executive dysfunction.\n     - *Management:* First-line treatment is **psychotherapy (CBT) and/or SSRIs (Sertraline is first-line** due to low excretion into breast milk). Severe refractory PPD can be treated with neuroactive steroid GABA-A receptor modulators (**brexanolone, zuranolone**).\n  3. **Postpartum Psychosis:**\n     - Rare medical emergency (0.1\u20130.2%); presents with delusions, hallucinations, disorganization, and high risk of infanticide.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Postpartum blues:** Blues resolves by 2 weeks; symptoms lasting > 2 weeks with pervasive guilt and anhedonia represent clinical depression.\n  - **(C) Postpartum psychosis:** Characterized by overt delusions, bizarre beliefs, and hallucinations.\n  - **(D) Sheehan syndrome:** Ischemic pituitary necrosis causing agalactia, amenorrhea, and panhypopituitarism; does not present primarily as isolated unipolar depression.\n  - **(E) Generalized anxiety disorder:** Overlooks the dominant major depressive syndrome and anhedonia.",
    "pearl": "- **Postpartum Blues:** Peaks day 4\u20135, **resolves by 2 weeks** (14 days). Reassurance.\n- **Postpartum Depression:** Onset within 4\u20136 weeks, lasts **2 weeks**, SIGECAPS criteria, impaired bonding. First-line SSRI: **Sertraline** (minimal breast milk secretion).\n- **Postpartum Psychosis:** Delusions/hallucinations -**hospitalize immediately** (risk of infanticide).",
