    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "embryo cardiac activity at 6 weeks gestation, which is one week earlier than TAUS \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Dating the Pregnancy Ultrasound / Passage 311 / Slide 28 \u25cf Explanation: A. TAUS can detect a gestational sac and embryo cardiac activity approximately one week earlier than TVUS: Incorrect. Transvaginal ultrasound (TVUS) detects early structures one week earlier than TAUS, not the other way around. B. TVUS can detect a gestational sac at 5 weeks gestation and embryo cardiac activity at 6 weeks gestation, which is one week earlier than TAUS: Correct. Ultrasound is the most accurate method of dating a pregnancy. Due to the proximity of the transducer to the pelvic organs, TVUS can visualize pregnancy structures earlier than TAUS: TVUS detects a gestational sac at 5 weeks and an embryo with cardiac activity at 6 weeks. TAUS detects these at 6 weeks and 7 weeks, respectively (exactly one week later). C. Both TAUS and TVUS can reliably detect a gestational sac at 4 weeks gestation: Incorrect. At 4 weeks, a gestational sac is generally not visible on TAUS, and only rarely as a tiny bubble on TVUS. Reliable detection requires 5 weeks for TVUS. D. TAUS can detect a gestational sac at 5 weeks gestation and cardiac activity at 6 weeks, whereas TVUS requires 7 weeks for both: Incorrect. This reverses the capabilities of the two ultrasound modalities. E. TVUS is less accurate than TAUS for dating in the first trimester because the field of view is too narrow: Incorrect. TVUS is highly accurate and is the preferred modality for first-trimester dating, especially prior to 10-12 weeks.",
