    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 41-year-old female at 16 weeks gestation undergoes second-trimester maternal serum quadruple screening. The results show a pattern that is highly suggestive of fetal Down Syndrome (Trisomy 21). Which of the following Quad screen analyte patterns represents this finding?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Low human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), high inhibin A, low alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), low unconjugated estriol (uE3)",
      "B": "High hCG, low inhibin A, high AFP, high uE3",
      "C": "High hCG, high inhibin A, low AFP, low uE3",
      "D": "Low hCG, low inhibin A, low AFP, low uE3",
      "E": "High hCG, high inhibin A, high AFP, low uE3"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "\u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Aneuploidy screening / Slide Analytes in screening / Passage 308 / 312 / Slide 48 / 49 / 50 \u25cf Explanation: A. Low human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), high inhibin A, low alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), low unconjugated estriol (uE3): Incorrect. In Down Syndrome, hCG is characteristically high, not low. B. High hCG, low inhibin A, high AFP, high uE3: Incorrect. In Down Syndrome, inhibin A is high, while AFP and uE3 are low. C. High hCG, high inhibin A, low AFP, low uE3: Correct. Second-trimester quadruple screening is performed between 15 and 22 weeks gestation. Down Syndrome is the only aneuploidy where maternal serum hCG and inhibin A are elevated (\"the markers that go UP\"). The other two markers, AFP and unconjugated estriol (uE3), are characteristically low. 26 D. Low hCG, low inhibin A, low AFP, low uE3: Incorrect. This profile of all-low markers is classic for Trisomy 18 (Edwards Syndrome). E. High hCG, high inhibin A, high AFP, low uE3: Incorrect. AFP is characteristically decreased in Down Syndrome. Elevated AFP in the second trimester is associated with neural tube defects.",
    "pearl": "",
