    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 31-year-old female at 28 weeks gestation presents with severe headache and elevated blood pressure. A review of her medications reveals she was recently prescribed an antihypertensive medication by an outside provider who was unaware of her pregnancy. An ultrasound is performed and shows an amniotic fluid index (AFI) of 3 cm with no evidence of membrane rupture. Which of the following medications is most likely responsible for this amniotic fluid profile and is contraindicated in pregnancy?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Methyldopa",
      "B": "Labetalol",
      "C": "Nifedipine",
      "D": "Lisinopril",
      "E": "Hydralazine"
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "\u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Oligohydramnios / Passage 309 \u25cf Explanation: A. Methyldopa: Incorrect. Methyldopa is a centrally acting alpha-2 agonist that is safe and commonly used for chronic hypertension in pregnancy. B. Labetalol: Incorrect. Labetalol is a beta-blocker that is a first-line, safe option for hypertension in pregnancy. C. Nifedipine: Incorrect. Nifedipine is a calcium channel blocker that is safe and frequently used for blood pressure control in pregnant patients. D. Lisinopril: Correct. The lecture slides identify drug causes of oligohydramnios, which include prostaglandin synthetase inhibitors (like indomethacin) and ACE inhibitors. Lisinopril is an ACE inhibitor. ACE inhibitors are strictly contraindicated in the second and third trimesters of pregnancy because they interfere with fetal renal development, leading to fetal renal failure, anuria, and severe oligohydramnios (part of ACE inhibitor fetopathy). E. Hydralazine: Incorrect. Hydralazine is a direct vasodilator that is safe and used for acute management of severe hypertension in pregnancy.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
