    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "polymicrobial etiology including anaerobic and aerobic bacteria \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Postpartum endometritis / Passage 316 \u25cf Explanation: A. Postpartum endometritis; primary risk factor is Cesarean delivery; polymicrobial etiology including anaerobic and aerobic bacteria: Correct. Postpartum endometritis is an infection of the decidua and underlying myometrium/parametrium that typically develops 2 to 10 days postpartum. The diagnostic features are postpartum fever (>=38 degrees C or 100.4 degrees F), lower abdominal/pelvic pain, uterine tenderness, and foul-smelling lochia. The single most important risk factor is Cesarean delivery (especially after a trial of labor or prolonged rupture of membranes), which carries a significantly higher risk than vaginal delivery. The infection is characteristically polymicrobial, arising from ascending vaginal flora, and includes Group A/B Strep, Staphylococcus, Enterococcus, E. coli, Klebsiella, and various anaerobes. B. Postpartum endometritis; primary risk factor is vaginal delivery; monomicrobial etiology due to Streptococcus pyogenes: Incorrect. Endometritis is relatively uncommon after vaginal delivery (making Cesarean the primary risk factor), and it is characteristically polymicrobial, not monomicrobial. C. Mastitis; primary risk factor is breastfeeding; etiology due to Staphylococcus aureus: Incorrect. This patient's symptoms are localized to the pelvis (abdominal pain, uterine tenderness) rather than the breast, ruling out mastitis. 41 D. Pelvic inflammatory disease; primary risk factor is intrauterine device insertion; etiology due to Chlamydia trachomatis: Incorrect. While clinically similar, pelvic infection occurring in the immediate postpartum period is termed postpartum endometritis, which has a distinct polymicrobial etiology and is related to the route of delivery. E. Postpartum urinary tract infection; primary risk factor is bladder catheterization; monomicrobial etiology due to Escherichia coli: Incorrect. While UTIs can cause fever, they would present with dysuria, flank pain, or costovertebral angle tenderness, rather than pelvic tenderness and foul lochia.",
    "pearl": "",
