    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "artery \u2192 Helicine arteriole \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Pelvic Blood Supply / Slide Arterial Blood Supply / Passage 3 \u25cf Explanation: A. Internal iliac artery \u2192 Internal pudendal artery \u2192 Cavernosal artery \u2192 Helicine arteriole: Correct. The arterial blood supply to the penis arises from the internal iliac artery, which branches into the internal pudendal artery. This further branches into three arteries: the bulbourethral artery (supplying the corpus spongiosum), the dorsal penile artery (supplying the glans), and the cavernosal artery (supplying the corpus cavernosum via the terminal helicine arterioles). B. Internal iliac artery \u2192 Obturator artery \u2192 Bulbourethral artery \u2192 Corpus spongiosum: Incorrect. The obturator artery does not supply the erectile structures of the penis. C. External iliac artery \u2192 Deep femoral artery \u2192 Dorsal penile artery \u2192 Helicine arteriole: Incorrect. The external iliac artery supplies the lower limb, not the internal pelvic genital structures. D. Internal iliac artery \u2192 Middle rectal artery \u2192 Cavernosal artery \u2192 Glans: Incorrect. The cavernosal artery supplies the corpus cavernosum, and the middle rectal artery is involved in rectal vascularization. E. External iliac artery \u2192 Inferior epigastric artery \u2192 Bulbourethral artery \u2192 Cavernosal artery: Incorrect. The inferior epigastric artery is used for surgical revascularization, but does not congenitally branch into the bulbourethral or cavernosal arteries.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
