    "rationale": "Cyclase \u2192 increased cGMP \u2192 decreased intracellular Calcium \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Penile Smooth Muscle Relaxation / Passage 4 \u25cf Explanation: A. Sympathetic release of Norepinephrine \u2192 activation of Adenylate Cyclase \u2192 increased cAMP \u2192 decreased intracellular Calcium: Incorrect. Sympathetic activity (norepinephrine) drives contraction and detumescence, not relaxation. B. Parasympathetic release of Acetylcholine \u2192 activation of Phospholipase C \u2192 increased IP3 \u2192 increased intracellular Calcium: Incorrect. Increased intracellular calcium stimulates contraction, not relaxation. 160 C. Parasympathetic release of Nitric Oxide \u2192 activation of Phospholipase C \u2192 increased cAMP \u2192 increased intracellular Calcium: Incorrect. Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase (not phospholipase C) and decreases calcium. D. Parasympathetic release of Nitric Oxide \u2192 activation of Guanylyl Cyclase \u2192 increased cGMP \u2192 decreased intracellular Calcium: Correct. During erection, parasympathetic fibers release Nitric Oxide (NO) which activates guanylyl cyclase in cavernosal smooth muscle cells. This elevates cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP), leading to a reduction in intracellular calcium, triggering smooth muscle relaxation, arterial flow, and venous occlusion. E. Sympathetic release of Norepinephrine \u2192 activation of Guanylyl Cyclase \u2192 increased cGMP \u2192 increased intracellular Calcium: Incorrect. Norepinephrine activates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors to increase calcium, suppressing erection.",
