    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "of inhibitory function of the locus ceruleus during sleep allows testosterone-mediated excitatory function \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Nocturnal Erections / Passage 5 / 6 \u25cf Explanation: 161 A. 3 to 5 times per night during REM sleep; hypothesis: cessation of inhibitory function of the locus ceruleus during sleep allows testosterone-mediated excitatory function: Correct. Nocturnal Penile Tumescence (NPT) normally occurs 3 to 5 times per night during REM sleep, with each episode lasting 30 to 45 minutes. The leading hypothesis is that the cessation of the inhibitory function of the locus ceruleus during sleep allows testosterone-mediated excitatory circuits to activate, driving erection. B. 1 to 2 times per night during stage 4 non-REM sleep; hypothesis: excess sympathetic discharge from the amygdala: Incorrect. NPT is associated with REM sleep, and sympathetic activity promotes detumescence, not tumescence. C. 8 to 10 times per night during light stage 1 sleep; hypothesis: elevated prolactin secretion stimulating the anterior pituitary: Incorrect. Prolactin suppresses sexual function, and the frequency is 3 to 5 times per night. D. 3 to 5 times per night during non-REM sleep; hypothesis: dopamine secretion from the hypothalamus suppressing central testosterone: Incorrect. Spontaneous erections occur during REM sleep, and dopamine increases (rather than suppresses) libido and erection. E. 1 to 2 times per night during REM sleep; hypothesis: activation of the locus ceruleus releasing norepinephrine: Incorrect. The hypothesis involves the  cessation  of locus ceruleus activity, as the locus ceruleus is an inhibitory center.",
