    "topic": "Physiology of Male Sexual Function & (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 58-year-old male with a history of Parkinson's disease is started on levodopa therapy. He presents to his neurologist 1 month later reporting a marked increase in his libido and spontaneous erections. At the same time, his brother is undergoing androgen deprivation therapy for prostate cancer. Which of the following neurotransmitters is activated to increase libido in the patient, and how many weeks after withdrawing androgen therapy will his brother likely experience a decline in libido?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Acetylcholine; decline in libido in 1 to 2 weeks",
      "B": "Dopamine; decline in libido in 3 to 4 weeks",
      "C": "Serotonin; decline in libido in 6 to 8 weeks",
      "D": "Norepinephrine; decline in libido in 3 to 4 weeks",
      "E": "Histamine; decline in libido in 12 weeks"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "\u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Libido / Passage 6 \u25cf Explanation: A. Acetylcholine; decline in libido in 1 to 2 weeks: Incorrect. Dopamine is the primary neurotransmitter involved, and androgen withdrawal takes longer to register a libido decrease. B. Dopamine; decline in libido in 3 to 4 weeks: Correct. Central dopamine receptors are activated to drive libido and sexual desire. Administration of levodopa (a dopamine precursor) is clinically associated with increased libido, spontaneous erections, and NPT. Conversely, gonadal hormones play a major role: withdrawal of androgen therapy leads to a significant decline in libido in 3 to 4 weeks. C. Serotonin; decline in libido in 6 to 8 weeks: Incorrect. High serotonin levels generally decrease libido, and 6 to 8 weeks is longer than the typical 3-4 week decline seen after androgen withdrawal. D. Norepinephrine; decline in libido in 3 to 4 weeks: Incorrect. Dopamine is the classical neurotransmitter identified with central drive and levodopa-induced hypersexuality. E. Histamine; decline in libido in 12 weeks: Incorrect. Histamine is not the central neurotransmitter mediator, and 12 weeks is too long. 162",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
