    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 31-year-old male with a history of T10 spinal cord injury (Upper Motor Neuron lesion) is evaluated for erectile dysfunction. He reports that while he can achieve an erection with local physical tactile stimulation of the genitalia, he is completely unable to obtain an erection through sexual thoughts or visual stimuli. How is this clinical picture classified relative to UMN vs LMN neurogenic lesions?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "LMN injury; causes loss of reflexogenic erections while psychogenic erections remain intact",
      "B": "UMN injury; causes loss of reflexogenic erections while psychogenic erections remain intact",
      "C": "LMN injury; causes loss of both reflexogenic and psychogenic erections",
      "D": "UMN injury; causes decreased/loss of psychogenic response but reflexogenic erections remain intact",
      "E": "LMN injury; causes normal reflexogenic erections but impaired psychogenic erections"
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "reflexogenic erections remain intact \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Pathophysiology: Neurogenic / Passage 9 \u25cf Explanation: A. LMN injury; causes loss of reflexogenic erections while psychogenic erections remain intact: Incorrect. Lower motor neuron (LMN) lesions present with normal psychogenic erections but impaired/lost reflexogenic erections. B. UMN injury; causes loss of reflexogenic erections while psychogenic erections remain intact: Incorrect. In UMN lesions, reflexogenic erections are preserved because the sacral reflex arc is intact. C. LMN injury; causes loss of both reflexogenic and psychogenic erections: Incorrect. In LMN lesions, the patient can still obtain psychogenic erections via sympathetic pathways, but reflexogenic erections are lost. 163 D. UMN injury; causes decreased/loss of psychogenic response but reflexogenic erections remain intact: Correct. In Upper Motor Neuron (UMN) spinal injuries (typically above the sacral segments), the pathways from the brain are disrupted, leading to a loss of psychogenic erections. However, the sacral reflex arc (S2-S4) remains intact, allowing reflexogenic erections to occur in response to local tactile stimulation. E. LMN injury; causes normal reflexogenic erections but impaired psychogenic erections: Incorrect. This describes a UMN lesion profile.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
