    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 44-year-old male undergoes a penile duplex ultrasound with intracavernosal injection of vasoactive agents to evaluate his severe erectile dysfunction. The vascular technician reports a Peak Systolic Velocity (PSV) of 22 cm/s and an End Diastolic Velocity (EDV) of 1.5 cm/s. Which of the following is the correct clinical interpretation of these hemodynamic findings?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Normal arterial flow and normal veno-occlusive function",
      "B": "Severe veno-occlusive disease with normal arterial flow",
      "C": "Focal cavernosal arteriovenous malformation with normal venous drainage",
      "D": "Mild arterial insufficiency with severe venous leak",
      "E": "Severe arterial insufficiency with normal veno-occlusive function"
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "\u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Work up: Penile Duplex US / Passage 10 \u25cf Explanation: A. Normal arterial flow and normal veno-occlusive function: Incorrect. A PSV of 22 cm/s is significantly below normal. B. Severe veno-occlusive disease with normal arterial flow: Incorrect. EDV is normal (1.5 cm/s), and PSV is severely abnormal. C. Focal cavernosal arteriovenous malformation with normal venous drainage: Incorrect. This is a rare pathology not indicated by these classic clinical parameters. D. Mild arterial insufficiency with severe venous leak: Incorrect. EDV is low/normal (<= 3 cm/s), indicating that there is no venous leak. E. Severe arterial insufficiency with normal veno-occlusive function: Correct. On penile duplex ultrasound, severe arterial insufficiency is defined as a Peak Systolic Velocity (PSV) of < 25 cm/s (normal is > 30 cm/s). Normal veno-occlusive function is defined as an End Diastolic Velocity (EDV) of <= 3 cm/s (EDV > 3 cm/s is diagnostic of veno-occlusive disease/venous leak). Thus, this patient has severe arterial insufficiency with normal veno-occlusive function.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
