    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 26-year-old primigravida at 32 weeks gestation is undergoing a non-stress test while lying supine on an examination table. After 10 minutes, she becomes lightheaded, pale, nauseated, diaphoretic, and reports visual darkening. Her blood pressure, which was 114/72 mm Hg when seated, is now 78/46 mm Hg with a heart rate of 118/min. The fetal heart rate tracing demonstrates a new-onset deceleration to 95/min. The obstetrics nurse immediately assists the patient into the left lateral decubitus position. Within 2 minutes, her symptoms completely resolve, her blood pressure rises to 112/70 mm Hg, and the fetal heart rate recovers to 140/min. Which of the following hemodynamic changes directly precipitated this patient's acute hypotension while in the supine position?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Massive peripheral vasodilation induced by local uterine nitric oxide release",
      "B": "Mechanical compression of the inferior vena cava causing abrupt reduction in cardiac preload",
      "C": "Compression of the bilateral ureters triggering a neurogenic vasovagal reflex",
      "D": "Sudden pooling of blood in the maternal splanchnic circulation mediated by relaxin",
      "E": "Acute occlusion of the main pulmonary trunk by an amniotic fluid embolism"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Supine Hypotensive Syndrome (Aortocaval Compression):**\n  - **Pathophysiology:** When a pregnant woman in the late second or third trimester lies flat on her back (supine), the heavy gravid uterus compresses the **inferior vena cava (IVC)** against the vertebral column.\n  - **Hemodynamic Cascade:**\n    $$\\text{IVC Compression} \\rightarrow \\downarrow\\text{Venous Return} \\rightarrow \\downarrow\\text{Right Ventricular Preload} \\rightarrow \\downarrow\\text{End-Diastolic Volume} \\rightarrow \\downarrow\\text{Stroke Volume} \\rightarrow \\downarrow\\text{Cardiac Output} \\rightarrow \\text{Hypotension \\& Syncope}$$\n  - **Compensatory Response:** Baroreceptors detect the arterial pressure collapse, triggering sympathetic activation (reflex tachycardia, diaphoresis, pallor). However, compensatory vasoconstriction cannot fully overcome the profound drop in venous return.\n  - **Uteroplacental Perfusion:** Reduced maternal cardiac output directly impairs uterine artery perfusion pressure, leading to fetal hypoxemia and **late or prolonged fetal heart rate decelerations**.\n  - **Management:** Immediately roll the patient into the **left lateral decubitus position** (or manually displace the uterus to the left). This shifts the gravid uterus off the IVC and abdominal aorta, instantly restoring venous return, cardiac output, systemic blood pressure, and placental perfusion.\n  - **Resuscitative Hysterotomy:** In maternal cardiac arrest occurring in a pregnant patient at $\\ge 20$ weeks gestation, if spontaneous circulation is not restored within 4 minutes of CPR, immediate emergent delivery (perimortem Cesarean / resuscitative hysterotomy) must be performed at the bedside to decompress the IVC and allow effective maternal chest compressions.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Nitric oxide vasodilation:** Occurs tonically in pregnancy, but does not cause acute, positional hypotension that resolves instantly on turning.\n  - **(C) Ureteral compression:** Causes hydronephrosis, not acute postural hemodynamic collapse.\n  - **(D) Splanchnic pooling:** Relaxin relaxes pelvic ligaments, not systemic venous capacitance vessels in an acute positional manner.\n  - **(E) Amniotic fluid embolism:** Catastrophic, irreversible cardiogenic shock with respiratory arrest and DIC; does not resolve within 2 minutes simply by rolling onto the left side.",
    "pearl": "**Supine Hypotensive Syndrome:** Gravid uterus compresses the **IVC** when supine $\\rightarrow$ profound $\\downarrow$ **preload** $\\rightarrow$ $\\downarrow$ **cardiac output** $\\rightarrow$ hypotension and fetal decelerations. Relieved immediately by **left lateral decubitus positioning**.",
