    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 27-year-old pregnant woman at 30 weeks gestation undergoes pulmonary function testing. The growing fetus and enlarged gravid uterus have elevated her resting diaphragm by approximately 4 cm cephalad, compensated by an increase in the anterior-posterior and transverse chest diameters mediated by relaxin. Which of the following combinations of lung volumes and capacities is characteristically decreased in normal third-trimester pregnancy?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Functional residual capacity (FRC) and expiratory reserve volume (ERV)",
      "B": "Vital capacity (VC) and forced expiratory volume in 1 second ($FEV_1$)",
      "C": "Tidal volume (TV) and minute ventilation",
      "D": "Total lung capacity (TLC) and forced vital capacity (FVC)",
      "E": "Inspiratory capacity (IC) and peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR)"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Pulmonary Volumes and Capacities in Pregnancy:**\n  - **Elevated Diaphragm Effect:** The cephalad displacement of the diaphragm (~4 cm) by the gravid uterus compresses the lung bases, significantly reducing resting baseline lung volumes:\n    1. **Functional Residual Capacity (FRC):** Decreases by **20\u201330%** (the sum of ERV + RV).\n    2. **Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV):** Decreases by **20\u201340%**.\n    3. **Residual Volume (RV):** Decreases by **15\u201320%**.\n  - **Compensatory Increases (Hormonally Mediated):**\n    - Progesterone increases tidal volume ($V_T$) by 30\u201350%.\n    - Inspiratory Capacity (IC) increases by 10\u201315% to compensate for the decrease in FRC.\n  - **Parameters that Remain UNCHANGED:**\n    - **Vital Capacity (VC):** Remains unchanged.\n    - **Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 second ($FEV_1$):** Remains unchanged (no airflow obstruction).\n    - **$FEV_1/\\text{FVC}$ Ratio:** Normal ($>75\u201380\\%$).\n    - **Total Lung Capacity (TLC):** Remains normal or shows a minimal, clinically insignificant reduction (~5%).\n- **Clinical Significance:** Because **FRC (the oxygen reservoir of the lungs) is decreased by 20\u201330%** while maternal **metabolic oxygen consumption ($\\dot{V}_{O_2}$) is increased by 20\u201330%**, pregnant women **desaturate precipitously during periods of apnea** (e.g., during endotracheal intubation or induction of general anesthesia). Meticulous pre-oxygenation is mandatory.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) VC and $FEV_1$:** Remain completely normal throughout pregnancy. A reduction in $FEV_1$ indicates underlying reactive airway disease (asthma).\n  - **(C) TV and minute ventilation:** Both are markedly **increased** in pregnancy.\n  - **(D) FVC:** Preserved in normal pregnancy.\n  - **(E) Inspiratory capacity:** Actually **increases** to compensate for reduced FRC.",
    "pearl": "**Lung volumes in pregnancy:**\n- **$\\downarrow$ FRC, $\\downarrow$ ERV, $\\downarrow$ RV** (diaphragm pushed up 4 cm).\n- **$\\uparrow$ Tidal Volume, $\\uparrow$ Minute Ventilation** (progesterone effect).\n- **UNCHANGED: $FEV_1$, FVC, Vital Capacity**.\nRapid hypoxemic desaturation occurs during apnea due to $\\downarrow$ FRC combined with $\\uparrow$ $O_2$ consumption.",
