    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 29-year-old primigravida at 20 weeks gestation presents to the clinic with mild right flank fullness. She denies fever, chills, dysuria, hematuria, or uterine contractions. Urinalysis shows specific gravity 1.015, pH 6.0, negative leukocyte esterase, negative nitrites, and no red or white blood cells. Renal ultrasound demonstrates marked dilatation of the right renal pelvis and proximal right ureter with blunting of the calyces. The left renal pelvis and ureter show only minimal, trace dilatation. The resistive index of the intrarenal arteries is normal bilaterally. Which of the following factors best explains why physiologic hydronephrosis in pregnancy occurs predominantly on the right side?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Compression by the right ovarian vein and dextrorotation of the enlarging gravid uterus",
      "B": "Congenital absence of the right ureterovesical muscular valve",
      "C": "Direct mechanical compression of the right ureter by the right renal artery",
      "D": "Unilateral right-sided upregulation of smooth muscle progesterone receptors",
      "E": "Preferential implantation of the blastocyst on the right lateral uterine wall"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Physiologic Hydronephrosis of Pregnancy:**\n  - Present in up to **90% of pregnant women** by the third trimester.\n  - **Hormonal Component (Early):** High progesterone relaxes ureteral smooth muscle, decreasing peristalsis and ureteral tone.\n  - **Mechanical Component (Mid-to-Late):** As the uterus enlarges and rises out of the true pelvis into the abdomen, it mechanically compresses the ureters at the pelvic brim (pelvic brim crossing).\n- **Asymmetry (Right vs. Left):**\n  - Physiologic hydronephrosis and hydroureter occur predominantly on the **RIGHT side (Right > Left in >85% of cases)** due to two primary anatomical factors:\n    1. **Dextrorotation of the Uterus:** The enlarging gravid uterus rotates to the right as it rises out of the pelvis, placing direct mechanical compression on the right ureter against the iliopsoas muscle and pelvic brim.\n    2. **Right Ovarian Vein Complex:** The right ovarian vein crosses anteriorly over the right ureter at an acute angle near the pelvic brim before draining directly into the IVC; during pregnancy, the right ovarian vein dilates markedly, compressing the ureter.\n    3. **Left-Sided Protection:** The **sigmoid colon and its mesentery** cushion and protect the left ureter from direct anterior uterine compression.\n- **Clinical Significance:** Right-sided physiologic hydronephrosis can mimic acute right-sided pathology (e.g., nephrolithiasis, appendicitis). Urinary stasis in dilated renal collecting systems increases the risk of asymptomatic bacteriuria progressing to acute **pyelonephritis**.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Absence of valve:** Anatomic defect causing primary vesicoureteral reflux (VUR) in childhood, not the acquired right-sided hydronephrosis of pregnancy.\n  - **(C) Renal artery compression:** The renal artery is cephalad to the renal pelvis, not compressing the pelvic brim ureter.\n  - **(D) Progesterone receptors & (E) Right-sided implantation:** Both ureters possess identical progesterone sensitivity; implantation location does not govern anatomical ureteral compression.",
    "pearl": "**Physiologic Hydronephrosis of Pregnancy:** **RIGHT LEFT** (~90% right-sided). Caused by **dextrorotation of the uterus** and compression by the dilated **right ovarian vein**. Left ureter is protected and cushioned by the **sigmoid colon**.",
