    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 24-year-old primigravida at 14 weeks gestation presents with persistent daily heartburn (retrosternal burning) that worsens when lying flat after meals and is accompanied by frequent sour regurgitation. She has never experienced similar symptoms prior to pregnancy. Upper endoscopy is deferred, and she is counseled on lifestyle modifications and started on an antacid. Which of the following physiologic mechanisms is the primary initiator of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) in early pregnancy?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Progesterone-induced decrease in lower esophageal sphincter (LES) basal resting tone",
      "B": "Gastrin hypersecretion from placental syncytiotrophoblast proliferation",
      "C": "Increased gastric parietal cell $H^+/K^+$ ATPase pump activity driven by estrogen",
      "D": "Mechanical upward displacement of the stomach by the pelvic gravid uterus at 14 weeks",
      "E": "Selective destruction of the esophageal myenteric plexus by circulating autoantibodies"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Gastrointestinal Adaptations in Pregnancy:**\n  - GERD (heartburn) affects **50\u201380% of pregnant women**, frequently starting in the first or second trimester.\n  - **Hormonal Driver (Progesterone):**\n    - Progesterone is a potent smooth muscle relaxant. It inhibits calcium-dependent smooth muscle contractility throughout the gastrointestinal tract.\n    - At the gastroesophageal junction, progesterone causes a marked **reduction in lower esophageal sphincter (LES) resting tone**, allowing gastric acid and pepsin to reflux back into the distal esophagus.\n  - **Secondary Factors:**\n    - Delayed gastric emptying and prolonged small-bowel transit time (also mediated by progesterone).\n    - In late pregnancy (third trimester), mechanical upward and lateral displacement of the stomach by the large gravid uterus increases intragastric pressure, further exacerbating reflux. However, in early pregnancy (14 weeks), when the uterus is barely palpable at the level of the pubic symphysis/lower pelvis, **progesterone-induced LES relaxation is the primary driver**.\n  - **Other Progesterone-Mediated GI Effects:**\n    - **Constipation:** Decreased colonic motility and increased colonic water absorption.\n    - **Biliary Sludge & Gallstones:** Decreased gallbladder motility and incomplete emptying combined with estrogen-induced cholesterol supersaturation of bile.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Gastrin hypersecretion & (C) Increased $H^+/K^+$ ATPase:** Basal gastric acid secretion is actually unchanged or slightly *decreased* during early pregnancy.\n  - **(D) Mechanical upward displacement:** Becomes significant in the late second and third trimesters ($>24\u201328$ weeks); at 14 weeks, the uterus has not expanded high enough into the upper abdomen to displace the stomach.\n  - **(E) Myenteric destruction:** Etiology of achalasia, which causes aperistalsis and failure of the LES to *relax*, presenting with dysphagia to solids and liquids.",
    "pearl": "**GI changes in pregnancy (PROGESTERONE effects):**\n- **$\\downarrow$ LES tone** $\\rightarrow$ **GERD / heartburn** (most common GI complaint).\n- **$\\downarrow$ Colonic motility** $\\rightarrow$ **Constipation**.\n- **$\\downarrow$ Gallbladder contractility** $\\rightarrow$ **Biliary stasis & cholesterol gallstones**.",
