    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 27-year-old pregnant woman at 34 weeks gestation presents to her obstetrician with severe low back pain that has progressively worsened over the last two months. She states that her balance feels altered and she has developed a \"waddling\" gait. Physical examination reveals an accentuated anterior curvature of the lumbar spine, increased pelvic joint mobility, and bilateral sacroiliac joint tenderness. There is no lower extremity motor weakness, sensory deficit, or hyperreflexia. Which of the following biomechanical and hormonal adaptations of pregnancy is responsible for her clinical presentation?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Compensatory lumbar lordosis with relaxin-mediated ligamentous laxity",
      "B": "Herniation of the L4\u2013L5 intervertebral disc compressing the traversing L5 nerve root",
      "C": "Pathologic thoracic kyphosis secondary to osteopenia of pregnancy",
      "D": "Down-regulation of parathyroid hormone-related peptide causing subperiosteal bone resorption",
      "E": "Progesterone-induced spasm of the piriformis muscle entrapping the sciatic nerve"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Musculoskeletal Adaptations in Pregnancy:**\n  - **Shift in Center of Gravity:**\n    - As the fetus, placenta, amniotic fluid, and uterus grow anteriorly, the maternal **center of gravity shifts forward and downward**.\n    - To maintain balance and keep the center of gravity over the feet, the musculoskeletal system compensates by increasing the anterior tilt of the pelvis and accentuating the curvature of the lower spine, producing **progressive lumbar lordosis**.\n    - This anterior curvature strains the paraspinal muscles, intervertebral ligaments, and posterior facet joints, making **lower back pain** one of the most common musculoskeletal complaints of late pregnancy.\n  - **Role of Relaxin & Progesterone:**\n    - **Relaxin** (secreted by the corpus luteum and placenta) and progesterone induce remodeling of collagen and increase the water content of pelvic connective tissues.\n    - This promotes **marked laxity and widening of the pubic symphysis** (normal widening up to 4\u20138 mm) and **sacroiliac joints**.\n    - While this ligamentous softening facilitates fetal passage through the pelvic birth canal during labor, it produces pelvic girdle instability and causes the characteristic **\"waddling gait\"** of third-trimester pregnancy.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Lumbar disc herniation:** Characterized by radicular dermatomal pain (\"sciatica\") radiating below the knee, positive straight leg raise, and focal neurologic deficits (e.g., foot drop, diminished reflexes); absent here.\n  - **(C) Thoracic kyphosis & osteopenia:** Normal pregnancy does not cause pathologic thoracic kyphosis or severe osteopenic collapse.\n  - **(D) PTHrP:** Secreted by the placenta and breast to mobilize calcium for fetal skeletal mineralization and lactation, but does not cause acute back pain or ligamentous laxity.\n  - **(E) Piriformis spasm:** Can cause sciatica, but does not account for the generalized lumbar lordosis, SI joint mobility, and pubic widening.",
    "pearl": "**Musculoskeletal changes in pregnancy:**\n- **$\\uparrow$ Lumbar lordosis** compensates for anterior shift in the center of gravity $\\rightarrow$ low back pain.\n- **Relaxin** causes **ligamentous laxity** in the **pubic symphysis and sacroiliac joints** $\\rightarrow$ pelvic instability and \"waddling gait\" to facilitate delivery.",
