    "topic": "Lecture 1.2: Maternal Physiological Changes in Pregnancy",
    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 25-year-old primigravida at 12 weeks gestation presents for her first prenatal visit. She feels well and denies dysuria, urinary frequency, urgency, flank pain, or fever. Physical examination is unremarkable. Urinalysis demonstrates:\n- Specific gravity: 1.018\n- pH: 6.5\n- Leukocyte esterase: Positive\n- Nitrites: Positive\n- Microscopic exam: 15\u201320 WBCs/hpf, 2\u20135 RBCs/hpf, many bacteria\nClean-catch urine culture grows $>100,000$ CFU/mL of *Escherichia coli* susceptible to amoxicillin-clavulanate, cephalexin, and nitrofurantoin. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management, and what major complication does this treatment prevent?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Reassurance without antibiotics; prevents unnecessary drug exposure during organogenesis",
      "B": "Treatment with oral cephalexin; prevents progression to acute pyelonephritis and preterm labor",
      "C": "Oral ciprofloxacin for 14 days; prevents maternal cartilage arthropathy",
      "D": "Single-dose oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole; prevents neonatal kernicterus",
      "E": "Inpatient intravenous ampicillin and gentamicin; prevents renal tubular necrosis"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Asymptomatic Bacteriuria in Pregnancy:**\n  - Defined as isolation of a single uropathogen at **$\\ge 100,000$ CFU/mL** in a clean-catch urine specimen from an individual without symptoms of a urinary tract infection.\n  - Occurs in **2\u201310% of pregnancies**, with *Escherichia coli* causing ~80% of cases.\n- **Why We MUST Treat Asymptomatic Bacteriuria in Pregnancy:**\n  - In non-pregnant women, asymptomatic bacteriuria is benign and does not require treatment.\n  - In pregnant women, **physiologic urinary stasis** (progesterone-induced ureteral smooth muscle dilation and hypomotility + mechanical compression of ureters at the pelvic brim) allows bacteria to ascend easily from the bladder into the upper urinary collecting system.\n  - Without treatment, **20\u201340% of pregnant women with asymptomatic bacteriuria develop acute pyelonephritis**, which carries high maternal and fetal morbidity (maternal sepsis, ARDS, preterm premature rupture of membranes, and **preterm labor/delivery**).\n  - Antibiotic treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria reduces the risk of pyelonephritis to $<3\\%$.\n- **Safe Antibiotic Options in Pregnancy:**\n  - **First-Line:** **Cephalexin**, **Amoxicillin-clavulanate**, or **Nitrofurantoin** (nitrofurantoin is safe in the first and second trimesters, but avoided near term [37\u201342 weeks] due to the risk of neonatal hemolytic anemia from immature erythrocyte glutathione reductase).\n  - **Fosfomycin** (single 3g oral dose) is an effective alternative.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) No treatment:** Correct for non-pregnant women, but contraindicated in pregnancy due to the 30% risk of pyelonephritis and preterm labor.\n  - **(C) Ciprofloxacin (Fluoroquinolones):** Contraindicated in pregnancy due to the risk of fetal chondrotoxicity and cartilage damage.\n  - **(D) Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole:** Trimethoprim is an antifolate (risk of neural tube defects in the 1st trimester); sulfamethoxazole displaces bilirubin from albumin, increasing the risk of **neonatal kernicterus** if given in the 3rd trimester near delivery.\n  - **(E) IV ampicillin + gentamicin:** Regimen for inpatient acute pyelonephritis, not uncomplicated asymptomatic bacteriuria.",
    "pearl": "**Asymptomatic Bacteriuria in Pregnancy:** **ALWAYS SCREEN** (at 12\u201316 weeks) and **ALWAYS TREAT** (e.g., **cephalexin**, **amoxicillin-clavulanate**, or **nitrofurantoin**). Untreated asymptomatic bacteriuria has a **30% risk of progressing to acute pyelonephritis and preterm labor** due to progesterone-mediated ureteral dilation and stasis.",
