    "id": "W3_Lecture_1_2_Maternal_Physiologic_Changes_30Q_19",
    "week": "W3",
    "lecture": "Lecture 1.2: Maternal Physiological Changes in Pregnancy",
    "file": "Lecture_1_2_Maternal_Physiologic_Changes_30Q.md",
    "qnum": 19,
    "topic": "Lecture 1.2: Maternal Physiological Changes in Pregnancy",
    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 30-year-old woman at 28 weeks gestation presents with abdominal fullness and mild bilateral lower extremity swelling that worsens toward the end of the day. Physical examination reveals $1+$ bilateral pitting pretibial edema up to the mid-shins. Her blood pressure is 114/72 mm Hg, urine dipstick shows no protein, and serum creatinine is 0.5 mg/dL. Her platelet count is $240,000/\\mu\\text{L}$, and liver enzymes are normal. Which of the following physiologic mechanisms is the primary contributor to dependent peripheral edema in normal pregnancy?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Decreased plasma oncotic pressure and increased femoral venous hydrostatic pressure",
      "B": "Glomerular podocyte effacement leading to massive urinary albumin wasting",
      "C": "Progesterone-induced selective constriction of the renal efferent arterioles",
      "D": "Direct cytotoxic endothelial injury caused by circulating anti-angiogenic factors (sFlt-1)",
      "E": "Elevated right ventricular end-diastolic pressure from tricuspid annular dilation"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology of Dependent Edema in Pregnancy:**\n  - Mild dependent peripheral edema of the lower extremities occurs in up to **80% of healthy pregnant women** during the second and third trimesters.\n  - **Starling Forces Involved:**\n    $$\\text{Fluid Filtration} \\propto (P_c - P_i) - \\sigma(\\pi_c - \\pi_i)$$\n    1. **Increased Capillary Hydrostatic Pressure ($P_c$):**\n       - The heavy gravid uterus compresses the IVC and the common iliac veins at the pelvic brim.\n       - This mechanical obstruction impedes venous return from the lower extremities, causing a marked rise in **femoral and popliteal venous hydrostatic pressure** (from ~10 cm $H_2O$ non-pregnant to $>30$ cm $H_2O$ in late pregnancy when standing or supine).\n    2. **Decreased Plasma Oncotic Pressure ($\\pi_c$):**\n       - Massive plasma volume expansion (~50%) dilutes circulating plasma proteins (serum albumin decreases from ~4.5 g/dL non-pregnant to **$3.0\u20133.5$ g/dL** in pregnancy).\n       - The reduced colloid oncotic pressure promotes fluid transudation from the intravascular space into the interstitial space of dependent tissues.\n- **Clinical Distinction from Preeclampsia:**\n  - Dependent pretibial edema alone is **physiologic** if blood pressure is normal ($<140/90$ mm Hg) and there is no significant proteinuria ($<300$ mg/day) or end-organ signs (headache, vision changes, RUQ pain, thrombocytopenia). Edema is no longer a diagnostic criterion for preeclampsia.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Podocyte effacement:** Cause of nephrotic syndrome (proteinuria $>3.5$ g/day with hypoalbuminemia $<3.0$ g/dL), not physiologic pregnancy.\n  - **(C) Efferent arteriolar constriction:** Stimulated by Angiotensin II to maintain GFR in hypovolemia, not the cause of peripheral edema.\n  - **(D) sFlt-1:** Soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1 is elevated in **preeclampsia**, where it binds VEGF and PlGF, producing widespread systemic endothelial dysfunction.\n  - **(E) Right ventricular pressure:** Right heart pressures remain normal in healthy pregnancy.",
    "pearl": "**Physiologic Edema of Pregnancy:** Driven by **increased femoral venous hydrostatic pressure** (IVC compression by gravid uterus) and **decreased plasma oncotic pressure** (hemodilution of albumin to $3.0\u20133.5$ g/dL). Normal finding if blood pressure and urine protein are normal.",
