    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 30-year-old male is undergoing a microscopic evaluation of his semen as part of an infertility workup. The pathologist notes that a large portion of the sperm cells have small, structurally defective heads that lack a functioning acrosome. Which of the following best describes the physiological function of the acrosome?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It produces specialized enzymes required to penetrate the outer layers of the ova",
      "B": "It contains dense spiral-shaped mitochondria that generate ATP for propeller movement",
      "C": "It houses the condensed haploid paternal DNA required for fertilization",
      "D": "It acts as a flagellar propeller to drive forward motility through the cervix",
      "E": "It secretes alkaline fluid to neutralize the acidic vaginal environment"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "layers of the ova \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Mature Sperm / Passage 4 \u25cf Explanation: A. It produces specialized enzymes required to penetrate the outer layers of the ova: Correct. The acrosome is a cap-like organelle covering the anterior half of the sperm head. It contains hydrolytic enzymes (such as acrosin and hyaluronidase) that are released during the acrosome reaction, allowing the sperm to digest and penetrate the outer protective layers of the oocyte (zona pellucida and corona radiata) to achieve fertilization. B. It contains dense spiral-shaped mitochondria that generate ATP for propeller movement: Incorrect. Mitochondria are located in the midpiece (middle piece) of the sperm, not the acrosome. C. It houses the condensed haploid paternal DNA required for fertilization: Incorrect. The sperm nucleus (located in the head behind the acrosome) carries the DNA. D. It acts as a flagellar propeller to drive forward motility through the cervix: Incorrect. The tail (flagellum) is the structure that acts as a propeller for movement. E. It secretes alkaline fluid to neutralize the acidic vaginal environment: Incorrect. Alkaline fluid is produced by the seminal vesicles and prostate gland, not by individual sperm cells. 141",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
