    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 28-year-old male with a history of chronic productive cough, recurrent sinusitis, and bronchiectasis presents with his wife due to primary infertility. A semen analysis reveals a normal semen volume but complete azoospermia. Physical examination reveals bilateral, palpable, normal-sized testes, but the bilateral vas deferens are completely absent. Genetic testing is ordered. Which of the following represents the most likely diagnosis, and on which chromosome is the mutated gene located? 133",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Kartagener's Syndrome; mutated gene on Chromosome",
      "B": "Young's Syndrome; mutated gene on the Y chromosome (AZFc region)",
      "C": "Klinefelter's Syndrome; mutated gene on the X chromosome",
      "D": "Noonan Syndrome; mutated gene on Chromosome",
      "E": "Congenital Bilateral Absence of the Vas Deferens (CBAVD) associated with a CFTR mutation; mutated gene on Chromosome"
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "associated with a CFTR mutation; mutated gene on Chromosome 7 \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Sperm Abnormalities / Slide Chromosomal Anomalies / Passage 9 / 10 \u25cf Explanation: A. Kartagener's Syndrome; mutated gene on Chromosome 11: Incorrect. Kartagener's syndrome features a patent, normal vas deferens, and immotile sperm, not absent vas deferens. B. Young's Syndrome; mutated gene on the Y chromosome (AZFc region): Incorrect. Young's syndrome features a patent but obstructed vas deferens (obstructed by thick secretions) with motile cilia and bronchiectasis, and is not associated with Chromosome 7 or CFTR. C. Klinefelter's Syndrome; mutated gene on the X chromosome: Incorrect. Klinefelter's is a karyotype abnormality (47,XXY) presenting with small, firm testes, not absent vas deferens. D. Noonan Syndrome; mutated gene on Chromosome 12: Incorrect. Noonan syndrome does not present with bilateral absence of the vas deferens. E. Congenital Bilateral Absence of the Vas Deferens (CBAVD) associated with a CFTR mutation; mutated gene on Chromosome 7: Correct. Congenital Bilateral Absence of the Vas Deferens (CBAVD) is strongly associated with mutations in the CFTR gene (located on Chromosome 7). Approximately 60% of men presenting with CBAVD have identifiable CFTR mutations. It presents with azoospermia due to the complete, congenital absence of the vas deferens. While some patients also have pulmonary signs (bronchiectasis, atypical cystic fibrosis), others have isolated CBAVD.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
