    "rationale": "normal, patent vas deferens \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Sperm Abnormalities / Passage 9 \u25cf Explanation: 143 A. Absence of the vas deferens due to a CFTR mutation: Incorrect. This is CBAVD or Cystic Fibrosis. Kartagener's features a normal, patent vas deferens. B. Obstruction of the vas deferens by thick, viscous secretions with normal ciliary motility: Incorrect. This is Young's Syndrome, where the cilia are motile, but secretions cause obstruction. C. Exogenous suppression of ciliary function by elevated prolactin levels: Incorrect. Prolactin suppresses GnRH secretion centrally but does not cause primary structural immotility of the flagellum. D. Dysfunctional/immotile cilia and flagella (dynein arm defect) with normal, patent vas deferens: Correct. Kartagener's Syndrome (Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia) is a genetic defect in the dynein arms of cilia and flagella, rendering them immotile. This immotility affects the respiratory tract (causing bronchiectasis, sinusitis) and the sperm flagellum (causing complete asthenospermia/immotile sperm, although the sperm count is normal). The vas deferens in Kartagener's is completely normal and patent. E. Premature ciliary degeneration caused by mumps orchitis: Incorrect. Mumps orchitis causes testicular inflammation and atrophy but does not cause primary ciliary dyskinesia or situs inversus.",
