    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 21-year-old male is evaluated for delayed puberty and primary infertility. On physical exam, he has no facial or axillary hair, a microphallus, and small, soft testes. He is also noted to have a complete absence of his sense of smell (anosmia). His hormone profile reveals: low testosterone, low LH, and low FSH. Which of the following represents the most likely diagnosis, and what is the underlying pathophysiology?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Klinefelter's Syndrome; 47,XXY karyotype leading to primary testicular failure",
      "B": "Kallmann Syndrome; X-linked absence or migration failure of GnRH-producing neurons",
      "C": "Noonan Syndrome; \"Male Turner's\" autosomal dominant mutation leading to pulmonary stenosis",
      "D": "Prolactinoma; a pituitary adenoma secreting excess prolactin and suppressing LH and FSH",
      "E": "Sertoli-only Syndrome; germ cell failure with intact Leydig cells"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "GnRH-producing neurons \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Hypogonadotropic Hypogonadism / Passage 10 \u25cf Explanation: A. Klinefelter's Syndrome; 47,XXY karyotype leading to primary testicular failure: Incorrect. Klinefelter's is a hypergonadotropic hypogonadal state (elevated FSH/LH) and does not feature anosmia. B. Kallmann Syndrome; X-linked absence or migration failure of GnRH-producing neurons: Correct. Kallmann Syndrome is a form of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism caused by the failure of GnRH-producing neurons to migrate from the olfactory placode to the hypothalamus during development. This migration failure is associated with the genetic absence of GnRH, resulting in delayed/absent puberty, microphallus, and small, soft testes. Because the olfactory bulbs also fail to develop, the pathognomonic clinical feature is anosmia (absence of the sense of smell). Hormone levels show low testosterone with low/normal LH and FSH. C. Noonan Syndrome; \"Male Turner's\" autosomal dominant mutation leading to pulmonary stenosis: Incorrect. Noonan syndrome features hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, webbed neck, and short stature, and is not associated with anosmia. D. Prolactinoma; a pituitary adenoma secreting excess prolactin and suppressing LH and FSH: Incorrect. Prolactinomas suppress (rather than stimulate) LH and FSH secretion, and are not associated with developmental delayed puberty and anosmia. 144 E. Sertoli-only Syndrome; germ cell failure with intact Leydig cells: Incorrect. Sertoli-only syndrome features elevated FSH and normal testosterone, not hypogonadotropic hypogonadism or anosmia.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
