    "topic": "HPT Axis & Disorders of Male (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 31-year-old G0 male with a 2-year history of unexplained infertility is found to have complete azoospermia on two separate semen analyses. His hormone profile reveals: normal testosterone, normal LH, normal prolactin, but a markedly elevated FSH. A testicular biopsy is performed. Which of the following histological profiles is most consistent with this patient's diagnosis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Normal spermatogenesis with bilateral obstruction of the ejaculatory ducts",
      "B": "Complete absence of Sertoli cells with normal germ cell and Leydig cell populations",
      "C": "Severe Leydig cell hyperplasia with normal spermatids",
      "D": "Extensive infiltration of the seminiferous tubules by polymorphonuclear neutrophils",
      "E": "Presence of Sertoli cells, but a complete absence of germ cells and Leydig cells within the seminiferous tubules (Sertoli-only Syndrome)"
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "and Leydig cells within the seminiferous tubules (Sertoli-only Syndrome) \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Hypergonadotropic Hypogonadism / Slide Endocrine Abnormalities / Passage 11 / 12 \u25cf Explanation: A. Normal spermatogenesis with bilateral obstruction of the ejaculatory ducts: Incorrect. Normal spermatogenesis on biopsy with azoospermia suggests an obstructive etiology, but these patients have normal (not elevated) FSH levels. B. Complete absence of Sertoli cells with normal germ cell and Leydig cell populations: Incorrect. This histological pattern does not exist; Sertoli cells are required to support germ cells. C. Severe Leydig cell hyperplasia with normal spermatids: Incorrect. Leydig cell hyperplasia is not a feature of Sertoli-only syndrome. D. Extensive infiltration of the seminiferous tubules by polymorphonuclear neutrophils: Incorrect. This describes acute, infectious orchitis, not a congenital germ cell failure. E. Presence of Sertoli cells, but a complete absence of germ cells and Leydig cells within the seminiferous tubules (Sertoli-only Syndrome): Correct. Germ Cell Failure (Sertoli-only Syndrome) is characterized by the presence of Sertoli cells lining the seminiferous tubules, with a complete absence of germ cells (no spermatogonia, spermatocytes, or spermatids) and Leydig cells within the tubules. Because Sertoli cells are non-functioning or lack germ cells to support, there is a loss of inhibin B feedback, leading to a selective, marked elevation in serum FSH. Testosterone, LH, and prolactin levels remain normal. Sperm can still be retrieved in 25-50% of these patients using specialized micro-TESE.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
