    "rationale": "\u25cf Lecture Source: Slide HPT Axis: Testosterone and Estrogen Negative Feedback / Slide Gonadotoxins / Passage 3 / 13 \u25cf Explanation: A. High testosterone; high LH; high FSH: Incorrect. Exogenous testosterone suppresses the hypothalamus and pituitary, leading to a complete shutdown of LH and FSH. 146 B. Low testosterone; high LH; high FSH: Incorrect. This describes primary testicular failure, which is the opposite of the hypogonadotropic feedback suppression caused by exogenous steroids. C. Normal testosterone; normal LH; elevated FSH: Incorrect. Anabolic steroids suppress both LH and FSH. D. Low intratesticular testosterone; low LH; low FSH: Correct. Exogenous anabolic steroids (androgen derivatives) provide potent, continuous negative feedback on both the hypothalamus (suppressing GnRH) and the pituitary (suppressing LH and FSH). This severe gonadotropin suppression shuts down the endogenous stimulation of Leydig cells (by LH) and Sertoli cells (by FSH). Consequently, the intratesticular testosterone concentration (which is normally 100-fold higher than serum levels and required to drive spermatogenesis) drops to near-zero, leading to azoospermia and testicular atrophy. Serum testosterone may measure normal or high depending on the specific steroid assay, but the intratesticular level is profoundly low. E. Low testosterone; low LH; markedly elevated prolactin: Incorrect. While steroids suppress LH, they do not directly stimulate prolactin secretion.",
