    "stem": "A 33-year-old male with a history of primary infertility undergoes an initial semen analysis, which is reported as \"azoospermia.\" The patient is devastated and asks if this means he is permanently sterile. Which of the following represents the correct clinical guideline for confirming azoospermia, and what are the two main categories of azoospermia?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "A single specimen is sufficient to confirm permanent azoospermia; categorized as genetic versus environmental",
      "B": "It must be confirmed by finding no sperm in at least 2 separate semen specimens that have been centrifuged; categorized as obstructive versus non-obstructive",
      "C": "It must be confirmed by a direct testicular biopsy; categorized as autoimmune versus infectious",
      "D": "It requires a 24-hour continuous urine collection; categorized as pre-renal versus post-renal",
      "E": "It is diagnosed only after 5 years of failed IVF; categorized as idiopathic versus iatrogenic"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "semen specimens that have been centrifuged; categorized as obstructive versus non-obstructive \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Semen Analysis - WHO Criteria / Passage 14 \u25cf Explanation: A. A single specimen is sufficient to confirm permanent azoospermia; categorized as genetic versus environmental: Incorrect. A single specimen is never sufficient; transient factors can cause temporary azoospermia. B. It must be confirmed by finding no sperm in at least 2 separate semen specimens that have been centrifuged; categorized as obstructive versus non-obstructive: Correct. According to WHO criteria, a diagnosis of azoospermia (no sperm in the ejaculate) must never be made on a single sample. It requires at least 2 separate semen specimens, which must be centrifuged and microscopically examined to confirm the complete absence of sperm. Azoospermia is then clinically categorized into two main groups: obstructive (a mechanical blockage in the ductal system, such as CF, Young's, or vasectomy) versus non-obstructive (a primary production failure in the testes, such as Klinefelter's, Sertoli-only, or gonadotoxins). C. It must be confirmed by a direct testicular biopsy; categorized as autoimmune versus infectious: Incorrect. Azoospermia is diagnosed via semen analysis; testicular biopsy is a secondary step to characterize the etiology. D. It requires a 24-hour continuous urine collection; categorized as pre-renal versus post-renal: Incorrect. Semen analysis, not urine collection, is the diagnostic tool for azoospermia. 147 E. It is diagnosed only after 5 years of failed IVF; categorized as iatrogenic versus idiopathic: Incorrect. It is diagnosed during the initial fertility workup, not after failed treatment.",
