    "stem": "A 34-year-old male presents with primary infertility, decreased libido, and erectile dysfunction. His hormone profile reveals: low testosterone, low-to-normal LH, low-to-normal FSH, and a markedly elevated prolactin level of 250 ng/mL. A pituitary MRI is ordered and confirms a 1.2 cm pituitary mass. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line medical therapy for this patient's underlying condition?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) injections",
      "B": "Oral clomiphene citrate",
      "C": "Dopamine agonist therapy (e.g., bromocriptine or cabergoline)",
      "D": "Transsphenoidal surgical resection of the mass",
      "E": "Exogenous testosterone replacement gel"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "\u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Hypogonadotropic Hypogonadism / Slide Endocrine Abnormalities / Passage 10 / 12 / 13 \u25cf Explanation: A. Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) injections: Incorrect. While hCG can stimulate Leydig cells, the primary treatment for a prolactinoma is to directly suppress prolactin hypersecretion using dopamine agonists. B. Oral clomiphene citrate: Incorrect. Clomiphene is ineffective when high prolactin levels are actively suppressing the hypothalamic GnRH pulse generator. C. Dopamine agonist therapy (e.g., bromocriptine or cabergoline): Correct. Prolactinomas secrete excess prolactin, which directly suppresses the hypothalamic GnRH pulse generator, leading to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (low testosterone, low LH and FSH, causing infertility and erectile dysfunction). The first-line medical therapy for prolactinomas is dopamine agonist therapy (such as bromocriptine or cabergoline) . Dopamine acts as a natural inhibitor of prolactin release. This therapy reduces prolactin levels, shrinks the tumor, and restores normal gonadotropin secretion and fertility in up to 80-90% of patients. D. Transsphenoidal surgical resection of the mass: Incorrect. Surgery is reserved for patients who fail or cannot tolerate medical therapy with dopamine agonists. E. Exogenous testosterone replacement gel: Incorrect. Exogenous testosterone will provide further negative feedback, worsening spermatogenesis and doing nothing to shrink the pituitary tumor.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
