    "topic": "Lecture 2.1: Dangerous Exposures & Teratogens in Pregnancy",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 27-year-old primigravida presents at 30 weeks gestation for an urgent evaluation due to acute maternal viral illness and decreased fetal movements. Maternal serology demonstrates primary infection with Parvovirus B19 (positive IgM and positive viral PCR). Targeted fetal ultrasonography demonstrates scalp edema, marked bilateral pleural effusions, massive peritoneal ascites, and cardiomegaly with pericardial fluid accumulation. Doppler assessment of the fetal middle cerebral artery (MCA) reveals an elevated peak systolic velocity (MCA-PSV) of 2.1 multiples of the median (MoM), consistent with severe fetal anemia. Which of the following viral cellular targets and pathogenic mechanisms accounts for the development of hydrops fetalis in this setting?",
