    "rationale": "- **Parvovirus B19 Biology & Cellular Receptor:**\n  - Parvovirus B19 is a **small, single-stranded non-enveloped DNA virus** (the only human pathogenic parvovirus).\n  - It infects host cells by binding to the **P antigen (globoside)**, a glycosphingolipid expressed abundantly on the surface of **erythroid progenitor cells (erythroblasts and proerythroblasts)** in the bone marrow and fetal liver, as well as on cardiac myocytes and endothelial cells.\n- **Pathogenesis of Fetal Hydrops:**\n  1. **Arrest of Erythropoiesis:** The virus replicates inside fetal erythroid progenitor cells, inducing cytotoxic cell lysis and causing a complete temporary cessation of red blood cell production (pure red cell aplasia).\n  2. **Severe Fetal Anemia:** Because the fetal red blood cell half-life is short (~45\u201370 days) and red blood cell mass is expanding rapidly to support growth, failure of erythropoiesis causes catastrophic, profound fetal anemia (hemoglobin often $<2\u20135$ g/dL).\n  3. **High-Output Heart Failure:** Tissue hypoxia drives peripheral vasodilation and extreme compensatory cardiac output. The fetal heart decompensates, developing high-output failure.\n  4. **Hydrops Fetalis:** Severe anemia plus cardiac failure elevates capillary hydrostatic pressure and reduces plasma oncotic pressure, producing fluid extravasation into two or more fetal serous cavities: **scalp edema, ascites, pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, and placentomegaly**.\n- **Monitoring & Treatment:**\n  - **Doppler MCA-PSV:** Blood velocity in the middle cerebral artery increases during anemia due to decreased blood viscosity and hyperdynamic circulation. An MCA-PSV $>1.5$ MoM predicts severe fetal anemia.\n  - **Intrauterine Blood Transfusion (IUT):** Life-saving transfusion of O-negative, CMV-negative packed red cells into the umbilical vein under ultrasound guidance cures the anemia until maternal antibodies clear the infection.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Placental abruption:** Parvovirus does not cause acute decidual abruption; the hematologic failure is purely fetal marrow arrest.\n  - **(C) Myocarditis without anemia:** While viral myocarditis can contribute, severe aplastic anemia is the primary driver.\n  - **(D) Platelets & (E) Renal glomeruli:** Parvovirus does not cause nephrotic syndrome or isolated maternal thrombocytopenia.",
    "pearl": "**Parvovirus B19 in Pregnancy:**\n- Virus: **ssDNA non-enveloped virus**.\n- Receptor: **P antigen (globoside)** on **erythroid progenitor cells**.\n- Halts erythropoiesis $\\rightarrow$ **severe fetal anemia** $\\rightarrow$ high-output cardiac failure $\\rightarrow$ **HYDROPS FETALIS** (effusions, ascites, edema).\n- Screen with **Doppler MCA peak systolic velocity**; treat with **intrauterine umbilical blood transfusion**.",
