    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "- **Syphilis in Pregnancy & Management of Penicillin Allergy:**\n  - *Treponema pallidum* is a motile spirochete that readily crosses the placenta after 9\u201310 weeks gestation, leading to miscarriage, stillbirth, non-immune hydrops fetalis, or congenital syphilis.\n  - **Vertical Transmission Risk:** Highest during **primary and secondary syphilis (50\u201380%)**, and early latent syphilis (40%), decreasing to ~10% in late latent syphilis.\n- **THE CARDINAL BOARD RULE FOR SYPHILIS IN PREGNANCY:**\n  - **Parenteral Penicillin G (Benzathine Penicillin G) is the ONLY therapy with proven efficacy for treating maternal syphilis AND preventing or curing congenital syphilis in the fetus.**\n  - Other antibiotics that treat adult syphilis (e.g., doxycycline, erythromycin, ceftriaxone, azithromycin) either:\n    1. Do NOT reliably cross the placenta to treat the infected fetus (e.g., erythromycin has $<10\\%$ placental penetration).\n    2. Are teratogenic in pregnancy (e.g., doxycycline causes permanent fetal tooth discoloration and bone growth inhibition).\n    3. Have high rates of documented treponemal resistance (e.g., azithromycin).\n  - **Management of Penicillin Allergy in Pregnant Women:**\n    - **EVERY pregnant woman with a penicillin allergy and a diagnosis of syphilis MUST undergo penicillin allergy skin testing and, if positive, ORAL OR IV PENICILLIN DESENSITIZATION, followed immediately by standard BENZATHINE PENICILLIN G therapy.**\n    - There is **NO ACCEPTABLE ALTERNATIVE** to penicillin in pregnancy!\n- **Jarisch-Herxheimer Reaction:**\n  - Acute inflammatory response occurring within hours of starting penicillin due to massive treponemal lysis releasing endotoxin-like lipoproteins.\n  - Symptoms: fever, chills, rigors, headache, uterine contractions, and transient fetal heart rate decelerations.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Erythromycin:** Does not adequately cross the placenta; fetus remains untreated, resulting in congenital syphilis or stillbirth.\n  - **(B) Azithromycin:** High rates of macrolide resistance in *T. pallidum*; ineffective.\n  - **(C) Doxycycline:** Teratogenic (tetracycline class: permanent dental enamel hypoplasia and brown staining; skeletal hypoplasia) and avoided in pregnancy.\n  - **(D) Ceftriaxone:** Cross-reacts in severe IgE penicillin allergies; not approved by CDC as first-line therapy for maternal syphilis.",
    "pearl": "**Syphilis in Pregnancy (Absolute Board Favorite!):**\n- **Benzathine Penicillin G is the ONLY acceptable treatment.**\n- If the pregnant patient is allergic to penicillin: **MUST PERFORM PENICILLIN SKIN TESTING AND DESENSITIZATION**, then administer **PENICILLIN G**.\n- Doxycycline is teratogenic (teeth discoloration); erythromycin does NOT cross the placenta.",
