    "stem": "A 32-year-old woman at 18 weeks gestation presents for a routine visit. She mentions that she loves soft, imported French cheeses (such as unpasteurized Brie and Camembert) and frequently consumes deli meats and hot dogs without reheating them. The physician cautions her against consuming unpasteurized dairy products and unheated processed meats due to the risk of foodborne infection by an organism that can cause chorioamnionitis, spontaneous abortion, and severe neonatal sepsis. Which of the following microbiologic characteristics correctly identifies this foodborne pathogen?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Motile, gram-negative, oxidase-negative, lactose-fermenting bacillus",
      "B": "Spore-forming, anaerobic, gram-positive bacillus that produces lecithinase",
      "C": "Facultative intracellular, gram-positive rod with tumbling motility at room temperature that polymerizes host cell actin",
      "D": "Microaerophilic, comma-shaped, gram-negative rod that grows at 42\u00b0C",
      "E": "Obligate intracellular bacterium that forms reticulate and elementary bodies"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Listeria monocytogenes:**\n  - **Microbiology:**\n    - Small, facultatively anaerobic, **gram-positive rod (coccobacillus)**.\n    - Exhibits **characteristic \"tumbling motility\" at room temperature (20\u201325\u00b0C)** on wet mount (due to flagella), but is non-motile at 37\u00b0C.\n    - Demonstrates **narrow zone $\\beta$-hemolysis** on sheep blood agar and produces a positive **CAMP test** (similar to Group B Streptococcus).\n    - **Psychrophile:** Capable of survival and active replication at **refrigeration temperatures (4\u00b0C)** (explaining its transmission via contaminated refrigerated foods).\n  - **Transmission:** Consumption of contaminated **unpasteurized milk, soft cheeses (Brie, Camembert, feta), cold deli meats, hot dogs, and unwashed raw produce**.\n  - **Intracellular Pathogenesis:**\n    - Phagocytosed into host cells; secretes **listeriolysin O** (a pore-forming cytolysin) to escape the phagolysosome into the host cytoplasm.\n    - Uses host cell **actin polymerization (via the ActA protein)** to form \"actin rockets\" that propel the bacterium directly into neighboring cells, avoiding extracellular antibodies and complement.\n  - **Infection in Pregnancy:**\n    - Pregnant women have a **10- to 20-fold higher risk** of listeriosis due to suppressed cell-mediated (Th1) immunity.\n    - **Maternal Presentation:** Mild, flu-like bacteremic illness (fever, chills, backache, diarrhea).\n    - **Fetal / Neonatal Sequelae:** Hematogenous transplacental seeding leads to severe chorioamnionitis, spontaneous abortion, stillbirth, or premature delivery.\n    - **Granulomatosis Infantiseptica:** Severe, disseminated, early-onset neonatal infection characterized by **widespread microabscesses and granulomas** throughout multiple fetal organs (liver, spleen, lungs, brain) with high mortality.\n  - **Treatment:** First-line therapy is **intravenous Ampicillin** (often combined with gentamicin for synergy). (Note: Cephalosporins are completely ineffective against *Listeria* due to altered penicillin-binding proteins).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Gram-negative lactose fermenter:** *Escherichia coli*.\n  - **(B) Anaerobic spore-former with lecithinase:** *Clostridium perfringens*.\n  - **(D) Comma-shaped rod at 42\u00b0C:** *Campylobacter jejuni*.\n  - **(E) Reticulate and elementary bodies:** *Chlamydia trachomatis*.",
    "pearl": "**Listeria monocytogenes:**\n- **Gram-positive rod** with **tumbling motility at room temp**, grows in the **refrigerator (4\u00b0C)**.\n- Food sources: **Unpasteurized soft cheeses, cold deli meats**.\n- Spreads cell-to-cell via **\"actin rockets\"** (ActA).\n- Causes **abortion, preterm labor**, and **Granulomatosis Infantiseptica** (diffuse granulomas).\n- Treatment: **Ampicillin** (inherently resistant to cephalosporins!).",
