    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **NSAID Exposure in the Third Trimester:**\n  - Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs: ibuprofen, naproxen, indomethacin) reversibly inhibit **cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2)** enzymes, blocking the conversion of arachidonic acid into prostaglandins.\n  - **Fetal Ductus Arteriosus Patency:**\n    - In utero, patency of the **ductus arteriosus** (which shunts blood away from the high-resistance fetal lungs into the descending aorta) is actively maintained by low fetal oxygen tension and continuous vasodilation mediated by circulating **prostaglandin $E_2$ ($PGE_2$) and prostacyclin ($PGI_2$)**, produced by the placenta and ductal wall.\n    - **Premature Ductal Constriction / Closure:** When maternal NSAIDs are taken in the **third trimester (especially after 28\u201332 weeks)**, transplacental transfer inhibits fetal prostaglandin synthesis. Loss of $PGE_2$ leads to **acute constriction or premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus**.\n    - **Hemodynamic Consequences:** Right ventricular outflow faces a high-resistance closed circuit, causing **acute pulmonary arterial hypertension, tricuspid regurgitation, right ventricular hypertrophy/failure**, and hydrops. Postnatally, this can persist as life-threatening **Persistent Pulmonary Hypertension of the Newborn (PPHN)**.\n  - **Fetal Renal Impairment & Oligohydramnios:**\n    - Prostaglandins maintain fetal renal arteriolar vasodilation. NSAID-induced prostaglandin depletion causes renal vasoconstriction, reduced fetal GFR and urine output, producing **oligohydramnios**.\n  - **Indomethacin as a Tocolytic:** Indomethacin is sometimes used as a tocolytic to halt preterm labor, but its use is strictly limited to **$<32$ weeks gestation** and for a duration of **$\\le 48$ hours** with frequent ultrasound monitoring to prevent ductal constriction and oligohydramnios.\n  - **Contrast with Low-Dose Aspirin:** Daily low-dose aspirin (81 mg) irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1 (thromboxane $A_2$) without significantly reducing fetal systemic prostaglandins, and is safely used to prevent preeclampsia.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Lipoxygenase:** NSAIDs do not inhibit lipoxygenase (in fact, arachidonic acid can be shunted *to* leukotrienes, as seen in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease).\n  - **(C) Phospholipase $A_2$:** Inhibited by corticosteroids, not NSAIDs; NSAIDs decrease contractions, not increase them.\n  - **(D) Prostacyclin upregulation:** NSAIDs *inhibit* prostacyclin synthesis.\n  - **(E) Endothelin antagonism:** Not the mechanism of NSAIDs.",
    "pearl": "**NSAIDs in 3rd Trimester (Indomethacin, Ibuprofen):**\n- Inhibit COX $\\rightarrow$ **$\\downarrow$ $PGE_2$** $\\rightarrow$ **PREMATURE CLOSURE OF THE DUCTUS ARTERIOSUS**.\n- Leads to **pulmonary hypertension (PPHN)**, tricuspid regurgitation, and RV failure.\n- Inhibits fetal renal prostaglandins $\\rightarrow$ **Oligohydramnios**.\n- **Indomethacin tocolysis:** Max 48 hours, only before 32 weeks!",
