    "stem": "A 2-day-old infant born to an 18-year-old mother is examined in the newborn nursery. The infant's birth weight is at the 3rd percentile, and head circumference is at the 2nd percentile. Physical examination reveals microcephaly, short palpebral fissures, a smooth, flattened philtrum, and a thin, smooth vermilion border of the upper lip. Cardiac auscultation reveals a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border consistent with a ventricular septal defect. Neurodevelopmental follow-up reveals severe hyperactive behavior, impaired executive function, and an IQ of 65. The mother admits to chronic, heavy binge drinking throughout her pregnancy. Which of the following is the single leading preventable cause of intellectual disability and birth defects in the United States?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Fetal alcohol syndrome",
      "B": "Tobacco smoking",
      "C": "Congenital cytomegalovirus",
      "D": "Maternal phenylketonuria (PKU)",
      "E": "Intrauterine cocaine exposure"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Fetal Alcohol Syndrome (FAS):**\n  - Alcohol (ethanol) is a potent teratogen that readily diffuses across the placenta and blood-brain barrier.\n  - **Epidemiology:** FAS is the **#1 leading PREVENTABLE cause of intellectual disability and congenital malformations in the United States**. There is no known safe threshold of alcohol consumption during pregnancy.\n  - **Pathogenesis:** Ethanol and its metabolite acetaldehyde impair cellular division, induce widespread neuronal apoptosis, disrupt cell adhesion molecules (specifically L1 CAM), and impair cranial neural crest cell migration.\n  - **Diagnostic Triad of FAS:**\n    1. **Facial Dysmorphisms (Characteristically 3 Features):**\n       - **Smooth philtrum** (loss of the vertical groove between nose and upper lip).\n       - **Thin vermilion border of the upper lip**.\n       - **Short palpebral fissures** (small eye openings).\n       - Other features: epicanthal folds, flat midface, low nasal bridge.\n    2. **Growth Restriction:** Pre- and postnatal growth deficiency (height and/or weight $\\le 10\\text{th}$ percentile).\n    3. **Central Nervous System Abnormalities:** **Microcephaly**, structural brain defects (corpus callosum agenesis, cerebellar hypoplasia), intellectual disability (mean IQ ~70), and neurobehavioral dysfunction (ADHD, impaired executive function, impulse control deficits).\n  - **Associated Malformations:** Congenital heart defects (most commonly **Ventricular Septal Defects [VSD]** and Atrial Septal Defects [ASD]), limb contractures, and renal anomalies.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Tobacco smoking:** The leading preventable cause of **low birth weight and intrauterine growth restriction**, but does not typically cause gross facial dysmorphic syndromes or severe intellectual disability.\n  - **(C) Congenital CMV:** Leading *infectious* (non-genetic) cause of sensorineural hearing loss, but not the leading *preventable* cause of intellectual disability.\n  - **(D) Maternal PKU:** Causes microcephaly, intellectual disability, and cardiac defects due to toxic maternal phenylalanine levels, but is rare compared to FAS.\n  - **(E) Cocaine:** Causes placental abruption, low birth weight, and IUGR from vasoconstriction, but does not produce the classic FAS facial phenotype.",
    "pearl": "**Fetal Alcohol Syndrome (FAS):** **#1 preventable cause of intellectual disability**.\n- **Facial Triad:** **Smooth philtrum**, **thin upper lip (vermilion border)**, **short palpebral fissures**.\n- Microcephaly, growth restriction, and **cardiac defects (VSD/ASD)**.",
