    "stem": "A 22-year-old woman with bipolar disorder presents for pre-conceptional counseling. She is currently treated with sodium valproate. The physician explains that valproate is a potent teratogen that causes an approximate 10- to 20-fold increased risk of neural tube defects in the offspring. Valproic acid teratogenicity is primarily mediated by which of the following molecular mechanisms?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Direct inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase and impaired folate-dependent thymidylate synthesis",
      "B": "Histone deacetylase (HDAC) inhibition and interference with cellular folate absorption and metabolic pathways",
      "C": "Competitive antagonism of cellular retinoic acid receptors in the neural tube",
      "D": "Stimulation of hepatic cytochrome P450 3A4 causing accelerated degradation of pyridoxine",
      "E": "Disruption of fetal microtubule assembly via tubulin depolymerization"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Valproic Acid (Depakote) Teratogenesis:**\n  - Valproic acid is one of the most teratogenic antiepileptic medications, carrying an overall major congenital malformation rate of up to 10%.\n  - **Molecular Mechanisms:**\n    1. **Epigenetic Disruption via HDAC Inhibition:** Valproate acts as a potent **inhibitor of class I and II histone deacetylases (HDACs)**. By preventing histone deacetylation, it alters chromatin structure and causes aberrant transcription of developmental genes involved in neural tube closure and organogenesis.\n    2. **Folate Antagonism:** Valproate impairs intestinal absorption of dietary folates and interferes with endogenous enzymes in the folate-methionine one-carbon metabolic pathway, depriving rapidly proliferating neuroepithelial cells of the folate required for thymidylate and purine synthesis.\n  - **Classic Phenotype (Fetal Valproate Syndrome):**\n    1. **Neural Tube Defects (NTDs):** Highest risk of **spina bifida aperta / lumbosacral myelomeningocele** (~1\u20132% incidence with valproate, compared to 0.1% in the general population).\n    2. **Craniofacial Anomalies:** \"Valproate facies\" (trigonocephaly, tall forehead with bifrontal narrowing, epicanthal folds, broad nasal bridge, short nose, and long thin upper lip) and **cleft lip/palate**.\n    3. **Cardiovascular Defects:** Atrial septal defects, aortic coarctation, ventricular septal defects.\n    4. **Neurodevelopmental Impairment:** Significantly higher rates of autism spectrum disorder (ASD) and a documented reduction in childhood IQ (~8\u201310 points).\n  - **Prevention:** High-risk preconception folic acid supplementation: **4.0 mg (4000 mcg) daily**, started at least 1\u20133 months prior to conception.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) inhibition:** The primary mechanism of **Methotrexate** and **Trimethoprim**, not valproate.\n  - **(C) Retinoic acid receptor antagonism:** Mechanism of retinoid-like antagonists; valproate works via HDAC and folate pathways.\n  - **(D) CYP3A4 & Pyridoxine:** Valproate is an enzyme *inhibitor*, not an inducer; enzyme-inducing anticonvulsants (carbamazepine, phenytoin) induce CYP450 enzymes.\n  - **(E) Tubulin depolymerization:** Mechanism of colchicine and vinca alkaloids.",
    "pearl": "**Valproic Acid Teratogenicity:**\n- Mechanism: **Histone Deacetylase (HDAC) inhibition** $+$ **interferes with folate metabolism**.\n- #1 Malformation: **Neural Tube Defects (Spina Bifida / Myelomeningocele)**.\n- Also causes **cleft palate**, **cardiac defects**, and **Autism Spectrum Disorder**.\n- Requires **High-Dose Folic Acid (4.0 mg/day)** preconceptionally.",
