    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Diethylstilbestrol (DES) Exposure:**\n  - DES is a synthetic nonsteroidal estrogen prescribed widely between 1940 and 1971 to millions of pregnant women under the mistaken belief that it prevented miscarriages, preeclampsia, and premature labor.\n  - **Mechanism of Teratogenicity:**\n    - DES interferes with the normal fusion, elongation, and cavitation of the paramesonephric (M\u00fcllerian) ducts during female reproductive tract development.\n    - Normally, squamous epithelium from the urogenital sinus grows upward to replace the M\u00fcllerian columnar epithelium of the vagina. DES blocks this transformation, resulting in **vaginal adenosis** (retention of glandular columnar epithelium in the upper anterior vagina).\n  - **Long-Term Sequelae in \"DES Daughters\" (Females Exposed in Utero):**\n    1. **Clear Cell Adenocarcinoma of the Vagina and Cervix:** 40-fold increased risk; characteristically diagnosed in young women between ages 15 and 25 (mean age ~19 years).\n    2. **Structural M\u00fcllerian Anomalies:** Congenital **\"T-shaped\" uterine cavity**, hypoplastic uterus, cervical collar/hood, and transverse vaginal septa.\n    3. **Reproductive Dysfunction:** Significantly increased rates of ectopic pregnancy, recurrent spontaneous abortion, cervical incompetence, and primary infertility.\n  - **Sequelae in \"DES Sons\" (Males Exposed in Utero):**\n    - Increased risk of cryptorchidism, microphallus, epididymal cysts, and hypogonadism (no proven increased risk of testicular germ cell cancer).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Squamous cell carcinoma:** Associated with high-risk HPV (16 and 18) and lichen sclerosus, not in utero DES.\n  - **(C) Yolk sac tumor, (D) Choriocarcinoma, & (E) Dysgerminoma:** Germ cell and gestational trophoblastic neoplasms unrelated to in utero DES exposure.",
    "pearl": "**Diethylstilbestrol (DES) In Utero Exposure:**\n- **Clear Cell Adenocarcinoma of the Vagina/Cervix** in young women (teens to 20s).\n- **Vaginal Adenosis** (columnar epithelium persists in upper vagina).\n- Congenital **T-shaped uterus**, cervical collars, and recurrent pregnancy loss.",
