    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Perinatal Transmission of Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV):**\n  - **Transmission Mode:** More than **85\u201390% of neonatal HSV infections are acquired intrapartum** through direct contact of the neonate with infectious maternal viral shedding in the birth canal during vaginal delivery. Transplacental transmission (congenital HSV) is rare ($<5\\%$).\n  - **Primary vs. Recurrent Infection:**\n    - The risk of transmission during vaginal delivery with an **active primary maternal infection is 30\u201350%** (high viral titer, prolonged shedding, absence of maternal neutralizing IgG).\n    - The risk of transmission during vaginal delivery with a **recurrent infection is $<1\u20133\\%$** (lower viral load, maternal IgG antibodies cross the placenta to provide partial passive protection).\n- **ACOG Management Protocol:**\n  1. **Daily Antiviral Suppression:** All pregnant women with a documented history of genital HSV (recurrent or primary) should be initiated on **daily suppressive therapy with oral acyclovir or valacyclovir starting at 36 weeks gestation** until delivery. This reduces viral shedding, clinical recurrences at term, and the need for Cesarean delivery.\n  2. **Delivery Route Decision at Onset of Labor:**\n     - **Cesarean Delivery is MANDATORY IF:** The patient has **active genital lesions** (vesicles, ulcers) OR **prodromal symptoms** (vulvar burning, itching, tingling) at the onset of labor or membrane rupture.\n     - **Vaginal Delivery is PERMITTED IF:** The patient is completely **asymptomatic with no visible genital lesions**, even if she has a remote history of HSV.\n  3. **Invasive Fetal Monitoring:** Fetal scalp electrodes and vacuum/forceps extractors are **strictly avoided** in women with active or recent HSV because mucosal punctures provide a direct portal of entry for viral inoculation.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Transplacental transmission:** Transplacental transmission is rare; $>85\\%$ occurs during delivery through an infected birth canal.\n  - **(C) Mandatory Cesarean for all:** Overly aggressive; women without active lesions or prodrome can safely deliver vaginally.\n  - **(D) IV Foscarnet:** Highly nephrotoxic; reserved for acyclovir-resistant HSV, not routine labor prophylaxis.\n  - **(E) Scalp electrodes:** Strictly contraindicated; breaks the neonatal skin barrier and promotes direct inoculation.",
    "pearl": "**Genital HSV in Pregnancy:**\n- Start **oral acyclovir/valacyclovir suppression at 36 weeks**.\n- If **active lesions or prodromal symptoms** at labor onset $\\rightarrow$ **CESAREAN DELIVERY**.\n- If **no lesions/prodrome** $\\rightarrow$ **Vaginal delivery is safe**.\n- **Avoid fetal scalp electrodes** (prevents viral inoculation).",
