    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Congenital Toxoplasmosis (Toxoplasma gondii):**\n  - Obligate intracellular protozoan parasite whose definitive host is the domestic cat.\n  - **Transmission:** Ingestion of infectious **oocysts from cat feces / contaminated litter or soil**, or ingestion of tissue **cysts in raw/undercooked meat** (pork, lamb).\n  - **Maternal Presentation:** Usually asymptomatic or presents as a mild, self-limited, mononucleosis-like illness with posterior cervical lymphadenopathy (heterophile antibody-negative).\n  - **Classic Congenital Triad (The 3 C's / Brain & Eyes):**\n    1. **Chorioretinitis:** Cotton-like white patches of retinal necrosis with severe vitritis; leading cause of pediatric uveitis and blindness.\n    2. **Hydrocephalus:** Obstructive hydrocephalus due to aqueductal stenosis from periaqueductal inflammation and scarring.\n    3. **Intracranial Calcifications:** **Diffuse, scattered throughout the cerebral parenchyma and basal ganglia** (distinguishing it from the *periventricular* calcifications of CMV).\n  - **Other Features:** Convulsions/seizures, microcephaly, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, and \"blueberry muffin\" rash.\n  - **Treatment Protocol:**\n    - **Maternal infection without documented fetal transmission:** Treat with **Spiramycin** (macrolide that concentrates in the placenta to prevent transmission).\n    - **Confirmed fetal infection (positive amniotic fluid PCR or ultrasound abnormalities):** Treat with **Pyrimethamine, Sulfadiazine, and Folinic acid (Leucovorin)** (to treat the active fetal infection and prevent maternal bone marrow suppression).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Cataracts, deafness, PDA:** Congenital Rubella triad.\n  - **(C) Periventricular calcifications & ganciclovir:** Congenital CMV.\n  - **(D) Snuffles & penicillin:** Congenital Syphilis.\n  - **(E) Cicatricial scarring:** Congenital Varicella syndrome.",
    "pearl": "**Congenital Toxoplasmosis Classic Triad:**\n1. **Chorioretinitis**\n2. **Hydrocephalus**\n3. **Diffuse intracranial parenchymal calcifications**\nSource: **Cat feces/litter** or **undercooked meat**. Treat with **Spiramycin** (prophylaxis) or **Pyrimethamine + Sulfadiazine + Leucovorin** (fetal treatment).",
