    "rationale": "- **Antidepressant (SSRI) Use in Pregnancy:**\n  - Depression during pregnancy is common (~10\u201315%) and untreated depression carries substantial maternal and fetal risks: poor nutrition, self-harm, missed prenatal appointments, substance abuse, preterm delivery, low birth weight, and severe postpartum depression.\n  - **First-Trimester Teratogenic Risk:** Overall major malformation risk with SSRIs is low. **Paroxetine** has been associated in some registries with a slight increase in congenital cardiovascular defects (specifically VSDs and ASDs), so other SSRIs (such as **sertraline or citalopram**) are generally preferred. However, switching medications during stable pregnancy is not routinely recommended if the patient is well-controlled.\n  - **Third-Trimester Exposure Sequelae:**\n    1. **Poor Neonatal Adaptation Syndrome (Neonatal Behavioral Syndrome / SSRI Discontinuation):**\n       - Affects **up to 20\u201330% of neonates** exposed to SSRIs in the third trimester.\n       - *Onset:* Typically presents within the first **24 to 48 hours of life**.\n       - *Symptoms:* Mild, self-limited constellation of **jitteriness, irritability, tremors, weak or high-pitched cry, feeding difficulty, tachypnea, and sleep disturbances**.\n       - *Mechanism:* Thought to represent either mild neonatal withdrawal or transient central serotonergic hyperstimulation.\n       - *Prognosis:* **Completely self-limited**, typically resolving spontaneously within **2 to 14 days** with supportive care (swaddling, quiet environment, frequent small feedings).\n    2. **Persistent Pulmonary Hypertension of the Newborn (PPHN):** Very slight absolute risk increase (from ~1\u20132 per 1,000 to ~3 per 1,000 live births).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Radius aplasia:** Associated with thalidomide, Fanconi anemia, or VACTERL association.\n  - **(C) Ebstein anomaly:** Classic teratogenic anomaly of **lithium**.\n  - **(D) Deafness & microphthalmia:** Congenital rubella.\n  - **(E) Complete heart block:** Congenital heart block is pathognomonic for **neonatal lupus erythematosus** (maternal anti-Ro/SSA antibodies attacking the fetal AV node).",
    "pearl": "**SSRIs in Pregnancy:**\n- Untreated maternal depression is dangerous; **Sertraline** is often preferred.\n- Third-trimester exposure causes **Poor Neonatal Adaptation Syndrome** (jitteriness, tremor, weak cry, mild tachypnea) in ~25% of newborns; **self-limited and resolves within 1\u20132 weeks**.",
