    "rationale": "- **Varicella-Zoster Virus (VZV) in Pregnancy:**\n  - VZV is an enveloped, double-stranded DNA alphaherpesvirus. Primary infection (chickenpox) in pregnancy poses severe risks to both the mother (varicella pneumonia with up to 10\u201320% mortality) and the fetus.\n  - **Congenital Varicella Syndrome (CVS):**\n    - Occurs in approximately **1\u20132% of fetuses** whose mothers acquire primary varicella during **weeks 8 to 20 of gestation** (the highest vulnerability window corresponds to weeks 13\u201320).\n    - **Pathognomonic Manifestations (The 4 Classic CVS Hallmarks):**\n      1. **Cicatricial Skin Lesions:** Zig-zag, depressed, fibrotic **cutaneous scars in a dermatomal distribution** (cicatrix).\n      2. **Limb Hypoplasia:** Unilateral hypoplastic, rudimentary, or atrophic limbs with clubfeet or hypoplastic digits (due to viral destruction of the sensory/motor roots of the lumbosacral or brachial plexus).\n      3. **Ocular Abnormalities:** Chorioretinitis, microphthalmia, congenital cataracts, and optic atrophy.\n      4. **Neurologic Deficits:** Microcephaly, cortical atrophy, seizures, Horner syndrome, and severe intellectual disability.\n  - **Peripartum / Neonatal Varicella (Contrast with CVS):**\n    - When maternal chickenpox develops within **5 days before to 2 days after delivery**, the newborn acquires transplacental viremia without receiving protective maternal transplacental IgG antibodies.\n    - Results in **fulminant neonatal varicella** with disseminated visceral lesions (pneumonia, hepatitis, encephalitis) and a 30% mortality rate.\n    - **Prophylaxis:** **Varicella-Zoster Immune Globulin (VariZIG)** must be administered immediately to the neonate at birth.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Preconception:** Infection before conception does not cause congenital varicella syndrome.\n  - **(C) Weeks 30\u201334:** Maternal antibodies cross the placenta, protecting the fetus from severe congenital malformations.\n  - **(D) Peripartum window:** Causes acute, life-threatening *neonatal varicella* (disseminated chickenpox), not the chronic structural malformations of congenital varicella syndrome (limb hypoplasia and dermatomal cicatrices).\n  - **(E) Lactation:** VZV is not transmitted via breast milk to cause congenital malformations.",
    "pearl": "**Congenital Varicella Syndrome (Weeks 8\u201320 exposure):**\n- **Cicatricial (zig-zag) skin scarring** in a dermatomal distribution.\n- **Limb hypoplasia / atrophy** (neurologic denervation).\n- Chorioretinitis, cataracts, microcephaly.\n**Peripartum exposure (5 days before to 2 days after delivery):** High-mortality **neonatal varicella** $\nightarrow$ give **VariZIG** immediately!",
