    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Thalidomide Teratogenesis:**\n  - Thalidomide was prescribed widely in Europe, Canada, and Japan in the late 1950s and early 1960s as a \"safe,\" non-barbiturate sedative and anti-emetic for morning sickness.\n  - It caused a worldwide epidemic of catastrophic birth defects affecting over 10,000 children before being banned in 1961 (famously blocked in the US by FDA reviewer Frances Kelsey).\n  - **Molecular Mechanism (Immunomodulatory Drug / IMiD):**\n    - Thalidomide binds to **Cereblon (CRBN)**, the substrate-recognition component of the cullin-RING E3 ubiquitin ligase complex ($\\text{CRL4}^{\\text{CRBN}}$).\n    - Binding alters the substrate specificity of the ligase, causing recruitment, polyubiquitination, and proteasomal degradation of neo-substrates, including the zinc-finger transcription factors **SALL4** (spalt-like transcription factor 4) and **IKZF1/IKZF3** (Ikaros/Aiolos).\n    - Loss of SALL4 during weeks 3 to 8 post-conception disrupts the **apical ectodermal ridge (AER)** and limb bud outgrowth, as well as ear and cardiac development.\n  - **Classic Phenotype (Thalidomide Embryopathy):**\n    1. **Limb Reduction Defects:** **Phocomelia** (from Greek *phoke* [seal] and *melos* [limb]; long bones like humerus/radius/femur fail to form, leaving hands/feet attached directly to the torso like \"seal flippers\") and **amelia** (complete absence of limbs).\n    2. **Otologic Anomalies:** Microtia/anotia, sensorineural deafness.\n    3. **Cardiovascular & GI Defects:** Congenital heart malformations and duodenal atresia.\n  - **Current Medical Uses:** Thalidomide (and its derivatives lenalidomide, pomalidomide) is FDA-approved today for **Multiple Myeloma** (where degradation of IKZF1/IKZF3 kills malignant plasma cells) and **Erythema Nodosum Leprosum** (leprosy), under strict mandatory pregnancy prevention programs (STEPS / Revlimid REMS).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) DES:** Causes clear cell vaginal adenocarcinoma and T-shaped uterus.\n  - **(C) Methotrexate:** Causes severe craniofacial clefts, micrognathia, and skeletal hypoplasia via DHFR inhibition.\n  - **(D) Warfarin:** Causes chondrodysplasia punctata via VKORC1 inhibition.\n  - **(E) Misoprostol:** Can cause vascular disruption leading to Moebius syndrome (cranial nerve VI/VII palsy) or terminal transverse limb amputations, but is distinct from classic bilateral phocomelia.",
    "pearl": "**Thalidomide:**\n- Historical anti-emetic that binds **CEREBLON** $\\rightarrow$ ubiquitin degradation of **SALL4** $\\rightarrow$ limb outgrowth failure.\n- Classic defect: **PHOCOMELIA (\"flipper limbs\")** and amelia.\n- Used today for **Multiple Myeloma** (degrades IKZF1/IKZF3) under strict REMS pregnancy prevention!",
