    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A woman with preeclampsia has laboratory findings of thrombocytopenia (platelets <100,000), microangiopathic hemolysis, and hemoconcentration. What is the mechanism behind the hemoconcentration finding specifically?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Hemodilution from excessive IV fluid administration given the patient's history",
      "B": "Isolated bone marrow suppression according to current guidelines",
      "C": "Renal loss of plasma proteins with normal capillary function based on the presentation described",
      "D": "Capillary leakage due to endothelial damage, causing edema of the face and hands",
      "E": "Excessive plasma volume expansion with normal capillary integrity based on the presentation described"
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "Hemoconcentration in preeclampsia results from capillary leakage due to endothelial damage, causing fluid shift out of the intravascular space and into tissues, manifesting as edema of the face and hands, alongside thrombocytopenia (from platelet and fibrin deposition) and microangiopathic hemolysis (from RBC breakdown).",
    "pearl": "",
