    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 24-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with vaginal bleeding at 10 weeks gestation. She complains of severe, intractable nausea and vomiting (hyperemesis gravidarum) and feels excessively hot. Her uterus is palpated midway to the umbilicus, which is larger than expected for her gestational age. Her serum $\\beta$-hCG is $250,000 \\text{ mIU/mL}$. A transvaginal ultrasound reveals an enlarged uterine cavity filled with a heterogeneous mass of multiple cystic spaces (\"snowstorm\" appearance) and absolutely no fetal pole or amniotic sac. Which of the following best describes the pathogenesis of this patient's condition?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Fertilization of a normal ovum by two sperm simultaneously",
      "B": "Fertilization of an empty ovum by a single sperm that then duplicates its chromosomes",
      "C": "Implantation of an ectopic pregnancy in the cornua of the uterus",
      "D": "Malignant transformation of the intermediate trophoblasts",
      "E": "Failure of the fertilized zygote to undergo normal cleavage"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The triad of first-trimester vaginal bleeding, a uterus larger than dates, massively elevated hCG ($>100,000$), severe nausea, and a \"snowstorm\" (or \"bunch of grapes\") ultrasound with **no fetal parts** is the classic presentation of a **Complete Hydatidiform Mole**.\n- **Pathogenesis:** A complete mole is entirely paternal in origin. It occurs when a sperm fertilizes an **empty egg** (an ovum that has lost its maternal chromosomes). \n  - To survive, the single haploid sperm (23,X) must duplicate its own chromosomes, resulting in a **46,XX** karyotype. (Less commonly, two separate sperm can fertilize the empty egg, resulting in 46,XX or 46,XY). \n  - Because all genetic material is paternal, the trophoblast wildly over-proliferates (creating the massive cyst-like villi) and there is completely absent fetal tissue.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Normal ovum + 2 sperm:** This creates a **Partial Mole** (triploid, 69,XXY). It has fetal parts and less severe symptoms.\n  - **(C) Ectopic:** Would present with a mass in the fallopian tube and lower hCG, not a snowstorm in the uterus.\n  - **(D) Intermediate trophoblasts:** Forms a Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumor (PSTT), which occurs after a pregnancy and produces hPL, not hCG.\n  - **(E) Cleavage failure:** Causes early spontaneous abortion, not a rapidly growing molar mass.",
    "pearl": "A **Complete Mole** is caused by the fertilization of an **empty egg** by a single sperm that duplicates (46,XX). It has **NO fetal parts**, a \"snowstorm\" ultrasound, and astronomically high hCG.",
