    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **The p57 Stain:** This is the absolute gold standard immunohistochemical test to differentiate a Complete Mole from a Partial Mole on USMLE.\n- **The Genetics:** The *CDKN1C* gene produces the p57 protein. This gene is **imprinted** (silenced) on the paternal allele, meaning it is ONLY normally expressed from the **maternal** allele.\n- **Application:**\n  - **Complete Mole:** Contains ONLY paternal DNA (an empty egg fertilized by sperm). Because there is no maternal DNA, there is no active p57 gene. Therefore, a complete mole is strictly **p57 Negative**.\n  - **Partial Mole:** Contains maternal DNA (a normal egg fertilized by 2 sperm). Because the maternal DNA is present, the p57 protein is expressed. Therefore, a partial mole is **p57 Positive**.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Positive:** It would be negative in a complete mole.\n  - **(C) & (D):** The gene is paternally imprinted (turned off in the sperm) and maternally expressed (turned on in the egg).\n  - **(E) TP53:** Completely unrelated to p57 imprinting in molar pregnancies.",
    "pearl": "A **Complete Mole** is **p57 Negative** because it lacks maternal DNA. A **Partial Mole** is **p57 Positive** because maternal DNA is present.",
