    "rationale": "- **The Complication:** The patient has developed **Pre-eclampsia** (hypertension + proteinuria + headache/visual changes).\n- **The Rule:** By definition, classic pre-eclampsia occurs in the *third* trimester (after 20 weeks gestation). \n  - If a patient develops severe **pre-eclampsia BEFORE 20 weeks gestation** (typically in the late first or early second trimester), it is the absolute hallmark, pathognomonic sign of a **Hydatidiform Mole** (Complete Mole) or, less commonly, multiple gestations.\n- **Pathogenesis:** The massive, rapid, abnormal proliferation of trophoblastic tissue in a mole creates massive early placental ischemia/dysfunction, dumping anti-angiogenic factors (sFlt-1) into the maternal circulation much earlier and more aggressively than a normal pregnancy.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Chronic hypertension:** Would have high BP before pregnancy or before 20 weeks, but would not typically cause sudden 3+ proteinuria and severe headaches at 14 weeks without an underlying molar process.\n  - **(B) & (D) Renovascular disease:** Causes chronic hypertension, not acute pre-eclampsia.\n  - **(E) SLE:** Increases the risk of pre-eclampsia, but pre-eclampsia *before 20 weeks* strictly points to a molar pregnancy on the USMLE.",
