    "rationale": "- **Histology of a Complete Mole:** Because there is no maternal DNA to regulate growth, the placental tissue goes completely haywire.\n  1. **Diffuse Hydropic Swelling:** Because there is no fetus to pump blood through the placenta, the villi just fill up with fluid and swell massively (creating the grape-like vesicles). In a complete mole, **ALL** of the villi are swollen (diffuse).\n  2. **Diffuse Trophoblastic Proliferation:** The trophoblast cells (cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast) rapidly proliferate in a complete, 360-degree ring around the entire circumference of every villus.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Fetal RBCs / Focal proliferation:** These are the hallmarks of a **Partial Mole**. Because a partial mole has fetal tissue, you see fetal RBCs, and only *some* (focal) villi are swollen.\n  - **(C) Lack of villi / Invasion:** This is the absolute hallmark of **Choriocarcinoma** (a highly malignant tumor).\n  - **(D) Intermediate trophoblasts:** This defines a Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumor (PSTT).\n  - **(E) Normal villi:** Normal pregnancy.",
