    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** The presence of a non-viable fetus, *focal* (not diffuse) cystic changes in the placenta, and nucleated fetal RBCs is the classic presentation of a **Partial Hydatidiform Mole**.\n- **Genetics:** A partial mole is caused by the fertilization of a completely **normal ovum** (23, X) by **two separate sperm** (dispermy) (or one sperm that duplicates). \n  - This results in a **Triploid** karyotype: 69 chromosomes. The most common is **69, XXY** (but can be 69, XXX or 69, XYY). \n  - Because maternal DNA is present, fetal tissue *does* form (hence the fetal parts/RBCs), but the severe genetic imbalance makes it fundamentally non-viable, usually resulting in a missed abortion in the late first trimester.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) 46, XX & (B) 46, XY:** Diploid karyotypes; the classic genetics of a Complete Mole (empty egg + duplicated sperm).\n  - **(C) 45, X0:** Turner syndrome; causes cystic hygromas and spontaneous abortion, but not molar placental changes.\n  - **(E) 47, XX, +18:** Edwards syndrome.",
