    "rationale": "- **The Mimic:** A placenta with massive cystic spaces alongside a viable, diploid (46,XX) fetus is the hallmark of **Placental Mesenchymal Dysplasia (PMD)**.\n- **Pathogenesis:** PMD is a rare vascular anomaly of the placenta. It is characterized by massive stem villous hyperplasia and vascular dilation, which creates vesicles that look exactly like a molar pregnancy on ultrasound.\n- **The Key Difference:** \n  - A Partial Mole has a *triploid* (69,XXY) fetus that is severely growth-restricted and usually non-viable. \n  - PMD has a *normal, diploid* (46,XX or XY) fetus that can survive to term (though they are at high risk for fetal growth restriction and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome). \n  - PMD is completely benign and has absolutely zero risk of trophoblastic malignancy.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Complete mole:** Has no fetus whatsoever.\n  - **(B) & (D) PSTT / Choriocarcinoma:** Solid malignant tumors, not cystic placentas with viable fetuses.\n  - **(E) Vasa previa:** Unprotected fetal vessels crossing the cervical os; causes massive fetal hemorrhage when membranes rupture, not a cystic placenta.",
    "pearl": "**Placental Mesenchymal Dysplasia** mimics a partial mole on ultrasound (cystic placenta + fetus), but the fetus is a **normal diploid (46,XX)**, not triploid.",
