    "stem": "A 27-year-old woman is scheduled for a D&C for a confirmed partial hydatidiform mole. Her physician discusses the post-operative follow-up plan. Which of the following represents the most appropriate monitoring strategy for this patient following the evacuation of her partial mole?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "No follow-up is required as partial moles are entirely benign",
      "B": "Serial quantitative $\\beta$-hCG levels weekly until undetectable, followed by monthly checks for 6 months, while on reliable contraception",
      "C": "Immediate prophylactic total abdominal hysterectomy",
      "D": "A single prophylactic dose of intravenous Methotrexate",
      "E": "Routine Pap smears every 3 months for 2 years"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Post-Molar Follow-up:** Even though partial moles have a much lower risk of malignant transformation than complete moles, they *still* have a small risk of developing into persistent Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia (GTN), where molar tissue invades the myometrium and continues to grow.\n- **The Protocol:** For BOTH partial and complete moles, the standard of care after evacuation is strict monitoring of the tumor marker.\n  - **Serial $\\beta$-hCG:** Must be drawn weekly until it hits zero (undetectable) for 3 consecutive weeks, and then monthly for 6 months.\n  - **Contraception:** The patient MUST be placed on highly reliable contraception (usually **OCPs**) during this entire 6-month period. Why? Because if she gets pregnant normally, her hCG will rise, and the physician will not know if the rising hCG is from the new healthy pregnancy or from a deadly recurring Choriocarcinoma.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) No follow-up:** Malpractice; persistent GTN can occur and must be caught early.\n  - **(C) Hysterectomy:** Way too aggressive for a young woman who likely wants future children; only used for frank malignancy resistant to chemo, or in older women done with childbearing who don't want to do follow-up.\n  - **(D) Prophylactic chemo:** Not indicated unless the hCG starts rising or plateauing.\n  - **(E) Pap smears:** Screen for cervical cancer (HPV), completely unrelated to GTD.",
    "pearl": "After removing ANY molar pregnancy, you MUST follow **serial $\\beta$-hCG levels** to zero and place the patient on **OCPs** for 6 months to ensure a new pregnancy doesn't mask a developing malignancy.",
