    "stem": "A 32-year-old woman presents to the emergency department coughing up bright red blood (hemoptysis) and complaining of shortness of breath. She reports that she had a normal, uncomplicated vaginal delivery of a healthy infant 4 months ago. Her menstrual periods have not returned since the delivery. A chest X-ray reveals multiple bilateral, round, solid lung nodules. Her serum $\\beta$-hCG is $2,500,000 \\text{ mIU/mL}$. A biopsy of one of the lung nodules is performed. What is the expected histologic finding in this biopsy?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Hydropic, edematous chorionic villi with circumferential trophoblast proliferation",
      "B": "Malignant proliferation of cytotrophoblasts and syncytiotrophoblasts with a complete absence of chorionic villi",
      "C": "Sheets of intermediate trophoblasts invading the myometrium without villi",
      "D": "Well-differentiated squamous cells forming keratin pearls",
      "E": "Small, round blue cells forming Homer-Wright rosettes"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** A woman presenting with hemoptysis and lung nodules shortly after a pregnancy (even a *normal* pregnancy), combined with astronomically high $\\beta$-hCG, has **Gestational Choriocarcinoma**.\n- **Pathogenesis:** Choriocarcinoma is a highly aggressive malignant tumor of the trophoblast. While it most commonly arises after a Complete Mole, about 25% arise after a miscarriage, and 25% arise after a perfectly normal, full-term pregnancy. It aggressively invades blood vessels, metastasizing early to the **lungs** (causing the classic \"cannonball\" metastases and hemoptysis).\n- **Histology (The Hallmark):** The absolute defining feature of Choriocarcinoma on histology is a biphasic proliferation of malignant cytotrophoblasts (clear cells) and syncytiotrophoblasts (dark, multinucleated cells that secrete the hCG) that **completely lacks chorionic villi**. (If villi are present, it is a mole, not choriocarcinoma).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Hydropic villi:** This describes a Complete Mole (benign), not choriocarcinoma.\n  - **(C) Intermediate trophoblasts:** Describes a Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumor (PSTT), which secretes hPL, not massive hCG, and rarely metastasizes to lungs early.\n  - **(D) Keratin pearls:** Squamous cell carcinoma (e.g., cervical/lung).\n  - **(E) Homer-Wright rosettes:** Medulloblastoma or Neuroblastoma (pediatric tumors).",
    "pearl": "**Choriocarcinoma** is a highly malignant trophoblastic tumor that presents with high hCG and lung metastases. Histology shows malignant trophoblasts but **NO chorionic villi**.",
