    "stem": "A 25-year-old man presents to the urologist with a painless, enlarged left testicle. An ultrasound reveals a solid testicular mass. His serum $\\beta$-hCG is markedly elevated ($>50,000 \\text{ mIU/mL}$). A radical orchiectomy is performed, and pathology confirms a primary testicular choriocarcinoma. Which of the following best describes the expected response of this patient's tumor to standard Methotrexate-based chemotherapy compared to a woman with gestational choriocarcinoma?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It will have the exact same excellent response and high cure rate",
      "B": "It is highly resistant to chemotherapy and carries a very poor prognosis",
      "C": "It will only respond if the patient is also treated with high-dose testosterone",
      "D": "It will respond well initially, but uniquely metastasizes to the bone rather than the lungs",
      "E": "It will spontaneously regress without any chemotherapy"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Gestational vs. Non-Gestational Choriocarcinoma:** This is a critical distinction in pathology.\n  - **Gestational Choriocarcinoma (Female):** Arises from a pregnancy (mole, miscarriage, or normal). Because the tumor comes from fetal tissue, it contains paternal DNA. The mother's immune system recognizes this paternal DNA as foreign and actively attacks the tumor. This immune response, combined with chemotherapy (Methotrexate), makes gestational choriocarcinoma **highly curable** (often $>95\\%$).\n  - **Non-Gestational Choriocarcinoma (Male or Female):** Arises primarily in the gonads (testes or ovaries) from the patient's *own* germ cells. Because it is 100% the patient's own DNA, there is zero immune response against the tumor. Consequently, non-gestational choriocarcinoma is **highly resistant to chemotherapy** and carries a dismal, incredibly poor prognosis, often rapidly fatal.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Excellent response:** True for gestational, false for non-gestational.\n  - **(C) Testosterone:** Has no role in treating testicular cancer.\n  - **(D) Bone metastasis:** Both prefer hematogenous spread to the lungs.\n  - **(E) Spontaneous regression:** Testicular choriocarcinoma is highly aggressive and rapidly fatal, it does not regress. (Though the primary testicular mass *can* sometimes burn out leaving a scar, the metastases kill the patient).",
    "pearl": "**Gestational** choriocarcinoma (arising from pregnancy) responds incredibly well to chemotherapy. **Non-gestational** (primary testicular/ovarian) choriocarcinoma is highly resistant to chemo and carries a terrible prognosis.",
