    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia (GTN):** When hCG levels plateau or rise after a molar evacuation, the patient has developed malignant GTN. The two main types are Invasive Mole and Choriocarcinoma.\n- **Invasive Mole vs. Choriocarcinoma:**\n  - **Invasive Mole:** This is the most common form of GTN following a complete mole (about 15% of cases). The molar tissue (syncytiotrophoblasts, cytotrophoblasts, AND **chorionic villi**) invades directly into the myometrium. It is locally destructive (can cause uterine rupture) but it rarely metastasizes to distant organs (lungs).\n  - **Choriocarcinoma:** A much more aggressive malignancy (2-3% of moles) that lacks villi and aggressively metastasizes to the lungs early.\n- **The Application:** Because her imaging shows a mass invading the myometrium but a **perfectly clear chest X-ray** (no lung metastases), this strongly points to the locally destructive **Invasive Mole**. (Both are treated with Methotrexate, and biopsy is contraindicated due to severe bleeding risk).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Choriocarcinoma:** Would almost certainly have lung metastases given the time frame and rising hCG.\n  - **(C) PSTT:** Produces hPL, not massive rising hCG, and arises months/years after a normal pregnancy.\n  - **(D) Incomplete evacuation:** If tissue was just left behind, the hCG would remain low/steady, but the deep *invasion* into the myometrium indicates malignant behavior, not just leftover debris in the cavity.\n  - **(E) New pregnancy:** The patient should be on OCPs to prevent this. Furthermore, a new pregnancy wouldn't show a vascular mass invading the uterine muscle wall.",
    "pearl": "An **Invasive Mole** invades the myometrium but rarely metastasizes to the lungs. **Choriocarcinoma** is highly aggressive and classically metastasizes to the **lungs** early.",
