    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Metastatic Pattern of Choriocarcinoma:** Choriocarcinoma is incredibly angiocentric. It rapidly invades the venous system and travels directly to the right side of the heart, pumping into the pulmonary circulation.\n- **The Most Common Site:** The absolute most common site of metastasis (occurring in $\\sim 80\\%$ of metastatic cases) is the **Lungs**.\n- **Staging Protocol:** Therefore, every single patient diagnosed with GTN/Choriocarcinoma MUST have a **Chest X-ray or CT of the chest** to rule out pulmonary metastases. (If the chest is clear, it is highly unlikely the tumor has spread anywhere else. If the chest is positive, they usually undergo MRI of the brain and CT of the abdomen/liver, as the tumor can spread to the brain and liver next).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Brain MRI:** The brain is the second most common site, but you always check the lungs first.\n  - **(C) Bone scan:** Choriocarcinoma rarely metastasizes to bone (unlike prostate or breast cancer).\n  - **(D) Abdomen/Pelvis only:** Completely misses the lungs, the most vital site.\n  - **(E) PET scan:** Not the standard first-line staging modality; CT chest is standard.",
    "pearl": "The most common site of metastasis for **Gestational Choriocarcinoma** is the **Lungs**. A **Chest X-ray or CT Chest** is mandatory for staging.",
