    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Treatment of PSTT:** This is a crucial distinction in the management of Gestational Trophoblastic Disease (GTD).\n  - **Moles:** Treated with D&C.\n  - **Invasive Moles & Choriocarcinoma:** Extremely sensitive to chemotherapy (Methotrexate). Surgery (hysterectomy) is rarely needed and avoided to preserve fertility.\n  - **Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumor (PSTT):** Unlike the others, PSTT is highly **resistant to chemotherapy**. Fortunately, it is slow-growing and tends to remain localized to the uterus for a long time before spreading to the lymph nodes. Therefore, the absolute definitive treatment of choice is surgical excision: **Total Abdominal Hysterectomy**. (If it has metastasized, intense multi-agent chemo is required, but the primary mass must be surgically removed).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Chemo:** PSTT is chemo-resistant.\n  - **(C) Radiation:** Not a primary treatment for trophoblastic disease.\n  - **(D) D&C:** D&C cannot remove it because PSTT invades deep into the myometrium.\n  - **(E) Progestin:** Used for early endometrial hyperplasia, completely ineffective for PSTT.",
    "pearl": "Unlike Choriocarcinoma (which is cured by Methotrexate), **Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumor (PSTT)** is highly resistant to chemotherapy and is treated primarily with **Hysterectomy**.",
