    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A pathologist is teaching residents about the normal histology of the human placenta. To understand the origins of gestational trophoblastic disease, it is vital to know the function of the various trophoblast layers. Which specific layer of the normal placenta is directly responsible for synthesizing and secreting Human Chorionic Gonadotropin (hCG) into the maternal bloodstream?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Cytotrophoblast",
      "B": "Syncytiotrophoblast",
      "C": "Intermediate trophoblast",
      "D": "Extraembryonic mesoderm",
      "E": "Endometrial decidua"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Placental Anatomy & Function:** The outer layer of the early embryo (the blastocyst) is the trophoblast, which differentiates into two main layers:\n  1. **Cytotrophoblast:** The inner, cellular layer. It is highly proliferative and acts as the stem cell layer. \"Cyto makes Cells.\"\n  2. **Syncytiotrophoblast:** The outer layer. It is a massive, continuous, multinucleated mass with no cell boundaries (a syncytium). It directly invades the maternal endometrium and blood vessels. **\"Syncytio Synthesizes Hormones.\"** The syncytiotrophoblast is the sole source of **hCG**, which it dumps directly into the maternal blood to maintain the corpus luteum (saving the pregnancy).\n  - *(Note: The intermediate trophoblast anchors the placenta and secretes hPL).*\n- **Clinical Implication:** Because complete moles and choriocarcinomas have massive, wild proliferation of the *syncytiotrophoblast*, they produce astronomical levels of hCG.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Cytotrophoblast:** Stem cell layer; doesn't make hCG.\n  - **(C) Intermediate trophoblast:** Makes hPL.\n  - **(D) Mesoderm:** Forms the core of the villus (blood vessels/stroma).\n  - **(E) Decidua:** This is the maternal side of the placenta (modified endometrium).",
    "pearl": "The **Syncytiotrophoblast** is the outer, multinucleated layer of the placenta that synthesizes and secretes **$\\beta$-hCG**.",
