    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 40-year-old woman is diagnosed with Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumor (PSTT). Blood tests reveal markedly elevated levels of human placental lactogen (hPL). In a normal, healthy pregnancy, what is the primary physiological function of hPL?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "To maintain the corpus luteum until the placenta can synthesize its own progesterone",
      "B": "To induce maternal insulin resistance, ensuring an adequate supply of glucose for the developing fetus",
      "C": "To stimulate powerful uterine contractions during labor",
      "D": "To relax the pelvic ligaments and pubic symphysis to facilitate delivery",
      "E": "To suppress the maternal immune system from attacking the fetal allograft"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Function of hPL:** Human Placental Lactogen (also called human chorionic somatomammotropin) is secreted by the syncytiotrophoblast and intermediate trophoblasts during pregnancy.\n- **The Mechanism:** The fetus needs a constant, massive supply of glucose to grow. To ensure the fetus gets fed, hPL acts as an anti-insulin hormone in the mother. \n  - It induces significant **maternal insulin resistance**. \n  - This prevents the mother's cells from taking up glucose, leaving high levels of glucose circulating in the maternal blood, which then easily crosses the placenta to feed the baby.\n  - (This is the exact mechanism that causes Gestational Diabetes in women whose pancreases cannot pump out enough extra insulin to overcome the hPL). \n  - It also stimulates lipolysis in the mother so she can burn fatty acids for energy while the baby gets the glucose.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Maintain corpus luteum:** This is the function of **hCG**.\n  - **(C) Uterine contractions:** Function of **Oxytocin** and Prostaglandins.\n  - **(D) Relax ligaments:** Function of **Relaxin**.\n  - **(E) Suppress immunity:** Mediated by early pregnancy factor, HLA-G, and progesterone.",
    "pearl": "**Human Placental Lactogen (hPL)** induces **maternal insulin resistance**, keeping blood glucose high so it can cross the placenta and feed the fetus. It is the primary hormone responsible for Gestational Diabetes.",
