    "stem": "A 31-year-old woman is evaluated for persistent vaginal bleeding. She had a miscarriage 6 months ago. Ultrasound reveals a 3-cm solid mass in the myometrium. Her serum $\\beta$-hCG is $150 \\text{ mIU/mL}$. A biopsy is performed. The pathologist must distinguish between an Invasive Mole, Choriocarcinoma, and a Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumor (PSTT). The complete absence of which of the following structures on the biopsy slide would definitively rule out an Invasive Mole?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Intermediate trophoblasts",
      "B": "Syncytiotrophoblasts",
      "C": "Chorionic villi",
      "D": "Cytotrophoblasts",
      "E": "Fetal red blood cells"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Distinguishing GTD on Histology:** This is the most critical differential in trophoblastic pathology.\n  1. **Invasive Mole:** Features malignant invasion of the myometrium by **ALL THREE components:** Cytotrophoblasts, Syncytiotrophoblasts, AND **Chorionic Villi**. \n  2. **Choriocarcinoma:** Features malignant proliferation of Cytotrophoblasts and Syncytiotrophoblasts, but absolutely **NO Chorionic Villi**.\n  3. **PSTT:** Features proliferation of Intermediate Trophoblasts, with **NO Chorionic Villi**.\n- **The Application:** If the pathologist looks at the slide and there are zero chorionic villi, the diagnosis CANNOT be an invasive mole. It must be either choriocarcinoma or PSTT.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Intermediate trophoblasts:** Present in PSTT, but their absence doesn't rule out an invasive mole.\n  - **(B) Syncytio & (D) Cyto:** Present in both invasive moles and choriocarcinoma.\n  - **(E) Fetal RBCs:** Present only in Partial Moles, rarely seen in invasive complete moles or choriocarcinoma.",
    "pearl": "An **Invasive Mole** has **Chorionic Villi** invading the muscle. **Choriocarcinoma** and **PSTT** completely **LACK Chorionic Villi**.",
