    "qnum": 27,
    "topic": "Lecture 3.4: Gestational Trophoblastic Disease",
    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 30-year-old woman is diagnosed with high-risk, metastatic gestational choriocarcinoma. She is started on a multi-agent chemotherapy regimen known as EMA-CO (Etoposide, Methotrexate, Actinomycin D, Cyclophosphamide, and Vincristine). Which of the drugs in this specific regimen is an alkylating agent that works by covalently cross-linking DNA at the guanine N-7 position, preventing DNA replication and transcription?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Etoposide",
      "B": "Methotrexate",
      "C": "Actinomycin D (Dactinomycin)",
      "D": "Cyclophosphamide",
      "E": "Vincristine"
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "- **EMA-CO Regimen:** This is the standard aggressive regimen for high-risk GTN. You must know the mechanism of action of major chemotherapy drugs for Step 1.\n- **Cyclophosphamide:** This is a classic **Alkylating Agent** (specifically, a nitrogen mustard). \n  - It works by attaching an alkyl group to the **N-7 position of Guanine** bases in the DNA. \n  - This forms intra-strand and inter-strand **cross-links**, completely tangling the DNA. The cell cannot replicate or transcribe its DNA, leading to apoptosis. \n  - *(Major side effect to remember: Hemorrhagic cystitis, prevented with Mesna).*\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Etoposide:** Inhibits Topoisomerase II, causing double-strand DNA breaks.\n  - **(B) Methotrexate:** Inhibits Dihydrofolate Reductase (DHFR), preventing pyrimidine synthesis.\n  - **(C) Actinomycin D (Dactinomycin):** An antitumor antibiotic. It works by **intercalating** (sliding between) the DNA base pairs, blocking RNA polymerase. (Highly effective for choriocarcinoma and Wilms tumor).\n  - **(E) Vincristine:** Binds tubulin, preventing microtubule polymerization (blocks mitosis in metaphase).",
    "pearl": "**Cyclophosphamide** is an **alkylating agent** that cross-links DNA at the Guanine N-7 position. Beware of hemorrhagic cystitis (give Mesna).",
