    "rationale": "unintended, and globally, approximately 61% of these unintended pregnancies end in induced abortion, according to WHO data. \u25cf  A. Approximately 25% of all pregnancies in the United States are unintended, with the highest rates observed among high-income, married, college-educated women.  -  Incorrect  . Approximately 45% (not 25%) of  pregnancies in the U.S. are unintended, and the rates are highest among low-income women, ages 18\u201324, and women of color, while being lowest among high-income, white, college-educated, married women. \u25cf  B. Approximately 45% of all pregnancies in the United States are unintended, and globally, approximately 61% of these unintended pregnancies end in induced abortion, according to WHO data.  -  Correct  . Epidemiology  of unintended pregnancy shows that 45% of all pregnancies in the United States are unintended (unplanned but not necessarily unwanted). Globally, according to the WHO, approximately 61% of these unintended pregnancies end in an induced abortion. \u25cf  C. Unintended pregnancies are universally defined as unwanted by the parents, and approximately 10% of women of reproductive age (15\u201344 years) undergo an induced abortion annually in the U.S.  -  Incorrect  .  Unintended pregnancies can be mistimed but are not necessarily unwanted. Approximately 2% (not 10%) of women aged 15\u201344 years have an induced abortion annually in the U.S.. \u25cf  D. Approximately 75% of unintended pregnancies in the United States occur in women aged 35\u201344 years, and more than 90% of these end in spontaneous miscarriage.  -  Incorrect  . The highest rates are seen  in younger women (ages 18\u201324), not older women, and the majority do not end in miscarriage. \u25cf  E. The rate of unintended pregnancy has risen exponentially among higher-income, white, college-educated, married women over the past decade due to a shift away from long-acting reversible contraception.  -  Incorrect  . The rates remain lowest in this specific demographic, and LARCs have actually grown in popularity.",
