    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A G0 female is reviewing non-hormonal, non-invasive barrier and spermicidal contraceptive methods with her gynecologist. She wants to understand the differences in efficacy, usage instructions, and potential side effects of the Today vaginal sponge, the vaginal diaphragm, and external male condoms. 331 According to the provided sources on non-hormonal methods, which of the following statements correctly describes these contraceptive options?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The Today sponge is a polyurethane dome-shaped device containing nonoxynol-9 that must be moistened with water before insertion and has an equal first-year failure rate of 5% in both nulliparous and parous women.",
      "B": "The diaphragm is a dome-shaped silicone cup that must be used with spermicide, requires professional fitting by a provider (except for the one-size-fits-all Caya diaphragm), must be left in place for at least 6 hours after intercourse, and has a typical-use pregnancy rate of 12% per year.",
      "C": "External male condoms have a typical-use pregnancy rate of 5% per year, and approximately 15% of condoms break during intercourse due to manufacturing defects.",
      "D": "Spermicides containing nonoxynol-9 have a typical-use pregnancy rate of 5% per year and are highly protective against sexually transmitted infections, including HIV.",
      "E": "Both the Today sponge and the diaphragm must be removed immediately after intercourse to prevent the development of toxic shock syndrome."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "with spermicide, requires professional fitting by a provider (except for the one-size-fits-all Caya diaphragm), must be left in place for at least 6 hours after intercourse, and has a typical-use pregnancy rate of 12% per year. \u25cf  A. The Today sponge is a polyurethane dome-shaped device containing nonoxynol-9 that must be moistened with water before insertion and has an equal first-year failure rate of 5% in both nulliparous and parous women.  -  Incorrect  . The Today sponge does contain nonoxynol-9 and must be moistened with water. However, its failure rate is much higher and varies significantly by parity: 12% for nulliparous women and 24% for parous women at 1 year. \u25cf  B. The diaphragm is a dome-shaped silicone cup that must be used with spermicide, requires professional fitting by a provider (except for the one-size-fits-all Caya diaphragm), must be left in place for at least 6 hours after intercourse, and has a typical-use pregnancy rate of 12% per year.  -  Correct  . The diaphragm is a silicone barrier cup used with spermicide. Traditional diaphragms (like the Milex) require provider fitting, whereas the newer Caya is designed as a one-size-fits-all device. It must be left in place for at least 6 hours after intercourse to ensure spermicidal efficacy, and has a typical-use pregnancy rate of 12% per year. \u25cf  C. External male condoms have a typical-use pregnancy rate of 5% per year, and approximately 15% of condoms break during intercourse due to manufacturing defects.  -  Incorrect  . Male condoms have an 18% (not  5%) typical-use pregnancy rate, and approximately 3% (not 15%) break due to friction during use. \u25cf  D. Spermicides containing nonoxynol-9 have a typical-use pregnancy rate of 5% per year and are highly protective against sexually transmitted infections, including HIV.  -  Incorrect  . Standalone spermicide  has a very high failure rate of 28%. Furthermore, nonoxynol-9 can cause vaginal mucosal irritation, which may actually increase  the risk of HIV transmission, rather than  protecting against it. \u25cf  E. Both the Today sponge and the diaphragm must be removed immediately after intercourse to prevent the development of toxic shock syndrome.  -  Incorrect  . Diaphragms must be left in for at least 6 hours after intercourse. Removing them immediately would render them ineffective. 344",
