    "stem": "A 22-year-old female presents to the clinic requesting a highly effective, long-term, non-hormonal method of contraception. She has a history of heavy menstrual bleeding and severe dysmenorrhea. After counseling, she decides to undergo placement of a Copper T380A (ParaGard) intrauterine device. What is the primary mechanism of action of the copper IUD, what is its FDA-approved duration of use, and what are its most common side effects?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It constantly releases low doses of progesterone to inhibit ovulation; approved for 5 years; causes amenorrhea and breast tenderness.",
      "B": "It causes a sterile inflammatory response in the endometrium that is toxic to sperm and ova, preventing fertilization and impairing implantation; approved for 10 years (with evidence of effectiveness up to 12 years in older, lower-fertility patients); causes an increase in menstrual flow and cramps.",
      "C": "It thickens cervical mucus via copper-induced glycoprotein cross-linking; approved for 1 year; causes intermenstrual spotting and vaginal dryness.",
      "D": "It mechanically occludes both fallopian tube ostia; approved for 20 years; causes pelvic inflammatory disease and ovarian cysts.",
      "E": "It prevents the LH surge by releasing copper ions into the maternal portal circulation; approved for 3 years; causes significant systemic weight gain."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "that is toxic to sperm and ova, preventing fertilization and impairing implantation; approved for 10 years (with evidence of effectiveness up to 12 years in older, lower-fertility patients); causes an increase in menstrual flow and cramps. \u25cf  A. It constantly releases low doses of progesterone to inhibit ovulation; approved for 5 years; causes amenorrhea and breast tenderness.  -  Incorrect  . This describes a levonorgestrel-releasing hormonal IUD, not the copper IUD. \u25cf  B. It causes a sterile inflammatory response in the endometrium that is toxic to sperm and ova, preventing fertilization and impairing implantation; approved for 10 years (with evidence of effectiveness up to 12 years in older, lower-fertility patients); causes an increase in menstrual flow and cramps.  -  Correct  . The copper IUD (ParaGard T380A) is a non-hormonal device that functions by releasing copper ions into the uterine cavity, creating a sterile inflammatory response. This altered environment is highly toxic to sperm and ova, blocking fertilization, while also impairing endometrial receptivity to prevent implantation. It is FDA-approved for 10 years (with data showing efficacy up to 12 years in older patients). Its primary side effects are an increase in menstrual flow (menorrhagia) and dysmenorrhea (cramps). \u25cf  C. It thickens cervical mucus via copper-induced glycoprotein cross-linking; approved for 1 year; causes intermenstrual spotting and vaginal dryness.  - Incorrect  . Progesterone thickens cervical mucus, not  copper, and the copper IUD is approved for much longer than 1 year. \u25cf  D. It mechanically occludes both fallopian tube ostia; approved for 20 years; causes pelvic inflammatory disease and ovarian cysts.  -  Incorrect  . The IUD sits in the uterine cavity, not the fallopian tubes, and does not mechanically block them. \u25cf  E. It prevents the LH surge by releasing copper ions into the maternal portal circulation; approved for 3 years; causes significant systemic weight gain.  - Incorrect  . Copper does not enter the portal circulation  or affect the HPO axis to prevent the LH surge; it has entirely local, non-hormonal effects.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
