    "stem": "A 19-year-old female presents to the clinic on a Monday morning requesting emergency contraception. She notes that she had unprotected intercourse on Friday night (approximately 60 hours ago). She does not want to use any hormonal methods and is interested in the most effective emergency contraceptive option available. Within what timeframe must a copper IUD be inserted for emergency contraception, what is its efficacy rate, and is there evidence that other IUDs can be used?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It must be inserted within 48 hours; has a 90% efficacy rate; hormonal IUDs are strictly contraindicated for emergency contraception.",
      "B": "It must be inserted within 5 days (120 hours) of unprotected intercourse; is 99.9% effective, making it the most effective method of emergency contraception; and there is evidence that the levonorgestrel-releasing IUD (LNG-IUD) can also be effective.",
      "C": "It must be inserted within 24 hours; is 95% effective; and only copper IUDs can be used as emergency contraception.",
      "D": "It can be inserted up to 10 days after intercourse; is 80% effective; but no other IUD options are effective.",
      "E": "It must be inserted within 72 hours; is 91% effective; and the subdermal etonorgestrel rod can also be used as an alternative emergency option."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "intercourse; is 99.9% effective, making it the most effective method of emergency contraception; and there is evidence that the levonorgestrel-releasing IUD (LNG-IUD) can also be effective. \u25cf  A. It must be inserted within 48 hours; has a 90% efficacy rate; hormonal IUDs are strictly contraindicated for emergency contraception.  -  Incorrect  . The timeline is up to 5 days (not 48 hours), and there is evidence that the LNG-IUD is also effective for emergency contraception. \u25cf  B. It must be inserted within 5 days (120 hours) of unprotected intercourse; is 99.9% effective, making it the most effective method of emergency contraception; 345 and there is evidence that the levonorgestrel-releasing IUD (LNG-IUD) can also be effective.  -  Correct  . The copper IUD represents the  single most effective method of emergency contraception, achieving a 99.9% efficacy rate. To be effective, it must be inserted within 5 days (120 hours) of unprotected intercourse. Furthermore, there is clinical evidence that the 52 mg levonorgestrel-releasing IUD (LNG-IUD) can also be used effectively for this purpose. \u25cf  C. It must be inserted within 24 hours; is 95% effective; and only copper IUDs can be used as emergency contraception.  -  Incorrect  . The  timeline is 5 days, the efficacy is higher (99.9%), and LNG-IUDs represent an alternative option. \u25cf  D. It can be inserted up to 10 days after intercourse; is 80% effective; but no other IUD options are effective.  -  Incorrect  . The FDA-approved  timeline is 5 days, and the efficacy is vastly understated here. \u25cf  E. It must be inserted within 72 hours; is 91% effective; and the subdermal etonorgestrel rod can also be used as an alternative emergency option.  - Incorrect  . 72 hours is the standard limit for oral  Plan B, not the IUD (which is 5 days). The subdermal rod is not an emergency contraceptive.",
