    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A gynecologist is presenting a lecture on Long-Acting Reversible Contraception (LARC) to a group of family medicine residents. She highlights the clinical significance of LARC methods in reducing unintended pregnancy rates due to their high efficacy and superior continuation rates compared to Short-Acting Reversible Contraception (SARC). Which of the following correctly describes the efficacy and 1-year continuation rates of LARC methods compared to SARC methods?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "LARCs are approximately 2 times more effective than oral contraceptive pills; and both categories have identical 1-year continuation rates of approximately 75% across all age groups.",
      "B": "LARCs (IUDs and implants) are 20 times more effective than SARC methods (OCPs, patch, or ring); and LARCs have significantly higher 1-year continuation rates (around 80% for LNG-IUD, copper IUD, and implants) compared to OCPs and DMPA injections (around 50% or less).",
      "C": "SARC methods are more effective than LARCs because daily oral dosing maintains higher, more stable systemic hormone levels; OCPs have an 85% continuation rate.",
      "D": "LARCs are 5 times more effective than OCPs; but LARCs have lower continuation rates because most patients request removal within 6 months due to severe pelvic pain.",
      "E": "LARCs and SARC methods have identical typical-use pregnancy rates of 9% per year; but LARCs are preferred because they do not require a prescription."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "SARC methods (OCPs, patch, or ring); and LARCs have significantly higher 1-year continuation rates (around 80% for LNG-IUD, copper IUD, and implants) compared to OCPs and DMPA injections (around 50% or less). \u25cf  A. LARCs are approximately 2 times more effective than oral contraceptive pills; and both categories have identical 1-year continuation rates of approximately 75% across all age groups.  -  Incorrect  . LARCs are 20 times  (not 2 times) more effective in typical use because they remove the element of daily or weekly user compliance, and their continuation rates are vastly superior. \u25cf  B. LARCs (IUDs and implants) are 20 times more effective than SARC methods (OCPs, patch, or ring); and LARCs have significantly higher 1-year continuation rates (around 80% for LNG-IUD, copper IUD, and implants) compared to OCPs and DMPA injections (around 50% or less).  -  Correct  . Long-Acting  Reversible Contraception (LARC) methods are extremely effective because their efficacy is independent of patient compliance. In typical use, LARCs are 20 times more effective than typical use of Short-Acting Reversible Contraception (SARC) such as pills, patches, or rings. Furthermore, LARCs demonstrate much higher 1-year continuation rates (generally around 80% across age groups) compared to OCPs and DMPA injections, which have 1-year continuation rates of approximately 50% or lower. \u25cf  C. SARC methods are more effective than LARCs because daily oral dosing maintains higher, more stable systemic hormone levels; OCPs have an 85% continuation rate.  -  Incorrect  . SARC methods are less  effective in practice due to high rates of typical-use failure (9% failure for pills vs. <1% for LARCs). \u25cf  D. LARCs are 5 times more effective than OCPs; but LARCs have lower continuation rates because most patients request removal within 6 months due to 346 severe pelvic pain.  -  Incorrect  . LARCs are 20 times more effective, and their continuation rates are high, not low. \u25cf  E. LARCs and SARC methods have identical typical-use pregnancy rates of 9% per year; but LARCs are preferred because they do not require a prescription.  - Incorrect  . LARCs have typical-use pregnancy rates  of <1% (e.g., 0.05% for implant, 0.2% for LNG-IUD, 0.8% for copper IUD). They also require professional insertion and a prescription.",
