    "rationale": "for 3 years of use; it has the lowest failure rate of ALL contraceptive options (0.05%) and is approximately 20 times more effective than typical use of OCPs, the patch, or the ring. \u25cf  A. ParaGard; contains copper; approved for 10 years; has a failure rate of 0.8% and is less effective than male sterilization.  -  Incorrect  .  ParaGard is an IUD, not a subdermal implant. \u25cf  B. Mirena; contains levonorgestrel; approved for 8 years; has a failure rate of 0.2% and is equivalent to the patch.  -  Incorrect  . Mirena  is an IUD, not a subdermal implant, and is much more effective than typical use of the patch. \u25cf  C. Nexplanon; contains etonorgestrel (progestin-only); approved for 3 years of use; it has the lowest failure rate of ALL contraceptive options (0.05%) and is approximately 20 times more effective than typical use of OCPs, the patch, or the ring.  -  Correct  . The single-rod subdermal contraceptive  implant is marketed under the brand name  Nexplanon  . It contains  etonorgestrel  (a  progestin-only method), is approved for  3 years of use  , and holds the distinction  of having the lowest failure rate of all available contraceptive methods (0.05% failure rate), outperforming both male and female sterilization. \u25cf  D. Depo-Provera; contains medroxyprogesterone acetate; administered every 3 months; has a failure rate of 6% and is less effective than the vaginal ring.  - Incorrect  . Depo-Provera is an intramuscular or subcutaneous  injection, not a subdermal implant, and is more effective than the vaginal ring (6% failure vs. 9% failure). \u25cf  E. Skyla; contains levonorgestrel; approved for 3 years; has a failure rate of 2% and is the smallest implant available.  -  Incorrect  .  Skyla is a small hormonal IUD, not a subdermal implant.",
