    "rationale": "years, and are associated with a 20% rate of amenorrhea after 1 year; whereas Skyla and Kyleena are smaller, have a lower dose of LNG, are more likely to maintain ovulatory cycles, and are associated with more vaginal bleeding. \u25cf  A. Mirena/Liletta release 19-20 mcg LNG daily, are approved for 8 years, and are associated with a 20% rate of amenorrhea after 1 year; whereas Skyla and Kyleena 347 are smaller, have a lower dose of LNG, are more likely to maintain ovulatory cycles, and are associated with more vaginal bleeding.  -  Correct  . Mirena and Liletta are larger LNG-releasing intrauterine systems that release 19\u201320 mcg of levonorgestrel daily, are FDA-approved for up to 8 years, and lead to complete amenorrhea in approximately 20% of users by the end of the first year. In contrast, Skyla and Kyleena are smaller devices designed for easier insertion (using a 3.8 mm tube). Because they release lower daily doses of LNG, they are more likely to maintain ovulatory cycles and are clinically associated with more frequent vaginal bleeding or spotting rather than early amenorrhea. \u25cf  B. Mirena and Liletta are non-hormonal devices; while Skyla and Kyleena are combined estrogen-progesterone devices approved for 10 years.  -  Incorrect  . All four are progestin-only devices; none contain estrogen. \u25cf  C. Skyla and Kyleena are approved for 8 years and cause a 50% rate of complete amenorrhea within the first 3 months; whereas Mirena is approved for only 3 years.  -  Incorrect  . Skyla (3 years) and Kyleena (5  years) have shorter durations than Mirena (8 years) and lower amenorrhea rates, not higher. \u25cf  D. Mirena and Liletta are inserted using a 3.8 mm tube; whereas Skyla and Kyleena require a 10 mm tube and are associated with a higher rate of uterine perforation.  -  Incorrect  . Skyla and Kyleena are smaller  and use the narrower 3.8 mm tube for insertion, making them easier to place than the larger Mirena. \u25cf  E. All four LNG-IUS devices have identical hormone doses, dimensions, and amenorrhea rates, differing only in their brand names and manufacturers.  - Incorrect  . They differ in dimensions, daily hormone  release rates, approved durations of use, and clinical side effect profiles.",
