    "rationale": "tenderness, nausea (queasiness), bloating, headaches, and unscheduled (breakthrough) bleeding or amenorrhea; however, they do not appear to cause significant weight gain, mood changes, or a significant impact on sexual function or desire. \u25cf  A. COCPs are clinically proven to cause an average weight gain of 10-15 pounds in the first year, severe mood swings, and a significant decrease in sexual desire.  - Incorrect  . Large-scale clinical studies have refuted  these common claims, showing no significant weight gain or clinically significant mood and libido changes. \u25cf  B. COCPs commonly cause mild side effects such as breast tenderness, nausea (queasiness), bloating, headaches, and unscheduled (breakthrough) bleeding or amenorrhea; however, they do not appear to cause significant weight gain, mood changes, or a significant impact on sexual function or desire.  -  Correct  . Combined Hormonal Contraceptives (including COCPs, patches, and vaginal rings) are associated with several benign, transient side effects, including breast tenderness, nausea, bloating, headaches, and breakthrough bleeding. However, clinical evidence indicates that they do not cause significant weight gain, clinically significant mood alterations, or negative impacts on sexual desire. \u25cf  C. COCPs have absolutely zero side effects, and any symptoms reported by patients are purely psychosomatic.  -  Incorrect  . The  minor side effects (like nausea, breakthrough bleeding, and breast tenderness) are real and driven by estrogen and progestin. \u25cf  D. COCPs cause permanent, irreversible amenorrhea and severe, untreatable galactorrhea in more than 50% of users.  -  Incorrect  .  While some users experience reversible amenorrhea during use, it is not permanent. Galactorrhea is not a common side effect. \u25cf  E. COCPs do not cause nausea or headaches but are associated with a 100% rate of severe acne and hirsutism due to androgenic cross-reactivity.  -  Incorrect  . 349 Estrogen in COCPs increases sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG), which actually lowers free testosterone levels and clinically  improves  acne and hirsutism.",
