    "rationale": "100 mmHg) is associated with an up to 10-fold increased risk of myocardial infarction and stroke when combined with the use of estrogen-containing CHCs; therefore, CHCs represent a Category 4 (unacceptable health risk) in this patient. \u25cf  A. CHCs have no effect on cardiovascular outcomes, and she can safely continue her current prescription.  -  Incorrect  . Estrogen acts  on the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and blood vessels, significantly increasing the risk of cardiovascular events in patients with high blood pressure. \u25cf  B. Uncontrolled hypertension (systolic >= 160 mmHg or diastolic >= 100 mmHg) is associated with an up to 10-fold increased risk of myocardial infarction and stroke when combined with the use of estrogen-containing CHCs; therefore, CHCs represent a Category 4 (unacceptable health risk) in this patient.  -  Correct  . According to the provided sources on combined hormonal contraceptives, women with uncontrolled hypertension (defined as systolic >= 160 mmHg or diastolic >= 100 mmHg) have an up to  10-fold increased risk  of suffering  a myocardial infarction or stroke if they use estrogen-containing combined hormonal methods. Therefore, the CDC MEC classifies this scenario as  Category 4 (unacceptable  health risk)  , requiring a transition to non-estrogen methods (such as progestin-only options or the copper IUD). 351 \u25cf  C. CHCs will act as a mild vasodilator and help lower her blood pressure, making them a Category 1 option.  -  Incorrect  . Estrogen and  progestin typically  increase  or maintain blood pressure; they do not act as clinical vasodilators to lower it. \u25cf  D. Uncontrolled hypertension increases the risk of endometrial cancer when using CHCs, requiring a concurrent endometrial biopsy.  -  Incorrect  . Hypertension does not interact with CHCs to increase endometrial cancer risk (CHCs actually  decrease  the risk of endometrial cancer). \u25cf  E. She can continue CHCs safely if her daily estrogen dose is increased to 50 mcg of ethinyl estradiol to promote nitric oxide release.  -  Incorrect  . Higher doses of estrogen would further escalate her risk of thrombotic events and stroke.",
