    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 21-year-old female presents to the university health clinic requesting emergency contraception after her partner's condom broke during intercourse 36 hours ago. She has no chronic medical conditions and her BMI is 22 kg/m\u00b2. What are the mechanisms of action, approved timelines, and comparative efficacies of oral Levonorgestrel (Plan B) versus Ulipristal acetate (ella) for emergency contraception?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Plan B is a progesterone receptor antagonist that works up to 120 hours; ella is a pure estrogen pill that works up to 24 hours and is more effective in obese women.",
      "B": "Plan B is a progestin (1.5 mg) that works primarily by inhibiting the LH surge to prevent or delay ovulation, is approved for use up to 72 hours, and is less effective in women with a BMI > 25-30 kg/m\u00b2; ella is a selective progesterone receptor modulator that delays ovulation, is approved for use up to 120 hours, and maintains superior efficacy across a wider range of gestations and body weights.",
      "C": "Both methods work primarily by causing the immediate destruction and shedding of an already implanted blastocyst; they are 100% effective up to 10 days after intercourse.",
      "D": "Plan B is more effective than ella at 120 hours; and both methods are highly contraindicated in women with a history of venous thromboembolism.",
      "E": "Plan B is taken as a 5-day serial course of daily pills; whereas ella is a single intravaginal suppository that must be placed by a provider."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "the LH surge to prevent or delay ovulation, is approved for use up to 72 hours, and is less effective in women with a BMI > 25-30 kg/m\u00b2; ella is a selective progesterone receptor modulator that delays ovulation, is approved for use up to 120 hours, and maintains superior efficacy across a wider range of gestations and body weights. \u25cf  A. Plan B is a progesterone receptor antagonist that works up to 120 hours; ella is a pure estrogen pill that works up to 24 hours and is more effective in obese women.  -  Incorrect  . Plan B is a pure progestin agonist,  and ella is a selective progesterone receptor modulator. \u25cf  B. Plan B is a progestin (1.5 mg) that works primarily by inhibiting the LH surge to prevent or delay ovulation, is approved for use up to 72 hours, and is less effective in women with a BMI > 25-30 kg/m\u00b2; ella is a selective progesterone receptor modulator that delays ovulation, is approved for use up to 120 hours, and maintains superior efficacy across a wider range of gestations and body weights.  - Correct  . Levonorgestrel emergency contraception (Plan  B One-Step) consists of a single 1.5 mg dose of LNG. It acts primarily by  inhibiting  the luteinizing hormone (LH) surge  , thereby preventing or delaying ovulation. It  is FDA-approved for use within 72 hours of unprotected intercourse, and its efficacy decreases significantly in women with a BMI > 25\u201330 kg/m\u00b2. In contrast, Ulipristal acetate (ella) is a selective progesterone receptor modulator that delays ovulation even after the LH surge has started. It is approved for use up to 120 hours (5 days) and maintains its efficacy better than Plan B in women with higher body weights. \u25cf  C. Both methods work primarily by causing the immediate destruction and shedding of an already implanted blastocyst; they are 100% effective up to 10 days after intercourse.  -  Incorrect  . Neither Plan  B nor ella acts as an abortifacient; they do not disrupt an established, implanted pregnancy. \u25cf  D. Plan B is more effective than ella at 120 hours; and both methods are highly contraindicated in women with a history of venous thromboembolism.  -  Incorrect  . ella is superior to Plan B at 120 hours, and emergency oral contraception is classified as CDC MEC Category 1 (no restriction) for all women, including those with a history of VTE. 352 \u25cf  E. Plan B is taken as a 5-day serial course of daily pills; whereas ella is a single intravaginal suppository that must be placed by a provider.  -  Incorrect  . Both are oral, single-dose regimens.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
