    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** Multiple soft, fleshy, cauliflower-like (verrucous) lesions on the genitals are classic **Condylomata Acuminata (Genital Warts)**, caused by the Human Papillomavirus (HPV).\n- **Histopathology:** The absolute diagnostic hallmark of an active HPV infection in squamous epithelium is the presence of **Koilocytes**. \n  - A koilocyte is a mature squamous epithelial cell that has been structurally altered by the virus. \n  - It features a large, dark, wrinkled (raisin-like) nucleus (nuclear atypia) that is surrounded by a large, distinct, clear space in the cytoplasm (**a perinuclear halo**).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Henderson-Paterson bodies:** The hallmark of Molluscum Contagiosum (a Poxvirus causing umbilicated papules).\n  - **(C) Multinucleated giant cells:** The hallmark of Herpes Simplex Virus (causes painful vesicular ulcers, not fleshy warts).\n  - **(D) Broad-based budding yeast:** Blastomyces dermatitidis (a systemic fungus).\n  - **(E) Obscured borders:** Clue cells, the hallmark of Bacterial Vaginosis (causes fishy discharge, no warts).",
