    "rationale": "- **HPV Strains and Clinical Manifestations:** There are over 100 strains of the Human Papillomavirus. They are strictly categorized by their oncogenic potential (ability to cause cancer).\n  - **Low-Risk Strains (HPV 6 and 11):** These strains are responsible for over 90% of **Condylomata Acuminata (genital warts)**. They cause benign cellular proliferation but rarely, if ever, integrate into the host genome to cause high-grade dysplasia or cancer. (They also cause laryngeal papillomatosis in infants).\n  - **High-Risk Strains (HPV 16, 18, 31, 33):** These strains cause **Cervical, Anal, Vaginal, and Oropharyngeal Cancer**. Crucially, they usually do *not* cause visible external warts; they cause flat, invisible dysplastic lesions (CIN/SIL) that require acetic acid or Pap smears to identify.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) 16 & 18 & (D) 31 & 33:** High-risk strains causing cancer, not typical visible fleshy warts.\n  - **(C) HSV 1 & 2:** Cause Herpes (painful blisters).\n  - **(E) Adenovirus 40 & 41:** Cause viral gastroenteritis.",
