    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Treatment of Genital Warts:** Treatment focuses on physical destruction or immune clearance of the lesions.\n  - **Imiquimod:** This is a topical immune response modifier. It works by binding to **Toll-like receptor 7 (TLR7)** on local immune cells (macrophages/dendritic cells). This binding triggers a massive local release of **cytokines, specifically Interferon-alpha (IFN-$\\alpha$)**, TNF-alpha, and IL-6. This localized immune storm attacks and clears the HPV-infected cells. It does not directly kill the virus or the cells. It is a very common, patient-applied therapy.\n- **Pregnancy Considerations:** While cryotherapy or surgical excision are the absolute safest treatments in pregnancy, Imiquimod is often used off-label if topical medical therapy is required, as it has low systemic absorption compared to older cytotoxics. (However, Trichloroacetic Acid (TCA) is actually the *preferred* topical caustic agent in pregnancy). *Wait, let's look at the mechanism.* The question specifically asks for the drug that stimulates TLR7. That is undeniably Imiquimod. \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Podophyllin:** An antimitotic agent (binds tubulin, stopping metaphase). It is **strictly contraindicated in pregnancy** due to severe teratogenicity and systemic toxicity.\n  - **(B) TCA:** A strong acid that chemically burns/coagulates the wart. It does not stimulate the immune system (though it is safe in pregnancy).\n  - **(D) 5-FU:** A pyrimidine analog (chemotherapy). Contraindicated in pregnancy.\n  - **(E) Acyclovir:** Antiviral for Herpes (HSV), totally ineffective against HPV.",
    "pearl": "**Imiquimod** treats HPV warts by acting as an agonist at **Toll-like Receptor 7 (TLR7)**, triggering the local release of **Interferon-alpha** to stimulate cell-mediated immune clearance.",
