    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** Intense pubic itching (worse at night), pale blue macules (maculae ceruleae - caused by the insect's saliva/bite), and visible crab-like insects and nits (eggs) attached to the pubic hair is the classic presentation of **Phthiriasis pubis (Pubic Lice / \"Crabs\")**. It is caused by the ectoparasite *Phthirus pubis*.\n- **Treatment:** The first-line treatment for all lice (head, body, and pubic) is a topical pediculicide. The most commonly used, effective, and safe over-the-counter option is **Topical Permethrin (1% lotion)**. \n  - **Mechanism:** Permethrin works by binding to the voltage-gated **sodium channels** on the nerve cell membranes of the parasite. It locks the channels in the open position, causing continuous nerve depolarization, paralysis, and death of the insect.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Oral Ivermectin:** Used for severe, crusted (Norwegian) Scabies, or Strongyloides. It is an alternative for highly resistant lice, but topical permethrin is first-line.\n  - **(C) Cryotherapy & (D) 5-FU:** Treatments for HPV warts, not lice.\n  - **(E) Metronidazole:** Antibiotic/Antiprotozoal for Trichomonas/BV.",
