    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** Intense generalized pruritus (worse at night) with characteristic wavy, thread-like **burrows** in the interdigital web spaces and flexor surfaces is the pathognomonic presentation of **Scabies**, caused by the mite *Sarcoptes scabiei*.\n- **Pathogenesis of the Itch:** \n  - The female mite burrows into the stratum corneum of the epidermis to lay her eggs. As she burrows, she leaves behind feces (scybala).\n  - The intense, maddening itch of scabies is NOT caused simply by the mite crawling around. It is a **Type IV (Delayed) cell-mediated hypersensitivity reaction** mounted by the patient's immune system against the foreign antigens of the mite, its eggs, and its feces. \n  - This is why the intense itching does not start until 2-4 weeks *after* the initial infestation (it takes time for the T-cells to become sensitized), and why the itching persists for weeks even after the mites are completely killed by treatment (post-scabietic pruritus).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Neurotoxin:** Not the mechanism.\n  - **(C) Type II (IgG against basement membrane):** This is Bullous Pemphigoid (causes large blisters, not burrows).\n  - **(D) Mechanical destruction:** The burrowing causes the physical track, but the widespread, generalized rash and intense itch are due to the systemic immune hypersensitivity response.\n  - **(E) Type I (IgE):** This would cause immediate hives/anaphylaxis (like a bee sting or peanut allergy).",
    "pearl": "The characteristic lesion of **Scabies** is the **burrow** (often in web spaces of fingers). The intense itching is caused by a **Type IV (delayed) hypersensitivity** reaction to the mite and its feces.",
