    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** Firm, dome-shaped, flesh-colored papules with a **central umbilication** (dimple) are the absolute hallmark of **Molluscum Contagiosum**. In adults, it is usually sexually transmitted (groin/thighs); in children, it's transmitted by direct contact/fomites (trunk/arms).\n- **Virology:** Molluscum Contagiosum is caused by the Molluscum Contagiosum Virus (MCV), which belongs to the **Poxvirus** family.\n- **Poxvirus Characteristics:** Poxviruses are unique among all DNA viruses.\n  - They are the largest and most complex of all viruses (brick-shaped, enveloped, linear dsDNA).\n  - **The Exception:** While almost all other DNA viruses MUST enter the host cell's nucleus to use the host's DNA polymerase to replicate, Poxviruses carry their very own DNA-dependent RNA polymerase. Therefore, they are the ONLY DNA viruses that **replicate entirely within the host cell cytoplasm** (forming large inclusion bodies called Henderson-Paterson bodies).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Naked circular dsDNA:** Papillomavirus (HPV). Causes verrucous (cauliflower) warts, not umbilicated papules.\n  - **(C) Enveloped linear dsDNA (nucleus):** Herpesviruses (HSV). Cause painful vesicles, not firm papules.\n  - **(D) Naked ss(+)RNA:** Picornaviruses (Polio, Rhino, Hep A).\n  - **(E) ss(+)RNA with RT:** Retroviruses (HIV).",
    "pearl": "**Molluscum Contagiosum** presents as **umbilicated, dome-shaped papules**. It is caused by a **Poxvirus**, which is unique because it is a DNA virus that **replicates entirely in the cytoplasm**.",
