    "rationale": "- **Molluscum Histology:** Because the Poxvirus replicates entirely in the cytoplasm of the keratinocytes, it manufactures massive amounts of viral proteins and DNA in the cytoplasm, creating large viral factories. \n  - Under the microscope, these factories appear as massive, dense, pink (eosinophilic) **intracytoplasmic inclusions** that push the host cell nucleus to the side. \n  - These specific inclusions are eponymous and are called **Henderson-Paterson bodies** (or simply Molluscum bodies).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Cowdry Type A bodies:** Intranuclear inclusions seen in Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) and Varicella Zoster Virus (VZV).\n  - **(B) Negri bodies:** Eosinophilic intracytoplasmic inclusions found in Purkinje cells of the cerebellum or hippocampal neurons, diagnostic of **Rabies** virus.\n  - **(D) Mallory bodies:** Eosinophilic inclusions of damaged keratin intermediate filaments found in the hepatocytes of patients with **Alcoholic Liver Disease**.\n  - **(E) Councilman bodies:** Eosinophilic, apoptotic hepatocytes seen in severe viral hepatitis (e.g., **Yellow Fever** or Hep C).",
    "pearl": "The histologic hallmark of **Molluscum Contagiosum** is the presence of large, eosinophilic intracytoplasmic inclusions called **Henderson-Paterson bodies**.",
