    "rationale": "- **Treatment of Scabies:** Scabies mites are highly mobile and hide in microscopic numbers all over the skin, not just where the visible, itchy rash is located.\n- **The Protocol:** To effectively eradicate a scabies infestation, **Topical Permethrin 5% cream** must be applied to the **entire body surface area from the neck down** (including under the fingernails, between the toes, the gluteal cleft, and the umbilicus). In infants, it must also include the head and face. \n  - The medication must be left on overnight (typically **8 to 14 hours**) to ensure all mites and nymphs are exposed to the neurotoxin, and then washed off in the morning.\n  - Because it does not reliably kill eggs, a second total-body application is often recommended 1-2 weeks later to kill any newly hatched mites. All clothing and bedding must be washed in hot water.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) & (B) Local application:** This guarantees failure. The mites elsewhere on the body will survive and immediately reinfest the patient.\n  - **(C) Continuous application:** Unnecessary and increases the risk of systemic absorption and toxicity.\n  - **(E) Wash off immediately:** Will not provide enough exposure time to kill the mites.",
