    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Molluscum in Immunocompromise:** \n  - In a healthy, immunocompetent adult, Molluscum Contagiosum is usually localized to the genital region (if sexually transmitted) and is completely self-limiting. The body's **cell-mediated immune system (specifically CD4+ T-cells and macrophages)** eventually recognizes the viral antigens and mounts an inflammatory response, destroying the lesions over 6 to 12 months.\n  - **The HIV Patient:** In a patient with advanced AIDS (CD4 $<50$), the cellular immune system is completely destroyed. Without CD4+ T-cells, the body is completely blind to the Poxvirus. The virus replicates unchecked, spreading massively across the entire body, face, and neck. The lesions often grow very large (giant molluscum) and will not resolve unless the patient's CD4 count is restored using Anti-Retroviral Therapy (ART).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Helper virus:** Poxviruses are completely self-sufficient (they have their own RNA polymerase). They do not use reverse transcriptase.\n  - **(C) B-cell destruction:** HIV primarily destroys CD4+ T-cells, not B-cells. Furthermore, viral clearance of cutaneous lesions relies primarily on cellular immunity, not antibodies.\n  - **(D) Mutated strain:** The virus is standard; the *host's* immune system is the variable.\n  - **(E) Integration:** Poxviruses replicate strictly in the cytoplasm; they never enter the nucleus and never integrate into the host DNA.",
    "pearl": "Disseminated, severe, or giant **Molluscum Contagiosum** (especially on the face in adults) is highly suggestive of profound immunosuppression, specifically **HIV/AIDS** (due to the loss of CD4+ cell-mediated immunity).",
