    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 26-year-old woman with chronic Hepatitis B (HBsAg positive, HBeAg positive) delivers a full-term infant. Because the mother is highly infectious (HBeAg positive), there is a 90% chance the infant will acquire Hepatitis B during delivery if no interventions are taken. To maximally reduce the risk of vertical transmission, which of the following is the most appropriate management for the neonate immediately after birth?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Administer the Hepatitis B vaccine only",
      "B": "Administer Hepatitis B Immune Globulin (HBIG) only",
      "C": "Administer both the Hepatitis B vaccine and Hepatitis B Immune Globulin (HBIG) within 12 hours of birth",
      "D": "Initiate oral Tenofovir therapy for the neonate for 4 weeks",
      "E": "Isolate the infant and delay breastfeeding until the infant's immune system matures at 6 months"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Vertical Transmission of Hep B:** Without prophylaxis, infants born to HBeAg-positive mothers have a 90% chance of acquiring Hep B. Crucially, up to 90% of infants who acquire Hep B will develop **chronic, lifelong infection** (leading to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma), compared to only 5% of adults. Therefore, prevention is critical.\n- **The Protocol:** \n  - To prevent infection, the infant requires both **passive and active immunity** immediately.\n  - **Passive Immunity:** Hepatitis B Immune Globulin (**HBIG**) provides instant, pre-made antibodies to neutralize any virus the baby was exposed to in the birth canal.\n  - **Active Immunity:** The **Hepatitis B Vaccine** (given at a separate injection site) teaches the baby's immune system to make its own antibodies for long-term, lifelong protection.\n  - Both must be given **within 12 hours of birth**. This regimen reduces the transmission rate from 90% down to $< 5\\%$. (The mother should also continue breastfeeding, as it is safe once the baby receives prophylaxis).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Vaccine only:** Doesn't provide immediate protection against the massive viral load just encountered in the birth canal; the baby will get infected before their immune system can respond to the vaccine.\n  - **(B) HBIG only:** Provides immediate protection but wears off in a few months, leaving the baby vulnerable to future exposure.\n  - **(D) Tenofovir:** Antivirals are sometimes given to the *mother* in the 3rd trimester if her viral load is astronomically high, but they are not the post-exposure prophylaxis for the *neonate*.\n  - **(E) Isolate/No breastfeeding:** Unnecessary and harmful. Breastfeeding is encouraged.",
    "pearl": "Infants born to **HBsAg-positive mothers** must receive BOTH the **Hepatitis B Vaccine AND Hepatitis B Immune Globulin (HBIG)** within 12 hours of birth to prevent lifelong chronic infection.",
