    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "- **Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) for Hep B:** The protocol depends entirely on the immune status of the healthcare worker.\n  - The neutralizing antibody against Hep B is **Anti-HBs** (Surface Antibody). \n  - A titer of **$> 10\\text{ mIU/mL}$** is considered fully protective and implies robust, sterilizing immunity.\n- **The Scenario:** Because the student was fully vaccinated and her labs *prove* she has a highly protective antibody titer ($>100$), her immune system is fully equipped to instantly neutralize the virus introduced by the needlestick. \n  - She does not need pre-made antibodies (HBIG), nor does she need a booster vaccine, because her body already has the weapons it needs. The correct action is absolute reassurance.\n- **When to intervene:** \n  - If she were *unvaccinated* or had a titer $<10$, she would immediately receive **BOTH HBIG and the Vaccine series**.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A), (B), (C):** Completely unnecessary in a patient with proven protective antibody titers.\n  - **(D) Lamivudine:** Antivirals are used for *HIV* post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP), never for Hep B PEP.",
    "pearl": "A healthcare worker with a documented **Anti-HBs titer $10\\text{ mIU/mL}$** is fully immune to Hepatitis B. Following a high-risk exposure, **no treatment (no HBIG, no vaccine)** is required.",
