    "rationale": "- **Opportunistic Infections in HIV:** The risk of specific opportunistic infections correlates tightly with the absolute CD4 count.\n  - **CD4 $< 200$ cells/$\\mu$L:** The patient is at massive risk for **Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP)**, a severe, often fatal fungal pneumonia. \n- **Prophylaxis:** The standard of care mandates that the moment a patient's CD4 count drops below 200, you must initiate primary prophylaxis. The drug of choice for PCP prophylaxis is **Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (Bactrim)**. (It conveniently also provides cross-protection against *Toxoplasma gondii*, which becomes a major risk when CD4 drops below 100).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Azithromycin:** Used as prophylaxis against *Mycobacterium avium complex* (MAC) when the CD4 count drops **$< 50$**. (Her CD4 is 180, so PCP is the immediate threat).\n  - **(C) Fluconazole:** Used to *treat* Candida (thrush/esophagitis) or Cryptococcus, but it is not used for primary prophylaxis based on CD4 counts.\n  - **(D) Isoniazid:** Prophylaxis/treatment for Tuberculosis (PPD+ or IGRA+), not triggered strictly by a CD4 $< 200$.\n  - **(E) Valganciclovir:** Used to treat active Cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis (which occurs at CD4 $< 50$).",
