    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Neonatal HIV Prophylaxis:** Even if the mother's viral load is undetectable and the risk of transmission is incredibly low, the infant was still swimming in potentially infectious fluids and passed through the birth canal. Therefore, all exposed infants receive post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP).\n- **Low-Risk Infants:** If the mother was on ART and her viral load was fully suppressed, the infant is considered \"low-risk.\" The standard protocol is a single-drug regimen of **oral Zidovudine (AZT)** starting within 6-12 hours of birth and continuing for **4 weeks**. (Zidovudine is a Nucleoside Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitor - NRTI).\n- **High-Risk Infants:** If the mother was unsuppressed, had a high viral load, or received no prenatal care, the infant is \"high-risk\" and receives a full, aggressive **three-drug ART regimen** (e.g., AZT + Lamivudine + Nevirapine) for up to 6 weeks.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Ganciclovir:** Antiviral for CMV, not HIV.\n  - **(C) Three-drug ART:** Reserved for high-risk infants (mother unsuppressed), not low-risk infants.\n  - **(D) HBIG:** For Hepatitis B exposure.\n  - **(E) TMP-SMX:** Given to HIV-exposed infants starting at *6 weeks* of age (not birth) to prevent PCP until their HIV status is definitively proven negative.",
