    "stem": "A 25-year-old woman with well-controlled HIV presents for a preconception counseling visit. She and her HIV-negative husband wish to conceive a child. She is currently taking an ART regimen consisting of Tenofovir, Emtricitabine, and Dolutegravir. Her viral load has been undetectable for 3 years. The physician informs her that one of her medications acts by binding directly to the active site of an enzyme, preventing the integration of the double-stranded viral DNA into the host cell's chromosomes. Which specific drug in her regimen utilizes this mechanism of action?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Tenofovir",
      "B": "Emtricitabine",
      "C": "Dolutegravir",
      "D": "Ritonavir",
      "E": "Efavirenz"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **HIV Pharmacology:** \n  - The HIV virus is an RNA retrovirus. It uses Reverse Transcriptase to turn its RNA into double-stranded DNA. \n  - To establish a lifelong, permanent infection, the virus MUST insert this newly made DNA into the human host's chromosomal DNA. The viral enzyme responsible for this insertion is called **Integrase**.\n- **The Drug:** **Dolutegravir** (and other drugs ending in \"-tegravir\", like Raltegravir, Bictegravir) are **Integrase Strand Transfer Inhibitors (INSTIs)**. They bind to the active site of the integrase enzyme, paralyzing it, and preventing the virus from hiding in the host genome. Integrase inhibitors are currently the cornerstone of modern, highly effective first-line ART regimens.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Tenofovir & (B) Emtricitabine:** These are NRTIs. They inhibit *Reverse Transcriptase* by acting as fake nucleotides that cause chain termination.\n  - **(D) Ritonavir:** This is a Protease Inhibitor (PI). It stops the viral protease from cleaving the large polyprotein, resulting in immature, non-infectious virions. (It is also a potent CYP450 inhibitor used to \"boost\" other drugs).\n  - **(E) Efavirenz:** An NNRTI. It binds allosterically (not at the active site) to Reverse Transcriptase, changing its shape and rendering it inactive.",
    "pearl": "HIV drugs ending in **\"-tegravir\"** (Dolutegravir) are **Integrase Inhibitors**. They prevent the integration of the viral DNA into the host cell's chromosome.",
