    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 6-week-old infant, born to an HIV-positive mother who received no prenatal care, is brought to the clinic for follow-up. The pediatrician needs to determine if the infant actually contracted HIV from the mother. An ELISA test for anti-HIV IgG antibodies returns a strongly positive result. Which of the following is the most accurate interpretation of this test result?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The infant is definitively infected with HIV and requires lifelong three-drug ART",
      "B": "The infant is immune to HIV due to successful passive immunization",
      "C": "The test is inconclusive because maternal IgG antibodies cross the placenta and can persist in the infant's blood for up to 18 months",
      "D": "The test confirms the infant has mounted an active cellular immune response against the virus",
      "E": "The positive antibody test proves the Zidovudine prophylaxis failed"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosing HIV in Infants:** This is a classic board concept. \n  - The standard diagnostic test for HIV in adults is an antibody test (looking for IgG antibodies against the virus). \n  - **The Problem:** In infants, you **CANNOT use an antibody test** (ELISA or Western Blot). During the 3rd trimester of pregnancy, the mother actively pumps massive amounts of her own IgG antibodies across the placenta into the baby's blood to protect it. \n  - If the mother is HIV+, she will pump anti-HIV IgG antibodies into the baby. These maternal antibodies can circulate in the baby's blood for up to **18 months**. \n  - Therefore, a positive antibody test in a 6-week-old baby merely proves the *mother* has HIV, it tells you absolutely nothing about whether the *baby* is actually infected.\n- **The Correct Test:** To diagnose HIV in an infant $<18$ months old, you MUST use a virologic test that looks for the actual virus itself, not the antibodies. The gold standard is the **HIV DNA PCR** (or RNA PCR).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Definitively infected:** False, it's just maternal antibody.\n  - **(B) Immune:** HIV antibodies are not neutralizing/sterilizing; they do not provide immunity.\n  - **(D) Active cellular response:** The test measures humoral (IgG) antibody, and it's the mother's antibody, not the infant's.",
    "pearl": "You **CANNOT use antibody testing (ELISA)** to diagnose HIV in an infant $< 18$ months old due to the persistence of transplacental maternal IgG. You MUST use an **HIV DNA or RNA PCR**.",
