    "stem": "A 25-year-old man presents to the clinic with severe, painful swelling in his right groin. He reports that 3 weeks ago, he had a small, completely painless ulcer on his penis that healed and disappeared on its own within a few days. Now, his right inguinal lymph nodes are massively enlarged, red, and exquisitely tender. The nodes above and below the Poupart (inguinal) ligament are swollen, creating a visible groove or depression between them (\"Groove sign\"). Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Haemophilus ducreyi",
      "B": "Treponema pallidum",
      "C": "Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1, L2, L3",
      "D": "Chlamydia trachomatis serovars D-K",
      "E": "Klebsiella granulomatis"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The disease process is divided into two distinct stages:\n  1. **Primary Stage:** A small, **painless**, transient genital ulcer that heals quickly (and is often missed by the patient).\n  2. **Secondary Stage (Weeks later):** The infection travels via lymphatics to the regional nodes, causing massive, severely **painful** inguinal lymphadenopathy (buboes). The nodes can merge and form sinus tracts. The classic finding of swollen nodes separated by the tight inguinal ligament creates the pathognomonic **\"Groove Sign.\"**\n- **Diagnosis:** This entire clinical picture describes **Lymphogranuloma Venereum (LGV)**. It is caused strictly by the highly invasive **serovars L1, L2, and L3** of *Chlamydia trachomatis*. (These serovars prefer lymphatic tissue, unlike the others which prefer mucosal epithelium).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Haemophilus ducreyi:** Causes Chancroid. The ulcer is severely **painful** and deep/ragged.\n  - **(B) Treponema pallidum:** Causes Syphilis. The ulcer is painless, but the lymph nodes are firm, rubbery, and **painless** (not red, fluctuant, painful buboes).\n  - **(D) Chlamydia serovars D-K:** Cause classic mucosal cervicitis, urethritis, and PID, not the invasive lymphatic disease of LGV.\n  - **(E) Klebsiella granulomatis:** Causes Donovanosis. Beefy red, bleeding painless ulcers. The swelling in the groin is actually a subcutaneous granuloma (pseudobubo), not true lymphadenopathy.",
    "pearl": "**Lymphogranuloma Venereum (LGV)** is caused by **Chlamydia trachomatis L1-L3**. It starts with a transient **painless** ulcer, followed weeks later by massive, **painful** inguinal lymph nodes (buboes) with a classic **Groove sign**.",
