    "rationale": "- **Treatment of LGV:** LGV is caused by *Chlamydia trachomatis* (L1-L3). As with all Chlamydia infections, beta-lactams are useless because the bacteria lacks a peptidoglycan wall. You must use a protein synthesis inhibitor (a tetracycline or a macrolide).\n- **The Protocol:** \n  - For simple, mucosal Chlamydia (serovars D-K) causing cervicitis/urethritis, a 7-day course of Doxycycline is sufficient.\n  - However, because LGV (serovars L1-L3) is a deep, invasive, tissue-destructive infection of the lymphatic system, a much longer, aggressive course is required to achieve a cure. The absolute standard of care for LGV is **Oral Doxycycline (100 mg twice daily) for a full 21 days (3 weeks)**.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Ceftriaxone:** Treats Gonorrhea; completely ineffective against Chlamydia.\n  - **(C) Single-dose Azithromycin:** Used historically for simple mucosal Chlamydia, but hopelessly inadequate for a deep, 3-week invasive lymphatic infection like LGV.\n  - **(D) Metronidazole:** Treats Trichomonas/BV, not Chlamydia.\n  - **(E) Penicillin:** Treats Syphilis, not Chlamydia.",
    "pearl": "Because **Lymphogranuloma Venereum (LGV)** is a deep, invasive lymphatic infection caused by *Chlamydia*, it requires a prolonged, **21-day course of Doxycycline** for a complete cure.",
