    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Zika Virus Pathology:** \n  - Roughly 80% of all adults infected with the Zika virus are completely **asymptomatic**. The other 20% develop a mild, self-limiting illness (fever, maculopapular rash, arthralgia, non-purulent conjunctivitis).\n  - **The Fetal Risk:** The ability of the virus to cross the placenta and aggressively target fetal neural progenitor cells is completely independent of the mother's immune response or symptoms. \n  - **An asymptomatic mother has the exact same risk of transmitting the virus and having a baby with devastating microcephaly as a mother who was hospitalized with a severe rash and fever.**\n  - *(Note: The risk of severe microcephaly is highest if the infection occurs during the first trimester, when brain development is most critical, though infection at any time can cause damage).*\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Asymptomatic = safe:** Completely false. The virus replicates and crosses the placenta regardless of maternal symptoms.\n  - **(C) Male fetuses only:** Virus affects both sexes equally.\n  - **(D) Third trimester:** First trimester is actually the most dangerous time for microcephaly.\n  - **(E) Acyclovir:** There are absolutely no approved antiviral treatments or vaccines for the Zika virus. Acyclovir only works for Herpes (HSV/VZV).",
