    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 22-year-old woman presents with a newly discovered lump in her left breast. She noticed it while showering. She has no family history of breast cancer. On physical examination, there is a 2-cm, firm, rubbery, completely painless, and highly mobile mass in the upper outer quadrant of her left breast. The mass easily slips out from under the examiner's fingers. Fine-needle aspiration yields clusters of bland epithelial cells and uniform spindle cells. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Phyllodes tumor",
      "B": "Intraductal papilloma",
      "C": "Fibroadenoma",
      "D": "Medullary carcinoma",
      "E": "Fat necrosis"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A young woman ($<35$ years old) with a firm, rubbery, painless, and exquisitely **mobile (\"breast mouse\")** mass is the textbook presentation of a **Fibroadenoma**.\n- **Pathogenesis & Histology:** Fibroadenomas are benign biphasic tumors, meaning they consist of a proliferation of two different cell types: the glandular epithelium and the surrounding fibrous stroma. They are strictly benign and are estrogen-sensitive (meaning they can grow during pregnancy or prior to menstruation). \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Phyllodes tumor:** Also a biphasic fibroepithelial tumor, but occurs in *older* women (usually 50s) and grows very rapidly to a massive size. Histology shows \"leaf-like\" stromal projections. It can be malignant.\n  - **(B) Intraductal papilloma:** Presents with bloody nipple discharge, not a mobile mass.\n  - **(D) Medullary carcinoma:** A malignant tumor that can mimic a fibroadenoma (well-circumscribed mass in young women, often BRCA1), but it would not show bland spindle cells (stroma) on FNA; it would show high-grade malignant cells with a lymphocytic infiltrate.\n  - **(E) Fat necrosis:** Usually follows trauma, presents as a firm, fixed mass that can mimic cancer, often with skin retraction.",
    "pearl": "A **Fibroadenoma** is the most common benign breast tumor in women under 35. It is classically described as a firm, rubbery, painless, **highly mobile** mass. It is a biphasic tumor (epithelium + stroma).",
