    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 55-year-old postmenopausal woman notices a rapidly enlarging mass in her right breast over the past two months. Physical examination reveals a large, 8-cm, firm, bulky mass that is stretching the overlying skin. She undergoes a core needle biopsy, and subsequently, a wide local excision of the mass. The pathologist examines the gross specimen and notes a fleshy mass with cleft-like cystic spaces. Microscopic examination reveals a biphasic tumor with a highly cellular, atypical stroma that bulges into the cystic spaces, creating a characteristic \"leaf-like\" architectural pattern. Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding the biological behavior of this tumor?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It is universally benign and never recurs after excision",
      "B": "It spreads hematogenously to the lungs and bone in up to 20% of cases",
      "C": "It spreads primarily via the lymphatic system to the axillary lymph nodes",
      "D": "It regress spontaneously if estrogen replacement therapy is discontinued",
      "E": "It is considered a pre-malignant lesion that obligately progresses to invasive ductal carcinoma"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** A large, rapidly growing breast mass in an older woman (50s-60s) with histology showing a highly cellular stroma forming **\"leaf-like\" (phyllodes)** projections is a **Phyllodes Tumor**.\n- **Biological Behavior:** Like a fibroadenoma, it is a biphasic fibroepithelial tumor. However, the stromal component is hypercellular and can be malignant. \n  - Most Phyllodes tumors are benign, but a significant portion are borderline or frankly **malignant** (based on stromal atypia and mitoses).\n  - Even benign-appearing ones have a very high rate of local recurrence if not excised with wide margins.\n  - If they are malignant, they behave like sarcomas (because the malignancy is in the *stromal/mesenchymal* tissue, not the epithelium). Therefore, they spread **hematogenously** (to the lungs/bone), NOT via lymphatics.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Never recurs:** False. They have a very high rate of local recurrence.\n  - **(C) Lymphatic spread:** Carcinomas (epithelial) spread via lymphatics. Sarcomas and malignant Phyllodes tumors spread via blood. Axillary dissection is generally not indicated for Phyllodes tumors.\n  - **(D) Regress spontaneously:** False. They grow rapidly and require surgical excision.\n  - **(E) Obligate progression to IDC:** False. The malignant component is stromal (sarcomatous), not epithelial (carcinomatous).",
    "pearl": "A **Phyllodes Tumor** is a biphasic tumor that presents as a large, rapidly growing mass in older women. It has **\"leaf-like\"** stromal projections. If malignant, it spreads **hematogenously** (like a sarcoma), not via lymphatics.",
