    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A breastfeeding woman with a focal, wedge-shaped area of erythema, pain, swelling, and systemic fever has **Acute Mastitis**.\n- **Pathogenesis:** It is most commonly caused by *Staphylococcus aureus*. The bacteria enter the breast tissue through microscopic fissures or cracks in the nipple caused by breastfeeding. The milk stasis provides an excellent growth medium for the bacteria.\n- **Management:** The cornerstone of treatment is **antibiotics** (e.g., Dicloxacillin or Cephalexin to cover *S. aureus*) AND **continued drainage of the milk**. \n  - The patient MUST continue to breastfeed (or pump) from the affected breast. The infection is in the breast tissue (cellulitis), not primarily in the milk itself, and the acidic environment of the infant's stomach will neutralize any small amount of bacteria ingested. \n  - Stopping breastfeeding causes milk stasis, which severely worsens the infection and almost guarantees the progression from simple mastitis to a massive breast abscess.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A), (B), (C), (E):** All of these options involve stopping breastfeeding from the affected breast, which promotes milk stasis, exacerbates the infection, and increases the risk of abscess formation.",
    "pearl": "The treatment for **Acute Mastitis** includes antibiotics (Dicloxacillin) and **continuing to breastfeed** to ensure adequate milk drainage and prevent abscess formation.",
