    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A perimenopausal or postmenopausal multiparous woman with thick, sticky, **green-brown nipple discharge**, nipple retraction, and a subareolar mass is the classic presentation of **Mammary Duct Ectasia**.\n- **Pathogenesis:** The disease is characterized by chronic inflammation and dilation (ectasia) of the major subareolar lactiferous ducts. The ducts become blocked with thick, inspissated lipid secretions. If the duct ruptures, the lipid spills into the surrounding stroma, inciting a massive chronic inflammatory response. \n- **Histology:** The hallmark is a dense infiltration of **plasma cells** (it is sometimes called \"plasma cell mastitis\") and chronic inflammatory cells around the dilated ducts.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Periductal mastitis:** Highly associated with *smoking*. Pathogenesis involves squamous metaplasia of the duct, leading to keratin plugging, abscess, and fistula formation. Not primarily plasma cell-driven.\n  - **(C) Intraductal papilloma:** Causes *bloody* discharge, no prominent mass or nipple retraction.\n  - **(D) Inflammatory breast cancer:** Presents with rapid, diffuse swelling and *peau d'orange* due to dermal lymphatic invasion, not localized green discharge.\n  - **(E) Paget disease:** Presents as an eczematous, scaly, ulcerating lesion on the nipple surface.",
    "pearl": "**Mammary duct ectasia** presents with a thick, **green-brown nipple discharge**, a subareolar mass, and nipple retraction. Histology shows dilated ducts surrounded by **plasma cells**.",
