    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A subareolar abscess with nipple retraction in a **female smoker** is the pathognomonic presentation for **Periductal Mastitis (Zuska Disease)**.\n- **Pathogenesis:** The normal lactiferous ducts are lined by double-layered cuboidal/columnar epithelium. \n  - Toxins from **smoking** cause a relative Vitamin A deficiency at the duct epithelium, prompting the epithelium to undergo **squamous metaplasia**. \n  - The newly formed squamous cells produce massive amounts of **keratin**. \n  - The keratin sheds and completely plugs the duct. \n  - Behind the plug, secretions build up, the duct dilates, ruptures, and becomes secondarily infected, forming a subareolar abscess. When it ruptures to the skin surface, it forms a classic periareolar fistula.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Autoimmune destruction:** Pathogenesis of primary biliary cholangitis or Hashimoto's, not periductal mastitis.\n  - **(C) Obstruction of dermal lymphatics:** Pathogenesis of Inflammatory Breast Cancer (*peau d'orange*).\n  - **(D) Malignant transformation:** Fibroadenomas rarely become malignant, and it wouldn't present as an abscess in a smoker.\n  - **(E) Bacterial seeding of a hematoma:** This would be an infected fat necrosis, but the absolute link to smoking and nipple inversion points firmly to periductal mastitis (squamous metaplasia).",
    "pearl": "**Periductal Mastitis (Zuska Disease)** is a subareolar abscess highly associated with **smoking**. Toxins cause **squamous metaplasia**, leading to keratin plugging, abscess, and fistula formation.",
