    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** This patient has progressed from simple acute mastitis to a **Breast Abscess**. The key distinguishing feature is the presence of a localized, **fluctuant mass** (a pocket of pus), confirmed by ultrasound showing a fluid collection. This occurred because she stopped breastfeeding (causing milk stasis) and failed antibiotic therapy.\n- **Management:** Once an abscess forms, antibiotics alone are insufficient because they cannot penetrate the thick, avascular wall of the abscess cavity. The fundamental surgical principle for any abscess applies here: **source control via drainage**. \n  - The definitive treatment is either surgical **Incision and Drainage (I&D)** or, more commonly today, **ultrasound-guided needle aspiration** (often requiring multiple passes), combined with systemic antibiotics.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Resume breastfeeding:** She should continue feeding to prevent *further* stasis, but feeding alone will NOT drain a walled-off abscess cavity.\n  - **(B) Oxytocin:** Promotes milk letdown, but won't drain the pus.\n  - **(D) Mastectomy:** Vast overtreatment for a benign bacterial infection.\n  - **(E) Corticosteroids:** Immunosuppressive; would worsen the bacterial infection.",
    "pearl": "If acute mastitis progresses to a **Breast Abscess** (a fluctuant mass), antibiotics and breastfeeding are not enough; it requires **surgical drainage (I&D) or needle aspiration**.",
