    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A hard, painless breast mass in a patient with long-standing, often poorly controlled **Type 1 Diabetes**. Clinically and radiographically, it mimics breast cancer perfectly.\n- **Pathogenesis & Histology:** It is believed to be an autoimmune reaction against the matrix proteins in the breast, triggered by advanced glycation end products (hyperglycemia). \n  - The histology is highly specific: it features intense, thick, **keloid-like fibrosis** and a dense **lymphocytic infiltrate** that specifically surrounds the lobules and the small blood vessels (perivascular). There is no fat necrosis.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Fat necrosis:** Requires trauma and shows foamy macrophages and necrotic adipocytes, not keloid fibrosis and perivascular lymphocytes.\n  - **(C) Invasive ductal:** Malignant epithelial cells forming glands/cords in desmoplastic stroma. Biopsy ruled this out.\n  - **(D) Medullary carcinoma:** Has a dense lymphocytic infiltrate, but also has solid sheets of high-grade, pleomorphic malignant epithelial cells.\n  - **(E) Fibroadenoma:** Biphasic fibroepithelial tumor, usually in younger women without the intense inflammatory infiltrate.",
    "pearl": "**Diabetic mastopathy** presents as a hard breast mass (cancer mimic) in Type 1 Diabetics. Histology shows **keloid-like fibrosis and a perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate**.",
