    "topic": "Lecture 2.1: Breast Disorders and Pathology",
    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 55-year-old nulliparous woman is diagnosed with estrogen receptor-positive (ER+) invasive ductal carcinoma. During a discussion regarding her risk factors, she asks how her reproductive history contributed to her disease. Which of the following reproductive factors is most strongly associated with an increased risk of developing breast cancer in this patient?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Late menarche (age 16)",
      "B": "Early menopause (age 42)",
      "C": "Multiple term pregnancies (grand multiparity)",
      "D": "Nulliparity (never giving birth)",
      "E": "Prolonged duration of exclusive breastfeeding"
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "- **Estrogen and Breast Cancer Risk:** The vast majority of breast cancers are hormone-dependent. The primary driving risk factor is the **total lifetime cumulative exposure to estrogen**. \n- **Modifying the Risk:** Any event that increases the total number of menstrual cycles a woman has in her lifetime increases her risk, because every cycle subjects the breast epithelium to a surge of estrogen.\n  - **Risk Factors (Increased Estrogen exposure):**\n    - Early menarche ($<12$ years)\n    - Late menopause ($>55$ years)\n    - **Nulliparity:** Pregnancy halts the menstrual cycle for 9 months. Women who never get pregnant have continuous, uninterrupted menstrual cycles (and estrogen surges) their entire lives, significantly increasing their risk.\n    - Obesity (in postmenopausal women, adipose tissue converts androstenedione to estrone via aromatase).\n    - Hormone replacement therapy (HRT).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Late menarche & (B) Early menopause:** These *decrease* the number of lifetime menstrual cycles, thereby *decreasing* the risk of breast cancer.\n  - **(C) Multiple pregnancies & (E) Breastfeeding:** Both cause prolonged periods of amenorrhea (no menstrual cycles), thereby *decreasing* lifetime estrogen exposure and *decreasing* the risk of breast cancer.",
    "pearl": "Breast cancer risk is directly tied to **lifetime estrogen exposure**. Factors that increase the number of menstrual cycles (**Nulliparity**, early menarche, late menopause) increase the risk.",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Lecture_2_1__Breast_Disorders_and_Pathol_1",
        "text": "Review Anatomy\u2022Non-neoplastic diseases (i.e., benign breast disease)\u2022Proliferative and high-risk breast lesions\u2022Malignant breast disease\u2022Breast cancer sub-types\u2022Breast cancer incidence, risk factors and screening recommendations\u2022Breast cancer diagnosi"
      }
    ]
