    "file": "Lecture_2_1_Breast_Disorders_and_Pathology_30Q.md",
    "qnum": 16,
    "topic": "Lecture 2.1: Breast Disorders and Pathology",
    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 52-year-old woman presents for her annual physical examination. Her BMI is 34 kg/m$^2$. She went through menopause 2 years ago. She has no family history of cancer. Her physician explains that she is at an increased risk for developing breast cancer compared to a woman of normal weight. What is the primary biochemical mechanism driving this increased risk in this specific patient?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Increased production of estrogen by the ovaries",
      "B": "Increased peripheral conversion of androgens to estrogens by the aromatase enzyme in adipose tissue",
      "C": "Decreased production of sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG) by the liver",
      "D": "Direct stimulation of breast tissue by excessive serum insulin",
      "E": "Accumulation of lipophilic carcinogens in the breast fat pad"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Obesity and Breast Cancer Risk:** The relationship between obesity and breast cancer is dependent on menopausal status. \n  - **Postmenopausal Women:** Obesity strongly **increases** the risk of breast cancer. \n- **The Mechanism:** After menopause, the ovaries shut down and stop producing estrogen. However, the adrenal glands continue to produce androgens (like androstenedione). \n  - **Adipose (fat) tissue** contains high levels of the enzyme **aromatase**. \n  - Aromatase takes the adrenal androgens and converts them into **estrone** (a type of estrogen). \n  - Therefore, the more adipose tissue a postmenopausal woman has, the more aromatase she has, and the more estrogen she produces peripherally. This steady supply of unopposed estrogen constantly stimulates the breast tissue, significantly increasing the risk of ER+ breast cancer.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Ovarian estrogen:** She is postmenopausal; her ovaries have stopped producing estrogen.\n  - **(C) Decreased SHBG:** While obesity *does* lower SHBG (leading to more free estrogen), the massive peripheral *production* via aromatase is the primary driving mechanism.\n  - **(D) Insulin:** Hyperinsulinemia plays a role in overall cancer risk, but estrogen is the primary driver for breast cancer.",
    "pearl": "**Obesity** is a major risk factor for breast cancer in **postmenopausal** women because adipose tissue contains **aromatase**, which converts androgens into estrogen (estrone).",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Lecture_2_1__Breast_Disorders_and_Pathol_1",
        "text": "Review Anatomy\u2022Non-neoplastic diseases (i.e., benign breast disease)\u2022Proliferative and high-risk breast lesions\u2022Malignant breast disease\u2022Breast cancer sub-types\u2022Breast cancer incidence, risk factors and screening recommendations\u2022Breast cancer diagnosi"
      }
    ]
