    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 50-year-old woman undergoes a screening mammogram that reveals a cluster of branching microcalcifications in her right breast. A core needle biopsy is performed. The pathologist reports a malignant proliferation of epithelial cells completely filling the involved ducts. The cells are highly pleomorphic with hyperchromatic nuclei. In the absolute center of the involved ducts, there is a large zone of necrotic cellular debris that contains dystrophic calcification. The basement membrane of the ducts is completely intact. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Solid Ductal Carcinoma in Situ (DCIS)",
      "B": "Comedocarcinoma (Comedo subtype of DCIS)",
      "C": "Invasive Ductal Carcinoma",
      "D": "Lobular Carcinoma in Situ (LCIS)",
      "E": "Fibroadenoma"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnostic Breakdown:**\n  - **Malignant cells filling the duct + Intact basement membrane:** This defines **Carcinoma in Situ**. Because it's in the ducts, it's DCIS. (If the basement membrane were broken, it would be invasive).\n  - **The Subtype:** DCIS has several histologic subtypes (solid, cribriform, papillary, micropapillary, and comedo). The presence of high-grade, highly pleomorphic cells with a massive area of **central necrosis (comedonecrosis)** and dystrophic calcification is the absolute defining hallmark of the **Comedo** subtype of DCIS.\n- **Clinical Significance:** Comedocarcinoma is the most aggressive, highest-grade form of DCIS. It has the highest risk of subsequently breaking through the basement membrane and becoming true invasive ductal carcinoma if left untreated.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Solid DCIS:** Fills the duct completely but lacks the central necrotic core.\n  - **(C) Invasive Ductal Carcinoma:** By definition, the basement membrane must be breached.\n  - **(D) LCIS:** Arises in the lobules, has small uniform cells (no pleomorphism), lacks E-cadherin, and does NOT typically form necrosis or calcifications (usually invisible on mammogram).",
    "pearl": "**Comedocarcinoma** is the highest-grade subtype of DCIS. It is characterized by high-grade malignant cells with **central necrosis** (comedonecrosis) and dystrophic calcification, with an **intact basement membrane**.",
