    "id": "W2_Lecture_2_1_Breast_Disorders_and_Pathology_30Q_24",
    "week": "W2",
    "lecture": "Lecture 2.1: Breast Disorders and Pathology",
    "file": "Lecture_2_1_Breast_Disorders_and_Pathology_30Q.md",
    "qnum": 24,
    "topic": "Lecture 2.1: Breast Disorders and Pathology",
    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 48-year-old woman undergoes a prophylactic bilateral mastectomy due to a strong family history of breast cancer and a known BRCA1 mutation. Pathologic examination of the removed, grossly normal-appearing breast tissue reveals multiple foci of atypical, solid proliferations of cells within the terminal duct lobular units. The cells have small, uniform nuclei, lack E-cadherin, and do not distort the underlying lobular architecture. The basement membrane is intact. Which of the following represents the most accurate statement regarding the origin of these cells?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "They originate from the myoepithelial cells of the major lactiferous ducts",
      "B": "They arise from the stromal fibroblasts surrounding the acini",
      "C": "They originate from the epithelial cells of the terminal duct lobular unit (TDLU)",
      "D": "They are metastatic cells from an occult primary ovarian carcinoma",
      "E": "They arise from the squamous epithelium of the nipple surface"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** The histologic description of small, uniform, discohesive cells (lacking E-cadherin) expanding the lobules with an intact basement membrane is **Lobular Carcinoma in Situ (LCIS)**.\n- **Origin of Breast Lesions:** The vast majority of all breast pathology (both benign fibrocystic changes and malignant carcinomas) originates in one specific anatomical functional unit of the breast: the **Terminal Duct Lobular Unit (TDLU)**.\n  - The TDLU is the very end of the branching duct system where the duct meets the milk-producing lobules (acini).\n  - Both Ductal carcinomas (DCIS, IDC) and Lobular carcinomas (LCIS, ILC) arise from the epithelial cells of the TDLU. (The names \"ductal\" and \"lobular\" describe their morphologic appearance under the microscope, not necessarily strict separate anatomic origins; both start in the TDLU).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Myoepithelial cells:** These cells contract to eject milk. They are *lost* in invasive cancer and are not the primary origin of adenocarcinomas.\n  - **(B) Stromal fibroblasts:** Give rise to Fibroadenomas and Phyllodes tumors, not epithelial carcinomas (LCIS).\n  - **(D) Metastatic:** It is an *in situ* primary lesion, not a metastasis.\n  - **(E) Nipple surface:** Gives rise to primary SCC of the skin, not LCIS.",
    "pearl": "The vast majority of breast cancers, including both Ductal and Lobular carcinomas, originate in the epithelial cells of the **Terminal Duct Lobular Unit (TDLU)**.",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Lecture_2_1__Breast_Disorders_and_Pathol_1",
        "text": "Review Anatomy\u2022Non-neoplastic diseases (i.e., benign breast disease)\u2022Proliferative and high-risk breast lesions\u2022Malignant breast disease\u2022Breast cancer sub-types\u2022Breast cancer incidence, risk factors and screening recommendations\u2022Breast cancer diagnosi"
      }
    ]
