    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** Malignant cells infiltrating the stroma in a **\"single-file\"** line, without forming glands or causing a hard desmoplastic reaction, is the pathognomonic histologic description of **Invasive Lobular Carcinoma (ILC)**.\n- **Pathophysiology:** E-cadherin is a transmembrane protein responsible for intercellular adhesion (it acts like cellular \"glue\" holding epithelial cells together). \n  - ILC is driven by an acquired mutation that causes the complete **loss of E-cadherin expression**.\n  - Because the malignant cells have no \"glue,\" they cannot stick together to form complex 3D structures (like glands or nests). Instead, they just slip through the stroma individually, lining up in single-file rows (like cars on a highway). \n  - Because they don't form a hard clump or induce desmoplasia, ILC frequently presents as a vague thickening rather than a hard mass, and it is notoriously difficult to see on a mammogram.\n- **Clinical Implication:** Because of this stealthy growth pattern, ILC has a higher rate of being **bilateral** and multicentric compared to ductal carcinoma.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) p53, (B) HER2, (D) ER, (E) BRCA1:** Mutations in these genes drive cancer proliferation and survival, but the physical *morphology* (single-file lines) is entirely dictated by the lack of the structural adhesion protein E-cadherin.",
    "pearl": "**Invasive Lobular Carcinoma** features malignant cells marching in **single-file lines**. This is caused by a genetic loss of **E-cadherin** (an intercellular adhesion molecule).",
