    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 49-year-old woman presents to the emergency department in a panic. Over the past two weeks, her right breast has become massively swollen, heavy, red, and warm to the touch. Physical examination reveals a diffuse, erythematous breast that looks exactly like the skin of an orange (*peau d'orange*), with prominent, dimpled pores. There is no discrete, palpable mass. She is given a course of antibiotics for suspected mastitis, but she returns one week later with no improvement. A punch biopsy of the breast skin is performed. What is the most likely histologic finding?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Massive infiltration of the dermis by neutrophils and bacteria",
      "B": "A benign fibroadenoma compressing the axillary vein",
      "C": "Malignant epithelial cells physically plugging and obstructing the dermal lymphatic channels",
      "D": "An autoimmune granulomatous reaction destroying the sebaceous glands",
      "E": "Extramammary Paget cells migrating through the epidermis"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A rapidly progressive, diffuse erythema, swelling, and **\"peau d'orange\" (orange peel)** appearance of the breast that does NOT respond to antibiotics is the absolute classic presentation of **Inflammatory Breast Cancer**.\n- **Pathogenesis:** This is the most aggressive and lethal form of breast cancer. Despite the name, it is *not* a true inflammatory/infectious process. \n  - The redness and swelling are caused by highly aggressive malignant cells invading into and physically **plugging/blocking the dermal lymphatic channels** in the skin of the breast. \n  - Because the lymph fluid cannot drain, the breast becomes massively engorged and edematous. The swelling causes the skin to puff up around the hair follicles, pulling them inward and creating the dimpled \"orange peel\" look.\n- **Diagnosis:** It is a clinical diagnosis confirmed by a punch biopsy of the skin showing cancer cells trapped inside the dermal lymphatics. It is automatically staged as Stage III (or Stage IV if distant mets are present) at the time of diagnosis.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Neutrophils/bacteria:** This is Acute Mastitis. (Crucial differentiator: Mastitis responds to antibiotics and usually occurs during breastfeeding. Inflammatory cancer fails antibiotic therapy).\n  - **(B) Fibroadenoma:** Benign, doesn't cause diffuse swelling or peau d'orange.\n  - **(D) Autoimmune granulomas:** Granulomatous mastitis, presents as a hard mass, not peau d'orange.\n  - **(E) Paget cells:** Cause an eczematous nipple rash, not a swollen, orange-peel breast.",
    "pearl": "**Inflammatory Breast Cancer** presents with rapid swelling, redness, and **peau d'orange** (orange peel skin) due to **malignant cells blocking the dermal lymphatics**. It mimics mastitis but fails to improve with antibiotics.",
