    "stem": "A 35-year-old woman with a known BRCA1 mutation presents with a new, rapidly growing, 3-cm mass in her left breast. Physical examination reveals a well-circumscribed, relatively soft, fleshy mass. Due to its smooth borders, it initially resembled a fibroadenoma on ultrasound. A core biopsy is performed. The pathologist notes a solid, syncytial sheet of large, highly pleomorphic, high-grade malignant cells. The tumor cells are surrounded by a dense, prominent infiltrate of lymphocytes and plasma cells. What is the diagnosis and the typical prognosis of this specific subtype of breast cancer?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Invasive Ductal Carcinoma; extremely poor prognosis",
      "B": "Medullary Carcinoma; relatively good prognosis compared to standard IDC",
      "C": "Invasive Lobular Carcinoma; high risk of bilateral disease",
      "D": "Inflammatory Carcinoma; worst prognosis of all breast cancers",
      "E": "Phyllodes tumor; benign but locally aggressive"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** A breast tumor presenting as a well-circumscribed, fleshy/soft mass (mimicking a fibroadenoma) in a younger woman, with histology showing **solid sheets of high-grade pleomorphic cells surrounded by a massive lymphocytic infiltrate**, is a **Medullary Carcinoma**.\n- **Genetics:** This specific subtype occurs at a significantly higher rate in women with **BRCA1** mutations.\n- **Prognosis:** The paradox of Medullary Carcinoma is that despite looking incredibly ugly and high-grade under the microscope (anaplastic cells, lots of mitoses), it actually has a **better prognosis** than standard Invasive Ductal Carcinoma. \n  - The leading theory is that the massive, intense lymphocytic infiltrate represents a robust host immune response that effectively walls off and contains the tumor, limiting its ability to metastasize early.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Standard IDC:** Typically rock-hard (desmoplasia), irregular, spiculated borders. Does not have a massive lymphocytic infiltrate.\n  - **(C) Invasive Lobular:** Single-file lines of cells lacking E-cadherin.\n  - **(D) Inflammatory:** Peau d'orange, diffuse swelling, lymphovascular invasion.\n  - **(E) Phyllodes tumor:** Biphasic tumor with leaf-like stroma.",
    "pearl": "**Medullary Carcinoma** features solid sheets of high-grade cells with a **prominent lymphocytic infiltrate**. It is associated with **BRCA1** mutations and paradoxically has a **good prognosis**.",
