    "stem": "A 50-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with invasive ductal carcinoma. The tumor tissue is sent for molecular profiling. The pathology report states that the tumor is \"Triple-Negative.\" The oncologist explains that this molecular subtype will dictate her treatment plan, as she is not a candidate for endocrine therapy or targeted biologics. Which of the following best describes the tumor biology and clinical behavior of Triple-Negative Breast Cancer (TNBC)?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It lacks Estrogen Receptor (ER), Progesterone Receptor (PR), and HER2/neu amplification; it is highly aggressive and commonly seen in BRCA1 carriers",
      "B": "It lacks Estrogen, Progesterone, and Androgen receptors; it is slow-growing and indolent",
      "C": "It lacks BRCA1, BRCA2, and p53 mutations; it is the most common subtype in elderly women",
      "D": "It expresses high levels of ER, PR, and HER2; it responds exceptionally well to Tamoxifen",
      "E": "It lacks E-cadherin, Cytokeratin, and Vimentin; it represents a completely undifferentiated sarcoma"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Molecular Subtyping:** Breast cancers are classified based on the presence of three key receptors: Estrogen Receptor (ER), Progesterone Receptor (PR), and Human Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor 2 (HER2/neu).\n- **Triple-Negative Breast Cancer (TNBC):** \n  - By definition, TNBC completely **lacks ER, PR, and HER2 over-expression**.\n  - **Clinical Behavior:** Because it lacks these receptors, you cannot treat it with anti-estrogen drugs (Tamoxifen/Aromatase inhibitors) or anti-HER2 targeted biologics (Trastuzumab). The only systemic treatment option is standard, highly toxic, multi-agent chemotherapy.\n  - TNBC is the **most aggressive** subtype. It is highly proliferative, has the highest rate of early visceral metastasis (brain, lungs), and the worst overall survival.\n  - **Demographics:** It is disproportionately common in younger women, African American women, and is the hallmark cancer of women with **BRCA1** germline mutations.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Androgen receptor/indolent:** TNBC refers to ER/PR/HER2. It is highly aggressive, not indolent.\n  - **(C) Lacks mutations:** TNBC actually has a very high rate of p53 and BRCA1 mutations.\n  - **(D) Expresses high levels:** That would be Triple-*Positive*.\n  - **(E) Sarcoma:** It is a carcinoma (epithelial), not a sarcoma.",
    "pearl": "**Triple-Negative Breast Cancer (TNBC)** lacks ER, PR, and HER2. It is highly aggressive, requires chemotherapy, has a poor prognosis, and is strongly associated with **BRCA1** mutations and African American ethnicity.",
