    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 55-year-old woman is diagnosed with an invasive breast cancer. Biopsy and immunohistochemistry reveal that the tumor cells overexpress a specific receptor tyrosine kinase that promotes aggressive cellular proliferation. The oncologist initiates treatment with Trastuzumab (Herceptin). This monoclonal antibody exerts its therapeutic effect by directly targeting and blocking the extracellular domain of which of the following cell surface receptors?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Estrogen Receptor (ER)",
      "B": "Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor Receptor (VEGFR)",
      "C": "Human Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor 2 (HER2/neu)",
      "D": "Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor (EGFR / HER1)",
      "E": "Programmed Cell Death Protein 1 (PD-1)"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **HER2+ Breast Cancer:** About 15-20% of breast cancers have an amplification of the *ERBB2* gene, leading to a massive overexpression of the **HER2/neu** protein on the cell surface.\n- **The Protein:** HER2/neu is a transmembrane **receptor tyrosine kinase** (part of the Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor family). When overexpressed, it constantly signals the cell to divide and proliferate, making HER2+ cancers highly aggressive and fast-growing.\n- **Targeted Therapy:** **Trastuzumab (Herceptin)** is a revolutionary monoclonal antibody designed specifically to bind to the extracellular domain of the HER2/neu receptor. \n  - By binding, it blocks the receptor from dimerizing and sending proliferation signals. \n  - It also flags the cancer cell for destruction by the immune system (Antibody-Dependent Cellular Cytotoxicity, ADCC).\n  - Its major side effect is a reversible **dilated cardiomyopathy**, so patients must have regular echocardiograms to monitor their Ejection Fraction.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Estrogen Receptor:** Located *intracellularly* (in the nucleus/cytoplasm). Targeted by Tamoxifen or Aromatase Inhibitors.\n  - **(B) VEGFR:** Targeted by Bevacizumab (Avastin) to stop angiogenesis.\n  - **(D) EGFR (HER1):** Targeted by Cetuximab or Erlotinib (used in colon or lung cancer).\n  - **(E) PD-1:** An immune checkpoint receptor targeted by Pembrolizumab (Keytruda).",
    "pearl": "**Trastuzumab (Herceptin)** is a monoclonal antibody used to treat breast cancers that overexpress **HER2/neu** (a receptor tyrosine kinase). The major side effect to monitor is **cardiotoxicity** (decreased LVEF).",
