    "stem": "A 16-year-old female (Keisha) G0 presents to her gynecologist's office accompanied by her mother, complaining of an achy, sharp pain in her right lower quadrant (RLQ) that started 2 days ago. She reports that the pain waxes and waxes, occasionally feeling sharp and crampy, \"like someone squeezing her insides\". Her last menstrual period started 2.5 weeks ago. She reports being able to eat normally and denies nausea, vomiting, fever, chills, dysuria, or urinary frequency. A pelvic ultrasound is performed and demonstrates a 5 cm thin-walled, simple, fluid-filled, unilocular cystic structure on her right ovary with no internal septations or solid components. The left ovary and uterus appear normal. Which of the following describes the correct microanatomy of this cyst, the underlying pathophysiology, and the appropriate clinical management?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "This represents a corpus luteum cyst; it is driven by high levels of progesterone, arises from a corpus albicans that failed to involute, and requires immediate surgical cystectomy to prevent localized rupture.",
      "B": "This represents a functional follicular cyst; it likely grew in the follicular phase of her cycle when a dominant follicle failed to ovulate or involute normally and continued to accumulate follicular fluid; she and her mother should be reassured that most of these resolve spontaneously over 4 to 6 weeks, and she should be counseled on the warning signs of ovarian torsion.",
      "C": "This represents an endometrioma; it is lined by ectopic endometrial tissue, is filled with dark \"chocolate-like\" degenerated blood, and requires immediate high-dose oral estrogen therapy to induce complete involution.",
      "D": "This represents a theca-lutein cyst; it is driven by highly elevated levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) from a gestational trophoblastic disease, and requires immediate referral for a bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy.",
      "E": "This represents a mature cystic teratoma; it arises from primitive germ cells, contains mature structures from all three germ layers, and requires urgent platinum-based systemic chemotherapy."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "follicular phase of her cycle when a dominant follicle failed to ovulate or involute normally and continued to accumulate follicular fluid; she and her mother should be reassured that most of these resolve spontaneously over 4 to 6 weeks, and she should be counseled on the warning signs of ovarian torsion. \u25cf  A. This represents a corpus luteum cyst; it is driven by high levels of progesterone, arises from a corpus albicans that failed to involute, and requires immediate surgical cystectomy to prevent localized rupture. - Incorrect. Corpus luteum cysts grow in the luteal phase (not 2.5 weeks after LMP in a typical cycle) and are driven by progesterone, but the case describes a simple follicular cyst which is by far the most common functional cyst and does not require surgery . \u25cf  B. This represents a functional follicular cyst; it likely grew in the follicular phase of her cycle when a dominant follicle failed to ovulate or involute normally and continued to accumulate follicular fluid; she and her mother should be reassured that most of these resolve spontaneously over 4 to 6 weeks, and she should be counseled on the warning signs of ovarian torsion. - Correct. Follicular cysts are non-neoplastic functional cysts that arise when a dominant follicle fails to rupture or regress, continuing to fill with fluid. They are benign, extremely common causes of pelvic pain, and typically resolve spontaneously within 4 to 6 weeks. Management is conservative with follow-up ultrasound. Counseling on warning signs of ovarian torsion is crucial. \u25cf  C. This represents an endometrioma; it is lined by ectopic endometrial tissue, is filled with dark \"chocolate-like\" degenerated blood, and requires immediate high-dose oral estrogen therapy to induce complete involution. - Incorrect. Endometriomas are \"chocolate cysts\" that contain thick, dark brown degenerated blood and do not appear as simple, thin-walled unilocular fluid cysts on ultrasound. \u25cf  D. This represents a theca-lutein cyst; it is driven by highly elevated levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) from a gestational trophoblastic disease, and requires immediate referral for a bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy . - Incorrect. Theca-lutein cysts are typically bilateral and multiple, driven by elevated hCG (e.g., molar pregnancy), and regress spontaneously when the hCG source is removed. \u25cf  E. This represents a mature cystic teratoma; it arises from primitive germ cells, contains mature structures from all three germ layers, and requires urgent platinum-based systemic chemotherapy . - Incorrect. Mature cystic teratomas (dermoid cysts) are benign neoplasms containing teeth, hair , sebum, and have a 267 complex, calcified, or hyperechoic appearance on ultrasound. They do not require chemotherapy .",
