    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 25-year-old female (Lindsey) G0 is brought to the emergency department after waking up from sleep with a sudden onset of severe left lower quadrant (LLQ) abdominal pain. She describes the pain as a \"knife stabbing her,\" accompanied by intermittent strong cramping. She reports that the pain has gotten progressively worse over the past 4 hours, doubling her over and making her nauseated. She notes that her abdomen hurt intensely whenever the car hit a bump on the road on the way to the hospital. On 255 physical exam, she has severe tenderness with localized guarding in the left lower quadrant. Pelvic ultrasound reveals an 8 cm complex cystic mass on her left ovary with no detectable Doppler blood flow to the ovary. Which of the following represents the correct diagnosis, the anatomic structure compromised, the intraoperative management, and the final pathology of this mass?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Appendicitis; the vermiform appendix is compromised; treated with an open appendectomy; and pathology will show acute neutrophilic infiltration.",
      "B": "Ruptured follicular cyst; the ovarian ligament is compromised; treated with systemic steroids; and pathology will show follicular hyperplasia.",
      "C": "Meigs' syndrome; the pelvic lymphatics are compromised; treated with a chest tube; and pathology will show a cellular fibroma with pleural mesothelial cells.",
      "D": "Ovarian torsion secondary to a mature cystic teratoma; the infundibulopelvic (suspensory) ligament of the ovary\u2014which contains the ovarian artery and vein\u2014is twisted, cutting off blood supply; intraoperatively, the ovary is detorsed and salvaged (as it was not necrotic), followed by an ovarian cystectomy; and pathology will show tissue from multiple germ layers (e.g., skin, hair, sebum).",
      "E": "Krukenberg tumor; the broad ligament is compromised; treated with a radical bilateral oophorectomy; and pathology will show signet-ring cells from gastric metastasis."
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "infundibulopelvic (suspensory) ligament of the ovary\u2014which contains the ovarian artery and vein\u2014is twisted, cutting of f blood supply; intraoperatively , the ovary is detorsed and salvaged (as it was not necrotic), followed by an ovarian cystectomy; and pathology will show tissue from multiple germ layers (e.g., skin, hair , sebum). \u25cf  A. Appendicitis; the vermiform appendix is compromised; treated with an open appendectomy; and pathology will show acute neutrophilic infiltration. - Incorrect. Appendix is on the right side (RLQ), and this patient has LLQ pain with an 8 cm left ovarian complex mass and no Doppler flow . \u25cf  B. Ruptured follicular cyst; the ovarian ligament is compromised; treated with systemic steroids; and pathology will show follicular hyperplasia. - Incorrect. Ruptured follicular cysts cause transient pain that does not twist the IP ligament or cut of f blood flow , and is not treated with steroids. \u25cf  C. Meigs' syndrome; the pelvic lymphatics are compromised; treated with a chest tube; and pathology will show a cellular fibroma with pleural mesothelial cells. - Incorrect. Meigs' syndrome is the triad of a benign ovarian fibroma, ascites, and pleural ef fusion. It is not an acute LLQ emergency cutting of f flow . \u25cf  D. Ovarian torsion secondary to a mature cystic teratoma; the infundibulopelvic (suspensory) ligament of the ovary\u2014which contains the ovarian artery and vein\u2014is twisted, cutting of f blood supply; intraoperatively , the ovary is detorsed and salvaged (as it was not necrotic), followed by an ovarian cystectomy; and pathology will show tissue from multiple germ layers (e.g., skin, hair , sebum). - Correct. Any large adnexal mass (typically > 5 cm, such as this 8 cm dermoid cyst) acts as a pivot, predisposing the ovary to rotate around its suspensory pedicle. This pedicle is the infundibulopelvic (IP) ligament, which contains the ovarian artery , vein, and nerves. T orsion cuts of f venous drainage first, leading to massive congestion, then blocks arterial inflow , causing ischemia. Management involves urgent detorsion in the operating room. If salvaged, the cyst is removed (cystectomy), and pathology of a mature teratoma shows mature elements of multiple germ layers (hair , sebum, thyroid tissue, bone). \u25cf  E. Krukenberg tumor; the broad ligament is compromised; treated with a radical bilateral oophorectomy; and pathology will show signet-ring cells from gastric metastasis. - Incorrect. Krukenberg tumors are bilateral solid metastatic masses, not an acute unilocular torsion.",
