    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A pathology resident is reviewing the gross and microscopic characteristics of mature cystic teratomas. She notes that these are the most common ovarian tumors in young women in their 20s and 30s. Which of the following statements is correct regarding the cellular origin, tissue composition, and potential complications of mature cystic teratomas?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "They are benign germ cell tumors that can contain mature tissues derived from one or all three germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm); they are most often dominated by ectodermal structures (skin, hair, sebaceous glands), which is why they are often called \"dermoid cysts\"; and while they are benign, rare malignant transformation (<1% of cases) can occur, most commonly presenting as squamous cell carcinoma.",
      "B": "They are malignant sex-cord stromal tumors that only contain endodermal cells (thyroid follicles); they always produce high levels of thyroid hormone leading to struma ovarii; and they are highly resistant to surgery.",
      "C": "They are benign epithelial tumors lined by a single layer of ciliated columnar cells; they arise from retrograde menstruation of endometrial glands; and they carry a 50% risk of malignant conversion to clear cell carcinoma.",
      "D": "They are malignant germ cell tumors composed entirely of primitive, undifferentiated neural crest cells; they present exclusively in postmenopausal women; and they always secrete high levels of alpha-fetoprotein (AFP).",
      "E": "They are metastatic tumors from a primary colorectal adenocarcinoma; they consist of signet-ring cells embedded in a dense desmoplastic stroma; and they always present as bilateral solid masses."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "tissues derived from one or all three germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, and 268 endoderm); they are most often dominated by ectodermal structures (skin, hair , sebaceous glands), which is why they are often called \"dermoid cysts\"; and while they are benign, rare malignant transformation (<1% of cases) can occur , most commonly presenting as squamous cell carcinoma. \u25cf  A. They are benign germ cell tumors that can contain mature tissues derived from one or all three germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm); they are most often dominated by ectodermal structures (skin, hair , sebaceous glands), which is why they are often called \"dermoid cysts\"; and while they are benign, rare malignant transformation (<1% of cases) can occur , most commonly presenting as squamous cell carcinoma. - Correct. Mature cystic teratomas (dermoids) are benign germ cell neoplasms composed of mature tissues representing multiple germ layers. They are dominated by ectodermal elements, such as skin, hair follicles, and sebaceous glands containing cheesy sebum. Although benign, they carry a small (<1%) risk of malignant transformation of one of their components, almost always presenting as a squamous cell carcinoma in an older woman. \u25cf  B. They are malignant sex-cord stromal tumors that only contain endodermal cells (thyroid follicles); they always produce high levels of thyroid hormone leading to struma ovarii; and they are highly resistant to surgery . - Incorrect. They are germ cell tumors, not sex-cord stromal. Struma ovarii is a specialized monodermal teratoma consisting of thyroid tissue, but it is benign and easily treated surgically . \u25cf  C. They are benign epithelial tumors lined by a single layer of ciliated columnar cells; they arise from retrograde menstruation of endometrial glands; and they carry a 50% risk of malignant conversion to clear cell carcinoma. - Incorrect. This describes endometrioid or clear cell carcinomas and their association with endometriosis, not teratomas. \u25cf  D. They are malignant germ cell tumors composed entirely of primitive, undif ferentiated neural crest cells; they present exclusively in postmenopausal women; and they always secrete high levels of alpha-fetoprotein (AFP). - Incorrect. This describes immature teratomas, which contain embryonic/fetal neural tissue and are malignant. Mature teratomas occur in reproductive-aged women and are benign. \u25cf  E. They are metastatic tumors from a primary colorectal adenocarcinoma; they consist of signet-ring cells embedded in a dense desmoplastic stroma; and they always present as bilateral solid masses. - Incorrect. This describes Krukenberg tumors.",
