    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "269 \u25cf  A. Yolk sac tumor; equivalent to embryonal carcinoma in males. - Incorrect. Y olk sac tumor is equivalent to yolk sac carcinoma (endodermal sinus tumor) in males and secretes AFP . \u25cf  B. Granulosa cell tumor; equivalent to Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor in males. - Incorrect. These are both sex-cord stromal tumors, but they are not equivalent in this manner . \u25cf  C. Dysgerminoma; equivalent to testicular seminoma in males. - Correct. Dysgerminoma is the most common malignant ovarian germ cell tumor , typically occurring in young women. Histologically , it consists of sheets of large, uniform, primitive germ cells with clear , glycogen-rich cytoplasm, distinct cell membranes, and central nucleolated nuclei, separated by thin fibrous septa containing a prominent lymphocytic infiltrate. Its exact male counterpart in the testis is seminoma, which displays an identical histological appearance. The classic tumor marker is Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH). \u25cf  D. Mature cystic teratoma; equivalent to dermoid cyst in males. - Incorrect. Mature teratomas are benign, not malignant, and do not present with sheets of clear cells and lymphocytic septations. \u25cf  E. Choriocarcinoma; equivalent to hydatidiform mole in males. - Incorrect. Choriocarcinoma is a malignant tumor of trophoblasts, equivalent to testicular choriocarcinoma in males.",
