    "rationale": "of alpha-fetoprotein (AFP). \u25cf  A. Dysgerminoma; secretes high levels of lactate dehydrogenase (LDH). - Incorrect. While LDH is the marker for dysgerminoma, it does not form Schiller-Duval bodies. \u25cf  B. Choriocarcinoma; secretes high levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG). - Incorrect. Choriocarcinoma secretes hCG, but its histology shows syncytiotrophoblasts and cytotrophoblasts without villi or Schiller-Duval bodies. \u25cf  C. Granulosa cell tumor; secretes high levels of inhibin and estradiol (E2). - Incorrect. This is a sex-cord stromal tumor that forms Call-Exner bodies, not Schiller-Duval bodies. \u25cf  D. Mucinous cystadenocarcinoma; secretes high levels of CA-19-9. - Incorrect. This is an epithelial malignancy , not associated with Schiller-Duval bodies. \u25cf  E. Yolk sac tumor (endodermal sinus tumor); secretes high levels of alpha-fetoprotein (AFP). - Correct. Y olk sac tumor (endodermal sinus tumor) is a highly malignant, rapidly growing germ cell tumor that dif ferentiates toward extraembryonic yolk sac lineages. The pathognomonic histological hallmark is the Schiller-Duval body , which resembles a primitive glomerulus (a central capillary vessel surrounded by a layer of tumor cells in an epithelial-lined space). 270 Because yolk sac tissue normally synthesizes alpha-fetoprotein, AFP is the highly specific serum tumor marker used for diagnosis and monitoring of this disease.",
