    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 54-year-old G2P2 female (Helen) presents to her gynecologist with a 5-month history of nonspecific abdominal pain and on-and-off vaginal spotting for the past 3 months. She reports that she went through menopause at age 51. An endometrial biopsy reveals a proliferative endometrium, but no hyperplasia or carcinoma. Pelvic ultrasound demonstrates a 6 cm solid, homogeneous right ovarian mass. Her left ovary and uterus appear normal. Laboratory evaluation reveals a normal CA-125, but significantly elevated serum estradiol (E2) and inhibin levels. What is the most likely pathological diagnosis, and what are the characteristic microscopic findings associated with this tumor?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Adult Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor; microscopically characterized by well-differentiated tubules lined by Sertoli cells and large plump pink Leydig cells containing Reinke crystals.",
      "B": "Adult granulosa cell tumor; microscopically characterized by sheets of granulosa cells punctuated by small, follicle-like fluid-filled spaces containing eosinophilic material (Call-Exner bodies) and tumor cells demonstrating longitudinal nuclear grooves (\"coffee-bean\" nuclei).",
      "C": "Brenner tumor; microscopically characterized by nests of transitional epithelium (resembling urothelium) with longitudinal nuclear grooves embedded in a dense fibrous stroma.",
      "D": "Ovarian fibroma; microscopically characterized by bundles of spindle-shaped, fibroblast-like cells producing collagen with no cellular atypia.",
      "E": "Dysgerminoma; microscopically characterized by sheets of uniform cells with clear cytoplasm separated by fibrous septa with infiltrating lymphocytes."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "sheets of granulosa cells punctuated by small, follicle-like fluid-filled spaces containing eosinophilic material (Call-Exner bodies) and tumor cells demonstrating longitudinal nuclear grooves (\"cof fee-bean\" nuclei). \u25cf  A. Adult Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor; microscopically characterized by well-dif ferentiated tubules lined by Sertoli cells and large plump pink Leydig cells containing Reinke crystals. - Incorrect. Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors produce testosterone and lead to virilization (hirsutism, temporal balding, clitoral hypertrophy), not estrogen-driven proliferative endometrium or postmenopausal spotting. \u25cf  B. Adult granulosa cell tumor; microscopically characterized by sheets of granulosa cells punctuated by small, follicle-like fluid-filled spaces containing eosinophilic material (Call-Exner bodies) and tumor cells demonstrating longitudinal nuclear grooves (\"cof fee-bean\" nuclei). - Correct. Granulosa cell tumors are the most common malignant sex-cord stromal tumors, classically presenting in postmenopausal women in their 50s. They are functional estrogen-producing tumors. This excess estrogen stimulates the endometrium, causing postmenopausal bleeding/spotting and a proliferative endometrium (or endometrial hyperplasia/carcinoma). Pathologically , they express inhibin and estradiol (E2). Histological hallmarks are Call-Exner bodies (small fluid-filled spaces resembling follicles) and \"cof fee-bean\" nuclei (longitudinal nuclear grooves). \u25cf  C. Brenner tumor; microscopically characterized by nests of transitional epithelium (resembling urothelium) with longitudinal nuclear grooves embedded in a dense fibrous stroma. - Incorrect. Brenner tumors are typically benign, asymptomatic epithelial tumors that do not produce estrogen or cause endometrial changes. \u25cf  D. Ovarian fibroma; microscopically characterized by bundles of spindle-shaped, fibroblast-like cells producing collagen with no cellular atypia. - Incorrect. Fibromas are non-functional and do not produce estrogen. \u25cf  E. Dysgerminoma; microscopically characterized by sheets of uniform cells with clear cytoplasm separated by fibrous septa with infiltrating lymphocytes. - Incorrect. Dysgerminomas are malignant germ cell tumors that do not produce estrogen or present in this clinical setting. 271",
