    "rationale": "primitive sex cords or mesenchymal stroma and morphologically resembles testicular cells; it is a rare sex-cord stromal tumor , accounting for less than 1% of all ovarian neoplasms. \u25cf  A. Mature cystic teratoma; arises from primitive germ cells; it is the most common benign tumor in young females. - Incorrect. T eratomas are germ cell tumors, not sex-cord stromal, and do not classically cause acute virilization or temporal balding. \u25cf  B. Granulosa cell tumor; arises from theca and granulosa cells; it accounts for 50% of all ovarian tumors. - Incorrect. Granulosa cell tumors produce estrogen (feminizing), not testosterone, and do not cause virilization. \u25cf  C. Fibroma; arises from primitive fibroblasts; it is the most common malignant sex-cord stromal tumor . - Incorrect. Fibromas are benign and non-functional, not malignant, and do not produce androgens. \u25cf  D. Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor (arrhenoblastoma); arises from primitive sex cords or mesenchymal stroma and morphologically resembles testicular cells; it is a rare sex-cord stromal tumor , accounting for less than 1% of all ovarian neoplasms. - Correct. Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors are androgen-producing sex-cord stromal tumors that morphologically resemble testicular cells (tubules lined by Sertoli cells and Leydig cells containing Reinke crystals). They typically occur in young women (mean age 25) and produce high levels of testosterone and androstenedione, causing rapid virilization (hirsutism, voice deepening, temporal balding, clitoral hypertrophy , and oligomenorrhea/amenorrhea). They are rare, accounting for <1% of all ovarian tumors. \u25cf  E. Krukenberg tumor; arises from metastatic signet-ring cells; it is the most common metastatic tumor of the ovary . - Incorrect. This is a metastatic tumor from the stomach, not associated with androgen excess.",
