    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "sex-cord stromal tumor). \u25cf  A. Meigs' syndrome; the mass is an ovarian fibroma (a benign sex-cord stromal tumor). - Correct. Meigs' syndrome is defined by the clinical triad of a benign ovarian fibroma, ascites, and pleural ef fusion (hydrothorax). Ovarian fibromas are benign sex-cord stromal tumors composed of bundles of spindle-shaped, collagen-producing fibroblastic cells. The pathophysiological mechanism of the fluid accumulation is not fully understood, but both the ascites and pleural effusions completely resolve upon surgical removal of the benign ovarian fibroma. \u25cf  B. Lynch syndrome; the mass is an endometrioid adenocarcinoma. - Incorrect. Lynch syndrome is a hereditary DNA mismatch repair deficit predisposing to 272 colon, endometrial, and ovarian cancer , but does not present as benign fibromas with resolving ef fusions in Meigs' syndrome. \u25cf  C. Swyer syndrome; the mass is a dysgerminoma. - Incorrect. Swyer syndrome is 46,XY pure gonadal dysgenesis, which predisposes to gonadoblastoma and dysgerminoma, not fibromas. \u25cf  D. Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome; the mass is a mucinous cystadenoma. - Incorrect. Fitz-Hugh-Curtis is liver capsule perihepatitis from pelvic inflammatory disease, unrelated to benign fibromas. \u25cf  E. McCune-Albright syndrome; the mass is a luteinized thecoma. - Incorrect. McCune-Albright is a genetic endocrine disorder presenting with cafe-au-lait spots, fibrous dysplasia, and precocious puberty .",
