    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A pathology research group is investigating the \"dual pathogenesis\" model of ovarian serous carcinoma. They are comparing the clinical, genetic, and precursor profiles of low-grade serous carcinoma (LGSC) versus high-grade serous carcinoma (HGSC). According to this model, which of the following is correct regarding LGSC and HGSC precursor lesions, genetic mutations, and pathogenesis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "LGSC arises rapidly from normal ovarian epithelium with TP53 mutations; HGSC arises slowly from a follicular cyst with Kras mutations; both have low chromosomal instability.",
      "B": "LGSC arises from struma ovarii with ERBB2 mutations; HGSC arises from the endometrium with PTEN mutations; both have high MSI.",
      "C": "LGSC arises from theca-lutein cells with Kras mutations; HGSC arises from granulosa cells with p53 mutations; both are highly resistant to platinum-based chemotherapy.",
      "D": "LGSC arises from Brenner tumors with BRAF mutations; HGSC arises from Krukenberg tumors with BRCA1 mutations; both show low chromosomal instability.",
      "E": "LGSC develops slowly via a stepwise pathway from benign serous cystadenomas to borderline serous tumors, showing low chromosomal instability and frequent Kras, Braf, or ERBB2 mutations; HGSC develops rapidly from Serous Tubal Intraepithelial Carcinoma (STIC) in the fallopian tube fimbriae, demonstrating high chromosomal instability, TP53 mutations (almost always), and is highly associated with heritable BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutations."
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "serous cystadenomas to borderline serous tumors, showing low chromosomal instability and frequent Kras, Braf, or ERBB2 mutations; HGSC develops rapidly from Serous Tubal Intraepithelial Carcinoma (STIC) in the fallopian tube fimbriae, demonstrating high chromosomal instability , TP53 mutations (almost always), and is highly associated with heritable BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutations. \u25cf  A. LGSC arises rapidly from normal ovarian epithelium with TP53 mutations; HGSC arises slowly from a follicular cyst with Kras mutations; both have low chromosomal instability . - Incorrect. This is the exact opposite of the dual-pathogenesis model. \u25cf  B. LGSC arises from struma ovarii with ERBB2 mutations; HGSC arises from the endometrium with PTEN mutations; both have high MSI. - Incorrect. Struma ovarii is a specialized germ cell tumor , not the precursor to serous carcinoma. \u25cf  C. LGSC arises from theca-lutein cells with Kras mutations; HGSC arises from granulosa cells with p53 mutations; both are highly resistant to platinum-based chemotherapy . - Incorrect. These precursors are incorrect. \u25cf  D. LGSC arises from Brenner tumors with BRAF mutations; HGSC arises from Krukenberg tumors with BRCA1 mutations; both show low chromosomal instability . - Incorrect. Brenner and Krukenberg are not precursors to serous carcinomas. \u25cf  E. LGSC develops slowly via a stepwise pathway from benign serous cystadenomas to borderline serous tumors, showing low chromosomal instability and frequent Kras, Braf, or ERBB2 mutations; HGSC develops rapidly from Serous T ubal Intraepithelial Carcinoma (STIC) in the fallopian tube fimbriae, demonstrating high chromosomal instability , TP53 mutations (almost always), and is highly associated with heritable BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutations. - Correct. The \"dual-pathogenesis\" model classifies ovarian serous carcinomas into two distinct pathways: T ype I (LGSC) arises slowly in a stepwise fashion from serous cystadenomas through borderline serous tumors, showing low chromosomal instability and frequent Kras, Braf, or ERBB2 mutations. T ype II (HGSC) develops rapidly from a precursor in the fallopian tube fimbriae called Serous Tubal Intraepithelial Carcinoma (STIC). It is characterized by extreme 274 chromosomal instability , TP53 mutations in almost 100% of cases, and a strong association with hereditary BRCA1/BRCA2 mutations.",
