    "stem": "A 58-year-old postmenopausal female is found to have an adnexal mass on physical exam. Her primary care provider orders a serum Cancer Antigen 125 (CA-125) level, which returns significantly elevated. She is referred to a gynecologist, who explains the clinical utility and pathophysiological characteristics of CA-125. Which of the following is correct regarding CA-125 tissue expression, its utility as a screening test, and its clinical application in ovarian cancer management?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "CA-125 is synthesized and secreted by normal ovarian surface epithelial cells; it is a highly sensitive and specific screening test for the general population; and it is never elevated in benign conditions.",
      "B": "CA-125 is a viral protein secreted by HPV-infected cells; it is used to screen teenagers for cervical dysplasia; and it is a diagnostic gold standard for HSV.",
      "C": "CA-125 is made by yolk sac cells and is elevated in all germ cell tumors; it is a useful screening test for pediatric populations; and it helps diagnose mature teratomas.",
      "D": "CA-125 is synthesized by Sertoli-Leydig cells; it is used to screen postmenopausal women for virilization; and it is diagnostic for Meigs' syndrome.",
      "E": "CA-125 is an antigen expressed by cells lining the fallopian tubes, endometrium, endocervix, peritoneum, pleura, and pericardium (not normal ovarian epithelium); it is NOT a useful screening test for the general population because it can be elevated in benign conditions (such as PID, endometriosis, pregnancy, or fibroids); however, it serves as a highly useful tumor marker to monitor disease activity, evaluate response to chemotherapy, detect recurrences, and has higher positive predictive value in postmenopausal women with pelvic masses."
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "tubes, endometrium, endocervix, peritoneum, pleura, and pericardium (not normal ovarian epithelium); it is NOT a useful screening test for the general population because it can be elevated in benign conditions (such as PID, endometriosis, pregnancy , or fibroids); however , it serves as a highly useful tumor marker to monitor disease activity , evaluate response to chemotherapy , detect recurrences, and has higher positive predictive value in postmenopausal women with pelvic masses. \u25cf  A. CA-125 is synthesized and secreted by normal ovarian surface epithelial cells; it is a highly sensitive and specific screening test for the general population; and it is never elevated in benign conditions. - Incorrect. CA-125 is not a good screening test for the general population because of poor specificity (it is elevated in many benign conditions like endometriosis and fibroids). It is not expressed on normal ovarian surface epithelium. \u25cf  B. CA-125 is a viral protein secreted by HPV -infected cells; it is used to screen teenagers for cervical dysplasia; and it is a diagnostic gold standard for HSV . - Incorrect. CA-125 is a mucin-like glycoprotein, not a viral protein, and has no association with HPV or HSV . 278 \u25cf  C. CA-125 is made by yolk sac cells and is elevated in all germ cell tumors; it is a useful screening test for pediatric populations; and it helps diagnose mature teratomas. - Incorrect. Y olk sac tumors secrete AFP , not CA-125. \u25cf  D. CA-125 is synthesized by Sertoli-Leydig cells; it is used to screen postmenopausal women for virilization; and it is diagnostic for Meigs' syndrome. - Incorrect. Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors secrete testosterone and do not use CA-125 as a primary marker , nor is CA-125 diagnostic for Meigs' syndrome. \u25cf  E. CA-125 is an antigen expressed by cells lining the fallopian tubes, endometrium, endocervix, peritoneum, pleura, and pericardium (not normal ovarian epithelium); it is NOT a useful screening test for the general population because it can be elevated in benign conditions (such as PID, endometriosis, pregnancy , or fibroids); however , it serves as a highly useful tumor marker to monitor disease activity , evaluate response to chemotherapy , detect recurrences, and has higher positive predictive value in postmenopausal women with pelvic masses. - Correct. CA-125 (Cancer Antigen 125) is a glycoprotein antigen expressed on tissues derived from coelomic epithelium (fallopian tubes, endometrium, peritoneum, pleura) but not normal ovarian epithelium. Because it can be elevated in numerous benign inflammatory or hormonal processes (such as PID, pregnancy , fibroids, or endometriosis), it lacks the specificity required for general population screening. However , it is a highly valuable clinical marker to monitor response to therapy and detect recurrences in patients with diagnosed epithelial ovarian cancer . Its positive predictive value is significantly higher in postmenopausal women with adnexal masses.",
