    "stem": "A 38-year-old female presents to her gynecologist to discuss her risk of pregnancy complications. She is planning a pregnancy with her partner and notes that she had a complete hydatidiform mole evacuated 2 years ago. She has no other medical conditions. According to epidemiologic data on gestational trophoblastic disease (GTD), which of the following represents the most significant risk factors for the development of a hydatidiform mole?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Extremes of maternal age (age < 15-20 years and age > 35-40 years, with risk rising exponentially for women over age 40) and a personal history of a prior molar pregnancy (which increases the baseline risk approximately 10-fold).",
      "B": "History of combined oral contraceptive pill use for more than 5 years and early menarche.",
      "C": "Multiparity (more than 4 term pregnancies) and a history of pelvic inflammatory disease.",
      "D": "Obesity (BMI > 30 kg/m\u00b2) and a maternal history of gestational diabetes mellitus.",
      "E": "Active tobacco use (smoking > 10 cigarettes per day) and a history of endometriosis."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "years, with risk rising exponentially for women over age 40) and a personal history of a prior molar pregnancy (which increases the baseline risk approximately 10-fold). \u25cf  A. Extremes of maternal age (age < 15-20 years and age > 35-40 years, with risk rising exponentially for women over age 40) and a personal history of a prior molar pregnancy (which increases the baseline risk approximately 10-fold).  - Correct  . Epidemiology of gestational trophoblastic  disease highlights maternal age at the extremes of reproductive life (especially age > 40 years, where risk rises exponentially) and a prior history of a molar pregnancy (10-fold increased risk of recurrence, or approximately 1%) as the two most powerful risk factors. \u25cf  B. History of combined oral contraceptive pill use for more than 5 years and early menarche.  -  Incorrect  . Neither combined oral contraceptive  pill use nor early menarche are established risk factors for the development of hydatidiform moles. \u25cf  C. Multiparity (more than 4 term pregnancies) and a history of pelvic inflammatory disease.  -  Incorrect  . High parity does not increase  the risk of gestational trophoblastic disease, nor does pelvic inflammatory disease, which affects the fallopian tubes but is unrelated to molar pathophysiology. \u25cf  D. Obesity (BMI > 30 kg/m\u00b2) and a maternal history of gestational diabetes mellitus.  -  Incorrect  . These metabolic conditions  do not play an established primary role in the development of molar gestations. \u25cf  E. Active tobacco use (smoking > 10 cigarettes per day) and a history of endometriosis.  -  Incorrect  . Smoking and endometriosis  do not represent primary risk factors for hydatidiform moles.",
    "pearl": "",
