    "rationale": "co-existing with a normal, viable fetus (typically a diploid 46,XX complete mole and a separate normal diploid fetus); it carries a high risk of maternal complications (hemorrhage, preeclampsia) and a 15-20% risk of persistent gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN). \u25cf  A. Partial hydatidiform mole; arises from a single triploid fertilization; carries no risk of persistent disease.  -  Incorrect  . In a partial  mole, the fetus itself is triploid (69,XXY/XXX) and severely malformed. It is not a separate, normal diploid twin. \u25cf  B. Normal twin gestation with physiologic retroplacental hematoma; managed expectantly.  -  Incorrect  . A co-existing complete mole  is a distinct, pathologically abnormal twin, not a simple benign hematoma. \u25cf  C. Choriocarcinoma co-existing with an ectopic pregnancy; treated with immediate radical hysterectomy.  -  Incorrect  . This  describes a rare intrauterine twin gestation, not a combination of choriocarcinoma and ectopic pregnancy. \u25cf  D. A twin pregnancy consisting of a complete hydatidiform mole co-existing with a normal, viable fetus (typically a diploid 46,XX complete mole and a separate normal diploid fetus); it carries a high risk of maternal complications (hemorrhage, preeclampsia) and a 15-20% risk of persistent gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN).  -  Correct  . A twin pregnancy containing a normal,  viable fetus and a co-existing 299 complete mole is a rare obstetric condition. It represents a dizygotic twin gestation where one zygote developed into a normal diploid fetus with a normal placenta, and the other developed into an androgenetic complete mole. This gestation is high-risk, predisposing the mother to bleeding and early-onset preeclampsia. The risk of persistent post-molar GTN from the complete mole component remains approximately 15-20%, similar to a singleton complete mole. \u25cf  E. Invasive mole arising within a single placenta; treated with immediate termination and high-dose methotrexate.  -  Incorrect  .  This is a twin gestation, and invasive mole is a post-evacuation process of myometrial invasion, not a twin placental structure.",
