    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "of labor with oxytocin or prostaglandins is highly contraindicated as a primary evacuation method due to the risk of inducing strong uterine contractions that can force hydropic villi into pelvic veins, leading to trophoblastic pulmonary embolization. \u25cf  A. Medical induction with high-dose oral misoprostol; suction curettage is contraindicated.  -  Incorrect  . Suction curettage is  the standard first-line therapy, and medical induction is contraindicated due to embolization risk. \u25cf  B. Sharp curettage under general anesthesia; vacuum aspiration is contraindicated.  -  Incorrect  . Sharp curettage is associated  with a high risk of uterine perforation and Asherman syndrome, and vacuum suction is the preferred method of evacuation. \u25cf  C. Radical hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy; induction of labor is contraindicated.  -  Incorrect  . While induction of  labor is contraindicated, radical hysterectomy is not the routine first-line therapy for patients who wish to preserve fertility. \u25cf  D. Expectant management; medical intervention is contraindicated.  -  Incorrect  . Molar pregnancies must be evacuated promptly to avoid life-threatening complications (hemorrhage, preeclampsia, choriocarcinoma progression). \u25cf  E. Suction curettage under ultrasound guidance; medical induction of labor with oxytocin or prostaglandins is highly contraindicated as a primary evacuation method due to the risk of inducing strong uterine contractions that can force hydropic villi into pelvic veins, leading to trophoblastic pulmonary embolization.  - Correct  . Suction curettage under ultrasound guidance  is the gold-standard first-line therapy for evacuating a hydatidiform mole. Medical induction of labor with uterotonics (oxytocin/prostaglandins) is highly contraindicated because strong uterine contractions can force fragments of the highly vascular, hydropic chorionic villi into open pelvic venous sinuses, leading to maternal pulmonary embolization of trophoblastic tissue and acute respiratory distress. 300",
