    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "for 3 consecutive weeks, and then monthly for 6 months; strict contraception (typically combined OCPs) is mandatory to prevent a new pregnancy, which would cause a rise in beta-hCG that would be impossible to distinguish from a post-molar recurrence or progression to choriocarcinoma. \u25cf  A. Serum beta-hCG should be monitored weekly until undetectable for 3 consecutive weeks, and then monthly for 6 months; strict contraception (typically combined OCPs) is mandatory to prevent a new pregnancy, which would cause a rise in beta-hCG that would be impossible to distinguish from a post-molar recurrence or progression to choriocarcinoma.  -  Correct  .  Following molar evacuation, patients require strict monitoring with weekly serum beta-hCG levels until they are normal/undetectable for 3 consecutive weeks, followed by monthly measurements for 6 months. Reliable contraception (OCPs are preferred as they suppress pituitary LH that could interfere with hCG assays) is mandatory. If the patient becomes pregnant, her rising beta-hCG would make it impossible to evaluate for gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN). \u25cf  B. Serum beta-hCG should be checked every 3 months for 2 years; contraception is not necessary.  -  Incorrect  . The frequency is too  low, and contraception is highly necessary. \u25cf  C. Serum beta-hCG should be checked daily for 1 week and then every 6 months for 5 years; contraception is managed with an IUD.  -  Incorrect  . Daily monitoring is unnecessary, and immediate post-evacuation IUD insertion is avoided due to the risk of uterine perforation. \u25cf  D. No serological monitoring is required if the post-evacuation ultrasound is clear; she can attempt to conceive immediately.  -  Incorrect  .  All patients must undergo serial beta-hCG monitoring because complete moles carry a 15-20% risk of progressing to persistent GTN. \u25cf  E. Serum beta-hCG is monitored monthly for 12 months; the patient must undergo mandatory bilateral tubal ligation to prevent recurrence.  -  Incorrect  . Mandatory permanent sterilization is inappropriate and not indicated.",
