    "rationale": "over four consecutive weekly measurements), which is diagnostic for post-molar Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia (GTN) and requires immediate oncologic evaluation and staging. \u25cf  A. This represents normal resolution of post-molar beta-hCG, and she should continue monthly surveillance.  -  Incorrect  . In normal  resolution, beta-hCG levels drop steadily. A persistent level around 4,400 mIU/mL is abnormal. \u25cf  B. This represents a plateau in beta-hCG levels (variation <= 10% over four consecutive weekly measurements), which is diagnostic for post-molar Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia (GTN) and requires immediate oncologic 301 evaluation and staging.  -  Correct  . According to FIGO/ACOG criteria, a plateau in beta-hCG levels is defined as a variation of less than or equal to 10% across four consecutive weekly measurements (over a 3-week period: days 1, 7, 14, and 21). This is diagnostic for post-molar GTN and warrants immediate clinical evaluation, staging, and initiation of chemotherapy. \u25cf  C. This represents a new intrauterine pregnancy, and she should be scheduled for a prenatal ultrasound.  -  Incorrect  . A new pregnancy  would show rapidly rising, doubling beta-hCG levels rather than a flat, steady plateau. \u25cf  D. This represents laboratory error, and the beta-hCG test should be repeated in 3 months.  -  Incorrect  . Persistent plateaus across four  weekly tests are not laboratory error, and delaying evaluation for 3 months would be highly dangerous. \u25cf  E. This represents a benign theca-lutein reaction, and she should be managed expectantly.  -  Incorrect  . Ovarian theca-lutein cysts  do not produce beta-hCG; they are driven  by  beta-hCG produced by active trophoblastic  tissue.",
