    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "presence of hydropic chorionic villi, which are completely absent in gestational choriocarcinoma (a pure epithelial malignancy). \u25cf  A. Placental site trophoblastic tumor; distinguished by the presence of multinucleated giant cells.  -  Incorrect  . PSTT consists  of monomorphic intermediate trophoblasts invading the myometrium, lacking chorionic villi. \u25cf  B. Epithelioid trophoblastic tumor; distinguished by the presence of nests of squamous-like cells.  -  Incorrect  . ETT is a distinct  tumor that lacks chorionic villi. \u25cf  C. Invasive mole (chorioadenoma destruens); distinguished by the presence of hydropic chorionic villi, which are completely absent in gestational choriocarcinoma (a pure epithelial malignancy).  -  Correct  . An invasive mole (chorioadenoma destruens) is characterized by the presence of hydropic chorionic villi and proliferating trophoblasts that invade deep into the myometrium or local pelvic vasculature. The critical histopathological distinction between an invasive mole and choriocarcinoma is the presence of chorionic villi: invasive moles retain villous structures, whereas choriocarcinoma is a high-grade malignant epithelial neoplasm that completely lacks chorionic villi. \u25cf  D. Complete hydatidiform mole; distinguished by the absence of myometrial invasion.  -  Incorrect  . Benign complete moles are restricted  to the endometrial cavity; once they invade the myometrium, they are classified as invasive moles. \u25cf  E. Choriocarcinoma; distinguished by the presence of a normal fetus and placenta.  -  Incorrect  . Choriocarcinoma does not contain  a normal fetus, placenta, or any chorionic villi. 302",
