    "rationale": "epithelial neoplasm of trophoblastic origin that is defined by the complete absence of chorionic villi, the presence of which would exclude this diagnosis and point to an invasive mole. \u25cf  A. Placental site trophoblastic tumor; the absence of villi confirms a benign process.  -  Incorrect  . PSTT also lacks villi, but it  is composed of intermediate trophoblasts, lacks a biphasic cytotrophoblast/syncytiotrophoblast population, and is a neoplastic, potentially malignant process. \u25cf  B. Complete hydatidiform mole; the absence of villi is expected in early first-trimester moles.  -  Incorrect  . All hydatidiform  moles are defined by the presence of hydropic chorionic villi; their absence excludes a mole. \u25cf  C. Partial hydatidiform mole; the absence of villi indicates complete fetal resorption.  -  Incorrect  . Partial moles also contain  chorionic villi (characteristically scalloped with trophoblastic inclusions). \u25cf  D. Gestational choriocarcinoma; it is a highly malignant, pure epithelial neoplasm of trophoblastic origin that is defined by the complete absence of chorionic villi, the presence of which would exclude this diagnosis and point to an invasive mole. -  Correct  . Gestational choriocarcinoma is a highly  aggressive malignant epithelial neoplasm composed of a biphasic mixture of atypical syncytiotrophoblasts and cytotrophoblasts with extensive hemorrhage and necrosis. A defining histopathological feature is the complete absence of chorionic villi. If any chorionic villi are identified, the diagnosis of choriocarcinoma is excluded, and the lesion is classified as an invasive mole. \u25cf  E. Uterine leiomyosarcoma; the absence of villi is characteristic of smooth muscle malignancies.  -  Incorrect  . Leiomyosarcoma is a spindle-cell  smooth muscle malignancy, completely unrelated to trophoblastic proliferations or beta-hCG.",
