    "stem": "A 32-year-old female is diagnosed with metastatic gestational choriocarcinoma (FIGO Stage III: lung metastasis, FIGO Score 5). She is highly anxious and asks her oncologist about her prognosis and treatment. Which of the following is correct regarding the prognosis, treatment sensitivity, and standard management of gestational choriocarcinoma?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Gestational choriocarcinoma is highly sensitive to systemic chemotherapy, and even patients with advanced metastatic disease have excellent cure rates (>80-90%); low-risk patients (FIGO score < 7) are treated first-line with single-agent chemotherapy (typically Methotrexate or Actinomycin D).",
      "B": "It is highly resistant to all forms of chemotherapy and radiation, carrying a poor prognosis (<5% survival) and requiring immediate pelvic exenteration.",
      "C": "It is treated primarily with high-dose external beam pelvic radiation; chemotherapy is contraindicated due to liver toxicity.",
      "D": "It represents a benign, self-limiting condition that resolves spontaneously within 6 months with reliable contraception.",
      "E": "It is treated primarily with high-dose systemic corticosteroids combined with oral ciprofloxacin."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "chemotherapy, and even patients with advanced metastatic disease have excellent cure rates (>80-90%); low-risk patients (FIGO score < 7) are treated first-line with single-agent chemotherapy (typically Methotrexate or Actinomycin D). \u25cf  A. Gestational choriocarcinoma is highly sensitive to systemic chemotherapy, and even patients with advanced metastatic disease have excellent cure rates (>80-90%); low-risk patients (FIGO score < 7) are treated first-line with single-agent chemotherapy (typically Methotrexate or Actinomycin D).  -  Correct  . Gestational choriocarcinoma is an exceptionally chemosensitive solid malignancy. Prior to the development of chemotherapy, it was universally fatal, but modern regimens achieve cure rates exceeding 80% to 90%, even in patients with advanced metastatic disease. Treatment is directed by FIGO risk-scoring: low-risk patients (score < 7) receive single-agent chemotherapy (Methotrexate or Actinomycin D), while high-risk patients (score >= 7) receive multi-agent chemotherapy (typically the EMA-CO regimen). \u25cf  B. It is highly resistant to all forms of chemotherapy and radiation, carrying a poor prognosis (<5% survival) and requiring immediate pelvic exenteration.  -  Incorrect  . Choriocarcinoma is highly sensitive, not resistant, to chemotherapy and has an excellent prognosis. \u25cf  C. It is treated primarily with high-dose external beam pelvic radiation; chemotherapy is contraindicated due to liver toxicity.  -  Incorrect  . Chemotherapy is the primary treatment of choice, and radiation is rarely used. \u25cf  D. It represents a benign, self-limiting condition that resolves spontaneously within 6 months with reliable contraception.  -  Incorrect  .  Gestational choriocarcinoma is a highly malignant, aggressive, and rapidly fatal cancer if left untreated. 304 \u25cf  E. It is treated primarily with high-dose systemic corticosteroids combined with oral ciprofloxacin.  -  Incorrect  . Corticosteroids and  antibiotics are not active against choriocarcinoma.",
