    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "intermediate trophoblasts of the placental site (anchoring villi); it characteristically expresses hPL on immunohistochemistry and is associated with only mildly elevated serum beta-hCG, unlike the massive beta-hCG elevations typical of choriocarcinoma. \u25cf  A. Choriocarcinoma; arises from syncytiotrophoblasts; secretes high beta-hCG and low hPL.  -  Incorrect  . Choriocarcinoma features  a classic biphasic cytotrophoblast/syncytiotrophoblast cytology, produces massive levels of beta-hCG, and is typically hPL-negative or only focally positive. \u25cf  B. Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumor (PSTT); arises from intermediate trophoblasts of the placental site (anchoring villi); it characteristically expresses hPL on immunohistochemistry and is associated with only mildly elevated serum beta-hCG, unlike the massive beta-hCG elevations typical of choriocarcinoma.  - Correct  . Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumor (PSTT)  is a rare GTN subtype that arises from the clonal neoplastic proliferation of intermediate trophoblasts at the placental site (anchoring villi). Clinically, it typically presents months to years after a pregnancy (often a term delivery) with abnormal bleeding, a mildly elevated serum beta-hCG level (typically < 1,000 mIU/mL), and significantly elevated  human  placental lactogen (hPL)  . Histologically, it displays monomorphic intermediate trophoblasts infiltrating between myocytes, which strongly express hPL on immunohistochemical staining. \u25cf  C. Complete hydatidiform mole; arises from cytotrophoblasts; secretes high progesterone and is p57-negative.  -  Incorrect  . This  is an invasive, neoplastic post-pregnancy tumor (PSTT), not a benign complete molar gestation. \u25cf  D. Epithelioid trophoblastic tumor; arises from syncytiotrophoblasts; secretes high CA-125 and low hPL.  -  Incorrect  . ETT also arises  from intermediate trophoblasts (chorionic type), not syncytiotrophoblasts, and is negative or only weakly positive for hPL. \u25cf  E. Dysgerminoma; arises from primitive germ cells; secretes high LDH and is calretinin-positive.  -  Incorrect  . Dysgerminoma is  an ovarian germ cell tumor, not a gestational trophoblastic neoplasm of the uterine wall.",
