    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 28-year-old male is evaluated for infertility. Semen analysis shows normal sperm parameters except for a reduced sperm count (oligospermia). A testicular biopsy is performed, and the pathologist maps the stages of spermatogenesis within the seminiferous tubules. In spermatogenesis, what is the correct ploidy, chromatid count (C), and cellular progress of the germ cells during the transition from spermatogonium to mature spermatozoa?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Primary spermatocytes are haploid (1N, 2C) and undergo mitosis to form secondary spermatocytes.",
      "B": "Spermatogonia are diploid (2N, 2C), replicate their DNA to become primary spermatocytes (2N, 4C), which complete Meiosis I to yield haploid secondary spermatocytes (1N, 2C), and subsequently complete Meiosis II to form haploid spermatids (1N, 1C) that mature into spermatozoa via spermiogenesis.",
      "C": "Secondary spermatocytes are diploid (2N, 4C) and undergo Meiosis I to form secondary spermatids.",
      "D": "Spermatids are diploid (2N, 2C) and undergo spermiogenesis to reduce their chromosome count to haploid (1N).",
      "E": "Primary spermatocytes are haploid (1N, 4C) and immediately mature into spermatozoa without undergoing any meiotic divisions."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "become primary spermatocytes (2N, 4C), which complete Meiosis I to yield haploid secondary spermatocytes (1N, 2C), and subsequently complete Meiosis II to form haploid spermatids (1N, 1C) that mature into spermatozoa via spermiogenesis. A. Primary spermatocytes are haploid (1N, 2C) and undergo mitosis to form secondary spermatocytes.: Incorrect. Primary spermatocytes are diploid (2N, 4C) and undergo Meiosis I to form haploid secondary spermatocytes. B. Spermatogonia are diploid (2N, 2C), replicate their DNA to become primary spermatocytes (2N, 4C), which complete Meiosis I to yield haploid secondary spermatocytes (1N, 2C), and subsequently complete Meiosis II to form haploid spermatids (1N, 1C) that mature into spermatozoa via spermiogenesis.: Correct. Spermatogenesis begins with diploid spermatogonia (2N, 2C) undergoing DNA replication to form diploid primary spermatocytes (2N, 4C). Primary spermatocytes complete Meiosis I to yield haploid secondary spermatocytes (1N, 2C). Secondary spermatocytes quickly complete Meiosis II to form haploid spermatids (1N, 1C), which mature into spermatozoa via spermiogenesis. C. Secondary spermatocytes are diploid (2N, 4C) and undergo Meiosis I to form secondary spermatids.: Incorrect. Secondary spermatocytes are haploid (1N, 2C) and undergo Meiosis II to form haploid spermatids. D. Spermatids are diploid (2N, 2C) and undergo spermiogenesis to reduce their chromosome count to haploid (1N).: Incorrect. Spermatids are haploid (1N, 1C) and undergo morphological spermiogenesis with no further reduction in chromosomal count. E. Primary spermatocytes are haploid (1N, 4C) and immediately mature into spermatozoa without undergoing any meiotic divisions.: Incorrect. Primary spermatocytes are diploid (2N, 4C) and must undergo two meiotic divisions.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
