    "stem": "A couple undergoing in vitro fertilization (IVF) is briefed on sperm preparation. The embryologist explains that spermatozoa ejaculated from the male must undergo capacitation to become competent to fertilize an oocyte. What are the two primary steps of capacitation, where do they typically occur in vivo, and what are their respective mechanisms?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It occurs in the vagina and female reproductive tract; the head is capacitated via destabilization of the acrosomal membrane (initiated by vaginal fluids and enzymes removing proteins, carbohydrates, and cholesterol), and the tail is hyper-activated via a calcium (Ca++) influx that stimulates hypermobility.",
      "B": "It occurs in the epididymis; the head is capacitated via the addition of a thick glycoprotein coat, and the tail is hyper-activated via a sodium (Na+) efflux.",
      "C": "It occurs in the seminiferous tubules; the head is capacitated via progesterone-mediated cleavage, and the tail is hyper-activated via cyclic AMP destruction.",
      "D": "It occurs in the fallopian tube ampulla; the head is capacitated by direct fusion with the corona radiata, and the tail is hyper-activated by potassium (K+) influx.",
      "E": "It occurs in the uterine cervix; the head is capacitated by the addition of cholesterol, and the tail is hyper-activated by local estrogen secretion."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "capacitated via destabilization of the acrosomal membrane (initiated by vaginal fluids and enzymes removing proteins, carbohydrates, and cholesterol), and the tail is hyper-activated via a calcium (Ca++) influx that stimulates hypermobility. A. It occurs in the vagina and female reproductive tract; the head is capacitated via destabilization of the acrosomal membrane (initiated by vaginal fluids and enzymes removing proteins, carbohydrates, and cholesterol), and the tail is hyper-activated via a calcium (Ca++) influx that stimulates hypermobility.: Correct. Capacitation is the penultimate step in sperm maturation, occurring within the vagina and female reproductive tract. It consists of: (1) destabilization of the acrosomal sperm head membrane, initiated by vaginal fluids and enzymes removing surface proteins, carbohydrates, and cholesterol to weaken the head so it can penetrate the oocyte; and (2) chemical changes in the tail where a calcium (Ca++) influx stimulates hypermobility. B. It occurs in the epididymis; the head is capacitated via the addition of a thick glycoprotein coat, and the tail is hyper-activated via a sodium (Na+) efflux.: Incorrect. Epididymal maturation occurs before ejaculation and does not involve capacitation. C. It occurs in the seminiferous tubules; the head is capacitated via progesterone-mediated cleavage, and the tail is hyper-activated via cyclic AMP destruction.: Incorrect. Capacitation does not occur in the seminiferous tubules, and is not stimulated by cAMP destruction. D. It occurs in the fallopian tube ampulla; the head is capacitated by direct fusion with the corona radiata, and the tail is hyper-activated by potassium (K+) influx.: Incorrect. Capacitation begins lower in the female tract prior to contact with the egg, and involves calcium rather than potassium influx. E. It occurs in the uterine cervix; the head is capacitated by the addition of cholesterol, and the tail is hyper-activated by local estrogen secretion.: Incorrect. Capacitation removes cholesterol from the cell membrane rather than adding it. 318",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
