    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 28-year-old female presents to the emergency department with acute unilateral pelvic pain and vaginal bleeding. Her beta-hCG is 2,400 mIU/mL, and transvaginal ultrasound reveals an empty uterus and a complex adnexal mass. She is diagnosed with an ectopic pregnancy. According to the \"Sites and Frequency of Ectopic Pregnancy\" slide, which of the following statements is correct regarding ectopic pregnancy sites and their respective frequencies? 310",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The vast majority of ectopic pregnancies are cervical, representing 70% of all cases.",
      "B": "Abdominal ectopic pregnancies represent 12% of all cases, while ovarian ectopics are exceptionally rare at <1%.",
      "C": "Cesarean scar ectopics are the most common tubal ectopics, accounting for 95% of cases.",
      "D": "Tubal pregnancies represent 95 to 96% of all ectopic pregnancies, with the ampullary region being the most common site at 70%, followed by the isthmic (12%), fimbrial (11%), and interstitial/cornual (2 to 3%) regions.",
      "E": "The fimbrial region is the least common site for tubal ectopic pregnancy, accounting for only <1% of cases."
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "pregnancies, with the ampullary region being the most common site at 70%, followed by the isthmic (12%), fimbrial (11%), and interstitial/cornual (2 to 3%) regions. 320 A. The vast majority of ectopic pregnancies are cervical, representing 70% of all cases.: Incorrect. Cervical ectopic pregnancies represent <1% of all cases. B. Abdominal ectopic pregnancies represent 12% of all cases, while ovarian ectopics are exceptionally rare at <1%.: Incorrect. Abdominal ectopic pregnancies represent 1% of all cases, and ovarian ectopic pregnancies account for 3%. C. Cesarean scar ectopics are the most common tubal ectopics, accounting for 95% of cases.: Incorrect. Cesarean scar ectopic pregnancies represent <1% of cases, and are not located within the fallopian tubes. D. Tubal pregnancies represent 95 to 96% of all ectopic pregnancies, with the ampullary region being the most common site at 70%, followed by the isthmic (12%), fimbrial (11%), and interstitial/cornual (2 to 3%) regions.: Correct. According to the \"Sites and Frequency of Ectopic Pregnancy\" slide, tubal pregnancies represent the vast majority of ectopic pregnancies (95\u201396%). Within the fallopian tube, the ampullary region is the most common site (70%), followed by the isthmic (12%), fimbrial (11%), and interstitial/cornual (2\u20133%) regions. Remaining sites include ovarian (3%), abdominal (1%), cervical (<1%), and cesarean scar (<1%) locations. E. The fimbrial region is the least common site for tubal ectopic pregnancy, accounting for only <1% of cases.: Incorrect. The fimbrial region accounts for 11% of tubal ectopic pregnancies, while the interstitial/cornual region is the least common tubal site at 2\u20133%.",
