    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 2-year-old boy is brought to the pediatric emergency department with painless rectal bleeding (melena) and abdominal pain. Technetium-99m pertechnetate scintigraphy (Meckel's scan) is positive, and he is diagnosed with Meckel's diverticulum containing ectopic gastric tissue. What is the embryological connection of the vitelline duct, when does it normally obliterate, and what are its three associated congenital anomalies?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It connects the amniotic cavity to the yolk sac and obliterates during week 3; anomalies include patent urachus, urachal cyst, and Meckel's diverticulum.",
      "B": "It connects the fetal bladder to the cloaca and obliterates during week 12; anomalies include Meckel's diverticulum, patent urachus, and spina bifida.",
      "C": "It connects the neural tube to the gut and obliterates during week 5; anomalies include anencephaly and Meckel's diverticulum.",
      "D": "It connects the chorionic cavity to the placenta and obliterates during week 10; anomalies include patent ductus arteriosus and Meckel's diverticulum.",
      "E": "Also called the omphalomesenteric duct, it connects the yolk sac to the midgut lumen and normally obliterates during Week 7 of development; anomalies include patent vitelline duct (meconium discharge from umbilicus), vitelline duct cyst (increased risk for volvulus), and Meckel's diverticulum."
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "to the midgut lumen and normally obliterates during Week 7 of development; anomalies include patent vitelline duct (meconium discharge from umbilicus), vitelline duct cyst (increased risk for volvulus), and Meckel's diverticulum. A. It connects the amniotic cavity to the yolk sac and obliterates during week 3; anomalies include patent urachus, urachal cyst, and Meckel's diverticulum.: Incorrect. The vitelline duct connects the yolk sac to the midgut lumen (not the amniotic cavity), obliterates in Week 7 (not Week 3), and patent urachus/urachal cysts are allantoic anomalies, not vitelline anomalies. B. It connects the fetal bladder to the cloaca and obliterates during week 12; anomalies include Meckel's diverticulum, patent urachus, and spina bifida.: Incorrect. The bladder-to-cloaca connection is the allantois/urachus, and spina bifida is a neural tube defect. C. It connects the neural tube to the gut and obliterates during week 5; anomalies include anencephaly and Meckel's diverticulum.: Incorrect. The vitelline duct has no connection to the neural tube, and anencephaly is a neural tube closure defect. 324 D. It connects the chorionic cavity to the placenta and obliterates during week 10; anomalies include patent ductus arteriosus and Meckel's diverticulum.: Incorrect. The vitelline duct connects yolk sac to midgut, and patent ductus arteriosus is a cardiovascular anomaly. E. Also called the omphalomesenteric duct, it connects the yolk sac to the midgut lumen and normally obliterates during Week 7 of development; anomalies include patent vitelline duct (meconium discharge from umbilicus), vitelline duct cyst (increased risk for volvulus), and Meckel's diverticulum.: Correct. The vitelline (omphalomesenteric) duct connects the primary yolk sac to the midgut lumen of the developing embryo. It normally obliterates during Week 7 of development. Failure of the vitelline duct to close leads to: (1) Patent vitelline duct (total failure to close, resulting in meconium discharge from the umbilicus); (2) Vitelline duct cyst (partial closure, creating a cyst that increases the risk for volvulus); and (3) Meckel's diverticulum (slight failure to close, leaving an outpouching of the ileum that can contain ectopic gastric tissue, causing painless rectal bleeding).",
