    "id": "W2_Notebook_W4_5_Breast_Disorders_20Q_141",
    "week": "W2",
    "lecture": "Breast Disorders (Notebook)",
    "file": "Notebook_W4_5_Breast_Disorders_20Q.md",
    "qnum": 141,
    "topic": "Breast Disorders (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A clinical pathologist is presenting a lecture on breast histology and pathology to a group of second-year medical students. She discusses the microanatomy of the terminal duct lobular unit (TDLU) and explains how identifying specific cell types under light microscopy can help differentiate benign proliferative processes from invasive malignancies. Which of the following correctly pairs the breast cell type, its normal physiological function, and its diagnostic utility in clinical pathology?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Luminal epithelial cells; they form the outer cell layer of the acini, help with milk ejection during lactation in response to oxytocin, and are completely lost in ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS).",
      "B": "Myoepithelial cells; they form the outer cell layer of the ducts and acini, contract to help with milk ejection during lactation, and their presence throughout a lesion confirms a benign or pre-invasive process, whereas their loss is a hallmark of invasive carcinoma.",
      "C": "Stromal fibroblasts; they synthesize the basement membrane, secrete milk proteins during the luteal phase, and are positive for cytokeratins on immunohistochemistry.",
      "D": "Myoepithelial cells; they form the inner luminal layer, produce milk in response to prolactin, and are lost in DCIS.",
      "E": "Goblet cells; they line the major lactiferous ducts, secrete mucinous lubrication, and serve as the cell of origin for LCIS."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "and acini, contract to help with milk ejection during lactation, and their presence throughout a lesion confirms a benign or pre-invasive process, whereas their loss is a hallmark of invasive carcinoma. \u25cf  A. Luminal epithelial cells; they form the outer cell layer of the acini, help with milk ejection during lactation in response to oxytocin, and are completely lost in ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS).  -  Incorrect  . Luminal epithelial  cells form the inner cell layer of ducts and acini and are responsible for milk production in the TDLUs. They do not form the outer layer, nor are they lost in DCIS. \u25cf  B. Myoepithelial cells; they form the outer cell layer of the ducts and acini, contract to help with milk ejection during lactation, and their presence throughout a lesion confirms a benign or pre-invasive process, whereas their loss is a hallmark of invasive carcinoma.  -  Correct  . The breast  ducts and acini are lined by a double layer of cells: an inner layer of luminal epithelial cells and an outer layer of myoepithelial cells. Myoepithelial cells are contractile cells that assist in milk ejection. In pathology, demonstrating the presence of myoepithelial cells surrounding a proliferation of epithelial cells confirms that the process is benign (e.g., sclerosing adenosis) or pre-invasive (e.g., DCIS). The complete loss of the myoepithelial cell layer is a critical diagnostic hallmark of invasive breast carcinoma. \u25cf  C. Stromal fibroblasts; they synthesize the basement membrane, secrete milk proteins during the luteal phase, and are positive for cytokeratins on immunohistochemistry.  -  Incorrect  . Stromal fibroblasts  do not secrete milk proteins (epithelial cells do) and are cytokeratin-negative (epithelial cells are cytokeratin-positive). \u25cf  D. Myoepithelial cells; they form the inner luminal layer, produce milk in response to prolactin, and are lost in DCIS.  -  Incorrect  . Myoepithelial  cells form the outer cell layer, not the inner luminal layer, and do not produce milk. \u25cf  E. Goblet cells; they line the major lactiferous ducts, secrete mucinous lubrication, and serve as the cell of origin for LCIS.  -  Incorrect  . Goblet cells are not typical constituents of normal breast architecture, and the cell of origin for LCIS is the terminal duct lobular unit epithelial cell.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
