    "topic": "Breast Disorders (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 34-year-old female smoker presents with an abrupt onset of a firm, painful right breast mass. She reports having a history of recurrent breast abscesses in the same breast. Physical examination reveals a tender, firm mass with overlying skin erythema. A core needle biopsy is performed and demonstrates non-caseating granulomas and micro-abscesses confined to the breast lobules. No acid-fast bacilli or fungal elements are seen. What is the most likely diagnosis, a major epidemiological association, and the recommended treatment strategy according to the lecture materials?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Chronic granulomatous mastitis; associated with smoking; treatment involves corticosteroids, antibiotics, or surgical drainage, while avoiding wide surgical excision as it frequently leads to chronic non-healing wounds.",
      "B": "Acute lactational mastitis; associated with breastfeeding; treatment involves urgent surgical excision and holding breastfeeding.",
      "C": "Periductal mastitis; associated with nulliparity; treatment requires immediate bilateral simple mastectomy.",
      "D": "Medullary carcinoma of the breast; associated with BRCA1 mutations; treatment involves high-dose chemotherapy and radiation.",
      "E": "Tuberculosis of the breast; associated with immunosuppression; treatment requires a 9-month course of four-drug antitubercular therapy."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "treatment involves corticosteroids, antibiotics, or surgical drainage, while avoiding wide surgical excision as it frequently leads to chronic non-healing wounds. \u25cf  A. Chronic granulomatous mastitis; associated with smoking; treatment involves corticosteroids, antibiotics, or surgical drainage, while avoiding wide surgical excision as it frequently leads to chronic non-healing wounds.  -  Correct  . Chronic granulomatous mastitis is a benign, chronic inflammatory process characterized by non-caseating granulomas and micro-abscesses restricted to the breast lobules. It is highly associated with smokers and can mimic carcinoma or recurrent bacterial abscesses. According to the lecture materials, standard management includes steroids, antibiotics, or simple surgical drainage. Wide surgical excision is specifically avoided because it frequently results in chronic, non-healing wounds and sinus tracts. \u25cf  B. Acute lactational mastitis; associated with breastfeeding; treatment involves urgent surgical excision and holding breastfeeding.  -  Incorrect  . Histology of acute 189 mastitis shows neutrophils and abscesses, not granulomas, and surgical excision is not the treatment. \u25cf  C. Periductal mastitis; associated with nulliparity; treatment requires immediate bilateral simple mastectomy.  -  Incorrect  . Mastectomy  is not indicated for benign periductal mastitis. \u25cf  D. Medullary carcinoma of the breast; associated with BRCA1 mutations; treatment involves high-dose chemotherapy and radiation.  -  Incorrect  . Although medullary carcinoma can feature lymphoplasmacytic infiltrates, it does not consist of non-caseating granulomas confined to lobules, and the patient's pathology is benign. \u25cf  E. Tuberculosis of the breast; associated with immunosuppression; treatment requires a 9-month course of four-drug antitubercular therapy.  -  Incorrect  . TB of the breast is characterized by caseating granulomas with acid-fast bacilli, which are absent here.",
    "pearl": "",
