    "id": "W2_Notebook_W4_5_Breast_Disorders_20Q_144",
    "week": "W2",
    "lecture": "Breast Disorders (Notebook)",
    "file": "Notebook_W4_5_Breast_Disorders_20Q.md",
    "qnum": 144,
    "topic": "Breast Disorders (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 42-year-old female presents to her primary care physician with a painless, firm lump in her left breast. She underwent a stereotactic breast biopsy at that exact location 6 months ago for calcifications, which returned as benign. Mammography now reveals a calcified, ring-like lesion (\"oil cyst\") at the biopsy site. Core needle biopsy of the lump shows necrotic fat cells surrounded by foamy histiocytes, lymphocytes, plasma cells, and multinucleated giant cells. What is the diagnosis, and which of the following is correct regarding its etiology, clinical presentation, and imaging?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Fibrocystic change; it is driven by cyclic progesterone surges, presents as a painful bilateral mass, and is characterized radiographically by a \"blue-domed\" cyst.",
      "B": "Sclerosing adenosis; it is driven by increased estrogen exposure, presents as a painful, mobile mass, and always undergoes rapid enlargement before menstruation.",
      "C": "Fat necrosis; it is a benign condition typically caused by breast trauma (including surgery or biopsy), though up to 50% of patients may not recall the injury, and it is characterized radiographically by a calcified oil cyst.",
      "D": "Invasive lobular carcinoma; it is caused by loss of E-cadherin and characteristically presents with painful axillary lymphadenopathy on physical exam.",
      "E": "Benign Phyllodes tumor; it represents the benign proliferation of neoplastic stromal cells, presents as a rapidly enlarging mass, and is treated with steroids."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "trauma (including surgery or biopsy), though up to 50% of patients may not recall the injury, and it is characterized radiographically by a calcified oil cyst. \u25cf  A. Fibrocystic change; it is driven by cyclic progesterone surges, presents as a painful bilateral mass, and is characterized radiographically by a \"blue-domed\" cyst.  -  Incorrect  . Fibrocystic changes gross pathology  shows blue-domed cysts, but biopsy does not show necrotic fat cells and giant cells. \u25cf  B. Sclerosing adenosis; it is driven by increased estrogen exposure, presents as a painful, mobile mass, and always undergoes rapid enlargement before menstruation.  -  Incorrect  . Sclerosing adenosis features  fibrosis and acini proliferation, not necrotic adipocytes. \u25cf  C. Fat necrosis; it is a benign condition typically caused by breast trauma (including surgery or biopsy), though up to 50% of patients may not recall the injury, and it is characterized radiographically by a calcified oil cyst.  -  Correct  . Fat necrosis is a benign, painless breast lump. Its etiology is prior breast trauma or injury, which includes medical interventions such as breast surgery or prior biopsies. Up to 50% of patients do not recall a specific traumatic event because the injury can be remote or minor. Radiographically, mammography typically shows a calcified, ring-like lesion (\"oil cyst\"). Histology confirms the diagnosis by showing necrotic adipocytes (fat cells), lipid-laden foamy macrophages, lymphocytes, and multinucleated giant cells. \u25cf  D. Invasive lobular carcinoma; it is caused by loss of E-cadherin and characteristically presents with painful axillary lymphadenopathy on physical exam.  -  Incorrect  . Invasive lobular carcinoma consists  of malignant epithelial cells in a single-file pattern, not benign necrotic fat. \u25cf  E. Benign Phyllodes tumor; it represents the benign proliferation of neoplastic stromal cells, presents as a rapidly enlarging mass, and is treated with steroids.  - Incorrect  . Phyllodes tumor is a fibroepithelial lesion  with leaf-like stroma, completely different from fat necrosis. 190",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
