    "qnum": 145,
    "topic": "Breast Disorders (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 28-year-old female presents to the gynecology clinic with a 2-year history of bilateral breast pain and sensitive, \"lumpy\" breasts. She reports that her symptoms are cyclical, worsening significantly just before her menses and improving once menstruation begins. Physical exam reveals diffuse nodularity in the upper outer quadrants of both breasts. What is the most likely diagnosis, and which clinical feature is most characteristic in differentiating this condition from other breast pathologies?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Fibroadenoma; characterized by a well-demarcated, painless, highly mobile, solitary mass.",
      "B": "Intraductal papilloma; characterized by spontaneous, unilateral, single-duct bloody nipple discharge.",
      "C": "Inflammatory breast cancer; characterized by a rapidly enlarging, painful mass with overlying peau d'orange and warmth.",
      "D": "Sclerosing adenosis; characterized by diffuse microcalcifications on mammography mimicking invasive carcinoma.",
      "E": "Fibrocystic changes; characterized by the classic cyclic nature of her breast pain, worsening just before menses in response to hormonal fluctuations, accompanied by diffuse bilateral nodularity."
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "of her breast pain, worsening just before menses in response to hormonal fluctuations, accompanied by diffuse bilateral nodularity. \u25cf  A. Fibroadenoma; characterized by a well-demarcated, painless, highly mobile, solitary mass.  -  Incorrect  . Fibroadenomas are solitary,  well-circumscribed, highly mobile masses that are usually painless and do not present with diffuse bilateral nodularity. \u25cf  B. Intraductal papilloma; characterized by spontaneous, unilateral, single-duct bloody nipple discharge.  -  Incorrect  . Intraductal  papilloma classically presents with bloody nipple discharge, not diffuse cyclic pain and bilateral nodularity. \u25cf  C. Inflammatory breast cancer; characterized by a rapidly enlarging, painful mass with overlying peau d'orange and warmth.  -  Incorrect  .  Inflammatory breast cancer is a rapidly progressive malignancy presenting with peau d'orange and warmth, not a chronic 2-year history of cyclical bilateral pain. \u25cf  D. Sclerosing adenosis; characterized by diffuse microcalcifications on mammography mimicking invasive carcinoma.  -  Incorrect  .  Sclerosing adenosis is a benign proliferative lesion that can mimic carcinoma, but does not present as cyclic lumpy breasts with diffuse bilateral nodularity. \u25cf  E. Fibrocystic changes; characterized by the classic cyclic nature of her breast pain, worsening just before menses in response to hormonal fluctuations, accompanied by diffuse bilateral nodularity.  -  Correct  .  Fibrocystic changes are extremely common in premenopausal women under 35. The clinical presentation characteristically features cyclic breast pain or tenderness that worsens during the luteal phase (just before menses) in response to estrogen and progesterone fluctuations. Physical exam reveals diffuse bilateral \"lumpy-bumpy\" breasts (nodularity) most prominent in the upper outer quadrants.",
