    "qnum": 152,
    "topic": "Breast Disorders (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 14-year-old boy is accompanied by his mother to a general pediatrics clinic for a routine wellness visit. He has no physical complaints. His mother notes that over the past several months, he has been developing unilateral breast tissue. On physical examination, both chests are soft to palpation and nontender, but a soft, subareolar plaque of tissue is palpable on the left. The physician explains that this is physiologic gynecomastia, which commonly resolves spontaneously. Which of the following represents the correct histologic characteristic of the male breast, the underlying pathophysiology of gynecomastia, and the classic pharmacological agents associated with this condition?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The male breast consists of both ducts and lobules; spironolactone acts as an estrogen receptor antagonist; other drugs include tamoxifen and doxorubicin.",
      "B": "The male breast contains lobules but no ducts; spironolactone stimulates prolactin release; other drugs include paclitaxel and cyclophosphamide.",
      "C": "The male breast lacks ducts entirely; spironolactone acts as an aromatase antagonist; other drugs include anastrozole and trastuzumab.",
      "D": "The male breast contains only ducts and no lobules; gynecomastia is driven by an increased estrogen-to-androgen activity ratio; and associated pharmacological triggers can be remembered by the mnemonic \"Some Hormones Create Knockers\" (Spironolactone, Hormones, Cimetidine, Ketoconazole).",
      "E": "Both male and female breasts have identical ductal and lobular architecture; spironolactone downregulates progesterone; other drugs include metformin and insulin."
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "gynecomastia is driven by an increased estrogen-to-androgen activity ratio; and associated pharmacological triggers can be remembered by the mnemonic \"Some Hormones Create Knockers\" (Spironolactone, Hormones, Cimetidine, Ketoconazole). \u25cf  A. The male breast consists of both ducts and lobules; spironolactone acts as an estrogen receptor antagonist; other drugs include tamoxifen and doxorubicin.  - Incorrect  . The normal male breast contains only ducts  and stroma, completely lacking lobules. Spironolactone acts as an aldosterone antagonist with anti-androgenic effects, increasing estrogen-to-androgen activity. \u25cf  B. The male breast contains lobules but no ducts; spironolactone stimulates prolactin release; other drugs include paclitaxel and cyclophosphamide.  - Incorrect  . This is the exact opposite of the correct  anatomy. \u25cf  C. The male breast lacks ducts entirely; spironolactone acts as an aromatase antagonist; other drugs include anastrozole and trastuzumab.  -  Incorrect  . Male breasts have ducts but no lobules. \u25cf  D. The male breast contains only ducts and no lobules; gynecomastia is driven by an increased estrogen-to-androgen activity ratio; and associated pharmacological triggers can be remembered by the mnemonic \"Some Hormones Create Knockers\" (Spironolactone, Hormones, Cimetidine, Ketoconazole).  -  Correct  . Unlike the female breast, the normal male breast contains only fibrous stroma and a primitive ductal system, completely lacking lobules (acini). Gynecomastia occurs when there is an increased ratio of active estrogen to androgen activity, stimulating ductal and stromal proliferation. Pharmacological causes are highly classic and can be remembered by the mnemonic  \"Some Hormones Create Knockers\"  representing  Spironolactone, Hormones (estrogen/HCG), Cimetidine, and Ketoconazole  . \u25cf  E. Both male and female breasts have identical ductal and lobular architecture; spironolactone downregulates progesterone; other drugs include metformin and insulin.  -  Incorrect  . The architectures are highly  distinct, and the associated drugs listed are incorrect. 195",
