    "stem": "A 51-year-old female is diagnosed with invasive breast carcinoma. The surgical pathology report notes that the tumor cells are arranged in orderly, single-file lines and 184 targetoid patterns surrounding normal ducts, with a complete loss of membrane immunohistochemical staining for E-cadherin. Which of the following is correct regarding the histological classification, risk of bilateral disease, and metastatic patterns of this tumor compared to invasive ductal carcinoma (IDC)?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It is invasive ductal carcinoma; it arises from the major ducts, is highly associated with HER2 overexpression, and rarely presents bilaterally.",
      "B": "It is medullary carcinoma; it is characterized by sheets of high-grade cells with a prominent lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate and carries an excellent prognosis.",
      "C": "It is invasive squamous cell carcinoma; it is highly associated with long-standing lichen sclerosus and has a 100% cure rate with surgery alone.",
      "D": "It is invasive lobular carcinoma (ILC); it represents approximately 22% of all breast cancers, is more frequently bilateral and multifocal, can be underestimated on imaging/physical exam due to lack of a robust desmoplastic response, and has a propensity for unusual visceral metastases (e.g., stomach, gallbladder, peritoneum).",
      "E": "It is secretory carcinoma; it is characterized by intracellular vacuole formation, affects primarily pediatric populations, and is highly responsive to estrogen."
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "approximately 22% of all breast cancers, is more frequently bilateral and multifocal, can be underestimated on imaging/physical exam due to lack of a robust desmoplastic response, and has a propensity for unusual visceral metastases (e.g., stomach, gallbladder, peritoneum). \u25cf  A. It is invasive ductal carcinoma; it arises from the major ducts, is highly associated with HER2 overexpression, and rarely presents bilaterally.  -  Incorrect  . This describes IDC, which has preserved E-cadherin expression, unlike this E-cadherin-negative tumor. 198 \u25cf  B. It is medullary carcinoma; it is characterized by sheets of high-grade cells with a prominent lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate and carries an excellent prognosis.  - Incorrect  . This is not the histological pattern of  medullary carcinoma, which is E-cadherin positive. \u25cf  C. It is invasive squamous cell carcinoma; it is highly associated with long-standing lichen sclerosus and has a 100% cure rate with surgery alone.  - Incorrect  . This is a fabricated connection to vulvar  pathology. \u25cf  D. It is invasive lobular carcinoma (ILC); it represents approximately 22% of all breast cancers, is more frequently bilateral and multifocal, can be underestimated on imaging/physical exam due to lack of a robust desmoplastic response, and has a propensity for unusual visceral metastases (e.g., stomach, gallbladder, peritoneum).  -  Correct  . The histological finding of  tumor cells arranged in orderly \"single-file\" lines (or cords)  and targetoid patterns  surrounding normal ducts is pathognomonic for  Invasive Lobular Carcinoma (ILC)  ,  which accounts for about 22% of all breast cancers. This pattern is driven by the complete  loss of E-cadherin expression  (due to CDH1 mutations), which impairs  cell-cell adhesion. Clinically, ILC has several unique features: it is more frequently  bilateral and multifocal  , can be underestimated on imaging and physical exam  because  it lacks a robust desmoplastic response (and thus doesn't form a well-circumscribed hard lump), and has a unique propensity for  unusual visceral metastases  to the gastrointestinal tract (stomach, gallbladder), peritoneum, and ovaries. \u25cf  E. It is secretory carcinoma; it is characterized by intracellular vacuole formation, affects primarily pediatric populations, and is highly responsive to estrogen.  - Incorrect  . This is a rare, distinct breast cancer  subtype.",
