    "stem": "A clinician is evaluating a patient suspected of having Bacterial Vaginosis. He reviews the Amsel Criteria to establish a clinical diagnosis before performing a saline wet mount examination. What are the components of the Amsel Criteria, and what represents a classic finding on saline wet mount?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Vaginal pH < 4.5, positive whiff test, cottage cheese discharge, and pseudophyphae on wet mount.",
      "B": "Vaginal pH >= 4.5, negative whiff test, frothy discharge, and motile flagellated protozoa.",
      "C": "Vaginal pH < 3.8, positive whiff test, purulent discharge, and intracellular diplococci.",
      "D": "Must have at least three of the following findings: vaginal pH > 4.5; presence of >20% \"clue cells\" (coccobacilli-studded squamous epithelial cells) on saline wet mount; positive amine \"whiff\" test (fishy odor upon adding 10% KOH); and a homogeneous, non-viscous, milky-white discharge adherent to the vaginal walls.",
      "E": "Vaginal pH > 4.5, presence of multinucleated giant cells, postcoital bleeding, and gram-positive rods."
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "4.5; presence of >20% \"clue cells\" (coccobacilli-studded squamous epithelial cells) on saline wet mount; positive amine \"whiff\" test (fishy odor upon adding 10% KOH); and a homogeneous, non-viscous, milky-white discharge adherent to the vaginal walls. 213 \u25cf  A. Vaginal pH < 4.5, positive whiff test, cottage cheese discharge, and pseudophyphae on wet mount.  -  Incorrect  . Normal vaginal  pH is < 4.5, which is typical for yeast, and whiff test is negative in yeast. \u25cf  B. Vaginal pH >= 4.5, negative whiff test, frothy discharge, and motile flagellated protozoa.  -  Incorrect  . This describes Trichomoniasis,  not the Amsel Criteria for BV. \u25cf  C. Vaginal pH < 3.8, positive whiff test, purulent discharge, and intracellular diplococci.  -  Incorrect  . Diplococci represent Gonorrhea,  and the pH is elevated, not depressed, in BV. \u25cf  D. Must have at least three of the following findings: vaginal pH > 4.5; presence of >20% \"clue cells\" (coccobacilli-studded squamous epithelial cells) on saline wet mount; positive amine \"whiff\" test (fishy odor upon adding 10% KOH); and a homogeneous, non-viscous, milky-white discharge adherent to the vaginal walls.  - Correct  . The Amsel Criteria are the clinical gold  standard for diagnosing Bacterial Vaginosis. At least three of the four criteria must be present. A saline wet mount shows \"clue cells,\" which are squamous epithelial cells whose membranes are heavily studded with coccobacilli, obscuring their borders. \u25cf  E. Vaginal pH > 4.5, presence of multinucleated giant cells, postcoital bleeding, and gram-positive rods.  -  Incorrect  . Multinucleated  giant cells represent HSV.",
