    "rationale": "chorioamnionitis, postpartum endometritis, and post-hysterectomy vaginal-cuff cellulitis; first-line treatment is oral metronidazole or clindamycin vaginal cream. \u25cf  A. Untreated BV increases the risk for preterm labor, chorioamnionitis, postpartum endometritis, and post-hysterectomy vaginal-cuff cellulitis; first-line treatment is oral metronidazole or clindamycin vaginal cream.  -  Correct  . Although Bacterial Vaginosis is a benign vaginal dysbiosis, it carries significant obstetric and gynecological risks if left untreated. It increases the risk of preterm labor, chorioamnionitis, postpartum endometritis, vaginal-cuff cellulitis after hysterectomy, and pelvic inflammatory disease. Treatment is metronidazole (oral or vaginal) or clindamycin vaginal cream. \u25cf  B. Untreated BV leads to a 100% rate of congenital rubella-like syndrome; treated first-line with high-dose penicillin G.  -  Incorrect  .  Congenital rubella is caused by the Rubella virus, not BV, and penicillin is not the treatment for BV. \u25cf  C. Untreated BV leads to bilateral fallopian tube occlusion (hydrosalpinx) driven solely by Lactobacillus; treated first-line with oral doxycycline.  -  Incorrect  . Fallopian tube occlusion is caused by Chlamydia/Gonorrhea (PID), and Lactobacillus is protective, not pathogenic. \u25cf  D. Untreated BV results in systemic septic arthritis and necrotizing skin lesions; treated first-line with ceftriaxone IM.  -  Incorrect  .  This describes disseminated Gonococcal infection (DGI), not BV. \u25cf  E. Untreated BV causes chronic vulvar lichen sclerosus and vaginal adenosis; treated first-line with topical steroids and estrogen.  -  Incorrect  . Lichen sclerosus and vaginal adenosis are non-infectious pathologies. 214",
