    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "steroids; most commonly caused by Candida albicans, but recurrent cases can be caused by Candida glabrata (confirmed by yeast culture); uncomplicated cases are treated with a single dose of oral fluconazole or vaginal azole creams, while recurrent cases require cultures and tailored therapy. \u25cf  A. High progesterone levels and oral contraceptives; caused by Candida glabrata; treated with doxycycline.  -  Incorrect  . Candida is  a fungus, and doxycycline is an antibacterial drug that actually  causes  yeast infections  by wiping out normal flora. \u25cf  B. Chronic douching and smoking; caused by Candida tropicalis; treated with metronidazole.  -  Incorrect  . Metronidazole is an anaerobic/anti-protozoal  drug, not an antifungal. \u25cf  C. Mismatch repair gene mutations and pelvic adhesions; caused by Candida krusei; treated with tinidazole.  -  Incorrect  . Tinidazole  treats trichomoniasis/parasites, not yeast. \u25cf  D. Low estrogen levels and postmenopausal state; caused by Candida albicans; treated with high-dose ceftriaxone.  -  Incorrect  . Ceftriaxone  is an antibacterial drug. \u25cf  E. Immunosuppression, diabetes, and taking antibiotics or steroids; most commonly caused by Candida albicans, but recurrent cases can be caused by Candida glabrata (confirmed by yeast culture); uncomplicated cases are treated with a single dose of oral fluconazole or vaginal azole creams, while recurrent 215 cases require cultures and tailored therapy.  -  Correct  . Risk factors for vulvovaginal candidiasis include diabetes mellitus (due to high glucose in vaginal secretions), immunosuppression, and the use of antibiotics or steroids (which deplete vaginal lactobacilli). While Candida albicans is the cause in over 90% of cases, recurrent cases can be caused by non-albicans species like Candida glabrata, which are resistant to standard fluconazole and require culture and specialized azoles.",
