    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "shows moving trichomonads slightly larger than PMNs but has only 55-60% sensitivity (meaning many are missed); therefore, a DNA probe of vaginal secretions (90% sensitivity) or culture (Diamond's medium, 90% sensitivity) is used. \u25cf  A. Chlamydia trachomatis; saline wet mount has 100% sensitivity; diagnosed via Gram stain showing intracellular inclusions.  -  Incorrect  .  Chlamydia is a bacterium that cannot be seen on standard saline wet mount. \u25cf  B. Candida albicans; saline wet mount has 90% sensitivity; diagnosed via a 10% KOH prep.  -  Incorrect  . Candida shows pseudohyphae,  not moving flagellates, and vaginal pH is normal (<4.5). 216 \u25cf  C. Gardnerella vaginalis; saline wet mount shows clue cells; diagnosed via the amine whiff test.  -  Incorrect  . This describes BV,  which does not present with a strawberry cervix. \u25cf  D. Trichomonas vaginalis (a parasitic protozoan); saline wet mount shows moving trichomonads slightly larger than PMNs but has only 55-60% sensitivity (meaning many are missed); therefore, a DNA probe of vaginal secretions (90% sensitivity) or culture (Diamond's medium, 90% sensitivity) is used.  -  Correct  . Trichomoniasis is caused by Trichomonas vaginalis, a flagellated protozoan. Speculum examination characteristically shows a thin, yellow-green, frothy, malodorous discharge and, in a minority of cases (2%), a classic \"strawberry cervix\" (petechial hemorrhages). A saline wet mount shows actively motile, flagellated trichomonads, but has poor sensitivity (55-60%). Thus, DNA probes (NAAT) of vaginal wall secretions are preferred for their high sensitivity (90%). Culture can be performed using Diamond's medium but is seldom done. \u25cf  E. Herpes Simplex Virus 2; saline wet mount shows multinucleated giant cells; diagnosed via Tzank smear.  -  Incorrect  . HSV is a virus  and Tzank smear is for vesicle fluid, not vaginal discharge.",
