    "rationale": "infected men develop symptomatic urethritis (dysuria, purulent discharge), making male-to-female transmission highly efficient (~50% after one episode); diagnosed via NAAT of endocervical or urethral swab. \u25cf  A. Neisseria gonorrhoeae; in contrast to women, up to 95% of infected men develop symptomatic urethritis (dysuria, purulent discharge), making male-to-female transmission highly efficient (~50% after one episode); diagnosed via NAAT of endocervical or urethral swab.  -  Correct  .  Gonorrhea is caused by Neisseria gonorrhoeae (Gram-negative intracellular diplococci). While the majority of women are asymptomatic, up to 95% of infected men develop acute, highly painful urethritis with thick, purulent discharge. Male-to-female transmission is highly efficient, with approximately a 50% transmission rate after a single sexual encounter. Diagnosis is confirmed via NAAT of an endocervical or urethral swab. \u25cf  B. Neisseria meningitidis; men are always completely asymptomatic, and transmission is inefficient; diagnosed via viral PCR.  -  Incorrect  . N. meningitidis causes meningitis, not gonorrhea. \u25cf  C. Treponema pallidum; both men and women present with painless, indurated ulcers; diagnosed via darkfield microscopy.  -  Incorrect  .  This describes Syphilis. 220 \u25cf  D. Gardnerella vaginalis; men develop severe prostatitis, while women are asymptomatic; diagnosed via saline wet mount.  -  Incorrect  .  Gardnerella causes BV, not gonorrhea, and does not classically cause severe acute prostatitis. \u25cf  E. Haemophilus ducreyi; both sexes present with painful, exudative ulcers; diagnosed via Tzanck smear.  -  Incorrect  . This describes  Chancroid.",
