    "rationale": "dose than previously used); because concomitant Chlamydia infection occurs in 20-30% of cases, she should be treated for both Chlamydia (with Doxycycline) if her Chlamydia status is unknown. 221 \u25cf  A. Azithromycin 1 gram orally as a single dose; Chlamydia is treated only if she develops chronic pelvic pain.  -  Incorrect  . Azithromycin  is no longer recommended as monotherapy for Gonorrhea due to rising resistance, and Chlamydia must be treated empirically, not delayed until pelvic pain develops. \u25cf  B. Penicillin G benzathine 2.4 million units IM; if Chlamydia is unknown, she should undergo immediate diagnostic laparoscopy.  -  Incorrect  . Penicillin does not treat Gonorrhea or Chlamydia, and laparoscopy is not indicated for uncomplicated cervicitis. \u25cf  C. Doxycycline 100 mg orally BID for 7 days; if Chlamydia is unknown, no additional therapy is required because doxycycline treats both.  -  Incorrect  . Doxycycline does not effectively treat Gonorrhea. \u25cf  D. Metronidazole 2 grams orally as a single dose; if Chlamydia is unknown, she should be started on long-term steroid therapy.  -  Incorrect  . Metronidazole does not treat Gonorrhea or Chlamydia. \u25cf  E. Ceftriaxone 500 mg intramuscularly as a single dose (higher dose than previously used); because concomitant Chlamydia infection occurs in 20-30% of cases, she should be treated for both Chlamydia (with Doxycycline) if her Chlamydia status is unknown.  -  Correct  . The current  CDC guideline for uncomplicated Gonorrhea is a single intramuscular dose of  Ceftriaxone 500 mg (increased from the older 250 mg dose to optimize cure rates). Because co-infection with Chlamydia occurs in up to 20-30% of cases, clinicians treat empirically for both: if Chlamydia infection has not been ruled out, she should also receive  Doxycycline 100 mg po BID for 7 days  .",
