    "rationale": "pathogen that causes infection through sexual contact, whereas the term \"sexually transmitted disease\" (STD) refers to a recognizable disease state that has developed from an infection. \u25cf  A. An STI is a chronic viral infection that can never be cured, whereas an STD is an acute bacterial infection that is easily resolved with a single dose of antibiotics. -  Incorrect  . This is a fabricated distinction; both  STIs and STDs can be bacterial, viral, fungal, or parasitic. \u25cf  B. The term \"sexually transmitted infection\" (STI) refers to a pathogen that causes infection through sexual contact, whereas the term \"sexually transmitted disease\" (STD) refers to a recognizable disease state that has developed from an infection. -  Correct  . According to the CDC Definitions cited  in the lecture slides, an  STI  refers to the presence of a pathogen that has infected the host through sexual contact, which can often remain completely asymptomatic and unnoticed. An  STD  refers to a recognizable, symptomatic disease state or clinical syndrome that has developed as a result of that chronic or acute infection. \u25cf  C. An STI is defined by the presence of visible genital ulcers and inguinal buboes, whereas an STD is characterized by asymptomatic systemic bacteremia.  - Incorrect  . This is the opposite of the clinical picture;  STDs are symptomatic recognizable states (like genital ulcers), while STIs can be asymptomatic. \u25cf  D. The term \"STI\" is used only for infections affecting male anatomy, whereas \"STD\" is used for infections affecting female anatomy.  -  Incorrect  . Both terms apply to all individuals regardless of anatomy. \u25cf  E. There is no biological or clinical difference; the term \"STI\" was completely retired by the CDC in 2021 and replaced entirely with \"STD\" in all clinical guidelines.  -  Incorrect  . The CDC and major guidelines  have increasingly transitioned from \"STD\" to \"STI\" to reduce stigma and emphasize that many infections are asymptomatic, but both terms remain defined.",
