    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A gynecologic infectious disease specialist is preparing a grand rounds presentation on the historical and modern aspects of Syphilis. She reviews the microbiology of the causative organism and why traditional laboratory techniques are insufficient for its detection. Which of the following correctly describes the etiologic agent of syphilis, its morphology, and its diagnostic characteristics?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It is caused by Haemophilus ducreyi, a Gram-negative coccobacillus that is easily cultured on chocolate agar.",
      "B": "It is caused by Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1-L3, which are obligate intracellular bacteria seen only on Giemsa stain.",
      "C": "It is caused by Treponema pallidum, a spirochete bacterium that is not amenable to traditional methods of culture or standard light microscopy, and is classically transmitted via skin-to-skin contact or vertical transmission.",
      "D": "It is caused by Klebsiella granulomatis, an encapsulated rod that demonstrates safety-pin-like intracellular Donovan bodies on tissue smear.",
      "E": "It is caused by a double-stranded DNA poxvirus that presents as cytoplasmically replicating virions with Cowdry A inclusions."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "is not amenable to traditional methods of culture or standard light microscopy, and is classically transmitted via skin-to-skin contact or vertical transmission. \u25cf  A. It is caused by Haemophilus ducreyi, a Gram-negative coccobacillus that is easily cultured on chocolate agar.  -  Incorrect  . This  is the etiologic agent of Chancroid, not Syphilis. \u25cf  B. It is caused by Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1-L3, which are obligate intracellular bacteria seen only on Giemsa stain.  -  Incorrect  . This is the etiologic agent of Lymphogranuloma Venereum (LGV). \u25cf  C. It is caused by Treponema pallidum, a spirochete bacterium that is not amenable to traditional methods of culture or standard light microscopy, and is classically transmitted via skin-to-skin contact or vertical transmission.  -  Correct  . Syphilis is caused by  Treponema pallidum  , an extremely  thin, coiled,  spirochete bacterium  . A major microbiological feature of T. pallidum  is that it  cannot be cultured on artificial agar  in the laboratory, nor can it be  visualized using standard light microscopy (requiring darkfield microscopy or immunofluorescence instead). It is highly contagious and transmitted via direct skin-to-skin contact (with active lesions) or vertically across the placenta. \u25cf  D. It is caused by Klebsiella granulomatis, an encapsulated rod that demonstrates safety-pin-like intracellular Donovan bodies on tissue smear.  -  Incorrect  . This is the etiologic agent of Granuloma Inguinale (Donovanosis). \u25cf  E. It is caused by a double-stranded DNA poxvirus that presents as cytoplasmically replicating virions with Cowdry A inclusions.  -  Incorrect  . This is a description of Molluscum contagiosum (poxvirus) mixed with HSV (Cowdry A). T. pallidum is a bacterium, not a virus. 244",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
