    "stem": "During a neurology rotation, a third-year medical student is examining a 62-year-old male with a history of untreated syphilis. On examination, the patient demonstrates progressive sensory ataxia, a broad-based slapping gait, severe shooting pains in his legs, a loss of deep tendon reflexes, and impaired proprioception in his lower extremities. His pupils constrict when focusing on a near object, but do not constrict when exposed to direct light. What stage of syphilis is this patient displaying, what are the specific names of his neurological and ocular findings, and are patients in this stage infectious to others?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Secondary syphilis; findings are condyloma lata and a strawberry cervix; the patient is highly infectious.",
      "B": "Secondary syphilis; findings are palmar rash and a primary chancre; the patient is moderately infectious.",
      "C": "Tertiary syphilis; findings are tabes dorsalis (sensory ataxia, loss of DTRs) and Argyll-Robertson pupils (\"prostitute's pupils\" that accommodate but don't react); and patients in this stage are NOT infectious.",
      "D": "Primary syphilis; findings are a painful bubo and a groove sign; the patient is not infectious.",
      "E": "Congenital syphilis; findings are \"snuffles\" and Hutchinson teeth; the patient is highly infectious."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "of DTRs) and Argyll-Robertson pupils (\"prostitute's pupils\" that accommodate but don't react); and patients in this stage are NOT infectious. \u25cf  A. Secondary syphilis; findings are condyloma lata and a strawberry cervix; the patient is highly infectious.  -  Incorrect  . Condyloma  lata and strawberry cervix are not the findings described, and secondary syphilis is indeed infectious, but this patient has late tertiary disease. \u25cf  B. Secondary syphilis; findings are palmar rash and a primary chancre; the patient is moderately infectious.  -  Incorrect  . A primary chancre  is primary syphilis, palmar rash is secondary, and the patient has tertiary neurosyphilis. \u25cf  C. Tertiary syphilis; findings are tabes dorsalis (sensory ataxia, loss of DTRs) and Argyll-Robertson pupils (\"prostitute's pupils\" that accommodate but don't react); and patients in this stage are NOT infectious.  -  Correct  .  This patient's constellation of symptoms represents late-stage  neurosyphilis  , which  is a form of  Tertiary Syphilis  . The progressive degeneration of the posterior columns of the spinal cord is called  Tabes dorsalis  , presenting as sensory ataxia, severe lancinating  pains, and loss of deep tendon reflexes and proprioception. His pupillary finding is the classic  Argyll-Robertson pupil  (also known historically as \"prostitute's pupils\"  because they accommodate but do not react to light, mirroring the clinical aphorism \"prostitutes accommodate but do not react\"). Importantly,  patients in the tertiary stage  of syphilis are not infectious to others  , as the spirochetes are locked within chronic  granulomatous gummatous lesions and are not actively shedding. \u25cf  D. Primary syphilis; findings are a painful bubo and a groove sign; the patient is not infectious.  -  Incorrect  . A painful bubo and groove  sign are clinical findings of LGV, and primary syphilis is highly infectious. \u25cf  E. Congenital syphilis; findings are \"snuffles\" and Hutchinson teeth; the patient is highly infectious.  -  Incorrect  . This is an adult patient  with late acquired syphilis, not congenital syphilis.",
