    "stem": "A 35-year-old HIV-positive male presents to the ophthalmology clinic complaining of bilateral blurry vision, floaters, and eye pain. On exam, he is diagnosed with panuveitis. Serological testing is positive for syphilis. He also notes a 2-week history of constant ringing in his ears (tinnitus) and dizziness (vertigo). According to your lecture materials, which of the following is correct regarding ocular syphilis and otosyphilis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "They can occur at any stage of syphilis (though more typical in early stages); panuveitis is the most common ocular manifestation, but others include conjunctivitis, uveitis, keratitis, optic neuropathy, and retinal vasculitis, which can cause permanent vision loss; and otosyphilis classically presents with tinnitus, vertigo, or sensorineural hearing loss.",
      "B": "They occur exclusively in the primary stage and always resolve spontaneously within 24 hours without treatment.",
      "C": "They are caused only by penicillin-resistant mutations and can only be treated with high-dose corticosteroids without antibiotics.",
      "D": "They are restricted entirely to patients over age 80 with late-stage cardiovascular aortitis.",
      "E": "They represent a primary bacterial infection of the sclera caused by Haemophilus ducreyi."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "early stages); panuveitis is the most common ocular manifestation, but others include conjunctivitis, uveitis, keratitis, optic neuropathy, and retinal vasculitis, which can cause permanent vision loss; and otosyphilis classically presents with tinnitus, vertigo, or sensorineural hearing loss. 248 \u25cf  A. They can occur at any stage of syphilis (though more typical in early stages); panuveitis is the most common ocular manifestation, but others include conjunctivitis, uveitis, keratitis, optic neuropathy, and retinal vasculitis, which can cause permanent vision loss; and otosyphilis classically presents with tinnitus, vertigo, or sensorineural hearing loss.  -  Correct  .  A critical diagnostic and treatment rule is that  ocular syphilis and otosyphilis can occur  at any stage of the disease (primary, secondary, latent, or tertiary), although they are most commonly reported during the early stages of infection.  Panuveitis  is  the most common ocular manifestation, but others include conjunctivitis, anterior uveitis, keratitis, optic neuropathy, and retinal vasculitis, which carry a high risk of  permanent vision loss  if untreated.  Otosyphilis  involves spirochetal invasion  of the inner ear, classically presenting with  tinnitus, vertigo, and sensorineural  hearing loss  . Because these represent cranial nerve/central nervous system invasion, they must be managed as neurosyphilis. \u25cf  B. They occur exclusively in the primary stage and always resolve spontaneously within 24 hours without treatment.  -  Incorrect  . They  can occur at any stage, do not resolve in 24 hours, and require aggressive intravenous antibiotic therapy to prevent permanent deficits. \u25cf  C. They are caused only by penicillin-resistant mutations and can only be treated with high-dose corticosteroids without antibiotics.  -  Incorrect  . Penicillin G resistance in T. pallidum remains virtually non-existent; they are treated with high-dose intravenous penicillin G, not corticosteroids alone. \u25cf  D. They are restricted entirely to patients over age 80 with late-stage cardiovascular aortitis.  -  Incorrect  . They can occur  in any age group, especially in younger HIV-positive patients during early infectious stages. \u25cf  E. They represent a primary bacterial infection of the sclera caused by Haemophilus ducreyi.  -  Incorrect  . They are caused  by Treponema pallidum, not H. ducreyi (the cause of chancroid).",
