    "stem": "A 28-year-old G2P1 pregnant female at 14 weeks gestation is diagnosed with secondary syphilis during her routine prenatal screening. Her medical chart indicates a documented history of severe anaphylaxis to penicillin. What is the standard first-line therapy for syphilis, and what is the recommended clinical management for this pregnant patient according to the ASCCP/CDC guidelines?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Standard therapy is oral doxycycline for 14 days; because she is pregnant and penicillin-allergic, doxycycline is the safest alternative as it has no risk of fetal toxicity.",
      "B": "Standard therapy is intravenous ceftriaxone; because she is allergic, she should undergo immediate surgical resection of the cervix.",
      "C": "Standard therapy is oral azithromycin; because she is pregnant, she should be managed with observation only until she delivers.",
      "D": "Standard therapy is intramuscular benzathine penicillin G; because penicillin is the ONLY acceptable treatment for syphilis in pregnancy, this patient must undergo confirmation of allergy, penicillin skin testing/graded challenge, and if positive, mandatory penicillin desensitization to receive penicillin G safely.",
      "E": "Standard therapy is topical permethrin cream; because she is allergic, she should be treated with oral ivermectin."
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "because penicillin is the ONLY acceptable treatment for syphilis in pregnancy, this patient must undergo confirmation of allergy, penicillin skin testing/graded challenge, and if positive, mandatory penicillin desensitization to receive penicillin G safely. \u25cf  A. Standard therapy is oral doxycycline for 14 days; because she is pregnant and penicillin-allergic, doxycycline is the safest alternative as it has no risk of fetal toxicity.  -  Incorrect  . Doxycycline is highly contraindicated  in pregnancy due to the risk of permanent fetal bone growth inhibition and tooth discoloration (tetracycline class effect). Furthermore, doxycycline is not an acceptable treatment for syphilis in a pregnant person. \u25cf  B. Standard therapy is intravenous ceftriaxone; because she is allergic, she should undergo immediate surgical resection of the cervix.  -  Incorrect  . Syphilis is a systemic bacterial infection, and surgical resection of the cervix is not a treatment for spirochetemia. \u25cf  C. Standard therapy is oral azithromycin; because she is pregnant, she should be managed with observation only until she delivers.  -  Incorrect  . Azithromycin has high rates of resistance and poor placental transfer, and observation is highly contraindicated due to the near-certainty of transmitting congenital syphilis to the fetus. \u25cf  D. Standard therapy is intramuscular benzathine penicillin G; because penicillin is the ONLY acceptable treatment for syphilis in pregnancy, this patient must undergo confirmation of allergy, penicillin skin testing/graded challenge, and if positive, mandatory penicillin desensitization to receive penicillin G safely.  - Correct  . The first-line treatment for acquired syphilis  is  Benzathine Penicillin G  . A critical clinical rule is that  Penicillin G is the  ONLY acceptable treatment for syphilis during pregnancy  , as alternative antibiotics (such  as doxycycline) are contraindicated, and others (such as macrolides) do not cross the placenta effectively to treat the fetus. Therefore, if a pregnant patient has a documented penicillin allergy, she must undergo confirmatory skin testing/graded challenge  . If she  is confirmed to be allergic, she MUST undergo mandatory penicillin desensitization  in an inpatient setting to allow her to safely receive Penicillin G therapy. \u25cf  E. Standard therapy is topical permethrin cream; because she is allergic, she should be treated with oral ivermectin.  -  Incorrect  .  Permethrin and ivermectin treat ectoparasites, not syphilis. 251",
