    "rationale": "painful ulcer with painful inguinal adenopathy, typically treated with azithromycin; LGV is caused by Chlamydia trachomatis L1-L3, starting as a painless ulcer that develops into painful buboes (pus-filled lymph nodes) and is treated with doxycycline; Granuloma inguinale is caused by Klebsiella granulomatis, presenting as a painless, \"beefy red\" ulcer with rolled edges, diagnosed via tissue biopsy showing intracellular Donovan bodies, and treated with azithromycin. \u25cf  A. Chancroid is caused by Treponema pallidum and presents with a painless, indurated ulcer; LGV is caused by a virus and presents with genital warts; Granuloma inguinale is caused by yeast and is treated with fluconazole.  - Incorrect  . Treponema pallidum causes Syphilis, not  Chancroid. LGV is bacterial (Chlamydia), and Granuloma inguinale is bacterial (Klebsiella), not yeast. \u25cf  B. Chancroid is a painless lesion caused by a poxvirus; LGV is a painful ulcer caused by Klebsiella; Granuloma inguinale is a beefy red mass caused by Chlamydia.  -  Incorrect  . Chancroid is extremely painful  and caused by H. ducreyi; LGV is caused by Chlamydia; and Granuloma inguinale is caused by Klebsiella. \u25cf  C. Chancroid is caused by Haemophilus ducreyi and presents as a painful ulcer with painful inguinal adenopathy, typically treated with azithromycin; LGV is caused by Chlamydia trachomatis L1-L3, starting as a painless ulcer that develops into painful buboes (pus-filled lymph nodes) and is treated with doxycycline; Granuloma inguinale is caused by Klebsiella granulomatis, presenting as a painless, \"beefy red\" ulcer with rolled edges, diagnosed via tissue biopsy showing intracellular Donovan bodies, and treated with azithromycin.  -  Correct  . This represents a high-yield clinical comparison of three uncommon ulcerative STIs:  1) Chancroid (Haemophilus ducreyi)  presents as a  painful  genital ulcer with painful inguinal lymphadenopathy/buboes  , treated with azithromycin.  2) LGV (Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1, L2, L3)  starts as a tiny  painless ulcer that goes unnoticed  , which then disseminates to the inguinal lymph nodes to form  painful, pus-filled buboes (often showing the \"groove sign\")  , treated with a  multi-week course of doxycycline.  3) Granuloma Inguinale or Donovanosis (Klebsiella granulomatis)  presents as a painless, \"beefy red\" ulcer with rolled edges of vascular granulation tissue  that spreads locally; diagnosis is confirmed via tissue biopsy showing  intracellular Donovan bodies  (Gram-negative rods with a \"safety-pin\" appearance  inside macrophages), treated with azithromycin. \u25cf  D. All three are caused by different serovars of Neisseria gonorrhoeae, always present with a \"strawberry cervix\", and are treated with a single dose of ceftriaxone.  -  Incorrect  . These are highly distinct  bacterial species, do not present with a strawberry cervix (which is Trichomonas), and are not treated with ceftriaxone. \u25cf  E. All three are benign, non-infectious autoimmune dermatoses that occur only in postmenopausal women with a history of lichen sclerosus, treated with high-dose topical steroids.  -  Incorrect  . All three are infectious,  sexually transmitted diseases that occur in sexually active populations and are treated with antibiotics, not steroids. 252",
