    "rationale": "typically transmitted sexually, is harmless, and typically resolves spontaneously in 6 to 12 months (though it can take up to 4 years), with treatment being optional or sometimes causing scarring. \u25cf  A. Molluscum contagiosum, caused by a Poxvirus; in adults, it is typically transmitted sexually, is harmless, and typically resolves spontaneously in 6 to 12 months (though it can take up to 4 years), with treatment being optional or sometimes causing scarring.  -  Correct  . The presentation  of painless,  dome-shaped papules with a distinct central umbilication  that  can express a white, cheesy core is pathognomonic for  Molluscum Contagiosum  . It is caused  by a  Poxvirus  . While traditionally considered a pediatric skin disease (spread via casual contact), in adults, it is predominantly transmitted via sexual contact. It is entirely harmless and  resolves spontaneously within 6 to 12 months  (up to 4 years).  Treatment (such as cryotherapy, curettage, or topicals) is optional and often avoided because it can result in localized scarring. \u25cf  B. Condyloma acuminata, caused by HPV 16 and 18; it is a direct precursor to invasive carcinoma and requires urgent radical surgical resection.  -  Incorrect  . Condyloma acuminata are genital warts caused by HPV 6/11 and are not direct precursors to cancer, nor do they show central umbilication. \u25cf  C. Scabies, caused by the human itch mite; it presents with severe nocturnal itching and requires full-body permethrin.  -  Incorrect  .  Scabies presents as a highly pruritic rash with burrows, not firm umbilicated papules with cheesy cores. \u25cf  D. Primary syphilis, caused by Treponema pallidum; it presents as a painless chancre that progresses to tertiary neurosyphilis if untreated.  -  Incorrect  . A primary chancre is an ulcer, not multiple umbilicated papules. \u25cf  E. Chancroid, caused by Haemophilus ducreyi; it is a painful, pus-filled lesion that requires high-dose IV penicillin.  -  Incorrect  . Chancroid  is painful and treated with azithromycin.",
