    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 28-year-old woman presents with severe right-sided pelvic pain that started suddenly after sexual intercourse. She is currently on day 24 of her menstrual cycle. She reports a history of irregular periods. A pelvic ultrasound reveals a 5-cm complex ovarian cyst with internal echoes and a \"ring of fire\" appearance on color Doppler. Her serum $\\beta$-hCG is negative. She is hemodynamically stable. Which of the following best describes the origin of this specific cystic lesion?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "An unruptured graafian follicle",
      "B": "A cystic teratoma containing sebaceous fluid",
      "C": "A highly vascularized corpus luteum that failed to regress",
      "D": "An inclusion cyst formed by invaginated surface epithelium",
      "E": "A mucin-secreting epithelial neoplasm"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A complex cyst presenting in the *luteal phase* (day 24) with sudden pain (often triggered by trauma/sex due to hemorrhage into the cyst) is the classic presentation of a **Corpus Luteum Cyst**.\n- **Pathogenesis:** After normal ovulation, the follicle collapses and becomes the corpus luteum (CL), which secretes progesterone. The CL is highly vascularized (hence the classic **\"ring of fire\"** on Doppler ultrasound). \n  - If the ovum is not fertilized, the CL normally degenerates (apoptosis) into a corpus albicans. \n  - If it fails to degenerate, it can continue to grow, fill with fluid/blood (hemorrhagic cyst), and cause pain or delayed menses. If it ruptures, it can cause severe hemoperitoneum.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Unruptured follicle:** A follicular cyst. Occurs in the follicular phase (pre-ovulation) and is a *simple*, non-vascular cyst, not complex.\n  - **(B) Teratoma:** Would show calcifications (teeth/bone) and fat on ultrasound.\n  - **(D) Inclusion cyst:** A small, microscopic cyst on the ovarian surface, not a large, hemorrhagic, vascular cyst.\n  - **(E) Mucinous neoplasm:** A true tumor, typically much larger and multi-septated.",
    "pearl": "A **Corpus Luteum Cyst** occurs *after* ovulation (luteal phase). It is highly vascular (**ring of fire** on Doppler) and prone to **hemorrhage**, presenting with sudden pelvic pain.",
