    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Ovarian Surface Epithelial Tumors:** These tumors account for 70% of all ovarian tumors. The epithelium covering the ovary is derived from the coelomic epithelium (which also forms the Mullerian ducts). Therefore, these tumors can differentiate into tissue that mimics the fallopian tube, the endometrium, or the endocervix.\n- **Serous Cystadenoma:** This is the **most common benign ovarian neoplasm**. \n  - **Macroscopy:** It is typically a simple, unilocular, thin-walled cyst filled with clear, watery (serous) fluid.\n  - **Microscopy:** It is lined by a single layer of **tall, ciliated, columnar epithelium** that perfectly mimics the normal lining of the **fallopian tube**.\n  - Because it is a benign adenoma (not an adenocarcinoma), there is no nuclear atypia, no invasion, and no complex solid growth.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Mature cystic teratoma:** Contains all 3 germ layers (skin, hair, teeth, sebum), not a simple serous fluid cyst lined by fallopian-type epithelium.\n  - **(C) Mucinous cystadenoma:** Lined by mucus-secreting, non-ciliated columnar cells that mimic the *endocervix* or *intestine*. It is usually multilocular and filled with thick, gelatinous mucin.\n  - **(D) Endometrioma:** Lined by endometrial glands and stroma, filled with old blood (\"chocolate cyst\").\n  - **(E) Brenner tumor:** A solid, pale yellow tumor containing nests of *transitional* epithelium (urothelium) surrounded by dense fibrous stroma (\"coffee bean\" nuclei).",
    "pearl": "A **Serous Cystadenoma** is the most common benign ovarian tumor. It is a unilocular cyst filled with watery fluid and lined by **ciliated columnar epithelium** resembling the **fallopian tube**.",
