    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** This is a classic presentation of a **Mucinous Cystadenoma**.\n- **Key Characteristics:** \n  - **Size:** They are famously capable of growing to absolutely massive proportions (often $>20\\text{ cm}$, weighing many kilograms).\n  - **Macroscopy:** Unlike serous tumors (which are usually unilocular and watery), mucinous tumors are characteristically **multilocular** (honeycombed with many septations) and filled with thick, viscous, gelatinous **mucin**.\n  - **Microscopy:** They are lined by a single layer of tall, **mucin-secreting, non-ciliated columnar cells**. The cells look exactly like the epithelium of the **endocervix** or the **intestinal mucosa** (goblet cells). Because it is a benign adenoma, the cells are well-differentiated with basally located, non-atypical nuclei.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Serous cystadenoma:** Unilocular, watery fluid, lined by *ciliated* fallopian-like cells. Rarely grows to 25 cm.\n  - **(B) Dysgerminoma:** A malignant germ cell tumor. It is a solid, fleshy tumor composed of \"fried egg\" cells, not a mucin-filled multilocular cyst.\n  - **(D) Krukenberg tumor:** A metastatic tumor from the GI tract (usually stomach) to the ovaries. It features mucin-secreting *signet ring cells* invading the stroma, and is usually a solid, bilateral mass, not a massive benign cyst.\n  - **(E) Fibroma:** A benign sex cord-stromal tumor. It is completely solid, hard, and white (made of fibroblasts), not cystic.",
