    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** This is the classic histological description of a **Brenner Tumor**.\n- **Characteristics of a Brenner Tumor:**\n  - It is a relatively rare, benign ovarian surface epithelial tumor.\n  - **Macroscopy:** Unlike the cystadenomas, Brenner tumors are almost always **solid**, firm, and pale yellow (resembling a fibroma).\n  - **Microscopy:** The absolute hallmark is the presence of nests of epithelial cells that perfectly resemble **transitional epithelium (urothelium)**, exactly like the lining of the bladder. These epithelial nests are surrounded by a dense, fibrous stroma.\n  - The nuclei of these epithelial cells frequently have a deep longitudinal groove, famously giving them the appearance of a **\"coffee bean.\"** (Note: Granulosa cell tumors *also* have coffee bean nuclei, but they secrete estrogen and cause bleeding; Brenner tumors are solid epithelial tumors that are usually asymptomatic).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Fallopian tube:** Mimicked by Serous tumors.\n  - **(B) Endometrial:** Mimicked by Endometrioid tumors (usually malignant).\n  - **(D) Intestinal/Endocervical:** Mimicked by Mucinous tumors.\n  - **(E) Squamous:** Found in Mature Cystic Teratomas (dermoid cysts), which contain skin/hair.",
