    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Benign vs. Malignant Serous Tumors:** \n  - **Benign (Cystadenoma):** Smooth, flat, single layer of ciliated cells. No atypia.\n  - **Malignant (Cystadenocarcinoma):** Characterized by complex, branching **papillary projections**, solid growth, severe nuclear atypia, invasion into the cyst wall, and the presence of **Psammoma bodies** (concentric calcifications).\n- **Genetics of High-Grade Serous Carcinoma:** This is the most common and most lethal ovarian cancer. The absolute hallmark genetic defect, present in over 95% of high-grade serous carcinomas, is a mutation in the **TP53 tumor suppressor gene**. \n  - (Note: Mutations in BRCA1 and BRCA2 massively increase the inherited *risk* of developing this exact tumor, but in the actual tumor cells themselves, a p53 mutation is the defining, universal molecular driver of high-grade serous carcinoma). \n  - *(Conversely, Low-grade serous carcinomas arise stepwise from benign cystadenomas and are driven by KRAS or BRAF mutations).*\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) KRAS & (B) BRAF:** Associated with *low-grade* serous carcinomas and mucinous carcinomas, not the classic high-grade serous carcinoma. (KRAS is also huge in pancreatic/colon cancer).\n  - **(D) c-MYC:** Burkitt lymphoma.\n  - **(E) RET:** Medullary thyroid cancer and MEN 2 syndromes.",
    "pearl": "**High-grade serous cystadenocarcinoma** is the most common malignant ovarian tumor. It features complex **papillae** and **psammoma bodies**, and is almost universally driven by a **TP53** mutation.",
