    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 35-year-old woman is diagnosed with a massive mucinous cystadenoma of the ovary. During surgical excision, the massive cyst accidentally ruptures, spilling large amounts of thick, gelatinous mucin into her peritoneal cavity. The surgeon immediately and aggressively washes out the abdominal cavity to prevent the implantation of mucin-secreting cells onto the peritoneal surfaces. If these cells were to successfully implant and continue secreting mucin, what severe clinical syndrome would result?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Meigs Syndrome",
      "B": "Pseudomyxoma Peritonei",
      "C": "Fitz-Hugh-Curtis Syndrome",
      "D": "Carcinoid Syndrome",
      "E": "Sheehan Syndrome"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **The Complication:** When a mucinous tumor ruptures and spills its contents into the abdomen, the mucin-secreting epithelial cells can implant on the peritoneum and viscera. They continue to grow and secrete massive amounts of jelly-like mucin, eventually filling the entire abdominal cavity with a thick, gelatinous ascites. This catastrophic condition is called **Pseudomyxoma Peritonei** (often referred to as \"jelly belly\").\n- **The Nuance:** While it *can* occasionally happen from a ruptured primary ovarian mucinous tumor, the vast majority of cases of Pseudomyxoma Peritonei actually originate from a **primary mucinous tumor of the Appendix** that ruptured and secondarily metastasized to the ovaries and peritoneum. (If a patient has bilateral mucinous ovarian tumors and pseudomyxoma peritonei, always check the appendix!).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Meigs Syndrome:** The triad of an ovarian Fibroma, ascites, and a right-sided pleural effusion. The fluid is transudative (watery), not thick mucin.\n  - **(C) Fitz-Hugh-Curtis:** RUQ pain and \"violin-string\" adhesions around the liver caused by PID (Chlamydia/Gonorrhea).\n  - **(D) Carcinoid Syndrome:** Flushing, diarrhea, right-sided heart failure caused by serotonin secretion from a metastatic carcinoid tumor.\n  - **(E) Sheehan Syndrome:** Postpartum pituitary necrosis due to massive hemorrhage.",
    "pearl": "**Pseudomyxoma Peritonei** (\"jelly belly\") is the massive accumulation of mucin in the abdomen. It is classically caused by a ruptured mucinous tumor of the **Appendix**, which often metastasizes to the ovaries.",
