    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 58-year-old woman is diagnosed with advanced, stage III high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma. Following surgical debulking, she is initiated on systemic chemotherapy. Her regimen includes a platinum-based agent (Carboplatin) and a taxane (Paclitaxel). How does Paclitaxel exert its cytotoxic effect on the rapidly dividing ovarian cancer cells?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It cross-links DNA strands, preventing DNA replication",
      "B": "It inhibits topoisomerase II, causing DNA strand breaks",
      "C": "It binds to tubulin and hyper-stabilizes polymerized microtubules, preventing the breakdown of the mitotic spindle",
      "D": "It acts as a fake pyrimidine, competitively inhibiting thymidylate synthase",
      "E": "It binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, halting protein synthesis"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Pharmacology of Taxanes (Paclitaxel):** The standard of care chemotherapy for ovarian cancer is a combination of a Platinum drug (Carboplatin/Cisplatin) and a Taxane (Paclitaxel).\n- **Mechanism of Paclitaxel:** Taxanes are **microtubule poisons**. \n  - Unlike Vinca alkaloids (Vincristine) which prevent microtubules from forming, Taxanes do the exact opposite. \n  - Paclitaxel binds to beta-tubulin and **hyper-stabilizes** the polymerized microtubules. It forces them to remain assembled and prevents them from breaking down (depolymerizing).\n  - During normal mitosis (anaphase), the mitotic spindle *must* depolymerize (shorten) in order to pull the sister chromatids apart into the two new daughter cells. Because Paclitaxel locks the spindle in place, the chromosomes cannot be separated. The cancer cell becomes permanently arrested in anaphase/metaphase and undergoes apoptosis. (Mnemonic: **Taxes** **stabilize** society / **Taxanes stabilize** microtubules).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Cross-links DNA:** This is the mechanism of the Platinum agents (Cisplatin/Carboplatin) used in her regimen, not Paclitaxel.\n  - **(B) Topoisomerase II inhibitor:** Etoposide.\n  - **(D) Fake pyrimidine:** 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU).\n  - **(E) 50S inhibitor:** Macrolide antibiotics (Azithromycin).",
    "pearl": "**Paclitaxel (Taxanes)** kills cancer cells by binding to tubulin and **hyper-stabilizing microtubules**, preventing the breakdown of the mitotic spindle during anaphase. (Cisplatin cross-links DNA).",
