    "stem": "During a pathologic review of a serous cystadenocarcinoma, the attending physician points out numerous psammoma bodies scattered throughout the papillary tumor stroma. The physician asks the medical student to name three other distinct neoplasms in the human body that are classically characterized by the presence of these same psammoma bodies. Which of the following lists is entirely correct?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Medullary thyroid carcinoma, meningioma, mesothelioma",
      "B": "Papillary thyroid carcinoma, meningioma, mesothelioma",
      "C": "Follicular thyroid carcinoma, glioblastoma, clear cell renal carcinoma",
      "D": "Papillary thyroid carcinoma, prolactinoma, melanoma",
      "E": "Small cell lung carcinoma, meningioma, mesothelioma"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Psammoma Bodies:** These are microscopic, concentric, laminated, whorled calcifications. They form when a single neoplastic cell dies (dystrophic calcification) and layers of calcium salts build up around it like a pearl. They are classically seen in tumors with **papillary** architecture (the tiny blood vessels in the papillae easily infarct, causing the tip to die and calcify).\n- **The Classic Four:** There are four major tumors heavily tested on the USMLE that feature Psammoma bodies. (Mnemonic: **PSaMMoma**):\n  1. **P**apillary carcinoma of the thyroid\n  2. **S**erous cystadenocarcinoma of the ovary\n  3. **M**eningioma (brain tumor)\n  4. **M**esothelioma (pleural tumor from asbestos)\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Medullary thyroid:** Features *Amyloid* stroma (calcitonin), not psammoma bodies.\n  - **(C) Follicular thyroid / Glioblastoma:** Do not have psammoma bodies.\n  - **(D) Prolactinoma / Melanoma:** Do not have psammoma bodies.\n  - **(E) Small cell lung:** Features neuroendocrine granules, not psammoma bodies.",
    "pearl": "**Psammoma bodies** are concentric calcifications found in 4 major tumors (**PSaMMoma**): **P**apillary thyroid carcinoma, **S**erous ovarian carcinoma, **M**eningioma, and **M**esothelioma.",
