    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** A solid ovarian mass in a female teenager/young adult with elevated **LDH** and classic histology is a **Dysgerminoma**.\n- **Tumor Characteristics:**\n  - It is a malignant germ cell tumor. (It is the exact female equivalent of the male testicular Seminoma).\n  - **Tumor Markers:** It classically produces **LDH**. (In about 5-10% of cases, syncytiotrophoblast giant cells are present, which can cause a *slight* elevation in hCG, but LDH is the primary marker). AFP is strictly normal.\n  - **Histology (The Hallmark):** The tumor is composed of sheets of large cells with clear cytoplasm and central nuclei (often described as **\"fried egg\" cells**). Crucially, the nests of tumor cells are separated by thin fibrous bands that are heavily infiltrated with **lymphocytes**.\n  - **Prognosis:** Despite being malignant, it is exquisitely sensitive to radiotherapy and chemotherapy, carrying an excellent prognosis.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Yolk sac tumor:** Marker is AFP. Histology shows Schiller-Duval bodies.\n  - **(C) Choriocarcinoma:** Marker is massively elevated hCG. Highly aggressive.\n  - **(D) Granulosa cell tumor:** Secretes estrogen. Histology shows Call-Exner bodies.\n  - **(E) Serous cystadenocarcinoma:** Occurs in older/post-menopausal women. Marker is CA-125. Histology shows papillae/psammoma bodies.",
    "pearl": "A **Dysgerminoma** is the most common malignant germ cell tumor in young females. Histology shows **\"fried egg\" cells** and fibrous septa with **lymphocytes**. The classic tumor marker is **LDH**.",
