    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** An ovarian mass in a young child/infant with massively elevated **AFP** and classic \"glomeruloid\" histology is a **Yolk Sac Tumor** (also known as an Endodermal Sinus Tumor).\n- **Tumor Characteristics:** \n  - It is a malignant germ cell tumor that mimics the extra-embryonic yolk sac. Because it is the most common germ cell tumor in young children, it is the classic diagnosis for a toddler with an ovarian (or testicular) mass.\n  - **Tumor Marker:** The yolk sac normally produces AFP. The tumor produces massive amounts of **Alpha-Fetoprotein (AFP)**.\n  - **Histology (The Hallmark):** The absolute defining histologic feature is the **Schiller-Duval body**. This is a structure where a central capillary is lined by tumor cells, sitting inside a cystic space. Under the microscope, it looks exactly like a primitive **glomerulus** in the kidney. \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Dysgerminoma:** Occurs in teens/young adults. Marker is LDH. \"Fried egg\" cells.\n  - **(B) Choriocarcinoma:** Marker is hCG. Mimics placental tissue.\n  - **(D) Immature teratoma:** Contains immature fetal tissue (usually neuroectoderm/neural tubes). Malignant, but no specific marker or Schiller-Duval bodies.\n  - **(E) Embryonal carcinoma:** Very rare in ovaries (common in testes). Highly aggressive, hemorrhagic, elevated hCG and AFP, but lacks Schiller-Duval bodies.",
    "pearl": "A **Yolk Sac Tumor** is an aggressive malignancy seen in young children/toddlers. It secretes high levels of **AFP**. The histologic hallmark is the **Schiller-Duval body**, which looks like a primitive **glomerulus**.",
