    "stem": "A 23-year-old woman is evaluated for hemoptysis (coughing up blood) and shortness of breath. A chest X-ray reveals multiple bilateral lung nodules (\"cannonball metastases\"). She had a spontaneous miscarriage three months ago and underwent a D&C. Her current serum $\\beta$-hCG level is $>1,000,000\\text{ mIU/mL}$. A biopsy of one of the lung nodules reveals a biphasic proliferation of malignant cytotrophoblasts and syncytiotrophoblasts, completely lacking chorionic villi. What is the most common route by which this aggressive tumor metastasized from her uterus to her lungs?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Direct contiguous extension through the diaphragm",
      "B": "Hematogenous spread via the venous system",
      "C": "Lymphatic spread to the para-aortic lymph nodes and thoracic duct",
      "D": "Seeding of the peritoneal cavity (transcoelomic spread)",
      "E": "Aspiration of tumor cells during her previous D&C procedure"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** The patient has a **Choriocarcinoma**, a highly malignant germ cell tumor composed of trophoblastic tissue (cytotrophoblasts and syncytiotrophoblasts) that completely **lacks chorionic villi**. \n  - It can arise from a normal pregnancy, a miscarriage, or most commonly, a molar pregnancy.\n  - The tumor marker is astronomically high **$\\beta$-hCG** (produced by the syncytiotrophoblasts).\n- **Metastasis:** Trophoblastic tissue is biologically programmed to aggressively invade blood vessels (that's how a normal placenta accesses the maternal blood supply). \n  - Because it is so fundamentally angiocentric, Choriocarcinoma spreads incredibly early and rapidly via **hematogenous (blood-borne) dissemination**.\n  - The classic first site of metastasis is the **Lungs** (presenting as hemoptysis and \"cannonball\" nodules on CXR). It can also spread to the brain and liver. \n  - Despite being widely metastatic at presentation, gestational choriocarcinoma is highly sensitive to chemotherapy (Methotrexate) and is often curable. (Non-gestational choriocarcinoma arising primarily in the ovary is much more resistant).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Direct extension:** Ovarian/Uterine cancers don't reach the lungs by growing through the diaphragm.\n  - **(C) Lymphatic spread:** This is the standard route for *epithelial* carcinomas (like cervical or serous ovarian cancer), but Choriocarcinoma uniquely prefers the blood.\n  - **(D) Peritoneal seeding:** This is how epithelial ovarian cancer spreads (causing ascites/omental caking), but not how tumors reach the deep lung parenchyma.\n  - **(E) Aspiration:** Not a mechanism for cancer metastasis.",
    "pearl": "**Choriocarcinoma** is a highly malignant tumor of trophoblasts that lacks villi and secretes massive **$\\beta$-hCG**. It aggressively invades blood vessels, causing early **hematogenous metastasis**, classically to the **Lungs**.",
