    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Immature vs. Mature Teratoma:** \n  - A *mature* teratoma (dermoid cyst) contains fully differentiated, adult-type tissues (skin, hair, bone) and is benign.\n  - An *immature* teratoma is a malignant, solid tumor that contains primitive, embryonic-like tissues.\n- **Grading:** The defining feature of an immature teratoma, and the sole tissue upon which the histologic grade (and thus the prognosis and treatment) is based, is the presence and quantity of **primitive neuroepithelium (neuroectoderm)**. \n  - Under the microscope, this looks like primitive neural tubes (rosettes of small, dark, hyperchromatic cells). \n  - A Grade 1 tumor has rare neuroepithelium; a Grade 3 tumor has abundant neuroepithelium. The higher the grade, the higher the risk of aggressive behavior and metastasis.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A), (C), (D):** While immature cartilage, thyroid, or muscle *can* be present in the tumor, the official grading system relies entirely on the neuroepithelium.\n  - **(E) Endodermal sinus:** These are Schiller-Duval bodies, found in Yolk Sac Tumors, not teratomas. (Though germ cell tumors can be mixed).",
    "pearl": "An **Immature Teratoma** is a malignant germ cell tumor. Its histologic grade and malignant potential are determined specifically by the amount of **primitive neuroepithelium (neuroectoderm)** present.",
