    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** A unilateral ovarian mass in a postmenopausal woman presenting with signs of estrogen excess (postmenopausal bleeding, endometrial hyperplasia, breast tenderness) is the absolute classic presentation of a **Granulosa Cell Tumor**.\n- **Tumor Characteristics:** This is the most common malignant Sex Cord-Stromal Tumor. \n  - Granulosa cells are the cells in the normal ovary that convert androgens to **Estrogen** (via aromatase). The tumor autonomously hypersecretes massive amounts of Estrogen.\n  - This unopposed estrogen causes the endometrial hyperplasia (and potentially endometrial cancer) and the vaginal bleeding. (In young girls, it causes precocious puberty).\n  - **Tumor Marker:** Inhibin.\n- **Histology (The Hallmark):** The cells arrange themselves in small rosettes around pink, eosinophilic fluid, mimicking a developing follicle. These are called **Call-Exner bodies**. The cells themselves have longitudinal grooves, famously called **\"coffee bean\" nuclei** (similar to a Brenner tumor).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Progesterone:** Secreted by the corpus luteum, not typically by Granulosa cell tumors.\n  - **(C) Testosterone:** Secreted by Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors (causes virilization/hirsutism, not bleeding/breast tenderness).\n  - **(D) AFP:** Yolk sac tumors.\n  - **(E) CA-125:** Surface epithelial tumors (Serous Cystadenocarcinoma), which do not secrete estrogen.",
    "pearl": "A **Granulosa Cell Tumor** is an ovarian mass that secretes massive amounts of **Estrogen**, causing postmenopausal bleeding and endometrial hyperplasia. Histology shows **Call-Exner bodies** and **coffee bean nuclei**.",
