    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The rapid onset of defeminization (amenorrhea) followed by severe, aggressive **virilization/masculinization** (deep voice, clitoromegaly, temporal balding, severe hirsutism) strongly points to a massive, autonomous source of **Androgens (Testosterone)**. \n- **Diagnosis:** In a young woman with a solid ovarian mass, this is the classic presentation of a **Sertoli-Leydig Cell Tumor**. \n  - These are rare Sex Cord-Stromal tumors containing cells that normally belong in the male testicle. The Leydig cells produce the testosterone.\n- **Histology:** The absolute diagnostic hallmark of Leydig cells (whether in a normal male testicle or in a female ovarian tumor) is the presence of **Reinke crystals**. These are rod-shaped, pink, eosinophilic crystals found within the cytoplasm of the Leydig cells.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Call-Exner bodies:** Granulosa cell tumor (secretes estrogen, causing bleeding, not virilization).\n  - **(B) Schiller-Duval bodies:** Yolk sac tumor (secretes AFP).\n  - **(D) Psammoma bodies:** Serous cystadenocarcinoma.\n  - **(E) Signet ring cells:** Krukenberg tumor (gastric metastasis).",
    "pearl": "A **Sertoli-Leydig Cell Tumor** is an ovarian mass that secretes **Testosterone**, causing rapid **virilization** (clitoromegaly, deep voice). The histologic hallmark is the **Reinke crystal**.",
