    "rationale": "- **Pharmacology of Overflow Incontinence:** Because the detrusor muscle is underactive and failing to contract, the goal is to stimulate the parasympathetic nervous system to force a contraction.\n- **The Drug:** **Bethanechol** is a direct-acting muscarinic (cholinergic) agonist. \n  - It binds directly to the M3 receptors on the detrusor muscle, stimulating a strong contraction. \n  - It is classically used to treat non-obstructive urinary retention (post-operative urinary retention, or neurogenic bladder atony). (Mnemonic: **Bethany Call** me to pee).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Oxybutynin & (C) Tolterodine:** These are Antimuscarinics. They block the M3 receptor and *relax* the bladder. Used for Urge Incontinence. Giving this to a patient with urinary retention would make it catastrophically worse.\n  - **(D) Mirabegron:** A Beta-3 agonist. Relaxes the bladder. Used for Urge Incontinence.\n  - **(E) Pseudoephedrine:** Alpha-1 agonist. Contracts the internal sphincter, making it harder to pee.",
    "pearl": "**Bethanechol** is a muscarinic agonist that forces the bladder to contract. It is used to treat non-obstructive urinary retention (**Overflow Incontinence**).",
