    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Imipramine for Incontinence:** While not often used first-line today due to systemic side effects, Imipramine (a classic TCA) is highly tested on boards for its unique dual pharmacology in the bladder.\n- **The Dual Mechanism:**\n  1. **Anticholinergic Activity:** Like many TCAs, Imipramine has strong, systemic anticholinergic properties. It blocks M3 receptors on the detrusor, relaxing the bladder and treating the **Urge Incontinence**.\n  2. **Alpha-1 Adrenergic Activity:** Imipramine blocks the reuptake of norepinephrine, leading to increased norepinephrine at the synaptic cleft. This stimulates the Alpha-1 receptors on the internal urethral sphincter, causing it to tightly contract and close, treating the **Stress Incontinence**.\n- **Clinical Application:** Because it hits both targets, it was classically used for bed-wetting (enuresis) in children and mixed incontinence in adults.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Muscarinic agonist:** Would cause the bladder to contract (worsening urge incontinence).\n  - **(B) Beta-3 agonist:** Mirabegron (treats urge only).\n  - **(D) Relaxing sphincter:** Would worsen stress incontinence.",
    "pearl": "**Imipramine** (a TCA) treats mixed urinary incontinence because it has strong **anticholinergic** effects (relaxes the bladder/treats Urge) and increases norepinephrine to stimulate **alpha-1 receptors** (contracts the sphincter/treats Stress).",
