    "topic": "Lecture 3.1: Infections of the Genital Tract Part 1",
    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 32-year-old pregnant woman at 16 weeks gestation is diagnosed with symptomatic Bacterial Vaginosis. The physician prescribes a 7-day course of oral Metronidazole. The patient calls the office two days later, panicking because she experienced severe nausea, vomiting, flushing, and a pounding headache after attending a wedding. She admits to drinking two glasses of champagne. Which of the following best describes the pharmacologic mechanism responsible for her severe adverse reaction?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Irreversible inhibition of cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1)",
      "B": "Inhibition of acetaldehyde dehydrogenase",
      "C": "Massive release of histamine from mast cells (Red Man Syndrome)",
      "D": "Blockade of the serotonin 5-HT3 receptor in the chemoreceptor trigger zone",
      "E": "Direct hepatotoxicity causing acute fulminant liver failure"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient experienced a severe **Disulfiram-like reaction** (flushing, nausea, vomiting, headache, hypotension) after consuming alcohol while taking **Metronidazole**.\n- **Pharmacology of Metronidazole:** Metronidazole is the first-line treatment for BV (and Trichomonas). It works by forming toxic free radical metabolites in the bacterial cell that damage DNA (bactericidal against anaerobes).\n- **The Adverse Effect:** Metronidazole strongly inhibits the enzyme **Acetaldehyde Dehydrogenase** in the liver. \n  - Normally, alcohol (ethanol) is metabolized to acetaldehyde, which is then quickly converted by acetaldehyde dehydrogenase into harmless acetic acid. \n  - When the enzyme is blocked by Metronidazole, **acetaldehyde rapidly accumulates** in the blood. Acetaldehyde is highly toxic and causes the severe, immediate symptoms of a hangover/disulfiram reaction. \n  - Patients MUST be counseled to avoid all alcohol (including mouthwashes and cough syrups) while taking Metronidazole and for 72 hours after finishing the course.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) COX-1 inhibition:** Aspirin/NSAIDs.\n  - **(C) Histamine release:** Vancomycin (Red Man Syndrome), caused by rapid IV infusion.\n  - **(D) 5-HT3 blockade:** Ondansetron (Zofran), an antiemetic that *prevents* nausea.\n  - **(E) Hepatotoxicity:** Acetaminophen overdose or Isoniazid, not the mechanism of the acute alcohol reaction with metronidazole.",
    "pearl": "**Metronidazole** (used for BV and Trichomonas) causes a severe **disulfiram-like reaction** if taken with alcohol due to the inhibition of **acetaldehyde dehydrogenase**.",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Lecture_3_1__Infections_of_the_Genital_T_1",
        "text": "Explain the risk factors, etiologic agent, clinical presentation, diagnostic work-up, first-line therapies, and prevention strategies for Bacterial Vaginosis."
      }
    ]
