    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** The classic presentation of **Trichomoniasis** includes:\n  - Profuse, **frothy, yellow-green**, foul-smelling discharge.\n  - Intense vaginal inflammation.\n  - **Strawberry cervix** (punctate cervical petechiae/hemorrhages), seen in $\\sim 20\\%$ of cases.\n  - Elevated pH ($> 4.5$, often quite high like 6.0).\n  - Wet mount shows **motile, flagellated protozoa** (*Trichomonas vaginalis*).\n- **Management:** Trichomonas is a highly contagious true Sexually Transmitted Infection (STI). \n  - The treatment of choice is a single dose of **oral Metronidazole** (or Tinidazole).\n  - **CRITICAL STEP:** Because it is an STI, if you only treat the patient, her partner will immediately re-infect her the next time they have sex (the \"ping-pong\" effect). Therefore, you MUST **treat the patient AND all of her sexual partners simultaneously**.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Fluconazole (patient only):** Treats Candida (yeast).\n  - **(B) Ceftriaxone + Azithromycin (both):** Treats Gonorrhea and Chlamydia.\n  - **(D) Metronidazole (patient only):** Treats Bacterial Vaginosis (BV is not strictly an STI, so partners are usually not treated for BV). But for Trichomonas, you MUST treat the partner.\n  - **(E) Topical clotrimazole:** Treats Candida.",
