    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Complications of Trichomoniasis:** While Trichomonas itself is easily treated and not usually life-threatening, it has massive public health implications.\n- **The Link to HIV:** The *Trichomonas* protozoan physically attaches to the vaginal and cervical squamous epithelium and destroys the cells (causing the intense inflammation and the punctate hemorrhages seen as a \"strawberry cervix\"). \n  - This widespread mucosal destruction and local hemorrhage completely destroys the physical barrier of the vagina.\n  - Furthermore, the inflammation recruits thousands of CD4+ T-cells (target cells for HIV) to the area.\n  - As a result, a woman with active Trichomoniasis has a **2 to 3-fold increased risk of acquiring HIV** if exposed, and a much higher risk of transmitting it if she is already positive. This is why aggressive diagnosis and treatment of Trichomonas is a cornerstone of HIV prevention.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Transports viruses:** It does not act as a Trojan horse for HIV.\n  - **(C) Destroys CD4 cells:** HIV destroys CD4 cells; Trichomonas *attracts* them to the surface, making them vulnerable targets.\n  - **(D) Metronidazole/HSV:** Metronidazole does not trigger HSV outbreaks.\n  - **(E) Cervical cancer toxin:** HPV causes cervical cancer, not Trichomonas.",
