    "rationale": "- **Virulence of N. gonorrhoeae:**\n  - **Attachment:** The absolute most critical first step for the bacteria is attaching to the non-ciliated columnar epithelial cells of the cervix. They accomplish this using long, hair-like appendages called **Pili (fimbriae)**.\n  - **Immune Evasion:** The pili are made of pilin proteins. The bacteria possess dozens of silent pilin genes. Through a process called **antigenic variation** (specifically, homologous recombination), they constantly shuffle these genes, continually changing the amino acid sequence (and thus the physical shape) of the pili on their surface. \n  - **Result:** By the time the host's B-cells make neutralizing antibodies against the pili, the bacteria have already changed the shape of the pili, rendering the antibodies useless. This is why a person can get Gonorrhea over and over again, and why we cannot make a vaccine.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Capsule:** *N. meningitidis* has a capsule (which is why we have a vaccine for it). *N. gonorrhoeae* is NOT encapsulated.\n  - **(B) Exotoxin A:** Produced by *Pseudomonas*.\n  - **(D) IgA protease:** *N. gonorrhoeae* does produce this (to chop up mucosal IgA antibodies), but it is the *pili* that undergo massive antigenic variation.\n  - **(E) LOS:** This acts as an endotoxin causing intense inflammation and sepsis, but the pili are responsible for attachment and antigenic variation.",
    "pearl": "*Neisseria gonorrhoeae* uses **Pili** to attach to the mucosa. The pili undergo massive **antigenic variation**, which prevents the host from developing lasting immunity and thwarts vaccine development.",
