    "rationale": "- **The Finding:** The patient has exquisite pain upon lateral movement of the cervix, known as **Cervical Motion Tenderness (CMT)** or the \"Chandelier Sign\" (because the patient jumps up to grab the chandelier in pain).\n- **The Diagnosis:** CMT is the hallmark, defining physical exam sign of **Acute Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID)**.\n- **The Pathophysiology:** The bacteria (Chlamydia/Gonorrhea) have ascended past the cervix, through the uterus, and heavily infected the **fallopian tubes (salpingitis)** and the surrounding peritoneal connective tissue (the broad ligament). \n  - The cervix is physically anchored to the uterus, which is anchored to the fallopian tubes and broad ligaments. \n  - When the physician moves the cervix, it acts like a joystick, yanking on the severely inflamed, swollen, pus-filled fallopian tubes and ligaments deep in the pelvis. This stretching of the inflamed tissue causes agonizing pain.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Ruptured cyst:** Causes pain, but moving the cervix doesn't specifically stretch the ovary as directly as it stretches the tubes/ligaments.\n  - **(C) Ischemic necrosis:** Cervicitis causes inflammation, not ischemic necrosis.\n  - **(D) Pubococcygeus spasm:** Causes vaginismus (pain with insertion), not CMT.\n  - **(E) Dilation:** An incomplete abortion causes cramping pain, but CMT is the classic sign of ascending infection (PID).",
