    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A young woman with a history of PID (Chlamydia/Gonorrhea) presenting with severe **Right Upper Quadrant (RUQ) pain**, fever, and referred pain to the right shoulder.\n- **Pathogenesis:** This is **Fitz-Hugh-Curtis Syndrome (Perihepatitis)**, a classic complication of severe PID. \n  - The purulent, infected fluid from the fallopian tubes leaks out into the peritoneal cavity and travels up the right paracolic gutter to pool around the liver. \n  - This causes an intense inflammation of the liver capsule (Glisson's capsule). As it heals, it forms dense, stringy fibrotic adhesions between the liver capsule and the anterior abdominal wall or diaphragm.\n- **Diagnosis:** Laparoscopy (or sometimes ultrasound/CT) shows the classic **\"violin string\" adhesions**. Liver function tests (AST/ALT) are typically *normal* because the infection is strictly on the *capsule* of the liver, not destroying the liver parenchyma itself.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Acute cholecystitis:** The ultrasound showed a normal gallbladder without stones.\n  - **(C) Ascending cholangitis:** Would present with jaundice, extremely high alk phos/bilirubin, and a dilated common bile duct.\n  - **(D) Appendicitis:** Usually RLQ pain, though it can point upward; the \"string adhesions\" to the liver are pathognomonic for Fitz-Hugh-Curtis.\n  - **(E) Hepatic adenoma:** Associated with OCP use; causes massive intraperitoneal bleeding/shock if ruptured.",
    "pearl": "**Fitz-Hugh-Curtis Syndrome** is a complication of PID (Chlamydia/Gonorrhea). The infection spreads to the liver capsule, causing **RUQ pain** and forming classic **\"violin string\" adhesions** between the liver and the abdominal wall.",
