    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Scenario:** The patient has a **Tubo-Ovarian Abscess (TOA)** that has likely ruptured or is leaking (evidenced by the rigid, board-like abdomen and rebound tenderness, indicating acute peritonitis/sepsis). She has also failed medical therapy (worsening despite 48 hours of IV antibiotics).\n- **Management:** A ruptured or failing TOA is an absolute **surgical emergency**. The pocket of pus is walled off, meaning the IV antibiotics cannot penetrate it. \n  - Source control is mandatory to save the patient's life from septic shock. \n  - The standard of care is **immediate drainage**. Today, this is often done via Interventional Radiology (ultrasound or CT-guided percutaneous/transvaginal drainage). If that is not possible or the patient is highly unstable, open or laparoscopic surgery (to wash out the abdomen and remove the necrotic tissue) is required.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Oral Azithromycin:** Downgrading therapy in a crashing septic patient will be fatal.\n  - **(C) BSO:** While sometimes necessary to remove a completely necrotic, destroyed ovary/tube, the first step in a young woman is to attempt to just drain the pus and salvage the organs if possible. You do not jump to permanent castration unless absolutely forced to.\n  - **(D) Corticosteroids:** Immunosuppressive; will accelerate the overwhelming sepsis.\n  - **(E) Observe:** Delaying source control in a failing, peritonitic patient will lead to death.",
    "pearl": "A **Tubo-Ovarian Abscess (TOA)** that ruptures (acute peritonitis) or fails to improve on IV antibiotics is a medical emergency requiring **immediate surgical or image-guided drainage**.",
