    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** The patient meets the minimal clinical criteria for **mild-to-moderate PID** (lower abdominal pain, new sexual partners, and cervical motion tenderness on exam). \n- **Treatment Strategy:** PID can cause devastating permanent damage (infertility) very quickly. Therefore, the absolute rule is to **treat empirically and immediately**. You NEVER wait for the lab results (NAAT) to come back before starting antibiotics.\n- **Outpatient vs. Inpatient:** Because she is hemodynamically stable, has mild symptoms, is not vomiting (can take pills), is not pregnant, and does not have an abscess, she can safely be treated as an **outpatient**. \n- **The Regimen:** The infection is often polymicrobial (Gonorrhea, Chlamydia, plus vaginal anaerobes). The standard outpatient regimen is:\n  1. **Ceftriaxone** (IM single dose) - covers Gonorrhea.\n  2. **Doxycycline** (oral for 14 days) - covers Chlamydia.\n  3. **Metronidazole** (oral for 14 days) - added to cover anaerobes (frequently involved in PID).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Admit for IV antibiotics:** Criteria for admission include pregnancy, severe nausea/vomiting, high fever, failing outpatient therapy, or presence of a TOA. She doesn't meet these.\n  - **(C) Fluconazole:** Treats yeast, not PID.\n  - **(D) Wait for results:** Never delay treatment for suspected PID; the risk of permanent tubal scarring is too high.\n  - **(E) Laparoscopy:** The gold standard for *definitive* diagnosis, but rarely performed for mild cases; clinical diagnosis and immediate empiric antibiotics are the standard of care.",
