    "stem": "A 25-year-old woman is diagnosed with primary genital herpes after presenting with painful vesicular lesions on her labia. The physician prescribes a 10-day course of oral Acyclovir. How does Acyclovir exert its antiviral effect to shorten the duration of the herpes outbreak?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It directly destroys the viral lipid envelope, neutralizing the virus",
      "B": "It inhibits the viral protease, preventing the cleavage of viral polyproteins",
      "C": "It requires activation by a viral thymidine kinase, and then acts as a chain terminator to inhibit viral DNA polymerase",
      "D": "It binds to the host cell CD4 receptor, blocking viral entry",
      "E": "It acts as a reverse transcriptase inhibitor"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Pharmacology of Acyclovir (and Valacyclovir/Famciclovir):** These are the cornerstone drugs for treating HSV and VZV infections.\n- **The Mechanism:** Acyclovir is a guanosine analog. It is administered as a prodrug.\n  1. **Activation:** It enters the infected cell, but to become active, it MUST be phosphorylated. The very first phosphate group can ONLY be added by a specific viral enzyme called **Viral Thymidine Kinase**. (This is brilliant because the drug is only activated inside cells that are actively infected with the virus; it remains harmless in healthy cells).\n  2. **Inhibition:** The host cell enzymes add two more phosphates. The active drug (Acyclovir triphosphate) is then incorporated into the growing viral DNA chain by the **Viral DNA Polymerase**. \n  3. **Termination:** Because acyclovir lacks a 3'-OH group, the polymerase cannot attach the next nucleotide. The chain synthesis stops abruptly (**chain termination**), halting viral replication.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Destroy envelope:** Soaps/detergents do this, not targeted antiviral drugs.\n  - **(B) Protease inhibitor:** Used for HIV (e.g., Darunavir) and Hepatitis C. HSV does not rely heavily on a classic polyprotein protease.\n  - **(D) CD4 block:** HIV entry inhibitors (Maraviroc).\n  - **(E) Reverse transcriptase inhibitor:** Used for HIV (NRTI/NNRTI). HSV is a DNA virus, it does not use reverse transcriptase.",
    "pearl": "**Acyclovir** must be phosphorylated by HSV **viral thymidine kinase** to become active. It then inhibits **viral DNA polymerase** via chain termination.",
