    "qnum": 14,
    "topic": "Pharmacology Masterclass Part 2: Cancer & Reproductive Infections",
    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 55-year-old woman is receiving Cisplatin for advanced ovarian cancer. Her oncologist closely monitors her renal function because Cisplatin is notoriously nephrotoxic. To prevent severe acute tubular necrosis (ATN) and protect her kidneys during the infusion, the physician aggressively hydrates the patient with IV normal saline and administers a specific rescue drug. Which of the following is the mechanism of this rescue drug?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It is an iron chelator that prevents free radical formation",
      "B": "It is a thiol-based scavenger that binds and detoxifies reactive platinum metabolites in the kidney",
      "C": "It provides active tetrahydrofolate to bypass inhibited DHFR",
      "D": "It binds to the sulfhydryl groups of Cyclophosphamide metabolites in the bladder",
      "E": "It is a recombinant uricase enzyme that degrades uric acid crystals"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Cisplatin Toxicity:** Cisplatin is incredibly toxic. Its major dose-limiting toxicities are severe **Nephrotoxicity** (Acute Tubular Necrosis), severe **Ototoxicity** (sensorineural hearing loss/tinnitus), and severe **Peripheral Neuropathy**. (Carboplatin is used more often today because it causes less renal/ear damage, though it causes more myelosuppression).\n- **Preventing Nephrotoxicity:** \n  1. **Aggressive IV Hydration:** You must flush the kidneys with massive amounts of saline (and often mannitol) to dilute the drug in the renal tubules.\n  2. **Amifostine:** This is the specific chemical rescue agent for Cisplatin. \n     - **Mechanism:** Amifostine is a prodrug that is activated by alkaline phosphatase in normal tissues (but not inside the acidic tumor). Once active, it acts as a **thiol (sulfhydryl) donor/free radical scavenger**. It literally binds to the highly reactive, toxic platinum metabolites sitting in the renal tubules and neutralizes them before they can destroy the kidney cells. \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Iron chelator:** **Dexrazoxane**; rescues the heart from Anthracyclines (Doxorubicin).\n  - **(C) Active THF:** **Leucovorin**; rescues the bone marrow/gut from Methotrexate.\n  - **(D) Binds bladder metabolites:** **Mesna**; rescues the bladder from hemorrhagic cystitis caused by Cyclophosphamide.\n  - **(E) Recombinant uricase:** **Rasburicase**; prevents tumor lysis syndrome.",
    "pearl": "The dose-limiting toxicities of **Cisplatin** are **Nephrotoxicity** and **Ototoxicity**. Nephrotoxicity is prevented with aggressive IV fluids and **Amifostine** (a free radical scavenger).",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Pharmacology_Masterclass_Part_2__Cancer__7",
        "text": "7. Review the pharmacologic therapies for reproductive tract infections, including antibiotics and antifungals used for pelvic inflammatory disease, vaginitis, and sexually transmitted infections."
      }
    ]
