    "qnum": 19,
    "topic": "Pharmacology Masterclass Part 2: Cancer & Reproductive Infections",
    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 22-year-old woman presents to the clinic with dysuria, increased vaginal discharge, and lower abdominal pain. A nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) from an endocervical swab returns positive for *Chlamydia trachomatis* and negative for *Neisseria gonorrhoeae*. She is not pregnant. The physician prescribes a 7-day course of oral Doxycycline. By what specific mechanism does Doxycycline eradicate this intracellular pathogen?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, inhibiting the translocation step of protein synthesis",
      "B": "It binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing the attachment of aminoacyl-tRNA to the mRNA-ribosome complex",
      "C": "It inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase (Topoisomerase II)",
      "D": "It irreversibly binds to penicillin-binding proteins, halting peptidoglycan cell wall cross-linking",
      "E": "It inhibits the synthesis of mycolic acids"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Chlamydia Treatment:** *Chlamydia trachomatis* is an obligate intracellular bacteria (it cannot make its own ATP). It completely lacks classic peptidoglycan in its cell wall, making all beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins/cephalosporins) completely useless.\n- **The Drugs:** The gold standard treatments are **Doxycycline** (a Tetracycline, given for 7 days) or **Azithromycin** (a Macrolide, given as a 1-time dose). Current CDC guidelines prefer Doxycycline as first-line.\n- **Mechanism of Doxycycline:** Tetracyclines are broad-spectrum, bacteriostatic protein synthesis inhibitors. \n  - They freely enter the bacterial cell and bind reversibly to the **30S ribosomal subunit**. \n  - By binding there, they physically block the A (acceptor) site, **preventing the incoming aminoacyl-tRNA from docking** with the mRNA. No new amino acids can be added, and protein synthesis halts.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) 50S subunit / Translocation:** This is the mechanism of **Macrolides** (Azithromycin/Erythromycin). (Mnemonic: *buy AT 30, CCEL at 50*. **A**minoglycosides/**T**etracyclines hit 30S. **C**hloramphenicol/**C**lindamycin/**E**rythromycin/**L**inezolid hit 50S).\n  - **(C) DNA gyrase:** Fluoroquinolones (Levofloxacin).\n  - **(D) Penicillin-binding proteins:** Beta-lactams (Ceftriaxone, Penicillin). Useless against Chlamydia.\n  - **(E) Mycolic acids:** Isoniazid (treats TB).",
    "pearl": "**Doxycycline** treats Chlamydia by binding to the **30S ribosome** and preventing **tRNA attachment**. Remember to avoid it in pregnancy and children $<8$ (binds calcium in bones/teeth causing discoloration).",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Pharmacology_Masterclass_Part_2__Cancer__7",
        "text": "7. Review the pharmacologic therapies for reproductive tract infections, including antibiotics and antifungals used for pelvic inflammatory disease, vaginitis, and sexually transmitted infections."
      }
    ]
