    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 30-year-old pregnant woman in her third trimester is diagnosed with severe, symptomatic vulvovaginal candidiasis (yeast infection). She asks her physician if she can simply take the single oral Fluconazole pill that she usually takes, because she hates the messy vaginal creams. The physician explains that oral Fluconazole is not recommended during pregnancy, and instead prescribes a 7-day course of topical Clotrimazole vaginal suppositories. Why is oral Fluconazole generally avoided during the first trimester of pregnancy?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It aggressively crosses the placenta and causes fatal fetal cardiac arrhythmias",
      "B": "High systemic doses have been associated with a specific pattern of birth defects including craniofacial, skeletal, and cardiac abnormalities",
      "C": "It severely suppresses the maternal bone marrow, leading to dangerous neutropenia",
      "D": "It acts as a powerful oxytocin agonist, triggering premature labor and delivery",
      "E": "The altered pH of the pregnant vagina renders the systemically absorbed drug completely ineffective"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Vulvovaginal Candidiasis in Pregnancy:** High estrogen levels in pregnancy increase vaginal glycogen, making pregnant women extremely susceptible to yeast infections.\n- **The Pharmacologic Rule:** \n  - The gold standard treatment for pregnant women is **Topical Azoles** (e.g., Clotrimazole or Miconazole vaginal creams/suppositories for 7 days). Because they act locally, there is virtually zero systemic absorption, making them 100% safe for the fetus.\n  - **Oral Fluconazole** is generally avoided, particularly in the first trimester. While a single $150\\text{ mg}$ dose might theoretically be low risk, high or chronic systemic doses of fluconazole are known **teratogens**. They interfere with embryonic sterol synthesis, causing a distinct pattern of severe birth defects (often resembling Antley-Bixler syndrome) including brachycephaly, cleft palate, bowing of the femurs, and congenital heart disease. It is safer to simply use the topical cream.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Cardiac arrhythmias:** Fluconazole can prolong the QT interval in the *mother*, but it doesn't cause fetal arrhythmias.\n  - **(C) Bone marrow:** Not a major side effect of azoles.\n  - **(D) Oxytocin agonist:** It has no uterotonic properties.\n  - **(E) pH ineffectiveness:** The drug works via the bloodstream reaching the mucosal tissue; pH does not deactivate it.",
    "pearl": "To treat a yeast infection in a **pregnant** woman, always use a **Topical Azole** (Clotrimazole vaginal cream). Avoid oral Fluconazole due to the risk of **teratogenic birth defects** (craniofacial/skeletal abnormalities).",
