    "topic": "Pharmacology Masterclass Part 1: Endocrine, Menopause, and Puberty",
    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 32-year-old woman presents to the clinic complaining of bilateral milky nipple discharge (galactorrhea) and the absence of menstrual periods for the past 6 months (amenorrhea). A pregnancy test is negative. Her serum prolactin level is markedly elevated at $150 \\text{ ng/mL}$ (normal $< 25$). An MRI of her brain reveals a 6-mm microadenoma in the anterior pituitary gland. Which of the following is the first-line pharmacologic treatment for this patient's condition?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Transsphenoidal surgical resection of the adenoma",
      "B": "Oral Cabergoline",
      "C": "Oral Risperidone",
      "D": "Intramuscular Leuprolide",
      "E": "Oral Metoclopramide"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis:** The patient has a **Prolactinoma**, a benign prolactin-secreting pituitary adenoma. The high prolactin inhibits GnRH, causing amenorrhea, and directly stimulates the breast, causing galactorrhea.\n- **Treatment of Prolactinoma:** Unlike almost every other pituitary tumor (which require surgery), the absolute first-line treatment for a prolactinoma (both micro- and macro-adenomas) is **Medical Therapy**.\n- **The Drugs:** Prolactin secretion is tonically inhibited by Dopamine from the hypothalamus. Therefore, the treatment is a **Dopamine Agonist**. \n  - **Cabergoline** and **Bromocriptine** are dopamine D2 receptor agonists. \n  - They bind to the lactotroph cells in the pituitary, forcefully shutting down prolactin secretion and actually causing the tumor itself to shrink and undergo apoptosis. Cabergoline is preferred over Bromocriptine because it is more effective and has fewer side effects (less nausea).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Surgery:** Reserved ONLY for patients who fail medical therapy, cannot tolerate the drugs, or have rapidly progressing visual field deficits (bitemporal hemianopsia) not responding to drugs.\n  - **(C) Risperidone & (E) Metoclopramide:** These are **Dopamine Antagonists**. Giving these would block the inhibitory dopamine signal, causing prolactin levels to skyrocket (a common cause of drug-induced hyperprolactinemia).\n  - **(D) Leuprolide:** GnRH agonist; would not shrink a prolactinoma.",
    "pearl": "The first-line treatment for a **Prolactinoma** is a dopamine D2 agonist, specifically **Cabergoline** (or Bromocriptine). It shrinks the tumor and lowers prolactin levels. Surgery is second-line.",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Pharmacology_Masterclass_Part_1__Endocri_5",
        "text": "5. Identify the appropriate pharmacologic treatments for hyperprolactinemia (e.g., dopamine agonists like cabergoline and bromocriptine) and describe their mechanisms and side effect profiles."
      },
      {
        "id": "LO_Pharmacology_Masterclass_Part_1__Endocri_9",
        "text": "9. Describe the pharmacologic treatment of precocious and delayed puberty, including the use of GnRH agonists and sex steroid replacement."
      }
    ]
