    "stem": "A 30-year-old woman is receiving treatment for a prolactinoma with Cabergoline. She discovers she is pregnant. She asks her endocrinologist what should be done with her medication during the pregnancy. Which of the following is the standard, evidence-based recommendation for the management of a microprolactinoma during pregnancy?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Double the dose of Cabergoline, as pregnancy naturally causes prolactinomas to rapidly expand",
      "B": "Switch to Methotrexate to halt the tumor growth",
      "C": "Discontinue the Cabergoline and observe the patient clinically throughout the pregnancy",
      "D": "Schedule an immediate transsphenoidal surgical resection in the second trimester",
      "E": "Switch to continuous Leuprolide therapy"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Prolactinomas in Pregnancy:** \n  - Normal pregnancy causes a massive, physiologic increase in estrogen. This estrogen directly stimulates the pituitary lactotrophs, causing the pituitary gland to naturally double in size and prolactin levels to naturally skyrocket (preparing the breasts for lactation).\n  - **The Rule for Microadenomas ($<10\\text{ mm}$):** The risk of a *micro*adenoma growing large enough during pregnancy to cause visual field defects or headaches is extremely low ($<2-3\\%$). Because the drug is no longer needed for fertility (she's already pregnant) and we want to minimize fetal drug exposure, the standard of care is to **discontinue the dopamine agonist (Cabergoline/Bromocriptine)** as soon as pregnancy is confirmed. You simply monitor the patient clinically for headaches or vision changes. You do *not* check prolactin levels during pregnancy because they will be naturally high anyway.\n  - *(Note: For Macroadenomas $>10\\text{ mm}$, the risk of symptomatic growth is much higher (20-30%), so they are often kept on Bromocriptine throughout the pregnancy).*\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Double dose:** Unnecessary for a microadenoma and exposes the fetus to more drug.\n  - **(B) Methotrexate:** An absolute teratogen; causes abortion/severe defects.\n  - **(D) Surgery:** Completely unnecessary for an asymptomatic microadenoma.\n  - **(E) Leuprolide:** GnRH agonist; contraindicated in pregnancy.",
    "pearl": "If a woman with a **microprolactinoma** ($<10\\text{ mm}$) becomes pregnant, you should **discontinue the dopamine agonist** (Cabergoline) and simply observe. The risk of clinically significant tumor growth is very low.",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Pharmacology_Masterclass_Part_1__Endocri_5",
        "text": "5. Identify the appropriate pharmacologic treatments for hyperprolactinemia (e.g., dopamine agonists like cabergoline and bromocriptine) and describe their mechanisms and side effect profiles."
      },
      {
        "id": "LO_Pharmacology_Masterclass_Part_1__Endocri_9",
        "text": "9. Describe the pharmacologic treatment of precocious and delayed puberty, including the use of GnRH agonists and sex steroid replacement."
      }
    ]
