    "topic": "Lecture 5.1: Lower Genital Tract Diseases - Set 2",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 40-year-old woman presents to her physician complaining of post-coital spotting (light bleeding after intercourse). Speculum examination reveals a soft, red, friable, 1-cm pedunculated mass protruding from the cervical os. The physician easily twists the mass off with ring forceps. Histopathologic examination of the mass is most likely to reveal which of the following?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Malignant squamous cells infiltrating a fibrous stroma",
      "B": "A thick-walled central blood vessel surrounded by hyperplastic endometrium",
      "C": "Hyperplastic endocervical columnar epithelium and loose fibromyxoid stroma with a prominent blood supply",
      "D": "Tightly packed whorls of smooth muscle cells",
      "E": "Stratified squamous epithelium with a central core of keratin"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** Post-coital bleeding is a classic symptom of cervical pathology. The finding of a soft, red, pedunculated mass protruding from the os is the pathognomonic presentation of an **Endocervical Polyp**.\n- **Pathology:** An endocervical polyp is a completely **benign** localized overgrowth of the endocervical mucosa. \n- **Histology:** It consists of hyperplastic **columnar epithelium** (the normal lining of the endocervix) covering a loose, edematous (fibromyxoid) stroma that contains a rich, prominent network of dilated blood vessels. Because the vessels are so superficial, the polyp is highly friable and bleeds easily when bumped during intercourse (post-coital bleeding).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Malignant squamous cells:** Cervical SCC (a hard, ulcerated mass).\n  - **(B) Hyperplastic endometrium:** Describes an *Endometrial* polyp. While they can prolapse through the cervix, endocervical polyps are much more common.\n  - **(D) Whorls of smooth muscle:** A prolapsed pedunculated submucosal fibroid (leiomyoma). It would be firm, not soft and friable.\n  - **(E) Squamous epithelium/keratin:** A skin tag or wart, not a mucosal polyp.",
    "pearl": "An **Endocervical Polyp** is a benign overgrowth of **columnar epithelium and stroma**. It is highly vascular and a classic, benign cause of **post-coital bleeding**.",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Lecture_5_1__Lower_Genital_Tract_Disease_21",
        "text": "Squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix"
      }
    ]
