    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **The Use of p16 in Pathology:** p16 (INK4a) is a host tumor suppressor protein. It is used as a surrogate biomarker to definitively prove that an active, high-risk HPV infection is driving dysplasia. \n- **The Mechanism of Upregulation:**\n  1. In a normal cell, p16 inhibits CDK4/6, which keeps the **Rb** protein active (hypophosphorylated). Active Rb holds the cell cycle hostage at the G1/S checkpoint.\n  2. When high-risk HPV infects the cell, its **E7** protein binds and completely destroys the **Rb** protein.\n  3. With Rb gone, the cell cycle spins out of control. The host cell recognizes that Rb is missing/destroyed. In a desperate, futile attempt to stop the runaway cell cycle, the cell massively upregulates the production of **p16** (trying to activate the Rb that is no longer there). \n- **The Result:** The dysplastic cells become absolutely stuffed with p16 protein, making p16 immunostaining a highly sensitive and specific test for high-grade HPV infections (CIN II/III).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) E6 stimulates p16:** E6 targets p53, not p16.\n  - **(C) p53 inhibits p16:** p53 induces p21, not p16.\n  - **(D) Viral protein:** p16 is a human (host) protein, not viral.\n  - **(E) Insertion:** The virus inserts its own DNA, not extra copies of the human p16 gene.",
    "pearl": "Diffuse **p16 positivity** on a cervical biopsy is the surrogate marker for high-risk HPV infection. It occurs because the HPV **E7 protein destroys Rb**, triggering a massive compensatory overproduction of p16 by the host cell.",
