    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A pathologist is examining a cervical biopsy taken from a 29-year-old woman. The pathologist diagnoses the lesion as Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia grade III (CIN III). Which of the following histologic descriptions defines the criteria for assigning a grade of CIN III rather than CIN I or CIN II?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Dysplastic cells with koilocytic change confined to the lower one-third of the epithelium",
      "B": "Dysplastic cells extending from the basal layer through the lower two-thirds of the epithelium",
      "C": "Dysplastic cells lacking maturation extending through the entire thickness of the epithelium, without breaking the basement membrane",
      "D": "Dysplastic cells that have breached the basement membrane and invaded the underlying cervical stroma",
      "E": "The presence of keratin pearls and intercellular bridges within the dysplastic tissue"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Grading of CIN:** Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia (CIN) is graded based on how far up from the basement membrane the dysplastic, immature, dividing cells extend into the epithelium.\n  - **CIN I:** Dysplasia is confined to the **lower 1/3** of the epithelium. (The upper 2/3 mature normally).\n  - **CIN II:** Dysplasia extends into the **lower 2/3** of the epithelium.\n  - **CIN III:** Dysplasia extends through the **entire thickness** of the epithelium (almost no maturation at the surface). It is synonymous with **Carcinoma in Situ (CIS)**. \n- **The Critical Boundary:** In all grades of CIN (I, II, and III), the **basement membrane remains perfectly intact**. The dysplastic cells have *not* invaded.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Lower 1/3:** Defines CIN I.\n  - **(B) Lower 2/3:** Defines CIN II.\n  - **(D) Breached basement membrane:** By definition, if the basement membrane is broken, it is no longer \"intraepithelial neoplasia\" \u2014 it is now **Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma**.\n  - **(E) Keratin pearls/bridges:** Features of well-differentiated invasive squamous cell carcinoma, not typical of *in situ* dysplasia.",
    "pearl": "**CIN III (Carcinoma in Situ):** Dysplasia involves the **entire thickness** of the epithelium, but the **basement membrane is intact**.",
