    "rationale": "- **HPV Strains and Cancer:**\n  - **Low-risk strains (6, 11):** Cause benign genital warts (condyloma acuminata). They rarely, if ever, cause cancer.\n  - **High-risk strains (16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58):** Cause dysplasia (CIN) and cancer. \n- **The Most Dangerous Strain:** Among the high-risk strains, **HPV 16** is by far the most aggressive and most prevalent. It is responsible for approximately **50% to 60%** of all cervical cancers worldwide. \n- **The Runner Up:** **HPV 18** is the second most common, responsible for about 10-15% of cervical cancers (and is particularly strongly associated with cervical *adenocarcinoma*). Together, 16 and 18 account for over 70% of all cervical cancers.\n- **Clinical Management:** In modern algorithms, finding ASC-US on a Pap smear triggers a reflex HPV test. If high-risk HPV (especially 16/18) is present, the patient is sent directly to colposcopy. If the HPV test is negative, she can just repeat the Pap in a year.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) 6 & (B) 11:** Low-risk, cause warts.\n  - **(D) 18 & (E) 31:** High-risk, but 16 is the most common single cause.",
    "pearl": "**HPV 16** is the single most common cause of **Cervical Squamous Cell Carcinoma** (responsible for $\\sim 60\\%$ of cases).",
