    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Cervical Cancer Subtypes:** \n  - The vast majority (80%) of cervical cancers are **Squamous Cell Carcinomas**. These arise from the squamo-columnar junction (the ectocervix) and present with visible lesions on speculum exam.\n  - However, about 15-20% of cervical cancers are **Adenocarcinomas**. These arise from the columnar (glandular) epithelium deep inside the **endocervical canal**. \n- **The Danger of Endocervical Lesions:** Because adenocarcinomas and their precursors (Adenocarcinoma in situ, AIS, or endocervical dysplasia) grow high up inside the canal, they are often missed by routine Pap smears (which primarily sample the outer cervix) and cannot be seen on colposcopy. \n  - If a patient has dysplasia (like CIN III or AIS) that extends deep into the endocervical canal (positive inner margin), they are at incredibly high risk for developing invasive **Adenocarcinoma**. (Note: HPV 18 is particularly strongly associated with cervical adenocarcinoma).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Squamous Cell Carcinoma:** Arises from the ectocervix (the outer margin).\n  - **(C) Clear Cell Carcinoma:** Associated with DES exposure.\n  - **(D) Small Cell Carcinoma:** A rare, aggressive neuroendocrine tumor of the cervix.\n  - **(E) Melanoma:** Rare; arises from melanocytes.",
    "pearl": "Dysplasia deep inside the **endocervical canal** is the precursor to Cervical **Adenocarcinoma** (often missed by Pap smears and strongly linked to HPV 18).",
