    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Cervical Cancer Screening Guidelines (USPSTF 2018):** These guidelines are highly tested on the USMLE.\n  - **$< 21$ years old:** NO screening, regardless of age of sexual debut. (HPV infections are incredibly common in teens but almost always clear spontaneously; screening leads to unnecessary, harmful procedures).\n  - **21 to 29 years old:** Screening begins at age 21. The recommendation is **Pap smear (cytology) alone every 3 years**. (Do NOT do routine HPV co-testing in this age group, because almost everyone will be positive for HPV and clear it, leading to massive overtreatment).\n  - **30 to 65 years old:** Three options:\n    1. Pap smear alone every 3 years.\n    2. Primary high-risk HPV testing alone every 5 years.\n    3. **Co-testing (Pap + HPV) every 5 years** (most commonly preferred).\n  - **$> 65$ years old:** Stop screening if she has had adequate prior negative screening and no history of CIN II/III in the past 20 years.\n- **Application:** The patient is exactly 21 years old. Therefore, she is due for her very first screening, which should be a **Pap smear alone**. Her vaccination status does NOT change screening guidelines (vaccines don't cover all oncogenic strains).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) No screening (vaccine):** Vaccine does not negate the need for screening.\n  - **(B) No screening (age 25):** Old guidelines or non-US guidelines; USPSTF says start at 21.\n  - **(D) HPV alone & (E) Co-testing:** Not recommended for women under 30 due to the high prevalence of transient, benign HPV infections.",
    "pearl": "Cervical cancer screening begins at **age 21** with a **Pap smear alone every 3 years**.\n**Co-testing (Pap + HPV)** begins at **age 30** (done every 5 years).\nPrior HPV vaccination does NOT change the screening schedule.",
