    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Stopping Screening (USPSTF Guidelines):** \n  - The guidelines recommend **stopping all cervical cancer screening in women over the age of 65**, provided they meet specific criteria for \"adequate prior screening.\"\n  - **Adequate prior screening:** Defined as having either 3 consecutive negative Pap smears OR 2 consecutive negative co-tests (Pap + HPV) within the last 10 years, with the most recent test occurring within the last 5 years.\n  - **No history of high-grade disease:** The patient must NOT have a history of CIN II, CIN III, or cervical cancer within the past 20 years.\n- **Application:** This 68-year-old woman has a perfect screening history, no history of dysplasia, and meets all criteria. Cervical cancer is a slow-growing disease (taking 10-20 years to develop from initial HPV infection). A new HPV infection at age 68 is incredibly unlikely to cause cancer in her remaining lifetime. Therefore, screening should be **discontinued**. (Continuing screening only exposes her to false positives and unnecessary, painful biopsies).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A), (B), (D):** Violate the guideline to stop screening at 65.\n  - **(E) Endometrial biopsy:** Only indicated if she had postmenopausal bleeding; she is asymptomatic.",
    "pearl": "Cervical cancer screening should be **stopped at age 65** if the patient has a history of adequate negative screening and no history of CIN II/III in the past 20 years.",
