    "qnum": 24,
    "topic": "Lecture 5.1: Lower Genital Tract Diseases - Set 2",
    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 45-year-old woman undergoes a total hysterectomy (removal of both the uterus and cervix) for symptomatic, benign uterine fibroids. She has a history of normal Pap smears and has never had cervical dysplasia or HPV. She asks her physician if she still needs to come in for her routine Pap smears now that her uterus is gone. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice according to current guidelines?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "She must continue Pap smears every 3 years to screen for vaginal cancer.",
      "B": "She must continue Pap smears every 5 years with HPV co-testing.",
      "C": "She no longer needs Pap smears and screening can be safely discontinued.",
      "D": "She only needs a Pap smear if she develops vaginal bleeding.",
      "E": "She should have a Pap smear annually because the surgery increases her risk of cancer."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Screening After Hysterectomy:**\n  - The Pap smear is specifically designed to scrape cells from the cervix (the squamo-columnar junction) to screen for cervical cancer.\n  - If a patient has had a **Total Hysterectomy** (which by definition includes the removal of the cervix) for **benign reasons** (like fibroids, endometriosis, or heavy bleeding) AND she has **no history of high-grade dysplasia (CIN II/III) or cervical cancer**, she no longer has a cervix to get cancer in.\n  - The risk of primary vaginal cancer is incredibly low. Therefore, USPSTF guidelines clearly state that **all screening (Pap smears) should be permanently discontinued** in these patients.\n- **The Exception:** If she had her cervix removed *because* of CIN II/III or cervical cancer, she MUST continue to get vaginal cuff Pap smears for 20 years to check for recurrence or vaginal dysplasia.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) & (B) Continue screening:** Vaginal cancer is too rare to justify population-wide screening of the vaginal cuff in low-risk women. \n  - **(D) Symptomatic screening:** If she bleeds, she needs an exam and a biopsy, not a screening Pap.\n  - **(E) Annually:** Hysterectomy for benign disease drops her risk to essentially zero.",
    "pearl": "**Discontinue Pap smears** completely in women who have had a total hysterectomy (cervix removed) for **benign indications** (e.g., fibroids) and have no prior history of high-grade dysplasia.",
