    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 55-year-old woman with a history of neglected, advanced cervical squamous cell carcinoma is admitted to the hospital with profound lethargy, confusion, and generalized edema. She has not urinated in 24 hours. Laboratory studies show a BUN of 95 mg/dL, Creatinine of 8.4 mg/dL, and Potassium of 6.8 mEq/L. A renal ultrasound shows severe bilateral hydronephrosis. What is the precise anatomic structure that the cervical tumor has invaded to cause this life-threatening complication?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The trigone of the bladder",
      "B": "The urethra",
      "C": "The uterine arteries",
      "D": "The ureters, as they pass under the uterine arteries within the parametrium",
      "E": "The renal pelvis directly"
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient is in acute post-renal kidney failure (anuria, severe uremia, hyperkalemia) due to bilateral obstructive uropathy (hydronephrosis) caused by advanced cervical cancer. \n- **Anatomic Relationship:** This is one of the most tested anatomical relationships in gynecology. \n  - The ureters travel down from the kidneys into the pelvis. Just before they enter the bladder, they pass through the **parametrium** (the cardinal ligament), running **directly underneath the uterine arteries** (the classic mnemonic: *\"water under the bridge\"*). \n  - The parametrium is located immediately lateral to the cervix.\n- **The Pathology:** As a cervical cancer grows and expands laterally, it invades the parametrium. Because the ureters are trapped in this tight space under the uterine artery, the hard, fibrotic tumor completely encases and compresses them, blocking the flow of urine from the kidneys to the bladder.\n- **The Outcome:** This bilateral ureteral obstruction leads to renal failure (uremia), which is the most common cause of death in cervical cancer.\n- **Why Other Choices Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Bladder trigone:** The tumor can invade the bladder, causing hematuria or a vesicovaginal fistula, but to cause *bilateral* hydronephrosis and complete anuria, it must compress the ureters higher up in the parametrium.\n  - **(B) Urethra:** Too far distal; cervical cancer doesn't typically reach the urethra.\n  - **(C) Uterine arteries:** Invading the arteries causes bleeding, not renal failure.\n  - **(E) Renal pelvis:** The cancer is in the pelvis, not up in the kidney.",
    "pearl": "The **ureters** pass through the parametrium, traveling **under the uterine artery** (\"water under the bridge\"). Lateral extension of **Cervical Cancer** compresses the ureters, causing bilateral **hydronephrosis and renal failure** (the most common cause of death).",
