    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 40-year-old woman is diagnosed with Stage IA1 squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix (microinvasive disease, invading less than 3 mm into the stroma). She strongly desires to have children in the future and begs her gynecologic oncologist for a treatment option that preserves her fertility. Assuming her lymph nodes are negative on imaging, which of the following is the most appropriate fertility-sparing surgical treatment for this early-stage cancer?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Radical trachelectomy or Cone biopsy (Conization)",
      "B": "Simple total hysterectomy",
      "C": "Radical hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy",
      "D": "Pelvic exenteration",
      "E": "High-dose systemic chemotherapy with Paclitaxel"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Treatment of Cervical Cancer:**\n  - **Standard Care:** For most early invasive cervical cancers, the standard of care is a **Radical Hysterectomy** (removal of uterus, cervix, parametria, and upper vagina). This obviously ends fertility.\n- **Fertility-Sparing Options:** If the cancer is caught extremely early (Stage IA1 - microinvasive), and the patient strongly desires fertility, the uterus can be spared.\n  - **Cone Biopsy (Conization):** If the invasion is $<3$ mm, a large cone of tissue containing the lesion and the entire transformation zone can be excised from the cervix. If the margins are clear of cancer, the remaining cervix and the entire uterus are left intact, allowing the patient to carry a pregnancy.\n  - **Radical Trachelectomy:** For slightly larger tumors (Stage IA2 or IB1), the entire cervix and parametrium are removed, but the body of the uterus (fundus) is preserved and sewn directly to the vagina. \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) & (C) Hysterectomy:** Removes the uterus; fertility is permanently lost.\n  - **(D) Pelvic exenteration:** A massive, morbid surgery removing the uterus, bladder, vagina, and rectum for centrally recurrent cancer.\n  - **(E) Chemotherapy:** Cervical cancer is treated primarily with surgery (early stage) or concurrent chemo-radiation (advanced stage). Chemo alone is not curative and does not spare fertility.",
    "pearl": "For very early, microinvasive (Stage IA1) cervical cancer in women desiring fertility preservation, a **Cone Biopsy (Conization)** or **Radical Trachelectomy** is the treatment of choice, leaving the uterus intact.",
