    "stem": "A 50-year-old woman is diagnosed with Stage IIB cervical cancer, meaning the tumor has invaded the parametrium but has not reached the pelvic sidewall. Because the tumor is locally advanced and bulky, she is not a candidate for primary surgical resection (radical hysterectomy). Which of the following is the standard of care, first-line treatment for locally advanced cervical cancer?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Chemotherapy alone with carboplatin",
      "B": "Concurrent chemoradiation therapy (External beam radiation + Brachytherapy + Cisplatin)",
      "C": "High-dose continuous progestin therapy",
      "D": "Targeted immunotherapy with Trastuzumab",
      "E": "Pelvic exenteration surgery"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Management of Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer (Stage IIB and higher):**\n  - Once the tumor has invaded beyond the cervix into the parametrium (the connective tissue webbing holding the uterus), surgery (radical hysterectomy) is no longer feasible because you cannot get clear surgical margins without severely damaging the bladder, ureters, or rectum.\n  - **The Standard of Care:** The definitive, curative treatment for locally advanced cervical cancer is **Concurrent Chemoradiation**. \n  - **The Regimen:** This involves pelvic **external beam radiation** to shrink the primary tumor and treat the lymph nodes, combined with **internal radiation (brachytherapy)** inserted directly into the vagina/cervix to deliver a massive dose of radiation to the tumor core. Low-dose **Cisplatin** chemotherapy is given concurrently; it acts as a \"radiosensitizer,\" making the tumor cells more vulnerable to the radiation.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Chemo alone:** Palliative for distant metastatic disease, but not curative for pelvic disease.\n  - **(C) Progestin:** Used for Endometrial hyperplasia/cancer, not cervical cancer.\n  - **(D) Trastuzumab:** For HER2+ breast/gastric cancer.\n  - **(E) Pelvic exenteration:** A salvage surgery for cancer that *recurs* centrally in the pelvis after maximum radiation has already failed. It is never first-line.",
    "pearl": "Locally advanced cervical cancer (invading the parametrium or beyond) is treated definitively with **Concurrent Chemoradiation** (Radiation + Cisplatin), NOT surgery.",
