    "qnum": 42,
    "topic": "Atypical Development of the (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 17-year-old female presents with primary amenorrhea. On physical examination, she has excellent, typical female breast development, but completely absent pubic and axillary hair. Her external genitalia are typically female. Pelvic ultrasound reveals a blind-ending vagina with no uterus, cervix, or fallopian tubes. Bilateral masses are palpable in her inguinal canals. Karyotype is 46,XY. The clinician wants to explain the difference between this condition (Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome, or CAIS) and Swyer syndrome (46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis). Which of the following clinical features most reliably distinguishes Swyer syndrome from Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS) at puberty? 51",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The presence of ambiguous external genitalia at birth in Swyer syndrome compared to typical female external genitalia in CAIS.",
      "B": "The presence of normal pubic and axillary hair in CAIS compared to completely absent hair in Swyer syndrome.",
      "C": "The presence of a uterus and fallopian tubes on ultrasound, combined with a lack of breast development in Swyer syndrome.",
      "D": "The presence of bilateral palpable inguinal masses (testes) in Swyer syndrome compared to typical ovaries in CAIS.",
      "E": "The presence of typical male levels of circulating testosterone in Swyer syndrome compared to low levels in CAIS."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "combined with a lack of breast development in Swyer syndrome. A. The presence of ambiguous external genitalia at birth in Swyer syndrome compared to typical female external genitalia in CAIS.:  Incorrect  . Both  Swyer syndrome and CAIS present with typical, non-ambiguous female external genitalia at birth, so external appearance at birth does not distinguish them. 63 B. The presence of normal pubic and axillary hair in CAIS compared to completely absent hair in Swyer syndrome.:  Incorrect  . Pubic and axillary  hair are completely absent or sparse in CAIS due to lack of androgen action, whereas in Swyer syndrome, sparse pubic hair develops under the influence of adrenal androgens. C. The presence of a uterus and fallopian tubes on ultrasound, combined with a lack of breast development in Swyer syndrome.:  Correct  . Swyer syndrome  is characterized by streak gonads, which produce no hormones; therefore, these patients have no estrogen to drive pubertal breast development, but they do have a normal uterus and fallopian tubes because no AMH was produced in utero. In contrast, patients with Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS) have functioning testes that produce AMH (leading to a complete absence of the uterus and fallopian tubes) and testosterone, which is aromatized peripherally to estrogen to drive typical female breast development. Thus, the presence of a uterus and a lack of breast development clearly distinguishes Swyer syndrome from CAIS. D. The presence of bilateral palpable inguinal masses (testes) in Swyer syndrome compared to typical ovaries in CAIS.:  Incorrect  . Swyer syndrome  has streak gonads (no ovaries) and CAIS has testes; neither has typical ovaries. E. The presence of typical male levels of circulating testosterone in Swyer syndrome compared to low levels in CAIS.:  Incorrect  . Circulating testosterone  levels are normal to high in CAIS, but extremely low in Swyer syndrome because streak gonads cannot synthesize sex steroids.",
