    "qnum": 43,
    "topic": "Atypical Development of the (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "An 18-year-old female is diagnosed with Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS). She has normal female breast development, a blind-ending vagina, no uterus or ovaries, and bilateral testes located in her inguinal canals. Her serum testosterone level is in the typical adult male range. The patient asks why she developed typical female breasts if she has male levels of testosterone and no ovaries. Which of the following represents the correct biological mechanism behind her breast development?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The pituitary gland hyper-secreted prolactin and FSH, which directly drove the maturation of the vestigial breast tissue.",
      "B": "The fetal testes hyper-secreted progesterone, which directly stimulated the alveolar and ductal growth of her breast tissue.",
      "C": "The adrenal glands hyper-secreted estrone under the influence of elevated cortisol, bypassing the insensitive androgen receptors.",
      "D": "The extremely high levels of circulating testosterone are peripherally aromatized into estrogens, which drive breast development in the absence of androgenic suppression.",
      "E": "The maternal placenta supplied estrogen in utero, which was stored in her adipose tissue and released continuously during puberty."
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "peripherally aromatized into estrogens, which drive breast development in the absence of androgenic suppression. A. The pituitary gland hyper-secreted prolactin and FSH, which directly drove the maturation of the vestigial breast tissue.:  Incorrect  . Prolactin  and FSH do not directly drive breast tissue growth during puberty in the absence of estrogen stimulation. B. The fetal testes hyper-secreted progesterone, which directly stimulated the alveolar and ductal growth of her breast tissue.:  Incorrect  . Progesterone  is not a primary hormone driving pubertal breast development, nor is it hyper-secreted by the fetal testes. C. The adrenal glands hyper-secreted estrone under the influence of elevated cortisol, bypassing the insensitive androgen receptors.:  Incorrect  .  Adrenal estrone is not the primary driver of breast development in CAIS, and the adrenal glands do not hyper-secrete estrogen in this manner. D. The extremely high levels of circulating testosterone are peripherally aromatized into estrogens, which drive breast development in the absence of androgenic suppression.:  Correct  . In CAIS, the testes secrete normal adult male levels of testosterone. Because the androgen receptor is completely non-functional, testosterone cannot exert its masculinizing effects. 64 However, the high levels of circulating testosterone are converted peripherally by the enzyme aromatase into estrogens (estradiol). In the absence of androgenic suppression (since androgens typically suppress breast development in typical males), these circulating estrogens act unopposed on breast tissue, driving normal, typical female breast development during puberty. E. The maternal placenta supplied estrogen in utero, which was stored in her adipose tissue and released continuously during puberty.:  Incorrect  .  Maternal placental estrogen is cleared rapidly after birth and is not stored or released during puberty to drive breast development.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
