    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 6-month-old infant is brought to the pediatric surgeon after her pediatrician palpated bilateral, firm, non-tender masses in her inguinal canals during a routine well-child exam. Her external genitalia are typically female with no ambiguity. Genetic analysis reveals a 46,XY karyotype, and a diagnosis of Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS) 52 is established. The surgeon explains to the parents that these inguinal masses are her testes, which failed to descend into a scrotum because testicular descent is a hormone-dependent process. What hormone is primarily responsible for the descent of the testes from the inguinal ring into the scrotum, and why did descent fail in this patient?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Dihydrotestosterone (DHT); it requires functioning 5-alpha-reductase, which is completely absent in CAIS.",
      "B": "Antimullerian Hormone (AMH); it requires functioning Sertoli cells, which did not differentiate in this patient.",
      "C": "Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG); it requires a functioning placenta, which did not produce enough hormone in utero.",
      "D": "Estrogen; it requires a functioning aromatase enzyme, which is blocked by the patient's Y chromosome.",
      "E": "Testosterone; it requires functioning androgen receptors, which are completely defective in this patient."
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "are completely defective in this patient. A. Dihydrotestosterone (DHT); it requires functioning 5-alpha-reductase, which is completely absent in CAIS.:  Incorrect  . 5-alpha-reductase deficiency  prevents DHT synthesis, which causes ambiguous external genitalia but does not prevent testicular descent to the same extent as a complete lack of androgen response. B. Antimullerian Hormone (AMH); it requires functioning Sertoli cells, which did not differentiate in this patient.:  Incorrect  . AMH is responsible for  Mullerian duct regression, not testicular descent. Sertoli cells do differentiate in CAIS and AMH is functional. C. Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG); it requires a functioning placenta, which did not produce enough hormone in utero.:  Incorrect  . While  hCG plays an early role, local testosterone action via androgen receptors is the primary driver of inguinal testicular descent, which fails due to receptor insensitivity. D. Estrogen; it requires a functioning aromatase enzyme, which is blocked by the patient's Y chromosome.:  Incorrect  . Estrogen is not the primary  hormone driving testicular descent, and aromatase is not blocked by the Y chromosome. E. Testosterone; it requires functioning androgen receptors, which are completely defective in this patient.:  Correct  . Testicular descent from the  abdomen, through the inguinal ring, and into the scrotum is a highly hormone-dependent process that requires testosterone and its active metabolite, dihydrotestosterone (DHT), acting on functioning androgen receptors. In Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS), the androgen receptors are completely non-functional due to an X-linked mutation. Therefore, the testes fail to descend and often remain in the abdominal cavity or become palpable in the inguinal canals, presenting as inguinal hernias in infancy.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
