    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 13-year-old child who was assigned female at birth and raised as a girl in a small village in the Dominican Republic is brought to a clinical geneticist. Over the past year, the child has experienced a deepening of the voice, significant development of skeletal muscle mass, and a dramatic enlargement of the clitoris, which now resembles a penis. In addition, bilateral masses in the labioscrotal folds have descended. Karyotype analysis reveals a 46,XY chromosomal complement. Endocrine evaluation shows high-normal levels of testosterone but extremely low levels of dihydrotestosterone (DHT). What is the clinical diagnosis, and what is the underlying genetic pathophysiology?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Swyer syndrome; a mutation in the SRY gene on the Y chromosome.",
      "B": "Persistent Mullerian Duct Syndrome; an autosomal recessive mutation in the AMH receptor gene.",
      "C": "Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia; an autosomal recessive mutation in the 21-hydroxylase gene.",
      "D": "5-alpha-reductase deficiency; an autosomal recessive mutation preventing the conversion of testosterone to DHT.",
      "E": "Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome; an X-linked recessive mutation in the androgen receptor gene."
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "preventing the conversion of testosterone to DHT. 65 A. Swyer syndrome; a mutation in the SRY gene on the Y chromosome.:  Incorrect  . Swyer syndrome (SRY mutation) results in a phenotypic female with streak gonads who does not virilize at puberty because there is no source of testosterone. B. Persistent Mullerian Duct Syndrome; an autosomal recessive mutation in the AMH receptor gene.:  Incorrect  . Persistent Mullerian Duct Syndrome  (PMDS) presents with typical male external genitalia at birth and normal pubertal development, with internal Mullerian organs found incidentally, not virilization of ambiguous genitalia. C. Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia; an autosomal recessive mutation in the 21-hydroxylase gene.:  Incorrect  . Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH)  virilizes XX females at birth, but this patient is 46,XY with low DHT and high testosterone. D. 5-alpha-reductase deficiency; an autosomal recessive mutation preventing the conversion of testosterone to DHT.:  Correct  . This is a classic presentation  of 5-alpha-reductase deficiency (often called 'guevedoces' or 'balls at 12' in the Salinas village of the Dominican Republic). This autosomal recessive condition prevents the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT). Because DHT is mandatory for the development of male external genitalia in utero, these 46,XY infants are born with female-appearing or ambiguous external genitalia. At puberty, the massive physiological surge of testosterone from the testes overcomes the enzymatic block, acting directly on tissues to cause profound virilization (phallus growth, deepening voice, muscle mass growth, and testicular descent). E. Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome; an X-linked recessive mutation in the androgen receptor gene.:  Incorrect  . Complete Androgen Insensitivity  Syndrome (CAIS) patients have completely insensitive receptors and never virilize at puberty; they develop breasts and remain phenotypic females.",
