    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A medical student is comparing the anatomical features of Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS) and 5-alpha-reductase deficiency. She notes that while both disorders occur in 46,XY individuals who have functioning testes, their internal ductal systems differ significantly. Which of the following correctly describes the status of the internal reproductive ducts (Wolffian and Mullerian) in patients with 5-alpha-reductase deficiency compared to CAIS?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "In both disorders, Wolffian structures are typically developed because they require testosterone, and Mullerian structures are typically developed because they require DHT.",
      "B": "In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, Mullerian structures are typically developed and Wolffian structures are absent; in CAIS, both systems are typically developed.",
      "C": "In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, Wolffian structures (epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles) are typically developed and Mullerian structures are regressed; in CAIS, both Wolffian and Mullerian structures are absent/rudimentary.",
      "D": "In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, both Wolffian and Mullerian structures are typically developed; in CAIS, both are absent/rudimentary.",
      "E": "In both disorders, Wolffian structures regress and Mullerian structures persist, resulting in a typical female internal reproductive tract with a uterus and tubes."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "vas deferens, seminal vesicles) are typically developed and Mullerian structures are regressed; in CAIS, both Wolffian and Mullerian structures are absent/rudimentary. A. In both disorders, Wolffian structures are typically developed because they require testosterone, and Mullerian structures are typically developed because they require DHT.: Incorrect  . Mullerian structures are regressed in both  conditions because both have functioning testes that secrete AMH. They are not typically developed. B. In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, Mullerian structures are typically developed and Wolffian structures are absent; in CAIS, both systems are typically developed.:  Incorrect  . Mullerian structures are regressed in both conditions because both have functioning testes that secrete AMH. They are not typically developed. C. In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, Wolffian structures (epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles) are typically developed and Mullerian structures are regressed; in CAIS, both Wolffian 66 and Mullerian structures are absent/rudimentary.:  Correct  . In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, testosterone levels and androgen receptors are completely normal. Testosterone directly stabilizes and drives the typical development of the Wolffian ducts (epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles) in utero, while AMH from the testes regresses the Mullerian ducts. In contrast, in CAIS, the androgen receptors are completely non-functional, meaning that the Wolffian ducts cannot respond to testosterone and therefore regress, leaving the patient with no internal male or female ductal structures (since AMH is still functional and regresses the Mullerian ducts). D. In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, both Wolffian and Mullerian structures are typically developed; in CAIS, both are absent/rudimentary.:  Incorrect  . Mullerian structures are regressed in both conditions because both have functioning testes that secrete AMH. They are not typically developed. E. In both disorders, Wolffian structures regress and Mullerian structures persist, resulting in a typical female internal reproductive tract with a uterus and tubes.:  Incorrect  . Mullerian structures are regressed in both conditions because both have functioning testes that secrete AMH. They are not typically developed.",
