    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "prevented the formation of Sertoli cells, so no AMH was produced to regress the Mullerian ducts. A. The high levels of adrenal androgens directly drove the differentiation of the Mullerian ducts while regressing the Wolffian ducts.:  Incorrect  . Adrenal  androgens do not drive Mullerian duct differentiation; paramesonephric development is a default pathway in the absence of AMH. B. The maternal placenta secreted massive amounts of estrogen, which selectively protected the Mullerian system from androgen-mediated regression.:  Incorrect  . Maternal estrogen is not required to protect the Mullerian ducts, and it does not halt androgen-mediated regression (which is prevented by the absence of testes/testosterone). C. The absence of SRY allowed typical ovarian differentiation and prevented the formation of Sertoli cells, so no AMH was produced to regress the Mullerian ducts.:  Correct  . In Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH) due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency in a 46,XX fetus, the lack of cortisol synthesis leads to hyper-secretion of pituitary ACTH, which overstimulates the adrenal cortex to produce massive amounts of adrenal androgens. However, because the fetus is XX with no SRY, the gonads differentiate typically into ovaries, and no Sertoli cells or AMH are present. In the absence of AMH, the Mullerian ducts typically persist to form the uterus, fallopian tubes, and upper vagina, which are protected from the high adrenal androgens (which virilize only the external genitalia). D. The 21-hydroxylase deficiency prevented the ovaries from responding to circulating pituitary gonadotropins, halting Wolffian duct growth.:  Incorrect  .  The mutated 21-hydroxylase enzyme is 67 involved in steroid synthesis in the adrenal cortex and does not interact with or block AMH receptors. E. The mutated 21-hydroxylase enzyme acted as a competitive agonist at the AMH receptor, blocking the regression of the uterus.:  Incorrect  .  The mutated 21-hydroxylase enzyme is involved in steroid synthesis in the adrenal cortex and does not interact with or block AMH receptors.",
