    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 16-year-old female presents to her gynecologist with primary amenorrhea. On physical examination, she is of normal height, has typical breast development, and typical female pubic and axillary hair. Her external genitalia are typically female. Pelvic ultrasound reveals a blind-ending vagina with a complete absence of the uterus, cervix, and fallopian tubes. Normal bilateral ovaries are visualized in the pelvis. Her karyotype is 46,XX, and her hormone levels are within the normal adult female range. What is her most likely diagnosis, and what embryological process failed to occur?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Uterine didelphys; complete failure of the bilateral paramesonephric ducts to fuse in the midline.",
      "B": "Septate uterus; complete failure of the Mullerian ducts to resorb their central vertical septum.",
      "C": "Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS); complete failure of the androgen receptors to respond to testosterone.",
      "D": "Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome; complete failure of development (agenesis) of the paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts.",
      "E": "Swyer syndrome; deletion of the SRY gene preventing testicular differentiation."
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "failure of development (agenesis) of the paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts. A. Uterine didelphys; complete failure of the bilateral paramesonephric ducts to fuse in the midline.:  Incorrect  . Uterine didelphys is a fusion  defect where the patient has two separate uteri and cervices, not a complete absence of the uterus and vagina. B. Septate uterus; complete failure of the Mullerian ducts to resorb their central vertical septum.: Incorrect  . A septate uterus is a minor resorption  defect where the uterus is present but divided by a septum; it does not cause absence of the uterus and vagina. C. Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS); complete failure of the androgen receptors to respond to testosterone.:  Incorrect  .  CAIS occurs in 46,XY individuals with testes, not 46,XX females with normal ovaries and typical pubic hair. D. Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome; complete failure of development (agenesis) of the paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts.:  Correct  . Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome (Mullerian agenesis) is characterized by the congenital absence of the uterus, fallopian tubes, and upper vagina in a 46,XX female. It is caused by the complete failure of development (agenesis) of the paramesonephric (Mullerian) 69 ducts. Because ovarian development and sex steroid production are completely normal, these patients undergo typical pubertal development, including typical breast development, normal female-pattern pubic/axillary hair (driven by typical adrenal/ovarian androgens), and normal female external genitalia, presenting only with primary amenorrhea. E. Swyer syndrome; deletion of the SRY gene preventing testicular differentiation.:  Incorrect  . Swyer syndrome occurs in 46,XY individuals and presents with streak gonads and a lack of breast development, not 46,XX with ovaries and typical breast development.",
