    "stem": "A pediatrician is performing a physical examination on a newborn male infant. On inspection of the penis, she notes that the urethral meatus is abnormally positioned on the ventral (underside) surface of the penile shaft, accompanied by a ventral curvature of the phallus (chordee). She explains to the parents that this is a common congenital anomaly and that circumcision should be postponed because the prepuce (foreskin) may be needed for future surgical reconstruction. 56 What is this congenital condition, and what is its embryological cause?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Epispadias; abnormal positioning of the genital tubercle leading to urethral opening on the dorsal surface.",
      "B": "Epispadias; incomplete fusion of the labioscrotal swellings in the midline.",
      "C": "Hypospadias; incomplete fusion of the urethral (urogenital) folds on the ventral surface of the penis.",
      "D": "Hypospadias; failure of the genital tubercle to migrate dorsally during the 4th week of development.",
      "E": "Phimosis; failure of the preputial cavity to canalize and separate from the glans penis."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "on the ventral surface of the penis. A. Epispadias; abnormal positioning of the genital tubercle leading to urethral opening on the dorsal surface.:  Incorrect  . Epispadias is characterized  by urethral opening on the dorsal surface, whereas this patient has a ventral urethral opening. B. Epispadias; incomplete fusion of the labioscrotal swellings in the midline.:  Incorrect  . Incomplete fusion of the labioscrotal swellings results in a bifid scrotum, not epispadias (which is a urethral opening on the dorsal surface). C. Hypospadias; incomplete fusion of the urethral (urogenital) folds on the ventral surface of the penis.:  Correct  . Hypospadias is a common congenital  anomaly caused by the incomplete fusion of the urethral (urogenital) folds on the ventral surface of the developing penis. This results in the urethral meatus being abnormally positioned on the underside (ventral aspect) of the penile shaft or glans. It is often accompanied by chordee (ventral curvature). Epispadias, by contrast, is characterized by the urethral opening on the dorsal (upper) surface and is caused by abnormal positioning of the genital tubercle. D. Hypospadias; failure of the genital tubercle to migrate dorsally during the 4th week of development.:  Incorrect  . Hypospadias is a fusion defect  of the urethral folds, not a failure of the genital tubercle to migrate. E. Phimosis; failure of the preputial cavity to canalize and separate from the glans penis.: Incorrect  . Phimosis is the inability to retract the  foreskin, which does not involve abnormal placement of the urethral meatus.",
    "pearl": "",
