    "topic": "Atypical Development of the (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A clinical geneticist is explaining the developmental pathway of Swyer syndrome (46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis) to a group of medical students. He notes that the genetic mutation directly impacts the very first step of the sexual differentiation cascade. Which of the following correctly describes the genetic trigger, the direct gonadal outcome, and the hormone levels expected in Swyer syndrome?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "SRY translocation to autosome -> hyper-activation of SOX9 -> bipotential gonads form dysgenic testes -> elevated testosterone and AMH.",
      "B": "SRY gene mutation/deletion -> failure of Testes-Determining Factor (TDF) -> bipotential gonads fail to form testes and become streak gonads -> no testosterone or AMH produced.",
      "C": "CYP21A2 gene mutation -> failure of 21-hydroxylase -> bipotential gonads form streak ovaries -> high adrenal androgens and high AMH.",
      "D": "Androgen receptor mutation -> failure to utilize testosterone -> bipotential gonads differentiate into ovaries -> high estrogen and low AMH.",
      "E": "5-alpha-reductase mutation -> failure of peripheral conversion of T to DHT -> bipotential gonads fail to form any gonadal tissue -> absent hormones."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "Factor (TDF) -> bipotential gonads fail to form testes and become streak gonads -> no testosterone or AMH produced. A. SRY translocation to autosome -> hyper-activation of SOX9 -> bipotential gonads form dysgenic testes -> elevated testosterone and AMH.:  Incorrect  . SRY translocation to autosomes would drive testicular differentiation, not streak gonads. B. SRY gene mutation/deletion -> failure of Testes-Determining Factor (TDF) -> bipotential gonads fail to form testes and become streak gonads -> no testosterone or AMH produced.: Correct  . Swyer syndrome is caused by a mutation or  deletion of the SRY gene on the Y chromosome, which encodes Testes-Determining Factor (TDF). In the absence of functional TDF, the bipotential gonads fail to differentiate into testes and instead develop as non-functional streak gonads. Because no functional testes are present, there is a complete lack of Leydig cells (no testosterone produced) and Sertoli cells (no AMH produced). Consequently, the Mullerian ducts develop into a typical uterus and fallopian tubes, and the external genitalia remain typically female, with extremely low estrogen and testosterone levels. C. CYP21A2 gene mutation -> failure of 21-hydroxylase -> bipotential gonads form streak ovaries -> high adrenal androgens and high AMH.:  Incorrect  .  CYP21A2 mutations cause CAH in XX females, who have normal ovaries, not XY with streak gonads. D. Androgen receptor mutation -> failure to utilize testosterone -> bipotential gonads differentiate into ovaries -> high estrogen and low AMH.:  Incorrect  . Androgen receptor mutations cause CAIS, where testes develop typically and secrete AMH, resulting in no uterus. E. 5-alpha-reductase mutation -> failure of peripheral conversion of T to DHT -> bipotential gonads fail to form any gonadal tissue -> absent hormones.:  Incorrect  . 5-alpha-reductase mutations affect peripheral DHT conversion and do not prevent testes formation. 72",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
