    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "During a lecture on Differences of Sexual Development (DSD), the instructor emphasizes a key clinical pearl: 'Not every DSD presents with ambiguous genitalia at birth.' Which of the following lists contains only DSD conditions that typically present with typical, non-ambiguous external genitalia at birth and are often only diagnosed at puberty or later?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Persistent Mullerian Duct Syndrome (PMDS), Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), and hypospadias.",
      "B": "Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome, 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, and Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH).",
      "C": "Swyer syndrome, Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), and Partial Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (PAIS).",
      "D": "Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), Partial Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (PAIS), and 5-alpha-reductase deficiency.",
      "E": "Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS), Swyer syndrome (46,XY gonadal dysgenesis), and Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome."
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "syndrome (46,XY gonadal dysgenesis), and Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome. A. Persistent Mullerian Duct Syndrome (PMDS), Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), and hypospadias.:  Incorrect  . CAH, PAIS, and 5-alpha-reductase  deficiency frequently present with ambiguous external genitalia at birth, making this list incorrect. B. Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome, 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, and Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH).:  Incorrect  .  CAH, PAIS, and 5-alpha-reductase deficiency frequently present with ambiguous external genitalia at birth, making this list incorrect. C. Swyer syndrome, Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), and Partial Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (PAIS).:  Incorrect  . CAH, PAIS, and 5-alpha-reductase  deficiency frequently present with ambiguous external genitalia at birth, making this list incorrect. D. Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), Partial Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (PAIS), and 5-alpha-reductase deficiency.:  Incorrect  . CAH, PAIS,  and 5-alpha-reductase deficiency frequently present with ambiguous external genitalia at birth, making this list incorrect. E. Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS), Swyer syndrome (46,XY gonadal dysgenesis), and Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome.:  Correct  . Not every Differences of Sexual Development (DSD) condition presents with ambiguous genitalia at birth. Patients with Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS), Swyer syndrome, and Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome are born with typically female external genitalia and are raised as girls, typically escaping clinical notice until they present with primary amenorrhea at puberty. In contrast, conditions like CAH in XX females, PAIS, and 5-alpha-reductase deficiency frequently present with ambiguous external genitalia at birth.",
