    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 22-year-old female presents to her gynecologist due to irregular menses, occurring only every 60 to 90 days. For the past year, she has noticed that her menstrual flow varies unpredictably from spotting to heavy, prolonged bleeding. An endocrine evaluation confirms that she is experiencing chronic anovulation. She asks her physician why her bleeding is so irregular and unpredictable if she is not ovulating. Which of the following describes the correct molecular pathophysiology of this patient's irregular bleeding?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Constant high levels of progesterone cause rapid endometrial atrophy, leading to continuous desquamation and spotting.",
      "B": "Lack of ovulation means no corpus luteum forms and no progesterone is produced; this results in unopposed estrogen causing continuous endometrial proliferation until the tissue outgrows its blood supply and sloughs unpredictably.",
      "C": "Elevated follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) directly stimulates endometrial spiral arteriole vasoconstriction, triggering chronic tissue necrosis and localized hemostatic failure.",
      "D": "Absence of LH pulsatility causes the endometrium to secrete high levels of prostaglandins, preventing the typical platelet plug formation during shedding.",
      "E": "A rapid drop in serum human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) prevents typical decidualization, forcing the endometrium to slough in disorganized, incomplete fragments."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "progesterone is produced; this results in unopposed estrogen causing continuous endometrial proliferation until the tissue outgrows its blood supply and sloughs unpredictably. \u25cf  A. Constant high levels of progesterone cause rapid endometrial atrophy, leading to continuous desquamation and spotting.  -  Incorrect  .  In anovulatory cycles, there is a complete  absence  of progesterone because no corpus  luteum is formed. \u25cf  B. Lack of ovulation means no corpus luteum forms and no progesterone is produced; this results in unopposed estrogen causing continuous endometrial proliferation until the tissue outgrows its blood supply and sloughs unpredictably. -  Correct  . Without ovulation, no corpus luteum forms,  and  no progesterone  is produced. The endometrium is subjected to continuous,  unopposed estrogen stimulation. This causes the endometrium to continuously proliferate without the stabilizing effects of progesterone. Eventually, the growing endometrium outgrows its blood supply, leading to irregular, unpredictable necrosis, sloughing, and anovulatory uterine bleeding. \u25cf  C. Elevated follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) directly stimulates endometrial spiral arteriole vasoconstriction, triggering chronic tissue necrosis and localized hemostatic failure.  -  Incorrect  . FSH acts on the ovaries  to stimulate folliculogenesis, not directly on the endometrium, and does not cause spiral arteriole vasoconstriction. \u25cf  D. Absence of LH pulsatility causes the endometrium to secrete high levels of prostaglandins, preventing the typical platelet plug formation during shedding.  - Incorrect  . Prostaglandin production is typical of  ovulatory menses; anovulatory bleeding is a structural/ischemic failure of a hyper-proliferated endometrium. \u25cf  E. A rapid drop in serum human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) prevents typical decidualization, forcing the endometrium to slough in disorganized, incomplete fragments.  -  Incorrect  . This mechanism describes a  miscarriage or pregnancy loss, not typical non-pregnant anovulatory uterine bleeding.",
