    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 19-year-old female collegiate cross-country runner presents to the sports medicine clinic with a 6-month history of secondary amenorrhea. Her height is 5 ft 5 in, her weight is 108 lbs, and her body mass index (BMI) is 18.0 kg/m\u00b2. She reports eating a highly restricted, low-fat, low-carbohydrate diet to optimize her running performance. The clinician explains that her amenorrhea is a form of Functional Hypothalamic Amenorrhea (FHA) driven by inadequate caloric intake relative to her high energy expenditure. Which of the following correctly describes the peripheral metabolic signals and hormonal alterations that suppress her HPO axis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Elevated insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) and decreased ghrelin directly inhibit pituitary FSH and LH gene expression.",
      "B": "Decreased ghrelin and elevated leptin levels act synergistically on the hypothalamus to downregulate GnRH receptors.",
      "C": "Hypoleptinemia due to a drop in fat mass reduces hypothalamic kisspeptin and GnRH secretion, while elevated ghrelin and hypercortisolemia further suppress LH pulsatility and GnRH release.",
      "D": "Decreased cortisol and elevated insulin stimulate rapid ovarian theca cell hyperplasia, producing massive levels of inhibitory androgens.",
      "E": "Elevated thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) suppresses kisspeptin gene transcription in the infundibular nucleus of the hypothalamus."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "kisspeptin and GnRH secretion, while elevated ghrelin and hypercortisolemia further suppress LH pulsatility and GnRH release. \u25cf  A. Elevated insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) and decreased ghrelin directly inhibit pituitary FSH and LH gene expression.  -  Incorrect  .  IGF-1 is typically decreased in malnutrition/underweight, and ghrelin is elevated, not decreased. \u25cf  B. Decreased ghrelin and elevated leptin levels act synergistically on the hypothalamus to downregulate GnRH receptors.  -  Incorrect  .  Underweight patients have elevated ghrelin and decreased (not elevated) leptin. 113 \u25cf  C. Hypoleptinemia due to a drop in fat mass reduces hypothalamic kisspeptin and GnRH secretion, while elevated ghrelin and hypercortisolemia further suppress LH pulsatility and GnRH release.  -  Correct  . In energy  deficiency/underweight disorders, the drop in adipose tissue mass causes  hypoleptinemia  (decreased leptin). Because leptin is a key metabolic signal that stimulates hypothalamic  kisspeptin  and  GnRH release, low leptin leads to suppressed GnRH pulsatility. Simultaneously, underweight states cause  elevated ghrelin  (which directly inhibits  LH pulsatility) and hypercortisolemia  from the chronic stress response  (which further suppresses GnRH secretion). \u25cf  D. Decreased cortisol and elevated insulin stimulate rapid ovarian theca cell hyperplasia, producing massive levels of inhibitory androgens.  -  Incorrect  . Cortisol is elevated, not decreased, and insulin levels are typically low (not elevated) in FHA/underweight. \u25cf  E. Elevated thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) suppresses kisspeptin gene transcription in the infundibular nucleus of the hypothalamus.  -  Incorrect  . TRH does not suppress kisspeptin; TRH is elevated in primary hypothyroidism, whereas FHA represents a state of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and central thyroid axis suppression.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
