    "id": "W1_Notebook_W4_5_Menstruation_Irregularities_20Q_84",
    "week": "W1",
    "lecture": "Menstruation Irregularities (Notebook)",
    "file": "Notebook_W4_5_Menstruation_Irregularities_20Q.md",
    "qnum": 84,
    "topic": "Menstruation Irregularities (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "An 18-year-old college freshman is brought to the student health center due to secondary amenorrhea. She had typical, regular menses up until 4 months ago when she moved to college. She reports feeling overwhelmed by her academic workload and social adjustments but denies any changes in weight, diet, or exercise. On examination, her BMI is 22.0 kg/m\u00b2. Pregnancy test is negative, and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) and prolactin levels are within typical limits. The clinician suspects stress-induced Functional Hypothalamic Amenorrhea (FHA). 102 Which of the following describes the correct neuroendocrine pathway mediating her stress-induced HPO axis suppression?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Stress-induced hypersecretion of corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) directly inhibits GnRH pulse frequency, while elevated cortisol suppresses HPO function at both the hypothalamic and pituitary levels.",
      "B": "Elevated ACTH directly binds to ovarian granulosa cell receptors, competitively blocking FSH-mediated aromatase activity.",
      "C": "Under-activation of the sympathetic nervous system prevents the typical adrenergic stimulation of hypothalamic GnRH neurons.",
      "D": "Chronic emotional stress stimulates high levels of endogenous opioids that selectively upregulate pituitary FSH and LH receptors, causing receptor downregulation.",
      "E": "Elevated levels of melatonin during the daytime shift the HPG axis into a permanent, non-pulsatile prepubertal state of quiescence."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "hormone (CRH) directly inhibits GnRH pulse frequency, while elevated cortisol suppresses HPO function at both the hypothalamic and pituitary levels. \u25cf  A. Stress-induced hypersecretion of corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) directly inhibits GnRH pulse frequency, while elevated cortisol suppresses HPO function at both the hypothalamic and pituitary levels.  -  Correct  . Chronic emotional stress activates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, leading to hypersecretion of CRH, ACTH, and cortisol.  CRH directly inhibits  GnRH pulse frequency  at the hypothalamus, and  cortisol suppresses HPO axis function  at both the hypothalamic (inhibiting GnRH) and anterior pituitary (inhibiting LH/FSH release) levels, resulting in stress-induced FHA. \u25cf  B. Elevated ACTH directly binds to ovarian granulosa cell receptors, competitively blocking FSH-mediated aromatase activity.  -  Incorrect  .  ACTH acts on the adrenal cortex, not directly on ovarian granulosa cells to block FSH or aromatase. \u25cf  C. Under-activation of the sympathetic nervous system prevents the typical adrenergic stimulation of hypothalamic GnRH neurons.  -  Incorrect  . Stress is associated with sympathetic  over-activation  , not under-activation. \u25cf  D. Chronic emotional stress stimulates high levels of endogenous opioids that selectively upregulate pituitary FSH and LH receptors, causing receptor downregulation.  -  Incorrect  . Opioids suppress GnRH  release but do not upregulate pituitary receptors to cause downregulation. \u25cf  E. Elevated levels of melatonin during the daytime shift the HPG axis into a permanent, non-pulsatile prepubertal state of quiescence.  -  Incorrect  . Melatonin regulates circadian sleep-wake cycles but does not shift the axis into a prepubertal state in this manner during chronic daytime stress.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
