    "id": "W1_Notebook_W4_5_Menstruation_Irregularities_20Q_89",
    "week": "W1",
    "lecture": "Menstruation Irregularities (Notebook)",
    "file": "Notebook_W4_5_Menstruation_Irregularities_20Q.md",
    "qnum": 89,
    "topic": "Menstruation Irregularities (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 34-year-old G1P1 female presents to her obstetrician 6 months postpartum complaining of severe fatigue, persistent cold intolerance, and a failure to resume her menses. She was unable to breastfeed because she did not produce any milk. Her prenatal course was unremarkable, but her delivery was complicated by a severe postpartum hemorrhage requiring emergency uterine artery embolization and blood transfusions. On physical examination, her blood pressure is 92/58 mmHg. Laboratory testing is notable for suppressed TSH, free T4, ACTH, FSH, LH, and prolactin. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the underlying pathophysiology of her condition?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Prolactinoma; a benign tumor compressing the pituitary stalk, causing global panhypopituitarism.",
      "B": "Sheehan's syndrome; ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary gland due to severe obstetric hemorrhage and hypotension.",
      "C": "Kallmann syndrome; congenital failure of GnRH-producing neurons to migrate to the preoptic area of the hypothalamus.",
      "D": "Primary Ovarian Insufficiency; autoimmune destruction of the ovarian follicles triggered by placental antigens.",
      "E": "McCune-Albright syndrome; a somatic activating mutation in the Gnas gene causing autonomous hormone hypersecretion."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "gland due to severe obstetric hemorrhage and hypotension. \u25cf  A. Prolactinoma; a benign tumor compressing the pituitary stalk, causing global panhypopituitarism.  -  Incorrect  . A prolactinoma would  present with elevated, not suppressed, prolactin. \u25cf  B. Sheehan's syndrome; ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary gland due to severe obstetric hemorrhage and hypotension.  -  Correct  .  This patient has Sheehan's syndrome  , which is characterized by  ischemic  necrosis of the anterior pituitary gland  . During pregnancy, the pituitary gland  hypertrophies and becomes highly vascular. If delivery is complicated by severe postpartum hemorrhage and systemic hypotension, the hypertrophied anterior pituitary suffers severe ischemia and necrosis. This leads to panhypopituitarism, presenting postpartum with an inability to lactate (due to lack of prolactin), failure to resume menses/secondary amenorrhea (due to lack of LH/FSH), fatigue, hypothyroid findings (due to lack of TSH), and hypotension. \u25cf  C. Kallmann syndrome; congenital failure of GnRH-producing neurons to migrate to the preoptic area of the hypothalamus.  -  Incorrect  .  Kallmann syndrome is a congenital disorder presenting with delayed puberty/primary amenorrhea and anosmia, not secondary panhypopituitarism following postpartum hemorrhage. \u25cf  D. Primary Ovarian Insufficiency; autoimmune destruction of the ovarian follicles triggered by placental antigens.  -  Incorrect  . POI  is characterized by primary ovarian failure, which would present with elevated (hypergonadotropic), not suppressed, FSH and LH. \u25cf  E. McCune-Albright syndrome; a somatic activating mutation in the Gnas gene causing autonomous hormone hypersecretion.  -  Incorrect  .  McCune-Albright syndrome causes hyper-functioning endocrinopathies and peripheral precocious puberty, which is the exact opposite of panhypopituitarism. 118",
