    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "of hypothalamic dopamine (which tonically inhibits prolactin) and GnRH from reaching the anterior pituitary, leading to \"stalk effect\" hyperprolactinemia and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. \u25cf  A. The granulomatous mass directly secretes prolactin, while the inflammatory cytokines suppress the ovarian response to FSH.  -  Incorrect  . Sarcoid granulomas do not synthesize or secrete prolactin. \u25cf  B. Compression of the pituitary stalk disrupts the descending transit of hypothalamic dopamine (which tonically inhibits prolactin) and GnRH from reaching the anterior pituitary, leading to \"stalk effect\" hyperprolactinemia and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism.  -  Correct  . Hypothalamic  dopamine and GnRH travel down the pituitary stalk to reach the anterior pituitary. Any compressive lesion (e.g., suprasellar tumors, metastases, or granulomatous sarcoid lesions) that disrupts the pituitary stalk  blocks the descending pathway  of dopamine  . Because dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin, this \"stalk effect\" leads to a loss of inhibition and mild hyperprolactinemia. Simultaneously, the blockage of GnRH transport prevents the stimulation of gonadotrophs, resulting in  hypogonadotropic  hypogonadism (suppressed LH and FSH) and secondary amenorrhea. \u25cf  C. Compression of the cavernous sinus prevents the typical venous drainage of prolactin, leading to passive pelvic congestion and anovulation.  -  Incorrect  . Pituitary stalk lesions do not act by compressing the cavernous sinus to block venous drainage or cause pelvic congestion. \u25cf  D. Granulomatous sarcoid lesions synthesize high levels of TRH, which directly stimulates the pituitary lactotrophs to secrete prolactin.  -  Incorrect  . TRH is not synthesized by sarcoid granulomas. \u25cf  E. The pituitary stalk compression stimulates high-frequency GnRH pulses, which selectively downregulates pituitary LH and FSH receptors.  -  Incorrect  . Compression blocks GnRH transport, suppressing rather than stimulating GnRH pulses.",
