    "topic": "Menstruation Irregularities (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 23-year-old female presents to her primary care physician due to a 1-year history of irregular menses, with cycles occurring every 45 to 75 days. On physical examination, her BMI is 28.5 kg/m\u00b2. She exhibits moderate hirsutism of the upper lip and chin and severe facial acne. To establish a diagnosis of Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS), the clinician utilizes the 2018 International Evidence-based Guideline Criteria (built on the consensus-based 2003 Rotterdam Criteria). 106 Which of the following describes the diagnostic requirements to confirm PCOS in this patient?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The patient must possess all three findings: clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, chronic anovulation, and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound.",
      "B": "The patient must have at least two of the three criteria: clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, ovulatory dysfunction (amenorrhea/oligomenorrhea), and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound (or elevated AMH), after excluding other causes of androgen excess.",
      "C": "The patient must have biopsy-confirmed polycystic ovaries and insulin resistance, regardless of her menstrual pattern or androgen levels.",
      "D": "The patient must have an elevated LH-to-FSH ratio greater than 3:1 on biochemical testing, along with a documented family history of diabetes.",
      "E": "The patient must have a documented 17-hydroxyprogesterone level greater than 200 ng/dL to establish biochemical hyperandrogenism."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "biochemical hyperandrogenism, ovulatory dysfunction (amenorrhea/oligomenorrhea), and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound (or elevated AMH), after excluding other causes of androgen excess. \u25cf  A. The patient must possess all three findings: clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, chronic anovulation, and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound.  -  Incorrect  . This describes the original,  highly restrictive Stein-Leventhal criteria; modern Rotterdam criteria only require two of the three findings. \u25cf  B. The patient must have at least two of the three criteria: clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, ovulatory dysfunction (amenorrhea/oligomenorrhea), and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound (or elevated AMH), after excluding other causes of androgen excess.  -  Correct  . Under  the  Rotterdam Criteria 119 (incorporated into the 2018/2023 International Guidelines), a diagnosis of Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) requires the presence of at least  two of the three  key clinical findings: 1) Clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, 2) Ovulatory dysfunction (amenorrhea or oligomenorrhea), and 3) Polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound (or elevated AMH as per 2023 guidelines). Crucially, other disorders of androgen excess (such as CAH, Cushing's, or adrenal tumors)  must be  excluded  . \u25cf  C. The patient must have biopsy-confirmed polycystic ovaries and insulin resistance, regardless of her menstrual pattern or androgens.  -  Incorrect  . Ovarian biopsy is never used to diagnose PCOS, and insulin resistance is a common metabolic feature but is not a formal diagnostic criterion. \u25cf  D. The patient must have an elevated LH-to-FSH ratio greater than 3:1 on biochemical testing, along with a documented family history of diabetes.  - Incorrect  . While an elevated LH-to-FSH ratio is a  common historical finding, it is not part of the formal Rotterdam criteria, nor is a family history of diabetes required. \u25cf  E. The patient must have a documented 17-hydroxyprogesterone level greater than 200 ng/dL to establish biochemical hyperandrogenism.  -  Incorrect  . 17-hydroxyprogesterone is used to screen for and  exclude  Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), not to establish biochemical hyperandrogenism in PCOS.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
