    "rationale": "tumors, and Cushing's syndrome; screened using total testosterone, 17-hydroxyprogesterone, and DHEAS. \u25cf  A. Hyperprolactinemia, Sheehan's syndrome, and Swyer syndrome; screened using FSH and LH levels.  -  Incorrect  . While hyperprolactinemia  is ruled out, Sheehan's and Swyer syndrome do not present with clinical hyperandrogenism (acne, hirsutism). \u25cf  B. Kallmann syndrome, Turner syndrome, and primary hypothyroidism; screened using TSH and free T4.  -  Incorrect  . These conditions  cause amenorrhea/delayed puberty but are not primary causes of androgen excess that mimic PCOS hirsutism. \u25cf  C. Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), androgen-producing tumors, and Cushing's syndrome; screened using total testosterone, 17-hydroxyprogesterone, and DHEAS.  -  Correct  . PCOS is a diagnosis of exclusion.  To confirm the diagnosis, alternative causes of androgen excess must be ruled out. The primary conditions to exclude are  Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH)  (screened  using 17-hydroxyprogesterone  ),  androgen-secreting tumors  of the ovary or adrenal glands (screened using  total testosterone and DHEAS  ), and  Cushing's syndrome  . \u25cf  D. McCune-Albright syndrome, primary ovarian insufficiency, and gestational diabetes; screened using oral glucose tolerance test.  -  Incorrect  . These are not primary disorders of androgen excess that require systematic exclusion before diagnosing PCOS. 120 \u25cf  E. Fragile X-associated primary ovarian insufficiency and Kallmann syndrome; screened using FMR1 gene repeat analysis.  -  Incorrect  .  These cause amenorrhea but do not cause hyperandrogenism.",
