    "qnum": 93,
    "topic": "Menstruation Irregularities (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A reproductive endocrinologist is explaining the complex neuroendocrine and metabolic pathophysiology of Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) to a group of medical students. 107 He stresses that PCOS is not simply an ovarian disorder but is characterized by a vicious cycle of pituitary and metabolic dysregulation. Which of the following correctly pairs the neuroendocrine and metabolic abnormalities that drive excess androgen production in PCOS?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Decreased GnRH pulse frequency favoring FSH release (which hyper-stimulates theca cells) and low insulin levels (which prevent adrenal steroidogenesis).",
      "B": "Constant, non-pulsatile GnRH secretion causing pituitary atrophy, combined with increased hepatic production of sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG).",
      "C": "Increased GnRH pulse frequency favoring LH release (which stimulates ovarian theca cells to produce excess androgens) and insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia (which directly stimulates ovarian/adrenal androgen production and reduces hepatic SHBG).",
      "D": "Elevated FSH levels driving rapid follicular maturation and ovulation of multiple oocytes, combined with elevated leptin levels that stimulate adrenal cortisol release.",
      "E": "Suppressed LH levels preventing theca cell steroidogenesis, combined with high-affinity binding of testosterone to circulating albumin."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "stimulates ovarian theca cells to produce excess androgens) and insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia (which directly stimulates ovarian/adrenal androgen production and reduces hepatic SHBG). \u25cf  A. Decreased GnRH pulse frequency favoring FSH release (which hyper-stimulates theca cells) and low insulin levels (which prevent adrenal steroidogenesis).  -  Incorrect  . PCOS is characterized  by  increased  (not decreased) GnRH pulse frequency and  hyperinsulinemia  (not low  insulin). \u25cf  B. Constant, non-pulsatile GnRH secretion causing pituitary atrophy, combined with increased hepatic production of sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG).  - Incorrect  . GnRH remains highly pulsatile, and hepatic  production of SHBG is  decreased (not increased) in PCOS, leading to higher free androgens. \u25cf  C. Increased GnRH pulse frequency favoring LH release (which stimulates ovarian theca cells to produce excess androgens) and insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia (which directly stimulates ovarian/adrenal androgen production and reduces hepatic SHBG).  -  Correct  .  The core pathophysiology of PCOS involves two primary axes: 1)  Increased GnRH pulse frequency  at the hypothalamus selectively stimulates  pituitary gonadotrophs to secrete LH over FSH  . This high LH stimulates  ovarian theca  cells  to synthesize excess androgens (testosterone and androstenedione). 2)  Insulin  resistance  leads to compensatory  hyperinsulinemia  , which directly stimulates  both ovarian and adrenal androgen production and  represses hepatic synthesis  of Sex Hormone-Binding Globulin (SHBG)  , resulting in elevated free, biologically  active androgens. \u25cf  D. Elevated FSH levels driving rapid follicular maturation and ovulation of multiple oocytes, combined with elevated leptin levels that stimulate adrenal cortisol release.  -  Incorrect  . FSH is relatively low/suppressed  in PCOS, which impairs follicular maturation and prevents ovulation. \u25cf  E. Suppressed LH levels preventing theca cell steroidogenesis, combined with high-affinity binding of testosterone to circulating albumin.  -  Incorrect  . LH levels are elevated, not suppressed, and SHBG is decreased, which increases free testosterone.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
