    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 29-year-old obese female is diagnosed with Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS). She is not currently interested in pregnancy but is concerned about her future health. She asks her physician about the long-term health risks associated with PCOS and why these risks develop. Which of the following correctly describes this patient's long-term health risks and their underlying pathophysiology?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Osteoporosis and osteopenia due to a lack of estrogen exposure from chronic anovulation.",
      "B": "Type II Diabetes Mellitus (2-5 fold increased risk requiring screening), atherogenic dyslipidemia, and a 2.7-fold increased risk of endometrial cancer due to chronic endometrial exposure to unopposed estrogen.",
      "C": "Primary ovarian insufficiency and early menopause due to rapid follicular depletion, and a high risk of cervical cancer due to chronic progesterone exposure.",
      "D": "Bilateral renal agenesis due to embryological link with the Mullerian ducts, and hypothyroidism due to TRH sequestration.",
      "E": "Persistent hypoprolactinemia due to hypothalamic exhaustion, and autoimmune adrenal insufficiency."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "screening), atherogenic dyslipidemia, and a 2.7-fold increased risk of endometrial cancer due to chronic endometrial exposure to unopposed estrogen. 121 \u25cf  A. Osteoporosis and osteopenia due to a lack of estrogen exposure from chronic anovulation.  -  Incorrect  . PCOS patients have normal  to elevated estrogen levels (since they do not ovulate, but continuous follicular growth produces estrogen, which is unopposed), so they are not at risk for hypoestrogenic osteoporosis. \u25cf  B. Type II Diabetes Mellitus (2-5 fold increased risk requiring screening), atherogenic dyslipidemia, and a 2.7-fold increased risk of endometrial cancer due to chronic endometrial exposure to unopposed estrogen.  -  Correct  . PCOS is associated with significant metabolic and oncological risks: 1)  Type II Diabetes Mellitus (2- to 5-fold increased risk, affecting 8\u201310% of patients;  all PCOS patients must be screened  ), 2)  Atherogenic dyslipidemia  (elevated LDL  and triglycerides, low HDL), and 3)  Endometrial cancer  (due to chronic endometrial  proliferation stimulated by unopposed estrogen  in the absence of progesterone,  carrying a  2.7-fold increased risk  or 9% lifetime risk). \u25cf  C. Primary ovarian insufficiency and early menopause due to rapid follicular depletion, and a high risk of cervical cancer due to chronic progesterone exposure.  -  Incorrect  . PCOS does not cause POI, and  they have low (not high) progesterone exposure. Cervical cancer is linked to HPV, not progesterone. \u25cf  D. Bilateral renal agenesis due to embryological link with the Mullerian ducts, and hypothyroidism due to TRH sequestration.  -  Incorrect  .  These are unrelated congenital anomalies or separate endocrine pathologies. \u25cf  E. Persistent hypoprolactinemia due to hypothalamic exhaustion, and autoimmune adrenal insufficiency.  -  Incorrect  . PCOS  does not cause hypoprolactinemia or adrenal insufficiency.",
    "pearl": "",
