    "rationale": "sensitivity in peripheral tissues; it improves glucose tolerance but is no longer indicated as first-line therapy for hyperandrogenemia, menstrual irregularity, or weight loss. \u25cf  A. It is the gold-standard, first-line therapy recommended for hyperandrogenemia, hirsutism, menstrual irregularity, and weight loss.  -  Incorrect  . According to international evidence-based guidelines, metformin is no longer first-line therapy for these indications. \u25cf  B. It acts as a direct LH receptor antagonist, completely halting ovarian androgen production, and is indicated as first-line therapy.  -  Incorrect  . Metformin does not act as an LH receptor antagonist. \u25cf  C. It decreases hepatic glucose output and increases insulin sensitivity in peripheral tissues; it improves glucose tolerance but is no longer indicated as first-line therapy for hyperandrogenemia, menstrual irregularity, or weight loss.  - Correct  . Metformin is an insulin sensitizer that  decreases  hepatic glucose output  and increases peripheral glucose utilization  . While it  improves glucose tolerance and can delay or prevent progression to Type II Diabetes in patients with known glucose intolerance,  it is no longer indicated as first-line  therapy  for hyperandrogenemia, menstrual irregularity, weight loss, or ovulation induction. \u25cf  D. It acts as an aromatase inhibitor to block the conversion of androgens to estrogens, and is the first-line therapy for ovulation induction.  -  Incorrect  . Metformin is not an aromatase inhibitor (letrozole is first-line for ovulation induction in PCOS). \u25cf  E. It is an exogenous progesterone-mimetic that directly induces withdrawal menses without affecting insulin or glucose pathways.  -  Incorrect  . Metformin has no direct progesterone activity; its effects are entirely metabolic. 123",
