    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "repeats; elevated FMR1 mRNA levels sequester vital RNA-binding proteins, causing cellular toxicity in the oocytes. \u25cf  A. Complete deletion of the SRY gene on the Y chromosome, preventing testicular differentiation and leading to accelerated follicular loss.  -  Incorrect  . This describes Swyer syndrome, which presents with primary amenorrhea and a 46,XY karyotype, not POI in a 46,XX female. \u25cf  B. Fragile X Premutation (FMR1 premutation) with 55-200 CGG repeats; elevated FMR1 mRNA levels sequester vital RNA-binding proteins, causing cellular toxicity in the oocytes.  -  Correct  . The  Fragile X Premutation  (characterized by  55 to 200 CGG repeats  in the  FMR1  gene) is the most common known  genetic cause of Primary Ovarian Insufficiency (termed FXPOI), seen in 12\u201328% of familial POI cases. The molecular pathophysiology involves the transcription of  elevated FMR1 mRNA levels  , which accumulate and  sequester vital RNA-binding proteins  ,  leading to cellular toxicity and accelerated apoptosis of the oocytes. \u25cf  C. A homozygous mutation in the CYP21A2 gene, causing 21-hydroxylase deficiency and subsequent adrenal androgen excess.  -  Incorrect  . This describes Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia, which does not cause POI. \u25cf  D. A somatic activating mutation in the Gnas gene, leading to Gs-protein overactivation and autonomous ovarian follicle depletion.  -  Incorrect  . This describes McCune-Albright syndrome, which causes peripheral precocious puberty, not simple POI. 124 \u25cf  E. Mutation in the KAL1 gene on the X chromosome, disrupting the migration of GnRH neurons from the olfactory placode.  -  Incorrect  .  This describes Kallmann syndrome, which causes hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, not POI.",
