    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 14-year-old girl is evaluated for primary amenorrhea. Her past medical history is notable for childhood Hodgkin lymphoma, which was treated at age 10 with pelvic radiation and chemotherapy, including high-dose alkylating agents. On physical examination, she has prepubertal breast development (Tanner Stage I) and absent pubic hair. Pelvic ultrasound reveals a very small, infantile uterus, a small cervix, and bilateral streak-like ovaries. Laboratory evaluation demonstrates severely elevated FSH and LH levels. What is the most likely explanation for her physical and imaging findings?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Alkylating agents are known ovarian toxins that caused Primary Ovarian Insufficiency (POI), and childhood pelvic radiation caused direct tissue injury resulting in a small uterus and cervix.",
      "B": "The chemotherapy selectively mutated the SRY gene, preventing typical ovarian maturation and uterine growth.",
      "C": "Pelvic radiation caused hypothalamic-pituitary suppression, resulting in hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with low FSH and LH.",
      "D": "Childhood lymphoma frequently metastasizes to the pituitary gland, compressing the pituitary stalk and preventing GnRH secretion.",
      "E": "Autoimmune antibodies to theca cells were generated in response to the Hodgkin lymphoma tumor antigens."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "Ovarian Insufficiency (POI), and childhood pelvic radiation caused direct tissue injury resulting in a small uterus and cervix. \u25cf  A. Alkylating agents are known ovarian toxins that caused Primary Ovarian Insufficiency (POI), and childhood pelvic radiation caused direct tissue injury resulting in a small uterus and cervix.  -  Correct  .  Chemotherapeutic agents, particularly  alkylating agents  , are highly toxic to  the ovaries and are a major cause of acquired Primary Ovarian Insufficiency (POI). Additionally, receiving  pelvic radiation before puberty  causes direct tissue injury to the  pelvic organs, resulting in a permanently small, infantile uterus and cervix  due  to a lack of local estrogen exposure and direct structural damage. Her elevated gonadotropins (FSH/LH) confirm primary ovarian failure (hypergonadotropic hypogonadism). \u25cf  B. The chemotherapy selectively mutated the SRY gene, preventing typical ovarian maturation and uterine growth.  -  Incorrect  .  This patient is a 46,XX female and does not have an SRY gene. \u25cf  C. Pelvic radiation caused hypothalamic-pituitary suppression, resulting in hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with low FSH and LH.  -  Incorrect  . The patient has elevated  FSH and LH, confirming primary gonadal failure  (hypergonadotropic), not central hypothalamic-pituitary suppression. \u25cf  D. Childhood lymphoma frequently metastasizes to the pituitary gland, compressing the pituitary stalk and preventing GnRH secretion.  -  Incorrect  . Pituitary metastasis is not typical of Hodgkin lymphoma, and if present, it would cause low (not high) FSH and LH. \u25cf  E. Autoimmune antibodies to theca cells were generated in response to the Hodgkin lymphoma tumor antigens.  -  Incorrect  . Her  POI is directly due to the toxic effects of alkylating chemotherapy and pelvic radiation, not tumor-induced autoimmunity.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
