    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 10-year-old girl is brought to her pediatrician by her mother, who is concerned about her daughter's development. On physical examination, the physician notes a small, raised breast bud with elevation of the breast and papilla as a small mound, along with sparse, pigmented, straight pubic hair along the labia majora. The patient's height velocity has recently increased. Which of the following correctly describes the patient's current Tanner stages and the expected timing of her menarche relative to these events?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Breast Stage II, Pubic Hair Stage II; menarche is expected to occur approximately 2 years after the onset of these changes",
      "B": "Breast Stage III, Pubic Hair Stage I; menarche is expected to occur immediately following peak height velocity",
      "C": "Breast Stage II, Pubic Hair Stage III; menarche is expected to occur approximately 6 months before peak height velocity",
      "D": "Breast Stage III, Pubic Hair Stage II; menarche is expected to occur 4 years after thelarche",
      "E": "Breast Stage I, Pubic Hair Stage II; menarche is expected to occur when breast development reaches Stage V"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "occur approximately 2 years after the onset of these changes A. This patient exhibits Tanner Stage II for breast development (thelarche, characterized by a small breast bud and elevation of the papilla) and Tanner Stage II for pubic hair (sparse, straight hair along the labia). In typical female pubertal progression, thelarche is the first physical sign of puberty (occurring on average between 9.5 and 10.2 years of age), and menarche typically occurs approximately 2 to 2.5 years after thelarche (and typically 6 months after peak height velocity is achieved). B. Breast Stage III, Pubic Hair Stage I; menarche is expected to occur immediately following peak height velocity:  Incorrect  . Breast Stage III  involves further enlargement of the breast and areola with no separation of their contours, which is more advanced than a small raised breast bud (Stage II). Also, menarche occurs after peak height velocity, not immediately following it. C. Breast Stage II, Pubic Hair Stage III; menarche is expected to occur approximately 6 months before peak height velocity:  Incorrect  . Sparse, straight  pubic hair along the labia majora is Tanner Stage II, not III (which features darker, coarser, curling hair). Menarche typically occurs approximately 6 months after (not before) peak height velocity. 87 D. Breast Stage III, Pubic Hair Stage II; menarche is expected to occur 4 years after thelarche: Incorrect  . Menarche usually occurs about 2 years,  not 4 years, after thelarche, typically between ages 11.9 and 12.6. E. Breast Stage I, Pubic Hair Stage II; menarche is expected to occur when breast development reaches Stage V:  Incorrect  . Tanner Stage I is prepubertal;  since she has a breast bud, she is at least Stage II. Menarche does not wait until Stage V (adult breast contour), but typically occurs at Breast Stage III or IV.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
