    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "suppression due to severe chronic systemic illness and corticosteroid use B. This patient represents hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (secondary hypogonadism), characterized by low gonadotropins (FSH/LH) and low sex steroids. The underlying mechanism is hypothalamic-pituitary suppression secondary to her severe chronic systemic illness (chronic renal insufficiency and severe asthma) and frequent exogenous systemic corticosteroid use. Chronic illness and corticosteroids inhibit the physiological pulsatile release of GnRH from the hypothalamus. A. Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism; primary gonadal failure due to autoimmune oophoritis: Incorrect  . Autoimmune oophoritis causes primary ovarian  failure (hypergonadotropic hypogonadism), which would present with elevated, not low, FSH and LH levels. C. Constitutional delay of growth and puberty; typical late-blooming pattern with no pathology: Incorrect  . While CDGP is a common cause of delayed  puberty with delayed bone age, this patient has severe chronic medical illnesses and exogenous steroid use, which actively suppress the HPG axis, making secondary hypogonadism from hypothalamic suppression the most accurate diagnostic classification. 89 D. Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism; isolated failure of olfactory bulb and GnRH neuron migration:  Incorrect  . Isolated failure of GnRH neuron  and olfactory bulb migration defines Kallmann syndrome. There is no mention of anosmia, and her history of severe asthma and renal insufficiency provides a clear systemic cause for hypothalamic suppression. E. Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism; accelerated oocyte loss due to sex chromosome aneuploidy:  Incorrect  . Sex chromosome aneuploidy (e.g.,  Turner syndrome) causes primary ovarian failure (hypergonadotropic hypogonadism) with high FSH/LH, which is not present in this patient.",
    "pearl": "",
