    "qnum": 67,
    "topic": "Normal and Abnormal Puberty (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 15-year-old phenotypic female is brought to the clinic due to delayed puberty. On physical examination, she has typical female external genitalia, but there is no breast development (Tanner Stage I) and no pubic hair. Her height is at the 50th percentile. Head MRI is normal. Serum FSH is 120 mIU/mL (elevated), LH is 55 mIU/mL (elevated), and estradiol is undetectable. Karyotype analysis reveals a 46,XY chromosomal complement. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Swyer syndrome",
      "B": "Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS)",
      "C": "Turner syndrome",
      "D": "Klinefelter syndrome",
      "E": "Kallmann syndrome"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "A. This patient has  Swyer syndrome  (46,XY complete  gonadal dysgenesis). A mutation in the SRY gene or downstream pathways prevents the bipotential gonads from differentiating into testes, so they develop as non-functional streak gonads. Because no ovaries or testes develop, the patient produces no sex steroids (low estrogen/testosterone), leading to hypergonadotropic hypogonadism  (high FSH/LH due to  loss of negative feedback) and 90 delayed puberty  (Tanner Stage I breasts and hair). However, because no AMH was produced, a typical uterus and fallopian tubes are present internally. B. Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS):  Incorrect  . CAIS is a 46,XY DSD, but because testes develop typically and produce testosterone (which is aromatized to estrogen) and AMH, these patients undergo  normal breast development  during puberty and have no uterus. C. Turner syndrome:  Incorrect  . Turner syndrome (45,X)  also presents with hypergonadotropic hypogonadism and delayed puberty, but their karyotype is  45,X  (or mosaic), and they typically present with somatic abnormalities such as short stature and a webbed neck. D. Klinefelter syndrome:  Incorrect  . Klinefelter syndrome  is a  47,XXY  chromosomal anomaly presenting in  phenotypic males  with small, firm testes,  tall stature, and infertility, not phenotypic females with delayed puberty. E. Kallmann syndrome:  Incorrect  . Kallmann syndrome  causes  hypogonadotropic hypogonadism  (low or normal FSH and LH), whereas this  patient has severely elevated FSH and LH, indicating primary gonadal failure.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
