    "rationale": "adrenal tumor D. This patient exhibits  peripheral precocious puberty  (GnRH-independent), as evidenced by his elevated testosterone levels and suppressed LH and FSH. The key clinical clue is  bilateral prepubertal testes (2 mL)  . In central precocious puberty,  the HPG axis is active, leading to bilateral testicular enlargement (>4 mL). In a Leydig cell tumor or testotoxicosis, unilateral or bilateral testicular enlargement occurs. The combination of virilization, elevated testosterone, suppressed gonadotropins, and  bilateral prepubertal  testes  indicates an  extratesticular source of androgens  , such as an  androgen-secreting  adrenal tumor  or Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia. A. Central precocious puberty due to a hypothalamic hamartoma:  Incorrect  . A hypothalamic hamartoma would cause Central Precocious Puberty (GnRH-dependent), which would activate the testes, resulting in bilateral testicular enlargement (>4 mL) and elevated gonadotropins (FSH/LH), neither of which is present here. B. Peripheral precocious puberty due to a testicular Leydig cell tumor:  Incorrect  . A Leydig cell tumor would cause asymmetric testicular enlargement (a unilateral palpable testicular mass), not symmetric prepubertal (2 mL) testes. C. Central precocious puberty due to idiopathic early activation of the HPG axis:  Incorrect  . Idiopathic CPP is GnRH-dependent and would present with pubertal-sized, enlarged testes (>4 mL) and pubertal levels of gonadotropins. 91 E. Familial male-limited precocious puberty (testotoxicosis):  Incorrect  . Testotoxicosis (familial male-limited precocious puberty) is caused by a constitutively active LH receptor mutation in the Leydig cells, leading to autonomous testosterone production and  bilateral testicular enlargement  (usually 4-8 mL, smaller than typical  central puberty but larger than prepubertal 2 mL).",
