    "stem": "A 15-year-old boy is brought to his pediatrician because he is much shorter than his peers and has not shown any signs of pubertal development. On physical examination, his height is below the 3rd percentile, but his growth velocity is normal for his bone age. He has no pubic hair and his testes are 2 mL bilaterally. His bone age is determined 81 radiographically to be 12.5 years. His father underwent a similar late growth spurt and did not shave until age 19. What is the most likely diagnosis and typical management?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Kallmann syndrome; lifelong pulsatile GnRH therapy",
      "B": "Craniopharyngioma; urgent neurosurgical resection",
      "C": "Constitutional delay of growth and puberty; reassurance and observation or short-term low-dose testosterone",
      "D": "Klinefelter syndrome; immediate initiation of high-dose daily testosterone",
      "E": "Primary testicular failure; lifelong testosterone replacement"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "observation or short-term low-dose testosterone C. This patient represents a classic case of  Constitutional  Delay of Growth and Puberty (CDGP)  , the most common cause of delayed puberty and  short stature in phenotypic males. It is characterized by a 'late-bloomer' pattern with a delayed bone age (which matches his height age of 12.5 rather than chronological age of 15), prepubertal physical findings, and a family 92 history of delayed puberty ('late-bloomer' father). The typical management is  reassurance and watchful waiting  because these patients eventually  undergo typical pubertal development and achieve a normal adult height. In select cases with significant psychological distress, a short course of low-dose testosterone (e.g., testosterone enanthate 50mg IM monthly for 3-6 months) can be used to 'jump start' puberty without causing premature epiphyseal fusion. A. Kallmann syndrome; lifelong pulsatile GnRH therapy:  Incorrect  . Kallmann syndrome is a form of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism caused by impaired GnRH neuron migration and is typically accompanied by anosmia. While bone age would be delayed, Kallmann syndrome is a permanent pathological defect, unlike CDGP, which is a benign variant. B. Craniopharyngioma; urgent neurosurgical resection:  Incorrect  . Neurosurgical resection is indicated for craniopharyngioma, a CNS tumor that would typically present with headache, visual field defects, and other pituitary hormone deficiencies, which are not present in this patient. D. Klinefelter syndrome; immediate initiation of high-dose daily testosterone:  Incorrect  . Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) typically presents in  tall  adolescent males with small, firm testes and hypergonadotropic hypogonadism (elevated FSH/LH), rather than short stature with delayed bone age. E. Primary testicular failure; lifelong testosterone replacement:  Incorrect  . Primary testicular failure (hypergonadotropic hypogonadism) presents with elevated FSH and LH levels and does not feature a simple delayed bone age that matches height age in a late-bloomer family pattern.",
    "pearl": "",
