    "rationale": "olfactory axons to migrate from the olfactory placode to the hypothalamus C. This patient has  Kallmann syndrome  , a form of congenital  hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. The defining clinical feature is  anosmia  or hyposmia  (inability to smell), combined with a failure to undergo pubertal development (low FSH/LH, prepubertal breasts/hair). Embryologically, Kallmann syndrome is caused by the  failure of GnRH-producing neurons and olfactory receptor axons to migrate  from the olfactory placode, through the cribriform plate, into the olfactory bulb and preoptic area of the hypothalamus. This is often associated with mutations in genes like  KAL1  or  FGFR1  . A. Turner syndrome; accelerated oocyte loss in the gonadal ridge:  Incorrect  . Turner syndrome (45,X) causes primary ovarian failure, which is a  hypergonadotropic  hypogonadism (high FSH/LH due to loss of negative feedback). Also, Turner syndrome is not associated with anosmia. B. MRKH syndrome; complete agenesis of the paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts:  Incorrect  . MRKH syndrome (Mullerian agenesis) presents with primary amenorrhea, but these patients 93 have typical ovaries, normal FSH/LH, and  normal breast and pubic hair development (Tanner Stage V) at age 16. D. Constitutional delay of growth and puberty; typical late-onset activation of the HPG axis: Incorrect  . Constitutional delay is a benign delay  of normal puberty and is not associated with anosmia (inability to smell). E. Swyer syndrome; deletion of the SRY gene on the Y chromosome:  Incorrect  . Swyer syndrome is characterized by streak gonads, leading to hypergonadotropic hypogonadism (elevated FSH/LH), not hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (low FSH/LH). It is also not associated with anosmia.",
