    "rationale": "B. This patient presents with classic somatic features of  Turner Syndrome  : short stature, broad 'shield' chest with widely spaced nipples, low posterior hairline, and lymphedema (pitting edema of the feet). Her elevated FSH and LH with undetectable estradiol indicate primary ovarian insufficiency (  hypergonadotropic hypogonadism  ). The  typical karyotype of Turner syndrome is  45,X  , and the underlying gonadal histology consists  of  fibrous 'streak' gonads  devoid of functional follicles, resulting from accelerated oocyte atresia during fetal life. A. 47,XXY; hyalinized seminiferous tubules:  Incorrect  .  47,XXY is Klinefelter syndrome, which presents in tall, phenotypic males with small, firm testes, not short stature females. C. 46,XX; typical ovaries containing primordial follicles:  Incorrect  . A 46,XX karyotype with normal ovaries containing follicles would not present with the severe somatic features of Turner syndrome or primary ovarian failure at age 13. D. 46,XY; dysgenic testes containing Sertoli cells:  Incorrect  . 46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis is Swyer syndrome. These patients are of normal or tall stature and do not exhibit the somatic features of Turner syndrome (shield chest, lymphedema). E. 47,XXX; typical ovaries with accelerated follicular atresia:  Incorrect  . 47,XXX (Triple X syndrome) typically presents as a tall female with normal pubertal development and fertility, not short stature with delayed puberty and infantile lymphedema.",
