    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "stimulation of GnRH receptors downregulates these receptors on pituitary gonadotroph cells, suppressing FSH and LH secretion C. This patient has Central Precocious Puberty (GnRH-dependent) secondary to a hypothalamic hamartoma (the most common organic brain lesion causing CPP). The first-line medical treatment is a long-acting GnRH agonist (such as leuprolide acetate). GnRH is physiologically secreted in a pulsatile fashion to stimulate FSH and LH release. Continuous, non-pulsatile administration of a long-acting GnRH agonist downregulates and desensitizes the GnRH receptors on pituitary gonadotroph cells. This suppresses the secretion of FSH and LH, halting pubertal progression and preserving final adult height by slowing bone age advancement. A. Daily oral estrogen; competitively blocks estrogen receptors in the breast tissue:  Incorrect  . Estrogen would further accelerate bone maturation and breast development, worsening her precocious puberty. B. Cyclic oral contraceptive pills; downregulates pituitary FSH and LH receptors via progesterone feedback:  Incorrect  . Oral contraceptive  pills do not provide the continuous, 95 profound suppression of the HPG axis required to arrest bone age acceleration in CPP, and they are not first-line therapy. D. Aromatase inhibitor (e.g., anastrozole); blocks the peripheral conversion of testosterone to estradiol:  Incorrect  . Aromatase inhibitors are used  in some forms of peripheral precocious puberty (such as McCune-Albright) to block estrogen synthesis, but they are not first-line for GnRH-dependent Central Precocious Puberty. E. Progestin-only pills; selectively inhibits the hypothalamic kisspeptin neurons:  Incorrect  . Progestins can suppress gonadotropins weakly but do not provide the robust, gold-standard downregulation of pituitary GnRH receptors achieved by GnRH agonists.",
