    "rationale": "GABA, Makorin ring finger protein 3 (MKRN3) A. The onset of puberty is driven by a complex interplay of hypothalamic activators and inhibitors that regulate the pulsatile release of GnRH. According to your lecture materials, the key GnRH Activators include Kisspeptin, Neurokinin B, and Glutamate. The key GnRH Inhibitors that maintain the HPG axis in a quiescent state during childhood include GABA and Makorin ring finger protein 3 (MKRN3). Loss-of-function mutations in the MKRN3 gene can lead to central precocious puberty. B. Activators: GABA, Leptin, Progesterone; Inhibitors: Kisspeptin, Estrogen, Glutamate: Incorrect  . GABA is a potent GnRH inhibitor (not activator)  during childhood, and Kisspeptin is a master activator of GnRH release, not an inhibitor. C. Activators: Dopamine, Prolactin, FSH; Inhibitors: Neurokinin B, LH, Testosterone:  Incorrect  . Prolactin is an inhibitor of GnRH release (hyperprolactinemia causes delayed puberty). FSH and LH are pituitary hormones, not hypothalamic neurochemical regulators. D. Activators: Kisspeptin, GABA, Melatonin; Inhibitors: Neurokinin B, Makorin ring finger protein 3 (MKRN3):  Incorrect  . GABA is an inhibitor, not an  activator. Melatonin is generally considered inhibitory or quiescent in human puberty rather than a direct acute activator. E. Activators: Glutamate, Serotonin, Cortisol; Inhibitors: GABA, Kisspeptin, ACTH:  Incorrect  . Kisspeptin is an activator, not an inhibitor, and cortisol/ACTH are stress-system hormones that suppress (not activate) the HPG axis, contributing to hypothalamic delay.",
