    "topic": "Normal and Abnormal Puberty (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 15-year-old girl is brought to her pediatrician because she has never had a menstrual period. On physical examination, her height is at the 50th percentile, and her body mass index is 20 kg/m2. She has typical female breast development (Tanner Stage V) and typical female pubic hair (Tanner Stage V). Pelvic examination reveals a bulging, tense, bluish membrane at the vaginal introitus. On rectal examination, a soft, distended mass is felt in the vagina. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the next step in management?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "M\u00fcllerian agenesis; immediate surgical creation of a neovagina",
      "B": "Imperforate hymen; surgical incision/resection of the hymenal membrane",
      "C": "Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome; urgent bilateral gonadectomy to prevent malignancy",
      "D": "Transverse vaginal septum; continuous high-dose oral contraceptive therapy to suppress menses",
      "E": "Constitutional delay of growth and puberty; reassurance and yearly observation"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "membrane B. This patient has an imperforate hymen, which is an obstructive canalization defect of the vaginal plate. At puberty, she has typical secondary sexual characteristics (Breast and Pubic Hair Tanner Stage V) because her HPG axis is fully functional and she is cycling. However, menstrual blood cannot escape, leading to trapped blood in the vagina (hematocolpos) and uterus (hematometra). This presents clinically as primary amenorrhea, cyclic pelvic pain, and a classic bulging, bluish membrane at the vaginal introitus. The recommended management is surgical incision/resection of the hymenal membrane to drain the retained blood and restore patency. A. M\u00fcllerian agenesis; immediate surgical creation of a neovagina:  Incorrect  . M\u00fcllerian agenesis (MRKH) presents with a blind-ending vagina but an absent uterus, so there is no menstrual shedding or bulging, hematocolpos, or bluish membrane at the introitus. 97 C. Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome; urgent bilateral gonadectomy to prevent malignancy:  Incorrect  . CAIS presents with absent/sparse  pubic hair and an absent uterus, so they do not develop cyclic hematocolpos or a bulging bluish membrane. D. Transverse vaginal septum; continuous high-dose oral contraceptive therapy to suppress menses:  Incorrect  . A transverse vaginal septum can  cause hematocolpos, but the septum is located higher in the vaginal canal and does not present as a bulging bluish membrane directly at the introitus (which is the site of the hymen). Suppression of menses with OCPs is not definitive treatment; surgery is required. E. Constitutional delay of growth and puberty; reassurance and yearly observation:  Incorrect  . This is a pathological, anatomical obstruction, not a benign physiological delay, and requires surgical correction, not reassurance and observation.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
