    "rationale": "women, with more than 70% clearing the infection within 1 year and approximately 90% clearing it within 2 years. \u25cf  A. Young women (<30 years old) are significantly less likely than older women to clear an acute HPV infection, with a mean duration of infection of 24 months.  - Incorrect  . Young women are  more  likely to clear the  infection than older women, and the mean duration is shorter (approx. 8 months). \u25cf  B. Approximately 90% of reproductive-age women will develop persistent, life-long infections that remain detectable below the standard clinical threshold.  -  Incorrect  . 90% of women clear or immunologically control the virus to undetectable levels within 2 years, rather than developing persistent life-long infections. \u25cf  C. In contrast to women, HPV infection in men is highly persistent, with over 50% of infected men remaining positive at 1 year.  -  Incorrect  .  Men are  less  likely to have 162 persistent infections (only about 6% remain positive at 1 year, compared to 20% of women). \u25cf  D. Young women clear HPV infections more rapidly than older women, with more than 70% clearing the infection within 1 year and approximately 90% clearing it within 2 years.  -  Correct  . Clinical data demonstrate  that young women (<30 years old) have highly active immune clearance of HPV. More than  70% clear the infection within 1 year  , and  approximately 90% clear it within 2 years  (compared to lower rates in women 30 years or older). \u25cf  E. True clearance of HPV never occurs; instead, the virus routinely establishes permanent latent infection in the deep stromal layers of the cervix.  -  Incorrect  . True clearance occurs in a large majority of patients, although latent/immune-controlled virus can remain in a subset.",
    "pearl": "",
