    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A gynecologic pathologist is examining cervical biopsy specimens obtained during colposcopy. She is comparing the histologic characteristics of Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia (CIN) grades to help the clinical team understand the corresponding cytologic Bethesda System terms. Which of the following statements correctly pairs the CIN grade, its histologic definition, and its corresponding Bethesda cytologic classification?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "CIN 1 is characterized by dysplastic cells involving the full thickness of the epithelium and corresponds to HSIL.",
      "B": "CIN 3 is characterized by dysplastic cells restricted to the lower 1/3 of the epithelium and corresponds to LSIL.",
      "C": "CIN 1 represents mild dysplasia with dysplastic cells restricted to the lower 1/3 of the epithelium and corresponds to LSIL; CIN 3 represents severe dysplasia/carcinoma in situ with dysplastic cells occupying more than 2/3 or the full thickness of the epithelium and corresponds to HSIL.",
      "D": "CIN 2 represents moderate dysplasia where the dysplastic changes are confined entirely to the superficial keratin layer and corresponds to NILM.",
      "E": "Both CIN 1 and CIN 3 represent invasive squamous cell carcinoma on histology, which corresponds to the cytologic category of ASCUS."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "to the lower 1/3 of the epithelium and corresponds to LSIL; CIN 3 represents severe dysplasia/carcinoma in situ with dysplastic cells occupying more than 2/3 or the full thickness of the epithelium and corresponds to HSIL. \u25cf  A. CIN 1 is characterized by dysplastic cells involving the full thickness of the epithelium and corresponds to HSIL.  -  Incorrect  . CIN  1 involves only the lower 1/3 of the epithelium, and corresponds to LSIL, not HSIL. \u25cf  B. CIN 3 is characterized by dysplastic cells restricted to the lower 1/3 of the epithelium and corresponds to LSIL.  -  Incorrect  . CIN  3 involves more than 2/3 or the full thickness of the epithelium and corresponds to HSIL. \u25cf  C. CIN 1 represents mild dysplasia with dysplastic cells restricted to the lower 1/3 of the epithelium and corresponds to LSIL; CIN 3 represents severe dysplasia/carcinoma in situ with dysplastic cells occupying more than 2/3 or the full thickness of the epithelium and corresponds to HSIL.  -  Correct  . The classification of cervical precursor lesions correlates histopathology with cytology:  CIN 1 (mild dysplasia)  is defined by dysplastic cells limited  to the  lower 1/3 of the epithelial layer  and corresponds cytologically to  LSIL  .  CIN 3  (severe dysplasia/carcinoma in situ)  involves dysplastic cells extending through  more than 2/3 or the full thickness of the epithelium  and corresponds cytologically to  HSIL  . \u25cf  D. CIN 2 represents moderate dysplasia where the dysplastic changes are confined entirely to the superficial keratin layer and corresponds to NILM.  - Incorrect  . CIN 2 involves dysplastic cells in the  lower 2/3 of the epithelium and corresponds to HSIL (if p16-positive). \u25cf  E. Both CIN 1 and CIN 3 represent invasive squamous cell carcinoma on histology, which corresponds to the cytologic category of ASCUS.  -  Incorrect  . Both CIN 1 and CIN 3 are pre-invasive, non-invasive lesions; they are not cancers, and they do not correspond to ASCUS.",
