    "rationale": "negative; at least 50% of CIN 2 lesions regress, and 32% to 47% of CIN 3 lesions regress. \u25cf  A. CIN 1 rarely regresses (<5% of cases), while CIN 3 regresses spontaneously in over 90% of cases.  -  Incorrect  . This is the exact  opposite of the actual clinical 166 behavior; CIN 1 has the highest regression rate, and CIN 3 is the most stable/progressive. \u25cf  B. The vast majority of CIN 1 lesions regress spontaneously to negative; at least 50% of CIN 2 lesions regress, and 32% to 47% of CIN 3 lesions regress.  -  Correct  . Pre-invasive cervical lesions have a high rate of spontaneous regression:  the vast majority of CIN 1 (LGSIL) lesions regress spontaneously  to negative within 1 year if untreated.  At least 50% of CIN 2 lesions regress  ,  and even severe pre-cancerous lesions (  CIN 3  ) will spontaneously regress in  32%  to 47% of cases  , with only a minority (12% to 40%) progressing to invasive cancer over many years. \u25cf  C. Regression is impossible for all three grades of CIN, and they always progress to invasive cancer within 5 years if left untreated.  -  Incorrect  . CIN frequently regresses spontaneously, and progression to cancer, when it does occur, typically takes many years or decades. \u25cf  D. CIN 1 and CIN 2 regress in approximately 10% of cases, while CIN 3 regresses in 75% of cases.  -  Incorrect  . Regression rates are  higher for lower grades (CIN 1 and 2), not lower. \u25cf  E. Spontaneous regression of CIN is restricted entirely to postmenopausal women, whereas young women under 30 always experience progression.  - Incorrect  . Young women under 30 have the highest rates  of spontaneous clearance and regression of both HPV and CIN.",
