    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "renal transplant), smoking, and postcoital bleeding; back pain, anorexia, and weight loss are late manifestations. 168 \u25cf  A. High parity, lack of physical exercise, and young age; typically presents with severe right upper quadrant pain and sudden weight gain.  -  Incorrect  . These are not risk factors or classic presentations of cervical cancer. \u25cf  B. Autoimmune oophoritis, early menopause, and low-risk HPV strains (6 and 11); typically presents with spontaneous galactorrhea.  -  Incorrect  . These are unrelated; low-risk strains 6 and 11 cause warts, not invasive cancer. \u25cf  C. Lynch syndrome, obesity, and unopposed estrogen; typically presents with cyclic hematuria and progressive hirsutism.  -  Incorrect  .  This is the risk factor profile for endometrioid endometrial cancer, not cervical squamous cell carcinoma. \u25cf  D. History of inadequate screening, immunosuppression (such as renal transplant), smoking, and postcoital bleeding; back pain, anorexia, and weight loss are late manifestations.  -  Correct  . The classic  clinical profile of invasive cervical cancer includes a  history of inadequate screening  (such as an unscreened immigrant),  immunosuppression  (which impairs the body's  ability to clear HPV), and smoking  (a known co-factor for HPV persistence). The  classic presentation is postcoital bleeding  or irregular spotting. Late manifestations  include back pain, anorexia, and weight loss, signaling advanced locally infiltrating disease. \u25cf  E. Estrogen-secreting ovarian tumors and Tamoxifen use; typically presents with secondary amenorrhea.  -  Incorrect  . These are risk  factors for endometrial cancer, not cervical cancer.",
