    "stem": "A 45-year-old female is diagnosed with invasive squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix after a biopsy of a cervical mass. Staging evaluation reveals that the tumor has invaded the paracervical tissue and parametrium bilaterally, but has not reached the pelvic sidewall (Stage IIb). What is the most important prognostic factor for cervical cancer, and what is the standard recommended treatment for early-stage (e.g., Stage Ib, confined to the cervix) versus advanced-stage (Stage IIb or higher) disease?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Mitotic count is the most important prognostic factor; early-stage is treated with pelvic exenteration, and advanced-stage is treated with observation.",
      "B": "Stage is the most important prognostic factor; early-stage disease (confined to the cervix) is treated surgically (such as radical hysterectomy), whereas advanced-stage disease (Stage IIb or higher with parametrial invasion) is treated with primary chemoradiation (radiation therapy plus cisplatin-based chemotherapy).",
      "C": "Karyotype is the most important prognostic factor; both stages are treated with high-dose estrogen and progestin therapy.",
      "D": "Tumor size is the most important prognostic factor; early-stage is managed with LEEP, and advanced-stage requires oral methotrexate alone.",
      "E": "Patient age is the most important prognostic factor; both stages are treated with palliative radiation and exenteration."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "disease (confined to the cervix) is treated surgically (such as radical hysterectomy), whereas advanced-stage disease (Stage IIb or higher with parametrial invasion) is treated with primary chemoradiation (radiation therapy plus cisplatin-based chemotherapy). \u25cf  A. Mitotic count is the most important prognostic factor; early-stage is treated with pelvic exenteration, and advanced-stage is treated with observation.  - Incorrect  . Exenteration is reserved for recurrent  disease, and advanced-stage requires active therapy, not observation. \u25cf  B. Stage is the most important prognostic factor; early-stage disease (confined to the cervix) is treated surgically (such as radical hysterectomy), whereas advanced-stage disease (Stage IIb or higher with parametrial invasion) is treated with primary chemoradiation (radiation therapy plus cisplatin-based chemotherapy).  -  Correct  . For invasive cervical cancer,  clinical stage is the single most important prognostic factor  . The treatment differs  dramatically based on stage: early-stage disease (Stage Ia, Ib, or IIa, confined to the cervix or upper vagina without parametrial involvement) is treated surgically (radical hysterectomy)  with lymph node dissection.  Advanced-stage disease (Stage  IIb or higher, characterized by parametrial/paracervical invasion) cannot be cured with surgery alone and is treated with primary concurrent chemoradiation  (external  beam radiation plus cisplatin-based chemotherapy). \u25cf  C. Karyotype is the most important prognostic factor; both stages are treated with high-dose estrogen and progestin therapy.  -  Incorrect  .  Karyotype is not a prognostic factor, and hormone therapy is not used to treat cervical cancer. \u25cf  D. Tumor size is the most important prognostic factor; early-stage is managed with LEEP, and advanced-stage requires oral methotrexate alone.  -  Incorrect  . LEEP is for pre-invasive CIN, and methotrexate is not the primary treatment for advanced cervical cancer. \u25cf  E. Patient age is the most important prognostic factor; both stages are treated with palliative radiation and exenteration.  -  Incorrect  .  Stage is the primary prognostic factor, and exenteration is not used as first-line therapy. 170",
