    "stem": "A 32-year-old female presents to the clinic complaining of multiple painless, raised, flesh-colored lesions on her vulva and perineal region that have gradually increased in size over the past 4 months. On physical examination, she has multiple raised, hyperkeratotic, papular, cauliflower-like lesions. A biopsy confirms condyloma acuminatum. What are the low-risk HPV strains responsible for this condition, and how does it differ from Vulvar Intraepithelial Neoplasia (VIN)?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Due to high-risk HPV strains 16 and 18; condyloma acuminatum is a malignant precursor, whereas VIN is always benign.",
      "B": "Due to low-risk HPV strains 31 and 45; condyloma acuminatum presents as flat, white, pruritic plaques.",
      "C": "Due to somatic activating mutations in the Gnas gene; condyloma acuminatum occurs only in postmenopausal women.",
      "D": "Due to mismatch repair gene mutations; condyloma acuminatum requires urgent radical vulvectomy.",
      "E": "Due to low-risk HPV strains 6 and 11; condyloma acuminatum presents as raised, hyperkeratotic warts, whereas VIN presents as flat dysplasia that is predominantly (87%) associated with high-risk HPV."
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "presents as raised, hyperkeratotic warts, whereas VIN presents as flat dysplasia that is predominantly (87%) associated with high-risk HPV. \u25cf  A. Due to high-risk HPV strains 16 and 18; condyloma acuminatum is a malignant precursor, whereas VIN is always benign.  -  Incorrect  .  This swaps the oncogenic potential; VIN is the pre-malignant precursor, and condyloma is benign and caused by low-risk strains 6 and 11. \u25cf  B. Due to low-risk HPV strains 31 and 45; condyloma acuminatum presents as flat, white, pruritic plaques.  -  Incorrect  . HPV 31 and 45  are high-risk strains, and condyloma presents as raised hyperkeratotic warts, not flat plaques. \u25cf  C. Due to somatic activating mutations in the Gnas gene; condyloma acuminatum occurs only in postmenopausal women.  -  Incorrect  .  Somatic Gnas mutations cause McCune-Albright syndrome, and condyloma is an infectious disease caused by HPV. \u25cf  D. Due to mismatch repair gene mutations; condyloma acuminatum requires urgent radical vulvectomy.  -  Incorrect  . Mismatch repair  mutations cause Lynch syndrome, and condyloma is managed conservatively (with topical therapies or simple excision/ablation). 171 \u25cf  E. Due to low-risk HPV strains 6 and 11; condyloma acuminatum presents as raised, hyperkeratotic warts, whereas VIN presents as flat dysplasia that is predominantly (87%) associated with high-risk HPV.  -  Correct  .  Condyloma acuminatum (genital warts)  is caused by  low-risk HPV  types 6 and 11  , which do not cause cancer and present clinically as raised, hyperkeratotic, cauliflower-like papules. In contrast,  Vulvar Intraepithelial Neoplasia (VIN)  is  a pre-malignant flat dysplasia that is predominantly (87%) associated with high-risk HPV types  (such as 16 and 18) and carries a risk of progression to invasive squamous cell carcinoma.",
