    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology (Two-Cell, Two-Gonadotropin Theory):** In the developing ovarian follicle, two somatic cell types cooperate to synthesize estrogen.\n- **Theca interna cells (outer, vascularized layer):** Stimulated by **LH**, they convert cholesterol into androgens (androstenedione and testosterone) via the enzyme desmolase.\n- **Granulosa cells (inner, avascular layer):** Stimulated by **FSH**, they upregulate the enzyme **aromatase**, which converts the theca-derived androgens into estrogens (estrone and estradiol). \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) LH:** Directly stimulates theca cells to produce androgens, not the granulosa cells to aromatize them (until luteinization occurs later).\n  - **(C) Prolactin:** Inhibits GnRH release; causes amenorrhea, does not stimulate aromatase.\n  - **(D) Progesterone:** Produced primarily by the corpus luteum after ovulation.\n  - **(E) hCG:** Produced by the syncytiotrophoblast of the placenta to maintain the corpus luteum, structurally similar to LH.",
    "pearl": "**Two-Cell Theory:**\n**LH $\\rightarrow$ Theca Cells $\\rightarrow$ Androgens (via Desmolase)**\n**FSH $\\rightarrow$ Granulosa Cells $\\rightarrow$ Estrogens (via Aromatase)**",
