    "rationale": "- **Follicle Histology:**\n  - **Primordial follicle:** The most immature and abundant resting follicle. Consists of a primary oocyte arrested in Dictyate (prophase I) surrounded by a *single layer of simple squamous (flattened) granulosa cells*.\n  - **Primary follicle:** The squamous granulosa cells transition into a *single or multiple layers of cuboidal cells*. The zona pellucida begins to form.\n  - **Secondary (Antral) follicle:** Characterized by the development of fluid-filled spaces between granulosa cells that eventually coalesce to form a single cavity called the **antrum**. The theca layers differentiate.\n  - **Graafian (Mature) follicle:** A large, fully developed antral follicle with the oocyte perched on a mound of cells (cumulus oophorus), ready for ovulation.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Primary follicle:** Has cuboidal, not flattened squamous, granulosa cells.\n  - **(B) Secondary follicle:** Has an antrum (fluid-filled cavity).\n  - **(D) Graafian follicle:** Massive antrum, cumulus oophorus, and corona radiata.\n  - **(E) Corpus luteum:** Forms *after* ovulation from luteinized granulosa and theca cells.",
