    "rationale": "- **Corpus Albicans Histology:** The structures described are **corpora albicantia** (singular: corpus albicans), which literally translates to \"white body.\"\n- **Pathophysiology:** If fertilization does not occur, the corpus luteum stops secreting progesterone after about 10-14 days. It undergoes luteolysis (apoptosis of the luteal cells and breakdown of the vasculature). Macrophages phagocytose the cellular debris, and fibroblasts lay down a dense scar of hyaline collagen (type I collagen). Over time, the ovary accumulates many of these white, avascular scars, representing past ovulatory cycles.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Endometriosis:** Would present as \"chocolate cysts\" containing old blood and hemosiderin-laden macrophages.\n  - **(B) Normal follicular atresia:** Follicles that degenerate before ovulation become atretic follicles, often leaving a small, wavy remnant of the zona pellucida, not a massive hyalinized scar like the corpus albicans.\n  - **(D) Implantation:** Would result in human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) rescue of the corpus luteum, preventing its immediate degeneration into a corpus albicans (it becomes the corpus luteum of pregnancy).\n  - **(E) Gartner's duct cyst:** A remnant of the mesonephric duct on the vaginal wall.",
    "pearl": "The **Corpus Albicans** is the white, acellular, hyalinized collagen scar that forms following the physiological apoptosis (luteolysis) of the **Corpus Luteum**.",
