    "rationale": "- **Fallopian Tube Anatomy:** The fallopian (uterine) tube is divided into four regions: Intramural, Isthmus, Ampulla, and Infundibulum.\n- **The Ampulla:** The ampulla is the longest and widest segment of the tube. Histologically, its mucosa is thrown into highly complex, extensive, branching labyrinthine folds (plicae) that create a maze-like lumen. This provides massive surface area for the nourishment and interaction of gametes.\n- **Clinical Significance:** The **ampulla** is the site where **fertilization** normally occurs. Consequently, it is also by far the **most common site for ectopic pregnancies** (~80% of tubal ectopics). \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Isthmus:** The narrow, thick-walled muscular segment closest to the uterus; has simpler mucosal folds.\n  - **(B) Intramural segment:** The portion traversing the uterine myometrium. Ectopics here are rare but extremely dangerous due to massive hemorrhage risk upon rupture.\n  - **(D) Infundibulum:** The funnel-shaped distal end that opens into the peritoneal cavity.\n  - **(E) Fimbriae:** Finger-like projections on the infundibulum that sweep the ovum into the tube.",
