    "rationale": "- **Histology of the Muscularis (Myosalpinx):** The muscular layer of the fallopian tube is responsible for the peristaltic contractions that help propel the ovum/zygote toward the uterus (working in tandem with the beating cilia). \n- **Layer Arrangement:** Like most of the human gastrointestinal and tubular tracts, the smooth muscle of the fallopian tube is arranged in two distinct layers: an **inner circular layer** and an **outer longitudinal layer**. \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Single longitudinal:** Incorrect; peristalsis requires circular squeezing.\n  - **(C) Inner long, mid circ, outer long:** This complex 3-layer pattern is characteristic of the lower third of the ureter or the body of the uterus (myometrium), not the fallopian tube.\n  - **(D) Striated skeletal:** The fallopian tube contains exclusively involuntary smooth muscle.\n  - **(E) Disorganized meshwork:** This describes the multi-directional woven pattern of the uterine myometrium, which must contract globally to expel a fetus, unlike the directional peristalsis of the tube.",
    "pearl": "The muscularis of the fallopian tube (myosalpinx) consists of an **inner circular** and **outer longitudinal** layer of smooth muscle to facilitate peristaltic transport of the zygote.",
