    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology of Menstruation:** The menstrual phase (Days 1-4) is triggered by the regression of the corpus luteum (luteolysis) in the absence of hCG from an embryo. \n- **Progesterone Withdrawal:** As the corpus luteum dies, serum **progesterone** (and estrogen) levels plummet. The abrupt **withdrawal of progesterone** is the primary trigger for menstruation. \n- **Mechanism:** Progesterone withdrawal causes the synthesis and release of prostaglandins (especially PGF2$\\alpha$) within the endometrium. This leads to intense, spasmodic contractions of the **spiral arteries**. The resulting prolonged ischemia and hypoxia cause necrosis of the stratum functionalis walls, leading to tissue sloughing and bleeding (menses).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Estrogen:** Estrogen also drops, but it is the lack of progesterone support that destabilizes the secretory endometrium and directly triggers the spiral artery spasms.\n  - **(C) LH & (D) FSH:** Pituitary hormones that drop back to baseline, but do not act directly on the spiral arteries.\n  - **(E) PGF2$\\alpha$:** Prostaglandins *cause* the vasospasm, but they are the *result* of progesterone withdrawal, not the hormone that is withdrawn.",
