    "rationale": "- **Endometrial Zonation:** The endometrium is divided into two layers:\n  1. **Stratum Functionalis:** The superficial 2/3. It proliferates and is shed during menstruation. Supplied by the highly coiled **spiral arteries**.\n  2. **Stratum Basalis:** The deep 1/3 adjacent to the myometrium. It contains the stem cells that regenerate the functionalis. It is *never* shed.\n- **Vascular Supply:** The **stratum basalis** is supplied by short, straight **basal arteries**. Because these basal arteries do not undergo the progesterone-withdrawal vasospasm that the spiral arteries do, the stratum basalis remains fully perfused and viable during menstruation.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Spiral arteries:** Supply the stratum functionalis. They undergo intense spasm and necrosis during menses.\n  - **(C) Arcuate arteries:** Circle the uterus within the myometrium; give rise to radial arteries.\n  - **(D) Radial arteries:** Penetrate the inner myometrium and branch into the straight and spiral arteries.\n  - **(E) Uterine arteries:** The main trunks that supply the entire organ.",
