    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology:** As a woman ages and undergoes puberty/pregnancy, the original squamocolumnar junction rolls outward (eversion), exposing the simple columnar epithelium of the endocervix to the acidic vagina. \n- **Metaplasia:** This acid exposure triggers **squamous metaplasia**\u2014the columnar cells are replaced by hardy stratified squamous cells, creating a new SCJ. \n- **Cyst Formation:** During this metaplastic process, the new squamous epithelium often grows over and covers the openings of the deep endocervical mucous crypts. The trapped columnar cells continue to secrete mucin, which backs up and balloons the crypt into a visible, benign **Nabothian cyst** on the surface of the cervix.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Gartner's duct cyst:** Mesonephric duct remnant on the lateral vaginal wall.\n  - **(B) Bartholin gland cyst:** Blockage of the greater vestibular gland duct in the posterior vulva/labia.\n  - **(D) Follicular cyst:** Unruptured Graafian follicle on the ovary.\n  - **(E) Endometriomas:** Blood-filled \"chocolate cysts\" on the ovary due to endometriosis.",
