    "rationale": "- **Cervical Crypts (\"Glands\"):** The endocervix does not have true tubular glands like the endometrium; instead, it has deep mucosal clefts/crypts lined by mucus-secreting columnar cells.\n- **Hormonal Regulation of Cervical Mucus:**\n  - **Estrogen (Follicular phase/Ovulation):** High estrogen levels cause the cervical crypts to secrete large amounts of **thin, watery, clear, and alkaline mucus**. This mucus is highly stretchable (demonstrating *spinnbarkeit*) and crystallizes into a distinct **fern-like pattern** on a slide. This specific consistency is designed to facilitate and nourish the swimming of spermatozoa through the cervix.\n  - **Progesterone (Luteal phase/Pregnancy):** Progesterone causes the mucus to become **scant, thick, highly viscous, and cellular**. This forms a dense cervical plug that blocks sperm and bacterial entry into the uterus.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Thick/viscous:** This is the effect of *Progesterone*, not estrogen.\n  - **(B) Stops production:** Estrogen *increases* production.\n  - **(D) Increases acidity:** Cervical mucus is alkaline (protecting sperm from the acidic vagina).\n  - **(E) Secretion of glycogen:** This occurs in the *vaginal* squamous epithelium, not the cervix.",
