    "rationale": "- **Cervical Blood Supply:** The cervix receives a robust blood supply primarily from the cervical branches of the **uterine artery** (with anastomoses from the vaginal artery).\n- **Anatomical Course:** The uterine artery travels medially from the internal iliac artery within the dense connective tissue at the base of the broad ligament, specifically known as the **cardinal ligament** (transverse cervical ligament).\n- **The Crucial Relationship:** As it travels through the cardinal ligament to reach the lateral aspect of the cervix, the uterine artery crosses directly superior and anterior to the **ureter** (\"water under the bridge\"). This makes the ureter highly susceptible to iatrogenic ligation during a hysterectomy.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Suspensory ligament:** Contains the *ovarian* artery (which crosses the ureter at the pelvic brim).\n  - **(C) Round ligament:** Contains the artery of Sampson, traveling to the labia.\n  - **(D) Mesosalpinx:** The upper part of the broad ligament enclosing the fallopian tube.\n  - **(E) Pelvic brim:** The site where the *ovarian* vessels cross the ureter.",
