    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Atrophic Vaginitis:** The patient is suffering from genitourinary syndrome of menopause (atrophic vaginitis) due to profound **estrogen deficiency**.\n- **Histologic Changes:** Estrogen is the trophic hormone for the vaginal epithelium. Without it, the stratified squamous epithelium becomes severely **atrophic (thinned out)**, often reducing to just a few layers of basal/parabasal cells.\n- **Loss of Glycogen:** Crucially, the estrogen-dependent **glycogen-rich intermediate and superficial cell layers are lost**. Without glycogen substrate, *Lactobacillus* cannot survive or produce lactic acid. As a result, the **vaginal pH rises to $>4.5$** (often 6.0-7.0), increasing susceptibility to infections and causing mechanical fragility (friability/bleeding) and loss of elasticity (rugae).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Keratinization:** The vagina normally remains non-keratinized. True keratinization (leukoplakia) would suggest a dermatologic condition like lichen sclerosus or SCC.\n  - **(B) Mucous glands:** The vagina normally has **NO** glands in its lamina propria. It is lubricated by transudate.\n  - **(D) uNK cells:** These belong in the secretory endometrium of the uterus, not the vagina.\n  - **(E) Columnar epithelium:** This describes vaginal adenosis (often DES-related), not postmenopausal atrophy.",
    "pearl": "Postmenopausal **estrogen deficiency** causes **vaginal atrophy**: thinning of the squamous epithelium, loss of glycogen, loss of lactobacilli, and a **rise in vaginal pH ($>4.5$)**.",
