    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Vaginal Lubrication Histology:** A unique and highly testable histological fact is that the **vaginal wall contains absolutely NO glands** (no mucous, sebaceous, or sweat glands in the lamina propria).\n- **Mechanism of Lubrication:** \n  1. **Basal Lubrication:** The everyday moisture of the vagina is primarily composed of mucus descending from the **endocervical crypts**, mixed with sloughed vaginal squamous cells and resident bacteria.\n  2. **Sexual Arousal (Transudation):** During sexual arousal, parasympathetic vasodilation causes massive engorgement of the dense venous plexus in the vaginal lamina propria. Increased hydrostatic pressure forces fluid to leak (transudate) between the squamous cells directly into the vaginal lumen, providing rapid and copious lubrication.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Bartholin glands:** Located in the vestibule (external genitalia), they secrete a small amount of mucus for vulvar lubrication, but do *not* lubricate the vaginal vault itself.\n  - **(B) Skene's glands:** Paraurethral glands that secrete into the vestibule near the urethral meatus.\n  - **(D) & (E) Apocrine/Sebaceous glands:** Absent in the vagina; they are found on the vulva/labia majora.",
    "pearl": "The histological vaginal wall has **NO GLANDS**. Vaginal lubrication during arousal is mediated entirely by **hemodynamic transudation** from the engorged subepithelial venous plexus.",
