    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **The Maturation Index (MI):** The MI is a cytologic ratio of the three cell types shed from the vaginal squamous epithelium: Parabasal (deepest), Intermediate, and Superficial (most mature).\n- **Hormonal Effects:**\n  - **Estrogen:** Drives aggressive proliferation and **complete maturation** of the squamous epithelium all the way to the top layer. An estrogen-dominant smear (e.g., just before ovulation) shows a high percentage of **Superficial cells** (large, flat, pink cells with tiny, dead/pyknotic nuclei). MI = 0/40/60.\n  - **Progesterone:** Halts maturation halfway. A progesterone-dominant smear (luteal phase or pregnancy) shows a predominance of **Intermediate cells** (folded edges, vesicular nuclei). MI = 0/95/5.\n  - **Lack of Estrogen (Menopause/Childhood):** The epithelium is thin and cannot mature. The smear is dominated by **Parabasal cells** (small, round cells with large nuclei). MI = 100/0/0.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Progesterone:** Skews the index toward intermediate cells, not superficial cells.\n  - **(C) Testosterone, (D) FSH, (E) Prolactin:** Do not directly dictate the cytological maturation of the vaginal squamous epithelium.",
    "pearl": "Vaginal Cytology:\n**Estrogen** drives maturation to **Superficial cells** (pyknotic nuclei).\n**Progesterone** drives maturation to **Intermediate cells**.\n**Atrophy (no estrogen)** leaves only **Parabasal cells**.",
