    "qnum": 22,
    "topic": "Typical Development of the (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 16-year-old female presents to her pediatrician due to primary amenorrhea (she has never had a menstrual period). On physical examination, she is of normal height, has typical female external genitalia, but has minimal pubic and axillary hair and no breast development. Karyotype analysis reveals a 46,XY chromosome complement. Further genetic testing demonstrates a complete deletion of the Sex-Determining Region of the Y chromosome (SRY gene). Which of the following describes the immediate biochemical product of this gene, and what is the direct developmental outcome of its absence in this patient?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Testes-Determining Factor (TDF); failure of the bipotential gonads to differentiate into testes, resulting in non-functional 'streak' gonads.",
      "B": "5-alpha-reductase; inability to convert testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT) in target organs.",
      "C": "Estrogen receptor; inability of the ovaries to respond to estrogen, leading to primary amenorrhea.",
      "D": "Antimullerian Hormone (AMH); failure of the Mullerian ducts to regress, resulting in a uterus and fallopian tubes.",
      "E": "Androgen receptor; failure of the external genitalia to respond to circulating testosterone, leading to feminization."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "to differentiate into testes, resulting in non-functional 'streak' gonads. A. Testes-Determining Factor (TDF); failure of the bipotential gonads to differentiate into testes, resulting in non-functional 'streak' gonads.:  Correct  .  The SRY gene on the Y chromosome encodes  Testes-Determining Factor (TDF)  , a master  transcription factor that drives the differentiation of the bipotential gonadal ridge into testes. In the absence of SRY or TDF (such as in Swyer syndrome, 46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis), the gonads fail to form testes and instead develop as non-functioning  streak gonads  . B. 5-alpha-reductase; inability to convert testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT) in target organs.:  Incorrect  . 5-alpha-reductase is an autosome-encoded  enzyme that converts testosterone to DHT; its deficiency does not affect testicular differentiation. C. Estrogen receptor; inability of the ovaries to respond to estrogen, leading to primary amenorrhea.:  Incorrect  . Estrogen receptors are encoded  on autosomes. In this XY patient, 38 there are no ovaries to begin with (as ovaries require XX and specific genes, and the lack of SRY leads to streak gonads, not ovaries). D. Antimullerian Hormone (AMH); failure of the Mullerian ducts to regress, resulting in a uterus and fallopian tubes.:  Incorrect  . AMH is produced by  Sertoli cells once the testes have already differentiated under SRY/TDF influence. While its absence would cause Mullerian persistence, AMH itself is not the direct product of the SRY gene. E. Androgen receptor; failure of the external genitalia to respond to circulating testosterone, leading to feminization.:  Incorrect  . The androgen  receptor is encoded by a gene on the X chromosome, not SRY, and its mutation causes Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (AIS).",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
