    "topic": "Typical Development of the (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A newborn male is evaluated by his pediatrician prior to discharge. On physical examination of the penis, the physician notes that the urethral meatus is abnormally located on the ventral (underside) surface of the penile shaft, accompanied by a ventral curvature of the penis (chordee). The physician advises the parents that circumcision should be postponed because the prepuce (foreskin) may be needed for future surgical reconstruction. What is this common congenital anomaly, and what is its embryological cause?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Epispadias; failure of the genital tubercle to migrate dorsally during the 4th week of development.",
      "B": "Phimosis; failure of the preputial space to canalize and separate from the glans penis.",
      "C": "Hypospadias; failure of the labioscrotal swellings to fuse in the midline to form the scrotum.",
      "D": "Epispadias; incomplete fusion of the Mullerian ducts with the posterior wall of the urogenital sinus.",
      "E": "Hypospadias; incomplete fusion of the urethral (urogenital) folds on the ventral surface of the penis."
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "on the ventral surface of the penis. 46 A. Epispadias; failure of the genital tubercle to migrate dorsally during the 4th week of development.:  Incorrect  . Epispadias is the abnormal urethral opening on the  dorsal (upper) surface of the penis, which is often associated with bladder exstrophy and is due to abnormal positioning of the genital tubercle. B. Phimosis; failure of the preputial space to canalize and separate from the glans penis.: Incorrect  . Phimosis is the inability to retract the  foreskin, which is physiological in newborns but is not an abnormal location of the urethral meatus. C. Hypospadias; failure of the labioscrotal swellings to fuse in the midline to form the scrotum.: Incorrect  . Failure of the labioscrotal swellings to  fuse in the midline results in a bifid scrotum or cleft scrotum, not hypospadias. D. Epispadias; incomplete fusion of the Mullerian ducts with the posterior wall of the urogenital sinus.:  Incorrect  . Epispadias is dorsal, not ventral,  and internal Mullerian ducts are not involved in forming the external male penile urethra. E. Hypospadias; incomplete fusion of the urethral (urogenital) folds on the ventral surface of the penis.:  Correct  . This patient has  hypospadias  , which  is characterized by the abnormal opening of the urethra on the  ventral (underside)  surface  of the penis. Embryologically, hypospadias is caused by the  incomplete fusion of the urethral (urogenital)  folds  during the development of the penile urethra. It is often associated with chordee.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
