    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **PCOS Comorbidities:** PCOS is essentially a metabolic syndrome of the ovary. The profound, chronic insulin resistance puts a massive strain on the pancreatic beta cells.\n- **The Risk:** Over time, the pancreas can fail to keep up with the extreme demand for insulin, leading to beta-cell exhaustion and the development of overt **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus**. Over 50% of women with PCOS will develop prediabetes or Type 2 Diabetes before the age of 40. \n- **Additional Risks:** They are also at very high risk for dyslipidemia (low HDL, high triglycerides), hypertension, obstructive sleep apnea, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), and early cardiovascular disease.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Type 1 Diabetes:** An autoimmune disease, not driven by insulin resistance.\n  - **(B) Osteoporosis:** PCOS patients are actually *protected* from osteoporosis because they have constant, high levels of estrogen (estrone) which maintains bone density.\n  - **(D) Primary Ovarian Insufficiency:** Menopause before 40. PCOS patients have plenty of follicles; they just don't ovulate them.\n  - **(E) Autoimmune thyroiditis:** Common in women, but not the primary metabolically driven complication of the insulin resistance seen in PCOS.",
