    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 29-year-old woman with PCOS asks her physician why her condition causes her to stop having regular periods. Her physician explains the concept of the \"two-cell, two-gonadotropin\" theory of ovarian steroidogenesis. In the setting of PCOS, the massively elevated levels of Luteinizing Hormone (LH) cause an overproduction of androgens by which specific ovarian cell type?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Granulosa cells",
      "B": "Theca interna cells",
      "C": "Oocytes",
      "D": "Germinal epithelium",
      "E": "Luteal cells"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Normal Ovarian Steroidogenesis:** Follows the \"Two-Cell, Two-Gonadotropin\" model.\n  1. **LH** acts on the **Theca Interna cells**. The theca cells take cholesterol and convert it into **androgens** (testosterone and androstenedione). Theca cells lack aromatase, so they cannot make estrogen.\n  2. The androgens diffuse across the basement membrane into the **Granulosa cells**.\n  3. **FSH** acts on the **Granulosa cells**, stimulating the enzyme **aromatase**, which converts the androgens into **estrogen** (estradiol).\n- **PCOS Pathophysiology:** In PCOS, the rapid GnRH pulses cause LH levels to be very high, while FSH levels remain relatively low. The high LH continuously hammers the **theca interna cells**, causing them to hypertrophy and pump out massive amounts of **androgens**. Because FSH is low, the granulosa cells cannot efficiently convert all these androgens to estrogen, leading to systemic hyperandrogenemia (hirsutism) and follicular arrest (anovulation/amenorrhea).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Granulosa cells:** Respond to FSH to make estrogen via aromatase.\n  - **(C) Oocytes:** Do not produce steroid hormones.\n  - **(D) Germinal epithelium:** Covers the ovary; origin of epithelial ovarian tumors.\n  - **(E) Luteal cells:** Form after ovulation to make progesterone; PCOS patients do not ovulate.",
    "pearl": "**LH** stimulates **Theca** cells to make **Androgens**.\n**FSH** stimulates **Granulosa** cells (via aromatase) to convert androgens into **Estrogen**.\nIn PCOS, high LH drives excess thecal androgen production.",
