    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 24-year-old woman presents to the clinic with complaints of irregular periods, occurring only 3 times in the last year. She also notes bothersome, thick dark hair growing on her upper lip and chin. She is a competitive cross-fit athlete with a BMI of 21 kg/m$^2$ (normal weight). A pelvic ultrasound is completely normal, showing no ovarian cysts. According to the Rotterdam criteria, does this patient meet the diagnostic requirements for Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS)?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "No, because she is not obese.",
      "B": "No, because she does not have polycystic ovaries on ultrasound.",
      "C": "No, because she has not had a trial of oral contraceptives.",
      "D": "Yes, because she has oligo-ovulation and clinical hyperandrogenism.",
      "E": "Yes, but only if her fasting insulin levels are elevated."
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "- **The Rotterdam Criteria:** The gold standard for diagnosing PCOS. A diagnosis requires the presence of **2 out of the following 3 criteria** (after excluding other causes like CAH, Cushing, or tumors):\n  1. **Oligo-ovulation or anovulation** (irregular or absent menstrual periods).\n  2. **Clinical and/or biochemical signs of hyperandrogenism** (hirsutism, acne, male-pattern balding, or elevated serum testosterone).\n  3. **Polycystic ovaries on ultrasound** ($\\ge 20$ follicles per ovary or ovarian volume $>10$ mL).\n- **Application to Patient:** \n  - Criteria 1: Irregular periods (oligo-ovulation) $\\rightarrow$ Met.\n  - Criteria 2: Facial hair (clinical hyperandrogenism) $\\rightarrow$ Met.\n  - Criteria 3: Normal ultrasound $\\rightarrow$ Not met.\n  - She meets 2 out of 3 criteria, so the diagnosis is definitively **PCOS**.\n- **Important Clue:** PCOS is a syndrome. You do **not** need to have actual \"polycystic ovaries\" on ultrasound to be diagnosed with PCOS, nor do you need to be obese (up to 20% of PCOS patients are lean/\"lean PCOS\").\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Not obese:** Obesity is common but NOT a diagnostic criterion.\n  - **(B) No cysts:** You only need 2 of 3 criteria; cysts are not strictly required.\n  - **(C) OCP trial:** Not required for diagnosis.\n  - **(E) Insulin levels:** Insulin resistance is the pathophysiology, but measuring insulin is NOT part of the diagnostic criteria.",
    "pearl": "**Rotterdam Criteria for PCOS (Need 2 of 3):**\n1. Irregular menses (oligo/anovulation).\n2. Hyperandrogenism (clinical or lab).\n3. Polycystic ovaries on ultrasound.\n*Note: You do NOT need cysts to have PCOS!*",
