    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis of Exclusion:** PCOS is a diagnosis of exclusion. You must rule out other causes of hyperandrogenism and anovulation before applying the Rotterdam criteria.\n- **The Mimic:** The most common genetic disorder that perfectly mimics PCOS (hirsutism, acne, irregular menses) is **Non-Classic (Late-Onset) Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH)**. \n- **Pathophysiology:** Non-classic CAH is caused by a mild deficiency of the **21-hydroxylase enzyme** in the adrenal gland. Because the block is mild, the patient does not present at birth with ambiguous genitalia or salt-wasting. Instead, they present at puberty with excess adrenal androgens.\n- **The Test:** The screening test to rule out 21-hydroxylase deficiency is a fasting, early morning serum **17-hydroxyprogesterone (17-OHP)** level. (The enzyme block causes a massive buildup of 17-OHP before it is shunted into the androgen pathway).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Urine free cortisol:** Screens for Cushing syndrome (also a mimic, but less common than non-classic CAH).\n  - **(C) Prolactin & (D) TSH:** Rule out causes of amenorrhea, but they do not typically cause severe hirsutism/hyperandrogenism.\n  - **(E) Glucose:** Done to manage PCOS metabolic risks, but does not rule out an endocrine mimic.",
    "pearl": "Before diagnosing PCOS, you MUST rule out **Non-Classic Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia** by checking an early morning **17-hydroxyprogesterone (17-OHP)** level.",
