    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 26-year-old woman is diagnosed with PCOS. She is deeply concerned about her fertility and asks if she will ever be able to have children. Her physician explains that her infertility is primarily due to anovulation, but that this can often be treated. What is the fundamental reason that women with PCOS do not ovulate regularly?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "They lack primordial follicles in the ovaries",
      "B": "High levels of androgens cause premature apoptosis of the oocytes",
      "C": "They fail to generate the mid-cycle LH surge required for ovulation",
      "D": "Thickened ovarian capsules physically trap the ovum",
      "E": "Excessive progesterone production suppresses GnRH"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology of Anovulation:** Ovulation is an explosive event triggered by one specific, massive hormonal signal: the **mid-cycle LH Surge**. \n- **The Missing Surge in PCOS:** \n  - To generate an LH surge, a single dominant follicle must grow large enough to secrete massive amounts of estrogen (switching negative feedback to positive feedback).\n  - In PCOS, the LH levels are constantly, steadily high, and FSH levels are too low to grow a dominant follicle. \n  - Because no follicle reaches maturity, there is no massive estrogen spike. Because there is no estrogen spike, the pituitary never receives the positive feedback signal. Therefore, the **LH surge never happens**, and the follicles simply arrest as cysts.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Lack follicles:** POI/Menopause. PCOS ovaries are packed with follicles.\n  - **(B) Androgen apoptosis:** While androgens alter the microenvironment, the primary mechanical failure is the lack of the LH surge.\n  - **(D) Thickened capsule:** An old theory (leading to \"ovarian drilling\" surgery), but not the primary neuroendocrine reason for anovulation.\n  - **(E) Excessive progesterone:** PCOS patients are anovulatory, so they have *zero* progesterone.",
    "pearl": "Patients with PCOS are anovulatory because their chaotic hormone levels (low FSH) prevent a follicle from maturing enough to trigger the **LH Surge** required for ovulation.",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Lecture_3_2__Menstrual_Irregularities_an_11",
        "text": "primary ovarian insufficiency"
      }
    ]
