    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Management of Hirsutism:**\n  - First-line: **COCPs**. They increase SHBG (binding free testosterone) and suppress ovarian LH/androgen production.\n  - If COCPs are insufficient after 6 months, add an anti-androgen.\n- **Spironolactone:** The most commonly used anti-androgen for PCOS hirsutism. It is a potassium-sparing diuretic that has a potent secondary mechanism: it acts as a **direct competitive antagonist at the androgen receptor**, preventing testosterone/DHT from binding in the hair follicles. (It also weakly inhibits steroidogenesis).\n- **Safety Note:** Spironolactone is highly teratogenic (it will demasculinize a male fetus). Therefore, it MUST only be given to women who are on reliable contraception (like the COCPs this patient is already taking).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Metformin:** An insulin sensitizer; good for metabolic issues but has weak/minimal effects on severe hirsutism.\n  - **(B) Clomiphene:** Used to induce ovulation for fertility, not for hair growth.\n  - **(D) Finasteride:** A 5$\\alpha$-reductase inhibitor; sometimes used, but Spironolactone is the preferred first-line anti-androgen.\n  - **(E) Letrozole:** An aromatase inhibitor; used for fertility in PCOS, not hirsutism.",
    "pearl": "If Oral Contraceptive Pills (COCPs) fail to control hirsutism in a PCOS patient, add **Spironolactone**, which acts as a **competitive androgen receptor antagonist**. (Must be used with birth control due to teratogenicity).",
