    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 28-year-old woman with PCOS is trying to conceive. She and her partner have been having unprotected intercourse for 18 months without success. A semen analysis for her partner is normal, and her fallopian tubes are patent on hysterosalpingogram. Her physician decides to prescribe the first-line medication to induce ovulation. The physician prescribes Letrozole. What is the primary mechanism of action of this medication in inducing ovulation?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It acts as an estrogen receptor antagonist in the hypothalamus",
      "B": "It acts as a direct GnRH agonist",
      "C": "It inhibits the enzyme aromatase, decreasing peripheral estrogen production",
      "D": "It directly stimulates FSH receptors on the granulosa cells",
      "E": "It acts as a dopamine agonist in the anterior pituitary"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Ovulation Induction in PCOS:** The goal is to temporarily increase FSH levels to force a follicle to mature.\n- **Letrozole:** Letrozole is an **aromatase inhibitor**. It is currently the recommended **first-line drug** for ovulation induction in PCOS (superior to clomiphene).\n- **Mechanism:** Letrozole blocks the enzyme aromatase in the ovaries and peripheral tissues, preventing the conversion of androgens to estrogen. This causes a sudden, sharp drop in systemic estrogen levels. The hypothalamus and pituitary sense this low estrogen and respond by **increasing the secretion of FSH** (removing negative feedback). The surge of endogenous FSH successfully stimulates a follicle to grow to maturity and ovulate.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Estrogen receptor antagonist:** This describes **Clomiphene citrate**, the *former* first-line drug, which blocks the receptor so the brain *thinks* estrogen is low.\n  - **(B) GnRH agonist:** Used for precocious puberty or endometriosis.\n  - **(D) Direct FSH stimulation:** This describes exogenous gonadotropin injections (recombinant FSH), which are expensive, second-line therapies carrying a high risk of multiple gestation.\n  - **(E) Dopamine agonist:** Cabergoline, used for prolactinomas.",
    "pearl": "**Letrozole (an aromatase inhibitor)** is the first-line drug for inducing ovulation in PCOS. It drops estrogen levels, which removes negative feedback, causing the pituitary to release **FSH**.",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Lecture_3_2__Menstrual_Irregularities_an_2",
        "text": "Affecting th e hypothalamus"
      }
    ]
