    "topic": "Lecture 3.2: Menstrual Irregularities and the HPO Axis - Set 2",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 28-year-old woman with PCOS is prescribed cyclic medroxyprogesterone acetate (Provera) to take for 10 days every month. She is not sexually active. What is the primary therapeutic goal of this specific intermittent dosing regimen?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "To induce regular ovulation",
      "B": "To increase circulating SHBG levels",
      "C": "To induce a controlled withdrawal bleed, preventing endometrial hyperplasia",
      "D": "To competitively block androgen receptors in the skin",
      "E": "To permanently downregulate the GnRH receptors"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Management of Endometrial Protection:** Women with PCOS have chronic anovulation, meaning they produce unopposed estrogen but zero progesterone. This puts them at high risk for endometrial hyperplasia and cancer.\n- **Cyclic Progestins:** If a patient cannot or will not take daily Combined OCPs (e.g., due to clotting risks, migraines with aura, or personal preference), they must still be protected. \n- **The Regimen:** The physician prescribes a progestin (medroxyprogesterone) to be taken for 10-14 days every 1-3 months. \n- **Mechanism:** The progestin mimics the luteal phase, halting the estrogen-driven proliferation of the endometrium and converting it to a secretory state. When the patient stops the pills after 10 days, the sudden drop in progestin mimics luteolysis, triggering a controlled **withdrawal bleed** (a period). This completely sheds the lining, resetting it and preventing cancer. (Note: This does NOT induce ovulation or provide contraception).\n- **Why Other Choices Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Induce ovulation:** Progestins prevent/suppress ovulation; Letrozole induces it.\n  - **(B) Increase SHBG:** Estrogen increases SHBG; progestins can sometimes decrease it.\n  - **(D) Block androgen receptors:** Spironolactone.\n  - **(E) Downregulate GnRH:** Continuous GnRH agonists (Leuprolide).",
    "pearl": "Intermittent/Cyclic **Progestins** are given to anovulatory patients (PCOS) purely to induce a **withdrawal bleed**, shedding the endometrium and protecting against **Endometrial Cancer**.",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Lecture_3_2__Menstrual_Irregularities_an_12",
        "text": "D iscuss, in even greater detail, the tw o most common causes of anovulatory uterine bleeding (functional hypothalamic amenorrhea and polycystic ovary syndrome) : their pathophysiology, clinical presentations, long term health risks , and t reatments."
      }
    ]
