    "rationale": "- **Management of POI:** Primary Ovarian Insufficiency causes women to lose estrogen decades earlier than normal. This prolonged estrogen deficit puts them at a severe, life-threatening risk for premature osteoporosis, cardiovascular disease, and neurocognitive decline.\n- **Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT):** Unlike natural menopause at age 51 (where HRT is controversial and used only short-term for severe symptoms), young women with POI **MUST be placed on systemic Hormone Replacement Therapy (Estrogen)** to replace the hormones their ovaries should naturally be making. \n  - Because she has an intact uterus, she must also be given a **Progestin** alongside the estrogen to protect her endometrium from hyperplasia/cancer.\n  - This therapy is continued until she reaches the natural age of menopause (~age 50-51).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Calcium alone:** Insufficient to stop the massive osteoclast activation caused by estrogen deficiency.\n  - **(C) Progestins alone:** Progestins do not protect bone or the heart; she needs estrogen.\n  - **(D) GnRH agonist:** This *causes* menopause and osteoporosis.\n  - **(E) Clomiphene:** Her ovaries are destroyed by chemo/radiation; they cannot respond to clomiphene.",
