    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **The Three Estrogens:**\n  1. **Estradiol (E2):** The most potent; produced by the **Ovaries** (dominant in reproductive years).\n  2. **Estriol (E3):** The weakest; produced by the **Placenta** (dominant in pregnancy).\n  3. **Estrone (E1):** A weak estrogen; produced by **Peripheral fat** (dominant in menopause).\n- **Menopausal Estrogen Source:** After menopause, the ovaries stop making estradiol. However, the adrenal glands continue to produce weak androgens (specifically **androstenedione**). This adrenal androstenedione circulates to peripheral **adipose (fat) tissue**, where the enzyme **aromatase** converts it into **Estrone**. \n- **Clinical Relevance:** This is why obese postmenopausal women have fewer hot flashes (more fat = more estrone) but a much higher risk of endometrial cancer (unopposed estrone stimulating the uterine lining).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Estradiol from adrenals:** The adrenals make androgens, not estradiol directly.\n  - **(C) Estriol:** Only present during pregnancy (made by placenta/fetal liver).\n  - **(D) Pituitary & (E) Endometrium:** Do not synthesize steroid hormones.",
    "pearl": "**Estradiol** = Ovary (reproductive years).\n**Estriol** = Placenta (pregnancy).\n**Estrone** = Adipose tissue aromatization (menopause).",
