    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Pharmacology of Tamoxifen:** Tamoxifen is a **Selective Estrogen Receptor Modulator (SERM)**. Its action depends entirely on the specific tissue it targets.\n- **Breast Tissue:** In the breast, Tamoxifen acts as a potent **antagonist**, blocking estrogen receptors. This starves ER-positive breast cancer cells, preventing recurrence.\n- **Uterine Tissue (Endometrium):** In the uterus, Tamoxifen acts as a partial **agonist**. It binds to estrogen receptors and actively stimulates the proliferation of the endometrial glands. \n- **Clinical Consequence:** Because it acts like estrogen in the uterus, long-term use of Tamoxifen creates a state of unopposed estrogen, significantly increasing the risk of **endometrial hyperplasia and endometrial cancer** (which is exactly what happened to this patient).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) GnRH agonist:** Leuprolide.\n  - **(C) Inhibits breakdown:** Not the mechanism of tamoxifen.\n  - **(D) Full antagonist everywhere:** This describes **Fulvestrant** (a pure estrogen receptor antagonist downregulator). \n  - **(E) Progesterone antagonist:** Mifepristone (used for medical abortion).",
    "pearl": "**Tamoxifen** (a SERM) is an **antagonist in the breast** (treats breast cancer) but an **agonist in the uterus** (causes endometrial hyperplasia/cancer).",
