    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** Postmenopausal bleeding + Endometrial cancer + A unilateral solid ovarian mass.\n- **Tumor Histology:** The classic histologic description of cells arranged in rosettes around eosinophilic fluid (**Call-Exner bodies**) with \"coffee-bean\" nuclei is pathognomonic for a **Granulosa Cell Tumor**.\n- **Pathophysiology:** Granulosa cell tumors are sex cord-stromal tumors. Normal granulosa cells produce estrogen (via aromatase). Granulosa cell tumors autonomously secrete **massive amounts of Estrogen**.\n- **The Consequence:** In a postmenopausal woman, this massive, autonomous production of estrogen creates a severe state of **unopposed estrogen**. This constantly stimulates the endometrium, directly causing the endometrial hyperplasia and adenocarcinoma that caused her bleeding.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Testosterone:** Secreted by Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors (causes virilization, not endometrial cancer).\n  - **(B) hCG:** Secreted by Choriocarcinoma/Dysgerminoma.\n  - **(D) AFP:** Secreted by Yolk sac tumors.\n  - **(E) Progesterone:** Would protect against endometrial cancer.",
