    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Risk Factors for Type I Endometrial Cancer:**\n  - **Obesity:** High peripheral aromatase $\\rightarrow$ constant, high estrone.\n  - **Nulliparity (Never being pregnant):** Pregnancy is a state of massive, continuous progesterone dominance (9 months where the endometrium is resting and protected). Never being pregnant means the uterus missed out on this massive protective \"break\" from estrogen.\n  - **Late Menopause / Early Menarche:** Increases the total number of years the endometrium is subjected to the estrogen-driven menstrual cycle.\n  - **PCOS:** Chronic anovulation means zero progesterone production.\n- **The Common Denominator:** Every single one of these classic risk factors fundamentally increases the total lifetime duration and intensity of the endometrium's exposure to **unopposed estrogen** (estrogen without sufficient protective progesterone).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Ovulation trauma:** This is the primary proposed mechanism for *Ovarian* epithelial cancer, not endometrial cancer.\n  - **(C) HPV:** Causes cervical/vaginal/vulvar cancer.\n  - **(D) Progesterone:** Increased progesterone (e.g., multiparity, OCP use) *decreases* the risk of endometrial cancer.\n  - **(E) PTEN mutation:** While PTEN mutations are very common in Type 1 endometrial cancer, obesity and nulliparity are physiologic states that drive proliferation, not inherited germline mutations (like Cowden syndrome).",
    "pearl": "The classic risk factors for Endometrial Cancer (**Obesity, Nulliparity, Early Menarche, Late Menopause, PCOS**) all share one common mechanism: they increase the lifetime exposure of the uterus to **Unopposed Estrogen**.",
