    "rationale": "TSH (sharing the same alpha subunit) and can cross-react with TSH receptors \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Choriocarcinoma / Passage 17 \u25cf Explanation: A. Seminoma; because beta-hCG stimulates the hepatic synthesis of thyroid hormone binding globulin: Incorrect. Seminomas rarely produce massive elevations of beta-hCG, and TBG elevation does not cause hyperthyroidism. B. Embryonal Carcinoma; because the tumor cells directly secrete active triiodothyronine (T3): Incorrect. Germ cells do not synthesize or secrete T3. C. Choriocarcinoma; because beta-hCG is structurally similar to TSH (sharing the same alpha subunit) and can cross-react with TSH receptors: Correct. Pure choriocarcinoma is an extremely aggressive GCT that disseminates hematogenously early, classically causing \"cannonball\" lung metastases and hemoptysis. It secretes massive amounts of beta-hCG. Because beta-hCG shares an identical alpha subunit with TSH, LH, and FSH, extremely high serum levels of hCG can cross-react with and stimulate TSH receptors in the thyroid gland, leading to hyperthyroidism. It also stimulates LH/FSH receptors, leading to bilateral gynecomastia. D. Yolk Sac Tumor; because alpha-fetoprotein cross-reacts with thyroid peroxidase in the thyroid gland: Incorrect. AFP does not cross-react with thyroid peroxidase. E. Mature Teratoma; because it frequently contains malignant thyroid follicles (struma testis): Incorrect. Struma testis is an ovarian teratoma variant, not a common cause of testicular hyperthyroidism.",
