    "rationale": "zone; hyperplasia of stroma occurs first; stimulators include FGF, VEGF, IGF, and KGF, while TGF-beta acts as the inhibitory \"brake\" mechanism \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Stromal Cell Role / Slide BPH: Morphology / Passage 20 / 21 \u25cf Explanation: A. Early BPH starts in the periurethral stromal cells of the transition zone; hyperplasia of stroma occurs first; stimulators include FGF, VEGF, IGF, and KGF, while TGF-beta acts as the inhibitory \"brake\" mechanism: Correct. Pathologically, early BPH begins in the periurethral stromal cells of the transition zone. Hyperplasia of the stroma occurs first, forming periurethral mesenchymal nodules, which are subsequently joined by proliferating glands. Cell proliferation is controlled by stromal growth factors: stimulators include FGF, VEGF, IGF, and KGF, while TGF-beta acts as the primary inhibitor or biological \"brake\" mechanism. B. Early BPH starts in the central zone glandular cells; hyperplasia of glands occurs first; stimulators include TGF-beta and VEGF, while KGF acts as the inhibitor: Incorrect. BPH does not begin in the central zone, and TGF-beta is an inhibitor, not a stimulator. 219 C. Early BPH starts in the peripheral zone stromal cells; hyperplasia of both compartments occurs simultaneously; stimulators include IGF and TGF-beta, while EGF acts as the inhibitor: Incorrect. BPH is a transition zone disease; peripheral zone is the site of cancer. D. Early BPH starts in the anterior fibromuscular stroma; hyperplasia of stroma occurs first; stimulators include KGF and TGF-beta, while VEGF acts as the inhibitor: Incorrect. Anterior stroma has no glandular elements and is not the site of clinical BPH. E. Early BPH starts in the periurethral stroma of the central zone; hyperplasia of glands occurs first; stimulators include EGF and FGF, while IGF acts as the inhibitor: Incorrect. Central zone is resistant to BPH.",
