    "rationale": "men by age 80; however, only 5-10% of these patients become symptomatic \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide BENIGN PROSTATIC HYPERPLASIA ( BPH ) / Passage 20 \u25cf Explanation: A. Histologic BPH is present in 30% of men by age 80, with 95% of them being highly symptomatic: Incorrect. This significantly underestimates the histologic prevalence and overestimates the symptomatic prevalence. B. Histologic BPH is present in 50% of men by age 50, with 50% of them being highly symptomatic: Incorrect. These parameters are clinically incorrect. C. Histologic BPH is present in 95% of men by age 60, with only 1% of them being symptomatic: Incorrect. These parameters are inaccurate. D. Histologic BPH begins as early as 45 years and affects 80% of men by age 80; however, only 5-10% of these patients become symptomatic: Correct. Benign prostatic hyperplasia begins pathologically as early as 45 years of age and progressively increases with age. By age 80, BPH is present histologically in approximately 80% of men. Despite this extremely high pathological prevalence, only 5-10% of these men develop clinically symptomatic, bothersome disease. E. Histologic BPH begins at age 65 and affects 100% of men by age 90; however, only 25% of these patients become symptomatic: Incorrect. These statistics are epidemiologically false.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
