    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "include urethral stricture, bladder neck contracture, urinary tract infection, bladder dysfunction, and stones \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide True or False? / Slide LUTS (Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms) / Slide BPH Take Away's / Passage 22 / 23 / 26 \u25cf Explanation: 220 A. Bigger prostate size is directly proportional to worse LUTS; other causes include renal cell carcinoma and high-dose metoprolol therapy: Incorrect. Prostate size does not correlate linearly with symptom severity. B. Bigger prostate size does not equal worse LUTS; other causes include urethral stricture, bladder neck contracture, urinary tract infection, bladder dysfunction, and stones: Correct. Clinical research and diagnostic guidelines confirm that a bigger prostate does not equal worse LUTS (some men with huge prostates have minimal symptoms, and vice versa). LUTS is a multifactorial symptom complex with diverse non-prostatic etiologies, including urethral strictures, bladder neck contractures, meatal stenosis, UTIs, prostatitis, bladder stones, and primary bladder detrusor dysfunction. C. Bigger prostate size is only associated with irritative symptoms; other causes are exclusively limited to neurogenic bladder and detrusor sphincter dyssynergia: Incorrect. Prostate size is not specific to irritative symptoms, and the etiologies are much broader. D. Bigger prostate size is a clinical requirement for diagnosing LUTS; other causes are limited to acute prostatitis and pelvic trauma: Incorrect. Prostatic hypertrophy is not a diagnostic prerequisite for LUTS. E. Bigger prostate size is inversely proportional to worse LUTS; other causes are exclusively caused by chronic bacterial prostatitis: Incorrect. This inverse relationship is biologically incorrect.",
