    "rationale": "emptying, urinary retention, straining, and terminal dribbling; Irritative: urinary urgency, frequency, nocturia, and urgency incontinence \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Types of LUTS / Passage 22 / 23 \u25cf Explanation: A. Obstructive: urinary urgency, frequency, and nocturia; Irritative: slow stream, hesitancy, and incomplete emptying: Incorrect. This completely reverses the two classes of symptoms. B. Obstructive: incomplete emptying, nocturia, and urgency incontinence; Irritative: hesitancy, slow stream, and straining: Incorrect. Nocturia and urgency incontinence are irritative (storage) symptoms, while hesitancy and slow stream are obstructive (voiding) symptoms. C. Obstructive: terminal dribbling, overflow incontinence, and frequency; Irritative: urgency, nocturia, and hesitancy: Incorrect. Frequency and hesitancy are mismatched. D. Obstructive: hesitancy, frequency, and nocturia; Irritative: terminal dribbling, incomplete emptying, and straining: Incorrect. This scrambles the voiding/storage clinical characteristics. E. Obstructive: urinary hesitancy, slow stream, incomplete emptying, urinary retention, straining, and terminal dribbling; Irritative: urinary urgency, frequency, nocturia, and urgency incontinence: Correct. Lower urinary tract symptoms are divided into Obstructive (voiding) symptoms 221 (hesitancy, slow stream, incomplete emptying, acute or chronic urinary retention, Valsalva/straining, terminal dribbling, and overflow incontinence) and Irritative (storage) symptoms (urgency, frequency, nocturia, and urgency incontinence).",
    "pearl": "",
