    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "bladder diverticula, and renal failure/hydronephrosis; trabeculated hypertrophy of the bladder/detrusor muscle \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Complications of BPH / Slide Hydronephrosis / Slide Bladder changes / Passage 25 \u25cf Explanation: A. Development of bladder neck contracture and squamous cell carcinoma; atrophy of the detrusor muscle: Incorrect. Untreated BPH causes hypertrophy, not atrophy, of the detrusor muscle. B. Renal cell carcinoma and testicular atrophy; fibrous scarring of the bladder mucosa with loss of detrusor tone: Incorrect. These are not standard BPH complications. C. Incomplete bladder emptying, urinary retention, UTI, stones, bladder diverticula, and renal failure/hydronephrosis; trabeculated hypertrophy of the bladder/detrusor muscle: Correct. Untreated BPH causes progressive bladder outlet obstruction, leading to chronic complications: urinary retention, recurrent UTIs, bladder calculi (stones), bladder diverticula, and retrograde pressure causing hydronephrosis and renal failure. The bladder muscle (detrusor) undergoes compensatory trabeculated hypertrophy to pump against the high-pressure obstruction. D. Development of acute prostatitis and seminal vesicle cysts; metaplasia of the urothelium into transitional cell carcinoma: Incorrect. Metaplasia to TCC is not a complication of BPH. E. Bilateral hydronephrosis and renal cell carcinoma; selective hypertrophy of the bladder mucosa with normal detrusor muscle: Incorrect. The hypertrophy is muscular (detrusor), not mucosal.",
    "pearl": "",
