    "stem": "A 66-year-old male presents for a routine visit. He has been taking a combination of tamsulosin and finasteride for his BPH for the past 2 years. He asks how these medications work to manage his condition. Which of the following correctly describes the mechanisms of action for both medications?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Tamsulosin is an alpha-blocker that relaxes the smooth muscle at the bladder neck to improve urinary flow; finasteride is a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor that blocks the conversion of testosterone to DHT, thereby shrinking the prostate",
      "B": "Tamsulosin is a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor that blocks the conversion of testosterone to DHT; finasteride is an alpha-blocker that relaxes the smooth muscle at the bladder neck",
      "C": "Tamsulosin is an anticholinergic that relaxes the bladder neck; finasteride is a beta-agonist that increases detrusor contractility",
      "D": "Tamsulosin is a beta-blocker that relaxes the bladder sphincter; finasteride is an androgen receptor antagonist that blocks systemic testosterone",
      "E": "Tamsulosin is an alpha-blocker that shrinks the prostate gland volume; finasteride is a phosphodiesterase inhibitor that increases nitric oxide release in the bladder neck"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "at the bladder neck to improve urinary flow; finasteride is a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor that blocks the conversion of testosterone to DHT, thereby shrinking the prostate \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide BPH Treatment / Slide BPH Take Away's / Passage 24 / 26 \u25cf Explanation: A. Tamsulosin is an alpha-blocker that relaxes the smooth muscle at the bladder neck to improve urinary flow; finasteride is a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor that blocks the conversion of testosterone to DHT, thereby shrinking the prostate: Correct. Tamsulosin is a selective alpha-1 adrenergic receptor antagonist (alpha-blocker) that acts rapidly to relax the prostatic smooth muscle at the bladder neck, improving voiding flow. Finasteride is a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor that blocks the intracellular enzymatic conversion of testosterone to its active metabolite DHT, leading to a reduction in prostatic volume (shrinking the prostate) over 3-6 months. 222 B. Tamsulosin is a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor that blocks the conversion of testosterone to DHT; finasteride is an alpha-blocker that relaxes the smooth muscle at the bladder neck: Incorrect. This reverses the pharmacologic mechanisms of the two drugs. C. Tamsulosin is an anticholinergic that relaxes the bladder neck; finasteride is a beta-agonist that increases detrusor contractility: Incorrect. Tamsulosin is an alpha-blocker, and finasteride is a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor. D. Tamsulosin is a beta-blocker that relaxes the bladder sphincter; finasteride is an androgen receptor antagonist that blocks systemic testosterone: Incorrect. Finasteride does not block the androgen receptor (that would be flutamide/bicalutamide), and tamsulosin is not a beta-blocker. E. Tamsulosin is an alpha-blocker that shrinks the prostate gland volume; finasteride is a phosphodiesterase inhibitor that increases nitric oxide release in the bladder neck: Incorrect. Alpha-blockers do not shrink the prostate; that is the role of 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors.",
