    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 38-year-old male is evaluated for recurrent pelvic pain and painful ejaculation. He underwent a prostatic massage secretion evaluation (4-glass test). The pathology lab reports no white blood cells in his Expressed Prostatic Secretions (EPS) or voided bladder urine 3 (VB3), and all cultures are negative. Which of the following represents his correct NIH Prostatitis classification category?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Category IIIa (Inflammatory CPPS)",
      "B": "Category IIIb (Non-inflammatory CPPS)",
      "C": "Category II (Chronic Bacterial Prostatitis)",
      "D": "Category IV (Asymptomatic Inflammatory Prostatitis)",
      "E": "Category I (Acute Bacterial Prostatitis)"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "\u25cf Lecture Source: Slide NIH Classification / Passage 6 / 28 \u25cf Explanation: 223 A. Category IIIa (Inflammatory CPPS): Incorrect. Category IIIa requires the presence of white blood cells in semen, EPS, or VB3. B. Category IIIb (Non-inflammatory CPPS): Correct. NIH Category III is Chronic Pelvic Pain Syndrome (CPPS), defined by chronic pelvic/perineal pain and voiding symptoms without documented urinary tract infections. It is subclassified into: (1) Category IIIa (Inflammatory: WBCs present in semen/EPS/VB3), and (2) Category IIIb (Non-inflammatory: completely negative for WBCs in semen/EPS/VB3). Because this patient has pelvic pain but lacks WBCs in his secretions, he is Category IIIb. C. Category II (Chronic Bacterial Prostatitis): Incorrect. Category II requires positive bacterial cultures. D. Category IV (Asymptomatic Inflammatory Prostatitis): Incorrect. Category IV is defined by a complete absence of pain or symptoms (usually diagnosed incidentally on biopsy or semen analysis). E. Category I (Acute Bacterial Prostatitis): Incorrect. Category I is an acute, high-grade bacterial infection presenting with systemic fever and sepsis.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
