    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 55-year-old male asks his physician about the biological function of PSA. He wants to know what PSA actually does in the body and where it is produced. Which of the following is correct regarding the biochemical properties, physiological function, and cellular localization of PSA?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "PSA is a 33 kd glycoprotein serine protease that liquifies semen to enhance sperm motility; on immunohistochemistry, it is positive in normal prostate apical secretory cells",
      "B": "PSA is an 80 kd steroid hormone that stimulates testosterone production; on immunohistochemistry, it is positive in basal cells",
      "C": "PSA is a 10 kd enzyme that degrades collagen in the basement membrane; on immunohistochemistry, it is positive in stromal cells",
      "D": "PSA is a 100 kd lipid carrier that concentrates zinc in the prostate; on immunohistochemistry, it is positive in neuroendocrine cells",
      "E": "PSA is a 55 kd chemokine that recruits neutrophils to the central zone; on immunohistochemistry, it is positive in squamous epithelial cells"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "to enhance sperm motility; on immunohistochemistry, it is positive in normal prostate apical secretory cells \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide What is Prostate Specific Antigen (PSA) and what does it do? / Passage 9 \u25cf Explanation: A. PSA is a 33 kd glycoprotein serine protease that liquifies semen to enhance sperm motility; on immunohistochemistry, it is positive in normal prostate apical secretory cells: Correct. PSA (Prostate Specific Antigen) is a 33 kilodalton (kd) glycoprotein that acts biochemically as a serine protease. Its physiological function is to liquify the semen coagulum, which enhances sperm motility. On immunohistochemistry, PSA shows strong cytoplasmic positivity in the apical secretory cells of normal prostatic glands. B. PSA is an 80 kd steroid hormone that stimulates testosterone production; on immunohistochemistry, it is positive in basal cells: Incorrect. PSA is an enzyme, not a steroid hormone, and is negative in basal cells. 225 C. PSA is a 10 kd enzyme that degrades collagen in the basement membrane; on immunohistochemistry, it is positive in stromal cells: Incorrect. PSA is 33 kd and is positive in apical secretory epithelium, not stromal cells. D. PSA is a 100 kd lipid carrier that concentrates zinc in the prostate; on immunohistochemistry, it is positive in neuroendocrine cells: Incorrect. These characteristics are biochemically false. E. PSA is a 55 kd chemokine that recruits neutrophils to the central zone; on immunohistochemistry, it is positive in squamous epithelial cells: Incorrect. This does not describe the biochemical structure or physiological role of PSA.",
