    "stem": "A 62-year-old male is counselled on prostate cancer screening. The physician reviews the Positive Predictive Value (PPV) of serum PSA testing based on the landmark Catalona study, as well as the risk of \"normal\" PSA levels. Which of the following represents the correct PPV ranges and clinical findings from this study?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "PSA 4.1-9.9 ng/ml has a PPV of 5%; PSA > 10 ng/ml has a PPV of 95%; 0% of men with CaP have normal PSA",
      "B": "PSA 4.1-9.9 ng/ml has a PPV of 50%; PSA > 10 ng/ml has a PPV of 100%; 10% of men with CaP have normal PSA",
      "C": "PSA 4.1-9.9 ng/ml has a PPV of 12%; PSA > 10 ng/ml has a PPV of 33%; 50% of men with CaP have normal PSA",
      "D": "PSA 4.1-9.9 ng/ml has a PPV of 26.1%; PSA > 10 ng/ml has a PPV of 52.9%; up to 25% of men with CaP can have a PSA in the \"normal\" range (<= 4.0 ng/ml)",
      "E": "PSA 4.1-9.9 ng/ml has a PPV of 75%; PSA > 10 ng/ml has a PPV of 90%; 5% of men with CaP have normal PSA"
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "of 52.9%; up to 25% of men with CaP can have a PSA in the \"normal\" range (<= 4.0 ng/ml) \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide SERUM PSA BENIGN vs CAP / Slide Serum PSA (total): Operating Characteristics / Passage 9 \u25cf Explanation: A. PSA 4.1-9.9 ng/ml has a PPV of 5%; PSA > 10 ng/ml has a PPV of 95%; 0% of men with CaP have normal PSA: Incorrect. These values are statistically incorrect. B. PSA 4.1-9.9 ng/ml has a PPV of 50%; PSA > 10 ng/ml has a PPV of 100%; 10% of men with CaP have normal PSA: Incorrect. This overestimates the positive predictive value of PSA. C. PSA 4.1-9.9 ng/ml has a PPV of 12%; PSA > 10 ng/ml has a PPV of 33%; 50% of men with CaP have normal PSA: Incorrect. These operating parameters are clinically false. D. PSA 4.1-9.9 ng/ml has a PPV of 26.1%; PSA > 10 ng/ml has a PPV of 52.9%; up to 25% of men with CaP can have a PSA in the \"normal\" range (<= 4.0 ng/ml): Correct. According to the Catalona et al. J Urol 1994 data, the Positive Predictive Value (PPV) for prostate cancer is 26.1% when serum PSA is between 4.1 and 9.9 ng/mL, and rises to 52.9% when PSA is > 10.0 ng/mL. Crucially, up to 25% of men diagnosed with prostate cancer have a PSA in the \"normal\" range (<= 4.0 ng/mL). E. PSA 4.1-9.9 ng/ml has a PPV of 75%; PSA > 10 ng/ml has a PPV of 90%; 5% of men with CaP have normal PSA: Incorrect. These parameters are clinically inaccurate.",
