    "stem": "A 60-year-old male is found to have a borderline total serum PSA of 4.5 ng/mL. To improve the Positive Predictive Value (PPV) for prostate cancer and avoid an unnecessary biopsy, the urologist evaluates alternative PSA assay measures. According to First Aid and the lecture, which of the following alternative biochemic profiles is classically observed in patients with prostate cancer (CaP) compared to benign conditions?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Decreased total PSA and increased % free PSA",
      "B": "Increased total PSA and decreased % free PSA (with decreased free PSA and increased PSA complexed to ACT)",
      "C": "Normal total PSA and selective elevation of un-complexed free PSA",
      "D": "Decreased total PSA and increased PSA complexed to ACT",
      "E": "Elevated % free PSA and decreased complexed PSA"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "free PSA and increased PSA complexed to ACT) \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide PSA Assay Alternatives: Trying to PPV for CaP / Passage 10 / 27 \u25cf Explanation: A. Decreased total PSA and increased % free PSA: Incorrect. This represents a benign profile. B. Increased total PSA and decreased % free PSA (with decreased free PSA and increased PSA complexed to ACT): Correct. In patients with prostatic adenocarcinoma (CaP), the 226 proportion of un-complexed (free) PSA is decreased, while the proportion of PSA complexed to alpha-1-antichymotrypsin (ACT) is increased. Therefore, the classic biochemical profile observed in prostate cancer is an  increased total  PSA  combined with a  decreased % free PSA  . C. Normal total PSA and selective elevation of un-complexed free PSA: Incorrect. This profile is not observed in CaP. D. Decreased total PSA and increased PSA complexed to ACT: Incorrect. Total PSA is elevated, not decreased, in CaP. E. Elevated % free PSA and decreased complexed PSA: Incorrect. This is the classic profile of a benign prostate, where free PSA is higher.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
