    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A family medicine practice is updating its screening protocols to align with the American Urological Association (AUA) guidelines for prostate cancer screening. Which 215 of the following patient scenarios represents the group that receives the greatest benefit from routine PSA screening, and what is the recommended screening interval?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Routine annual screening in all asymptomatic men aged 40\u201354 years",
      "B": "Routine screening every 6 months in all men aged 70+ years with major comorbidities",
      "C": "Routine annual screening in all men aged 35\u201345 years with no risk factors",
      "D": "Individualized screening every 5 years in men aged 45\u201350 years",
      "E": "Individualized/shared decision-making in men aged 55\u201369 years (greatest benefit of screening); recommended screening interval is every 2 years"
    },
    "correct": "E",
    "rationale": "(greatest benefit of screening); recommended screening interval is every 2 years \u25cf Lecture Source: Slide AUA Guidelines / Passage 10 / 27 \u25cf Explanation: A. Routine annual screening in all asymptomatic men aged 40\u201354 years: Incorrect. AUA guidelines do not recommend routine screening in men 40-54 years old unless they have strong risk factors. B. Routine screening every 6 months in all men aged 70+ years with major comorbidities: Incorrect. Routine screening is not recommended in patients 70+ years old. C. Routine annual screening in all men aged 35\u201345 years with no risk factors: Incorrect. PSA screening is strictly not recommended under age 40. D. Individualized screening every 5 years in men aged 45\u201350 years: Incorrect. The recommended interval is 2 years, not 5 years. E. Individualized/shared decision-making in men aged 55\u201369 years (greatest benefit of screening); recommended screening interval is every 2 years: Correct. According to AUA Guidelines, the cohort that derives the greatest absolute benefit from PSA screening is men aged 55\u201369 years. In this age group, screening should be individualized using shared decision-making to weigh the benefits of reducing mortality against the risks of overdiagnosis and treatment-related complications. The recommended screening interval is 2 years.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
