    "stem": "A 70-year-old male with progressive, metastatic prostatic adenocarcinoma is found to have extensive bone and pelvic lymph node metastases. Despite massive tumor volume filling the pelvis, a staging CT scan confirms that the tumor has not invaded the 217 rectal wall. Which of the following anatomical barriers is primarily responsible for preventing the direct posterior extension of prostate cancer into the rectum?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Denonvilliers' fascia",
      "B": "Anterior fibromuscular stroma",
      "C": "The prostatic capsule",
      "D": "The pelvic splanchnic fascia",
      "E": "The puboprostatic ligament"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "\u25cf Lecture Source: Slide Metastatic Prostate Cancer / Passage 35 \u25cf Explanation: A. Denonvilliers' fascia: Correct. While advanced prostatic adenocarcinoma routinely metastasizes to the regional obturator and pelvic lymph nodes, bones (causing osteoblastic lesions), and can directly invade the bladder, it very rarely invades the rectum posteriorly. This is because  Denonvilliers' fascia  (the rectovesical septum)  acts as an extremely tough, resilient physical and biological anatomical barrier separating the prostate from the rectum. B. Anterior fibromuscular stroma: Incorrect. The anterior stroma is located anteriorly, not posteriorly, and does not separate the prostate from the rectum. C. The prostatic capsule: Incorrect. The capsule is frequently penetrated (EPE) in advanced disease and does not prevent posterior spread. D. The pelvic splanchnic fascia: Incorrect. This fascia is not the primary dividing septum between the rectum and prostate. E. The puboprostatic ligament: Incorrect. This ligament anchors the prostate anteriorly to the pubic bone. 230 Prompt Every source is a full lecture (these are the entire presentations, not excerpts) that will be included on our upcoming US MD in-house reproductive systems exam. Our school has a policy that every question must test on a detail that falls within the scope of at least one of the lecture's learning objectives. Please generate an applied, representative STEP 1-style (A Step 1\u2013style question is a clinical vignette-based multiple-choice question that tests application of basic science concepts in a medical context. It presents a short patient scenario with relevant and irrelevant details, followed by a single best-answer question that requires identifying the underlying mechanism, diagnosis, or next step.) practice exam with 20 A-E multiple choice questions per source (i will tell you which sources) to test my mastery of the material ahead of the exam. The distractors must be common misconceptions. Each question set should only test on information discretely from the corresponding lecture, do not test on outside material. When a source lists lecture objectives, key points, etc., make sure that the question set covers them comprehensively . The idea is that if someone were to score a 100% on a given lecture\u2019 s associated 20-question practice set, it should imply that they have a high level of mastery over the important material in preparation for the exam. In terms of formatting, please break the practice exam up by session (source). only reveal answers in the form of an answer key section at the end of each set of questions. The answer key should give the answer as well as explain why that answer is correct, and why each incorrect answer is wrong. explain conceptually and fully , but in a way that makes sense and allows the reader to learn from it. As for the questions, remember to include 2 line breaks between each answer choice, as well as 2 line breaks in the answer keys between questions. Randomize the A-E correct answer distribution, making sure it is not predictable. Also do NOT use any latex formatting. use the source \"8.1 1 Endo W eek 3 Notebook Qs - Google Docs.pdf\" as a reference for the formatting style i am looking for , as well as for the way i want the answer keys to be structured. Let's begin with Set 1, for the lecture \"[Fillable PDF] IMC 534 - Female Reproductive Histology W orkbook.pdf\". 20 questions, numbered 1-20. include the answer key at the end of the set",
