    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 45-year-old female undergoes a hysteroscopic resection of a solitary, pedunculated intra-endometrial mass that was discovered during an infertility work-up. The pathology report describes the specimen as an endometrial polyp. Which of the following correctly describes the typical histologic components of an endometrial polyp and its associated clinical behavior? 128",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It is composed of hyperplastic smooth muscle cells (spindle cells), thick-walled blood vessels, and dense collagenous stroma; it is highly malignant.",
      "B": "It is composed of endometrial glands of variable size and shape, fibrotic endometrial stroma, and prominent thick-walled blood vessels; it is usually benign but can occasionally harbor malignancy.",
      "C": "It is composed entirely of atypical endometrial glands demonstrating high mitotic counts and severe cytological atypia; it always progresses to cancer.",
      "D": "It is composed of decidualized stroma and prominent spiral arterioles without endometrial glands; it is a normal physiological finding in the secretory phase.",
      "E": "It is composed of hemosiderin-laden macrophages and multinucleated giant cells within a hemorrhagic myometrium; it is a major cause of dysmenorrhea."
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "fibrotic endometrial stroma, and prominent thick-walled blood vessels; it is usually benign but can occasionally harbor malignancy. \u25cf  A. It is composed of hyperplastic smooth muscle cells (spindle cells), thick-walled blood vessels, and dense collagenous stroma; it is highly malignant.  - Incorrect. 139 This describes fibroids or a hybrid lesion, but polyps are mucosal growths of glands and stroma, and they are usually benign, not highly malignant. \u25cf  B. It is composed of endometrial glands of variable size and shape, fibrotic endometrial stroma, and prominent thick-walled blood vessels; it is usually benign but can occasionally harbor malignancy.  - Correct.  Endometrial polyps are common mucosal projections composed of endometrial glands of variable size and shape, fibrotic stroma, and characteristic thick-walled blood vessels. They are typically benign but can occasionally contain atypical hyperplasia or malignancy, and often present with abnormal bleeding or infertility. \u25cf  C. It is composed entirely of atypical endometrial glands demonstrating high mitotic counts and severe cytological atypia; it always progresses to cancer.  - Incorrect. Endometrial polyps do not typically consist of atypical glands, nor do they always progress to cancer. \u25cf  D. It is composed of decidualized stroma and prominent spiral arterioles without endometrial glands; it is a normal physiological finding in the secretory phase.  - Incorrect. This describes physiological changes in the endometrium during the secretory/luteal phase, not a polyp. \u25cf  E. It is composed of hemosiderin-laden macrophages and multinucleated giant cells within a hemorrhagic myometrium; it is a major cause of dysmenorrhea.  - Incorrect. This describes endometriosis or chronic hemorrhage, not an endometrial polyp.",
    "pearl": "",
    "learning_objectives": []
