    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "up to 25% to 43% of patients will have associated endometrial cancer already present at hysterectomy; therefore, a hysterectomy is highly recommended. 141 \u25cf  A. The risk of progression to cancer is 17% to 53% if untreated, and up to 25% to 43% of patients will have associated endometrial cancer already present at hysterectomy; therefore, a hysterectomy is highly recommended.  - Correct. Atypical endometrial hyperplasia (EIN) is a high-risk premalignant lesion. If left untreated, 17% to 53% of cases will progress to endometrial cancer. Furthermore, 25% to 43% of patients diagnosed with atypical hyperplasia on office biopsy are found to have a concurrent endometrial carcinoma detected at the time of hysterectomy. Therefore, a hysterectomy is highly recommended as the standard treatment. \u25cf  B. The risk of progression to cancer is 5% to 10%; combined oral contraceptive pills are the highly recommended first-line treatment.  - Incorrect. The progression risk for atypical hyperplasia (EIN) is much higher, and oral contraceptives are not the standard of care. \u25cf  C. The risk of progression to cancer is 1% to 3%; expectant management with a repeat biopsy in 3 months is highly recommended.  -  Incorrect. This describes the low risk and treatment of hyperplasia without atypia. \u25cf  D. The risk of progression to cancer is 100% within 1 year; immediate pelvic radiation and chemotherapy are the only recommended treatments.  - Incorrect. Progression is not 100%, and chemotherapy/radiation are not used to treat atypical hyperplasia. \u25cf  E. The risk of progression to cancer is negligible because atypical hyperplasia spontaneously regresses in over 95% of cases without intervention.  - Incorrect. Regression is rare for EIN without treatment, unlike hyperplasia without atypia.",
