    "stem": "A gynecologic oncologist is presenting a slide comparing Type 1 (Endometrioid) and Type 2 (Serous) endometrial carcinomas. She lists their epidemiological associations, histological characteristics, and classic molecular mutations. Which of the following correctly describes the features of Endometrioid endometrial carcinoma compared to Serous endometrial carcinoma?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Endometrioid carcinoma is typically high grade, arises in older women, is unrelated to estrogen exposure, and is characterized by TP53 mutations.",
      "B": "Serous carcinoma is the most common type, is highly associated with obesity, arises from atypical hyperplasia/EIN, and features PTEN and PIK3CA mutations.",
      "C": "Endometrioid carcinoma is characterized histologically by a high mitotic count and bizarre giant cells, whereas Serous carcinoma is low grade and has a favorable prognosis.",
      "D": "Endometrioid carcinoma is the most common type, is associated with atypical endometrial hyperplasia/EIN and estrogen exposure, is often low grade and ER/PR positive, and commonly features PTEN or PIK3CA mutations.",
      "E": "Both Endometrioid and Serous carcinomas arise from pre-existing benign endometrial polyps and are highly responsive to low-dose estrogen therapy."
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "with atypical endometrial hyperplasia/EIN and estrogen exposure, is often low grade and ER/PR positive, and commonly features PTEN or PIK3CA mutations. \u25cf  A. Endometrioid carcinoma is typically high grade, arises in older women, is unrelated to estrogen exposure, and is characterized by TP53 mutations.  - Incorrect. This describes Serous (\"Type 2\") endometrial carcinoma. \u25cf  B. Serous carcinoma is the most common type, is highly associated with obesity, arises from atypical hyperplasia/EIN, and features PTEN and PIK3CA mutations.  - Incorrect. Serous carcinoma is less common (Type 2), is not associated with obesity or estrogen, does not arise from EIN, and features TP53 mutations. \u25cf  C. Endometrioid carcinoma is characterized histologically by a high mitotic count and bizarre giant cells, whereas Serous carcinoma is low grade and has a favorable prognosis.  - Incorrect. Endometrioid is  typically low grade with a favorable prognosis, while Serous is high grade, aggressive, and carries a poor prognosis. \u25cf  D. Endometrioid carcinoma is the most common type, is associated with atypical endometrial hyperplasia/EIN and estrogen exposure, is often low grade and ER/PR positive, and commonly features PTEN or PIK3CA mutations.  - Correct. Endometrioid carcinoma (historically \"Type 1\") is the most common endometrial cancer. It is highly associated with atypical endometrial hyperplasia/EIN and unopposed 142 estrogen exposure. It is typically low grade, ER/PR positive, carries a favorable prognosis, and features classic molecular mutations in PTEN, PIK3CA, and mismatch repair genes. \u25cf  E. Both Endometrioid and Serous carcinomas arise from pre-existing benign endometrial polyps and are highly responsive to low-dose estrogen therapy.  - Incorrect. Estrogen stimulates (rather than treats) endometrioid cancer, and serous cancer is completely non-responsive to hormone therapy.",
