    "stem": "A 38-year-old G0 female with a strong family history of early-onset colon and endometrial cancer is referred for genetic counseling. She is found to have a germline mutation in the MSH2 gene, confirming a diagnosis of Lynch syndrome (Hereditary Nonpolyposis Colorectal Cancer, or HNPCC). What is the genetic mechanism of Lynch syndrome, and what are the clinical implications for this patient regarding uterine cancer risk?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by a mutation in the SRY gene, resulting in gonadal dysgenesis and a 100% risk of uterine leiomyosarcoma.",
      "B": "It is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by a mutation in the PTEN gene, preventing the conversion of androgens to estrogens in adipose tissue.",
      "C": "It is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by a mutation in a mismatch repair gene (such as MLH1, MSH2, PMS2, MSH6), resulting in DNA mismatch repair loss and a highly elevated risk of endometrial carcinoma.",
      "D": "It is a sporadic somatic mutation in the TP53 gene, causing autonomous serous endometrial carcinoma that is completely non-penetrant in families.",
      "E": "It is an autosomal dominant disorder that selectively blocks hepatic production of Sex Hormone-Binding Globulin (SHBG), leading to leiomyomatosis."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "mismatch repair gene (such as MLH1, MSH2, PMS2, MSH6), resulting in DNA mismatch repair loss and a highly elevated risk of endometrial carcinoma. \u25cf  A. It is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by a mutation in the SRY gene, resulting in gonadal dysgenesis and a 100% risk of uterine leiomyosarcoma.  - Incorrect. SRY mutations cause Swyer syndrome, which is not associated with Lynch syndrome or leiomyosarcoma. \u25cf  B. It is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by a mutation in the PTEN gene, preventing the conversion of androgens to estrogens in adipose tissue.  - Incorrect. PTEN mutations are common somatic alterations in endometrioid cancer, but Lynch syndrome is caused by MMR germline mutations, and PTEN does not regulate adipose aromatization. \u25cf  C. It is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by a mutation in a mismatch repair gene (such as MLH1, MSH2, PMS2, MSH6), resulting in DNA mismatch repair loss and a highly elevated risk of endometrial carcinoma.  - Correct. Lynch syndrome (Hereditary Nonpolyposis Colorectal Cancer) is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by a germline mutation in one of the DNA mismatch repair (MMR) genes (MLH1, MSH2, PMS2, MSH6). This leads to a loss of mismatch repair capability and subsequent genomic instability. Women with Lynch syndrome have a highly elevated lifetime risk of developing endometrial carcinoma (as well as colorectal and ovarian cancers), requiring intensive screening and often prophylactic hysterectomy. \u25cf  D. It is a sporadic somatic mutation in the TP53 gene, causing autonomous serous endometrial carcinoma that is completely non-penetrant in families.  - Incorrect. Lynch syndrome is a germline inherited syndrome, not a sporadic non-penetrant somatic mutation. \u25cf  E. It is an autosomal dominant disorder that selectively blocks hepatic production of Sex Hormone-Binding Globulin (SHBG), leading to leiomyomatosis.  - Incorrect. Lynch syndrome does not affect SHBG production or cause smooth muscle leiomyomatosis.",
