    "id": "W1_Notebook_W4_5_Uterus_Pathophysiology_and_20Q_111",
    "week": "W1",
    "lecture": "Uterus Pathophysiology and (Notebook)",
    "file": "Notebook_W4_5_Uterus_Pathophysiology_and_20Q.md",
    "qnum": 111,
    "topic": "Uterus Pathophysiology and (Notebook)",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 26-year-old female presents to her gynecologist with a history of chronic pelvic pain and severely painful menses (dysmenorrhea). She notes that her pain has worsened over the past year and is not relieved by over-the-counter NSAIDs. During a diagnostic laparoscopy, the surgeon visualizes multiple dark \"powder-burn\" lesions on the peritoneal surface of her bladder and several adhesions surrounding her ovaries. Which of the following correctly describes this condition, its hormonal dependency, and its typical demographic?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Endometriosis; it represents the presence of endometrial tissue outside the endometrium and myometrium, occurs in the reproductive age group, and the lesions are estrogen-dependent.",
      "B": "Adenomyosis; it occurs predominantly in postmenopausal women and is completely progesterone-independent.",
      "C": "Endometrial carcinoma; it represents a primary malignancy of the pelvic peritoneum that arises due to chronic progesterone suppression.",
      "D": "Pelvic inflammatory disease; it represents an infectious disease caused by a mismatch repair gene mutation and is treated with high-dose estrogen.",
      "E": "Leiomyosarcoma; it represents a rare, de novo smooth muscle malignancy that spreads hematogenously to the anterior abdominal wall."
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "outside the endometrium and myometrium, occurs in the reproductive age group, and the lesions are estrogen-dependent. \u25cf  A. Endometriosis; it represents the presence of endometrial tissue outside the endometrium and myometrium, occurs in the reproductive age group, and the lesions are estrogen-dependent.  - Correct. Endometriosis  is defined as the presence of endometrial glands and stroma outside the endometrium and myometrium (most commonly on the ovaries, peritoneum, and fallopian tubes). It occurs in women of reproductive age, and the ectopic lesions are highly estrogen-dependent, proliferating and bleeding in response to cyclic hormonal changes, which causes chronic pain, dysmenorrhea, and pelvic adhesions. \u25cf  B. Adenomyosis; it occurs predominantly in postmenopausal women and is completely progesterone-independent.  - Incorrect.  Adenomyosis is ectopic endometrial tissue inside the myometrium, occurring mostly in reproductive-age/perimenopausal women. \u25cf  C. Endometrial carcinoma; it represents a primary malignancy of the pelvic peritoneum that arises due to chronic progesterone suppression.  - Incorrect. Endometriosis is a benign disease, not a primary peritoneal malignancy. \u25cf  D. Pelvic inflammatory disease; it represents an infectious disease caused by a mismatch repair gene mutation and is treated with high-dose estrogen.  - Incorrect. 144 Pelvic inflammatory disease is an ascending infectious disease, not caused by MMR mutations or treated with estrogen. \u25cf  E. Leiomyosarcoma; it represents a rare, de novo smooth muscle malignancy that spreads hematogenously to the anterior abdominal wall.  - Incorrect. This patient has typical benign endometriosis, not a leiomyosarcoma.",
