    "rationale": "ovarian estrogen); Specimen B represents the secretory phase (regulated by corpus luteum progesterone). A. Specimen A represents the proliferative phase (regulated by ovarian estrogen); Specimen B represents the secretory phase (regulated by corpus luteum progesterone).:  Correct  . The menstrual cycle has two main endometrial phases: 1) The proliferative (follicular) phase, represented by Specimen A, where straight, narrow glands and straight spiral arteries grow under the influence of estrogen secreted by developing follicles; and 2) The secretory (luteal) phase, represented by Specimen B, where highly coiled, saw-tooth glands and highly coiled spiral arteries develop, and glands secrete glycogen-rich fluid under the influence of progesterone from the corpus luteum. B. Specimen A represents the secretory phase (regulated by corpus luteum progesterone); Specimen B represents the proliferative phase (regulated by ovarian estrogen).:  Incorrect  . This is reversed. Straight/narrow glands are characteristic of the proliferative phase, while coiled/saw-tooth glands are characteristic of the secretory phase. C. Specimen A represents the decidualized phase (regulated by hCG); Specimen B represents the menstrual phase (regulated by ovarian FSH).:  Incorrect  .  Specimen A does not show decidualization (large pale cells), and Specimen B does not show the hemorrhagic sloughing of the menstrual phase. The secretory phase is driven by progesterone, not FSH. D. Specimen A represents the menstrual phase (regulated by prostaglandins); Specimen B represents the proliferative phase (regulated by ovarian LH).:  Incorrect  . Specimen A shows active growth with mitotic figures, not the necrotic shedding of the menstrual phase. The proliferative phase is regulated by estrogen, not LH. E. Specimen A represents the follicular phase (regulated by ovarian LH); Specimen B represents the luteal phase (regulated by endometrial estradiol).:  Incorrect  . While the endometrial phases correspond to the ovarian follicular and luteal phases, the proliferative phase is driven by estrogen (not LH), and the secretory phase is driven by progesterone (not estradiol).",
