    "rationale": "- **Female Sex Determination:** While often taught as a \"default\" pathway, ovarian development actively requires the expression of specific \"anti-testis\" genes to suppress the male pathway.\n- **WNT4 and DAX1:** The **WNT4** gene (and downstream signaling molecules like $\\beta$-catenin and FOXL2) actively promotes ovarian follicle development and strongly represses SOX9 expression.\n- **Pathophysiology of Mutation:** If a 46,XX fetus has a loss-of-function mutation in WNT4, the repression of SOX9 is lifted. This allows some low-level SOX9 expression to occur, leading to the formation of both ovarian and testicular tissue within the same gonad (**ovotestis**). \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Normal ovaries:** Impossible without proper WNT4 signaling.\n  - **(B) Agenesis:** Agenesis is usually caused by WT1 or SF1 mutations, which prevent the bipotential ridge from forming entirely.\n  - **(D) Streak gonads:** Caused by accelerated atresia of follicles due to the absence of a second X chromosome (Turner syndrome, 45,X).\n  - **(E) Pure testes:** Requires the presence of SRY; WNT4 loss alone causes partial masculinization (ovotestis), not complete pure testes.",
    "pearl": "Ovarian development is an active process driven by **WNT4**. WNT4 actively suppresses SOX9. Loss of WNT4 in a 46,XX female leads to partial masculinization and **ovotestis** formation.",
