    "rationale": "- **Turner Syndrome (45,X):** \n  - In embryonic life, 45,X females have normal initial ovarian development, and primordial germ cells migrate normally.\n  - **Pathophysiology of Streak Gonads:** The long-term survival and maintenance of ovarian follicles absolutely require the presence of genes located on **both X chromosomes** (specifically regions that escape X-inactivation). Without a second X chromosome, the oocytes undergo accelerated atresia (apoptosis) starting in utero. By birth or early childhood, the ovaries are completely depleted of germ cells and degenerate into fibrous connective tissue bands known as **streak gonads**.\n- **Hormonal Consequences:** Without functional ovaries, there is no estrogen or inhibin production, leading to lack of secondary sexual characteristics (primary amenorrhea) and a massive compensatory increase in FSH and LH (hypergonadotropic hypogonadism).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) LH & (E) FSH:** Levels are actually vastly elevated in Turner syndrome due to lack of negative feedback from the absent ovaries.\n  - **(B) WNT4:** Located on chromosome 1, not the X chromosome; it is present and functions to initiate ovarian development early on.\n  - **(D) SRY:** Causes testis formation; its absence is required for ovaries to form initially.",
    "pearl": "**Turner Syndrome (45,X)** features normal early ovarian development followed by rapid follicular atresia due to the lack of a **second X chromosome**, resulting in **streak gonads** and hypergonadotropic hypogonadism.",
