    "rationale": "- **Uterine Embryology:** The uterus, cervix, and upper 1/3 of the vagina are formed by the **lateral fusion** of the bilateral **paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts** in the midline. \n- **Pathophysiology of Mullerian Anomalies:**\n  - **Bicornuate Uterus:** Caused by **incomplete fusion** of the upper portions of the Mullerian ducts, resulting in two separate uterine horns that join at the lower uterine segment or cervix.\n  - **Uterus Didelphys:** Caused by *complete failure of fusion*, resulting in a double uterus, double cervix, and often a double vagina.\n  - **Septate Uterus:** The ducts fuse normally, but the central septum fails to undergo apoptosis (resorb), leaving a midline partition.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Vaginal plate canalization failure:** Causes an imperforate hymen or transverse vaginal septum.\n  - **(C) Mullerian agenesis:** Causes Mayer-Rokitansky-K\u00fcster-Hauser syndrome (absent uterus).\n  - **(D) Mesonephric ducts:** Do not fuse; they form bilateral structures in males.\n  - **(E) Excessive apoptosis:** Complete apoptosis of the septum is the *normal* process to create a single uterine cavity.",
    "pearl": "**Bicornuate uterus** = Incomplete **fusion** of Mullerian ducts.\n**Septate uterus** = Failure of **resorption** of the central septum (highest risk for recurrent miscarriage, fixable with surgery).",
