    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 46,XX fetus is exposed to abnormally high levels of maternal androgens during the first trimester due to a maternal luteoma of pregnancy. Despite the heavy androgen exposure, the female fetus is born with a completely normal, functional uterus, fallopian tubes, and upper vagina, though she exhibits marked clitoromegaly and labial fusion. Why did the high levels of androgens not cause the formation of male internal ductal structures (epididymis, seminal vesicles) in this female fetus?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Female fetuses lack functional androgen receptors until puberty",
      "B": "Androgens induce apoptosis of the mesonephric ducts in females",
      "C": "The mesonephric ducts require local paracrine testosterone concentrations much higher than systemic levels to survive",
      "D": "The paramesonephric ducts competitively bind and neutralize all systemic androgens",
      "E": "Maternal androgens cannot cross the placenta"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology of Virilization:** In a 46,XX female exposed to excess androgens (e.g., from maternal tumors or Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia), the **external genitalia** virilize (clitoromegaly, labial fusion) because the genital tubercle is highly sensitive to systemic androgens.\n- **Internal Duct Dynamics:** However, the female internal genitalia (uterus/tubes) are perfectly normal because there are no Sertoli cells to make AMH, so the Mullerian ducts survive. \n- **Wolffian Duct Failure:** The male internal Wolffian ducts (epididymis, vas deferens) **fail to develop** despite the androgen excess. This is because Wolffian duct survival requires incredibly high, **local, paracrine concentrations of testosterone** directly diffusing from adjacent fetal Leydig cells within the testis. Systemic, circulating maternal or adrenal androgens are never high enough to rescue the Wolffian ducts in a female.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Androgen receptors:** Functional in female fetuses (hence the virilized external genitalia).\n  - **(B) Apoptosis:** Androgens *rescue* mesonephric ducts, they don't destroy them. They die by default without massive local testosterone.\n  - **(D) Neutralization:** No such mechanism exists.\n  - **(E) Placental transfer:** Some androgens do cross (though the placenta aromatizes most to estrogen, overwhelming doses can virilize the fetus).",
    "pearl": "In 46,XX CAH (or maternal androgen exposure), the **external genitalia virilize**, but the **Wolffian ducts degenerate** because they require massive **local** testosterone from adjacent testes to survive.",
