    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The infant has **$5\\alpha$-reductase deficiency**.\n- **Pathophysiology:** The enzyme $5\\alpha$-reductase converts testosterone into the much more potent **dihydrotestosterone (DHT)**. \n- **Embryologic Targets:** DHT is absolutely required for the masculinization of the external genitalia and the prostate. The tissues that form these structures\u2014the **genital tubercle** (forms glans penis), the **urogenital folds** (fuse to form ventral penile shaft), the **labioscrotal swellings** (fuse to form scrotum), and the **urogenital sinus** (forms prostate/bulbourethral glands)\u2014have high concentrations of $5\\alpha$-reductase. \n- **Outcome:** Without DHT, these structures default to the female/ambiguous phenotype at birth, despite normal internal male structures (epididymis/vas) which only require testosterone.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Wolffian ducts:** Depend directly on testosterone, not DHT.\n  - **(B) Mullerian ducts:** Depend on AMH for regression.\n  - **(D) Gonadal ridge:** Depends on SRY gene.\n  - **(E) Ureteric bud:** Forms the renal collecting system.",
    "pearl": "**DHT** acts on the **genital tubercle, urogenital folds, and labioscrotal swellings** to form the penis and scrotum. Deficiency in **$5\\alpha$-reductase** causes ambiguous external genitalia in a 46,XY male.",
