    "rationale": "- **Embryology of the Urethra:** In males, the **urogenital folds** close over the urethral groove (zipping up from the base to the tip) to form the spongy urethra and the ventral aspect of the penis.\n- **Pathophysiology:** Failure of the urogenital folds to completely fuse results in an abnormal opening of the urethra anywhere along the **ventral** surface of the penis. This condition is called **hypospadias**. It is commonly associated with ventral curvature of the penis (chordee) and cryptorchidism.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Epispadias:** Abnormal opening on the *dorsal* (top) surface of the penis. It is caused by faulty positioning of the genital tubercle, not failure of urogenital fold fusion.\n  - **(C) Cryptorchidism:** Failure of testicular descent; often co-occurs, but is a separate defect.\n  - **(D) Hydrocele:** Fluid trapped by failure of the processus vaginalis to close.\n  - **(E) Bladder exstrophy:** Severe abdominal wall defect strongly associated with epispadias, not hypospadias.",
    "pearl": "**Hypospadias (ventral opening)** = Failure of **urogenital folds** to fuse.\n**Epispadias (dorsal opening)** = Faulty positioning of the **genital tubercle**. (Remember: \"Hypo is below, Epi is above\").",
