    "rationale": "- **Homologies of the Genital Tubercle:** The genital tubercle is the most cranial structure of the indifferent external genitalia.\n  - **In males (DHT present):** It elongates and differentiates into the **glans penis** and the deep erectile tissues (corpora cavernosa).\n  - **In females (DHT absent):** It bends inferiorly and becomes the **glans clitoris** and the clitoral crura/vestibular bulbs.\n- **Pathophysiology of CAIS:** Because the androgen receptors are defective, the genital tubercle cannot respond to testosterone or DHT, so it defaults to the female pathway, forming a clitoris despite the 46,XY karyotype.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Urogenital folds:** Form the ventral penile shaft (male) or labia minora (female).\n  - **(C) Labioscrotal swellings:** Form the scrotum (male) or labia majora (female).\n  - **(D) Urogenital sinus:** Forms the prostate/bulbourethral glands (male) or lower vagina/Bartholin glands (female).",
