    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Comparing CAIS and $5\\alpha$-reductase deficiency (both 46,XY):** Both present at birth with female or ambiguous external genitalia. \n- **Internal Ducts:**\n  - **CAIS:** Androgen receptors are completely broken everywhere. High testosterone cannot save the Wolffian ducts. Result: **No male internal ducts.**\n  - **$5\\alpha$-reductase deficiency:** Androgen receptors work perfectly! The only problem is the lack of DHT. Because testosterone is normal, it successfully rescues the Wolffian ducts. Result: **Normal male internal ducts (epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles) are present** inside the abdomen.\n- **Puberty:** At puberty, the massive surge of testosterone in $5\\alpha$-reductase deficiency is enough to partially overcome the lack of DHT, causing virilization (deep voice, muscle growth, clitoral enlargement/phallic growth\u2014often termed \"Guevedoces\" or \"penis at 12\"). CAIS patients do not virilize because the receptor is broken; they feminize via aromatization of testosterone to estrogen.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Sertoli cells:** Normal in both (AMH destroys the uterus in both).\n  - **(C) Karyotype:** Both are 46,XY.\n  - **(D) DHT levels:** CAIS patients have *normal to high* DHT (it just can't bind), while $5\\alpha$-reductase patients have *low* DHT.\n  - **(E) Virilization:** 5$\\alpha$-reductase patients virilize; CAIS patients do not.",
    "pearl": "**$5\\alpha$-reductase deficiency** = **Has** male internal ducts (Testosterone works), virilizes at puberty.\n**CAIS** = **Lacks** male internal ducts (Receptor broken), feminizes at puberty.",
