    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has classic signs of **Turner syndrome (45,X)**.\n- **Pathophysiology:** Due to the absence of the second X chromosome, the ovarian follicles undergo rapid atresia, leading to fibrous **streak gonads**.\n- **Hormonal Profile:** Because the streak gonads are completely non-functional, they produce **virtually no estrogen and no inhibin**. The lack of estrogen removes negative feedback on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary. Consequently, the pituitary hyper-secretes gonadotropins in a futile attempt to stimulate the dead ovaries. This results in markedly **elevated FSH and LH** (hypergonadotropic hypogonadism). FSH is typically higher than LH due to the loss of inhibin B (which selectively inhibits FSH).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Low FSH/LH:** Represents hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (e.g., Kallmann syndrome, hypothalamic failure).\n  - **(C) High Estrogen:** Ovaries are dead, so estrogen is low.\n  - **(D) High Testosterone:** Seen in PCOS or androgen-secreting tumors.",
    "pearl": "**Turner syndrome (45,X)** is characterized by **Hypergonadotropic Hypogonadism**: extremely **low estrogen** and massively **elevated FSH and LH** due to streak gonads.",
