    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** **XYY syndrome** (sometimes historically called \"supermale\" syndrome) is caused by paternal nondisjunction during meiosis II (resulting in a YY sperm).\n- **Phenotype:** Most males with 47,XYY are phenotypically normal and fertile. They are completely unaware they have the condition. The classic, subtle phenotypic traits include **tall stature** (due to an extra copy of the SHOX gene on the Y chromosome), severe acne during adolescence, and a slightly higher risk of learning disabilities or behavioral issues (though intelligence is usually in the normal range).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Severe intellectual disability:** Not characteristic; most have normal IQ.\n  - **(B) Ambiguous genitalia:** Genitalia are perfectly normal male.\n  - **(D) Gynecomastia and small testes:** Classic for Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY).\n  - **(E) Primary amenorrhea:** Occurs in phenotypic females.",
