    "stem": "A 19-year-old male is evaluated for delayed puberty. He is tall and has long arms and legs. He reports that he has never been able to smell his mother's cooking or perceive foul odors. Examination reveals a prepubertal testicular volume and absent pubic hair. Laboratory tests show low serum testosterone, low FSH, and low LH. An MRI of the brain reveals absent olfactory bulbs. What is the primary embryologic defect in this syndrome?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Failure of paramesonephric duct fusion",
      "B": "Failure of GnRH-secreting neurons to migrate from the olfactory placode",
      "C": "Agenesis of the anterior pituitary gland",
      "D": "Nondisjunction of sex chromosomes during meiosis I",
      "E": "Mutation in the SRY gene"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** **Kallmann syndrome**.\n- **Pathophysiology:** GnRH-secreting neurons do not originate in the hypothalamus. They originate in the embryonic **olfactory placode** (outside the brain) and must migrate along the olfactory nerve tract through the cribriform plate into the hypothalamus.\n- **The Defect:** In Kallmann syndrome (often due to a mutation in the *KAL1* gene), this migration fails. The patient is born without olfactory bulbs (**anosmia**) and without GnRH neurons in the hypothalamus (**hypogonadotropic hypogonadism** $\\rightarrow$ low GnRH, low FSH/LH, low testosterone/estrogen, delayed puberty).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Paramesonephric ducts:** Uterine development.\n  - **(C) Anterior pituitary agenesis:** Would cause panhypopituitarism (low TSH, ACTH, etc).\n  - **(D) Nondisjunction:** Causes Klinefelter/Turner.\n  - **(E) SRY mutation:** Causes Swyer syndrome (XY female).",
    "pearl": "**Kallmann syndrome** = Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism + **Anosmia**. Caused by failure of **GnRH neurons** to migrate from the **olfactory placode**.",
