    "stem": "A 25-year-old woman with a history of three consecutive first-trimester miscarriages undergoes a hysterosalpingogram. The imaging reveals a single, normal-sized cervical canal but a uterine cavity that is divided completely down the middle by a thick band of tissue, forming two separate endometrial cavities. The external contour of the uterine fundus on MRI is smooth and completely normal (no indentation). Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Bicornuate uterus",
      "B": "Septate uterus",
      "C": "Uterus didelphys",
      "D": "Arcuate uterus",
      "E": "Unicornuate uterus"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Uterine Anomalies & Miscarriage:**\n  - **Septate Uterus:** The Mullerian ducts fuse perfectly (creating a smooth, normal external fundal contour), but the central median septum fails to undergo apoptosis (resorb). The resulting avascular fibromuscular septum divides the cavity. If an embryo implants on this avascular septum, it dies, leading to **recurrent miscarriages**. This is the most common anomaly associated with miscarriage and is easily treated via hysteroscopic resection.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Bicornuate uterus:** Incomplete fusion of the ducts. The external fundus has a deep, visible indentation (heart-shaped).\n  - **(C) Uterus didelphys:** Complete failure of fusion; two entirely separate hemi-uteri and two cervices.\n  - **(D) Arcuate uterus:** A mild, normal variant with a slight dip in the fundal cavity.\n  - **(E) Unicornuate uterus:** Agenesis of one of the Mullerian ducts.",
    "pearl": "**Septate uterus** has a **smooth external contour** with a divided cavity and causes **recurrent miscarriages**. **Bicornuate uterus** has an indented external contour (heart-shaped).",
