    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "- **Testicular Descent:**\n  1. **Phase 1 (Transabdominal):** The testes move from the retroperitoneal kidney area to the deep inguinal ring. This is driven by the swelling and shortening of the gubernaculum, a process mediated primarily by **Insulin-like 3 (INSL3)**, a peptide hormone secreted by the fetal Leydig cells.\n  2. **Phase 2 (Inguinoscrotal):** The testes move through the inguinal canal into the scrotum. This phase requires the calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) and is heavily dependent on **Testosterone** (and indirectly maternal hCG stimulating the Leydig cells).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Testosterone & (B) DHT:** Mediate the second phase (inguinoscrotal), but not the first.\n  - **(C) AMH:** Destroys Mullerian ducts; has no role in testicular descent.\n  - **(E) hCG:** Stimulates testosterone production, thus aiding phase 2, but INSL3 is the direct driver of phase 1.",
    "pearl": "Testicular descent phase 1 (transabdominal) is driven by **INSL3** (from Leydig cells). Phase 2 (inguinoscrotal) is driven by **Testosterone**.",
