    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Basal Body Temperature (BBT) Method:** BBT tracking is a classical method to confirm ovulation.\n- **Pathophysiology of the Thermogenic Shift:** Following the LH surge and ovulation, the remnants of the ruptured follicle become the **corpus luteum**. The corpus luteum secretes massive amounts of **progesterone**. Progesterone acts directly on the thermoregulatory center in the hypothalamus to reset the body's set-point, causing a noticeable increase in basal body temperature ($0.5-1.0^\\circ\\text{F}$).\n- **Clinical Implication:** Because progesterone is only secreted in significant amounts *after* ovulation, a sustained temperature rise retrospectively confirms that ovulation has occurred and that the woman is in the luteal phase of her cycle.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Estradiol:** Dominates the follicular phase (days 1-14). It actually slightly lowers BBT or keeps it at baseline.\n  - **(B) LH:** The LH surge triggers ovulation, but it does not directly cause the temperature spike.\n  - **(D) FSH:** Stimulates follicle growth; has no thermogenic effect.\n  - **(E) Inhibin B:** Secreted by granulosa cells to suppress FSH; not thermogenic.",
    "pearl": "A biphasic basal body temperature graph confirms ovulation. The temperature elevation in the luteal phase is strictly driven by **Progesterone** acting on the hypothalamus.",
