    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "- **The Feedback Switch:** Throughout the majority of the menstrual cycle, estrogen exerts *negative feedback* on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary, keeping FSH and LH levels low.\n- **The LH Surge:** However, exactly mid-cycle (around days 12-14), when the dominant follicle matures, it produces a massive, sustained surge of estradiol (usually exceeding 200 pg/mL for at least 48 hours). This sustained high level flips the switch in the anterior pituitary to **positive feedback**. \n- **Consequence:** The pituitary responds by dumping its stored gonadotropins, creating the massive **LH surge** (and a smaller FSH surge). The LH surge is the absolute prerequisite trigger for ovulation, which occurs approximately 24-36 hours after the LH surge begins.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) GnRH:** While GnRH pulse frequency increases, the *massive surge* triggered by positive feedback primarily involves the pre-synthesized LH stored in the pituitary.\n  - **(B) Progesterone:** Triggered *by* the LH surge/ovulation, not the cause of it.\n  - **(C) Inhibin A:** Rises in the luteal phase.\n  - **(E) Prolactin:** Not involved in the mid-cycle ovulatory surge.",
    "pearl": "The mid-cycle **LH Surge** (which triggers ovulation) is caused by the brief switch of **Estrogen** from negative feedback to **positive feedback** on the anterior pituitary.",
