    "stem": "An endocrinologist is measuring serum hormone levels in a healthy 25-year-old female throughout her menstrual cycle. During the early follicular phase (Days 1-4), serum estradiol and progesterone levels are at their lowest. In response to these low steroid levels, the pituitary selectively increases the secretion of Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH) more than LH. As the dominant follicle grows during the mid-follicular phase, it begins to secrete a specific peptide hormone that selectively suppresses FSH release from the pituitary without affecting LH. What is the identity of this peptide hormone?",
