    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Follicular Selection:** At the start of the cycle, high FSH recruits a cohort of primordial follicles to grow. As they grow, the granulosa cells of these follicles secrete estrogen and **Inhibin B**.\n- **Role of Inhibin B:** Inhibin B provides highly specific **negative feedback to the anterior pituitary**, selectively inhibiting the secretion of **FSH** (while leaving LH alone). \n- **The \"Survival of the Fittest\":** As FSH levels drop due to Inhibin B, only the single most advanced follicle (the dominant follicle) with the highest number of FSH receptors can survive the FSH-starved environment. The other recruited follicles undergo atresia. \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Activin:** Stimulates FSH release (the opposite of inhibin).\n  - **(C) AMH:** Secreted by small growing follicles; it reflects the total ovarian reserve but does not acutely suppress pituitary FSH during the cycle.\n  - **(D) Inhibin A:** Secreted primarily by the *corpus luteum* during the luteal phase (not the growing follicle).\n  - **(E) Somatostatin:** Inhibits Growth Hormone and TSH.",
    "pearl": "**Inhibin B** is secreted by the growing follicle in the *follicular phase* to suppress FSH. **Inhibin A** is secreted by the corpus luteum in the *luteal phase*. Both selectively inhibit FSH.",
