    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Cycle Timing:** A high progesterone level ($>10$ ng/mL) places this patient squarely in the **mid-luteal phase** (around Day 21).\n- **Luteal Phase HPO Axis:** During the luteal phase, the corpus luteum secretes massive amounts of progesterone (and moderate estrogen/inhibin A). \n- **Negative Feedback:** Progesterone is a powerful central inhibitor. It exerts profound **negative feedback** on the hypothalamus, significantly **slowing the pulse frequency of GnRH**. This slow GnRH pulse frequency favors FSH synthesis over LH, but because of the concurrent high estrogen and inhibin A, both FSH and LH release are heavily suppressed. This suppression prevents the recruitment and ovulation of new follicles while waiting to see if pregnancy occurs.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) High frequency GnRH:** Favors LH secretion and occurs in the late follicular phase, not the luteal phase. Progesterone slows the frequency.\n  - **(B) Positive feedback:** Occurs only briefly at mid-cycle (Day 12-14).\n  - **(D) FSH surging:** FSH is heavily suppressed in the luteal phase by Inhibin A, Estrogen, and Progesterone.\n  - **(E) Corpus luteum apoptosis:** Apoptosis (luteolysis) occurs late (Day 26) when LH levels have been low for a prolonged period, causing progesterone to plummet, not peak.",
    "pearl": "The **Luteal Phase** is dominated by **Progesterone**, which exerts powerful **negative feedback**, dramatically slowing GnRH pulse frequency and suppressing FSH/LH to prevent further ovulations.",
