    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology of PMS:** Premenstrual Syndrome (PMS) and its more severe variant, Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder (PMDD), are strictly linked to the **luteal phase** of the ovulatory menstrual cycle. \n- **Mechanism:** Symptoms begin 5-7 days before menses, during the peak and subsequent decline of **progesterone** (and estrogen) produced by the corpus luteum. It is hypothesized that patients with PMS/PMDD have an abnormal central nervous system (serotonergic/GABAergic) sensitivity to normal fluctuations of progesterone and its neuroactive metabolites (like allopregnanolone).\n- **Clinical Clue:** Because PMS requires ovulation and a luteal phase to occur, treatments that suppress ovulation (like continuous oral contraceptive pills or GnRH agonists) are highly effective at eliminating PMS symptoms. Symptoms always resolve with the onset of menses (when steroids drop to baseline).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A), (B), (C), (E):** PMS does not occur during the follicular or ovulatory phases. Symptoms strictly correlate with the presence of the corpus luteum (luteal phase) and resolve when menstruation begins.",
