    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has classic stigmata of **Turner Syndrome** (short stature, shield chest, short 4th metacarpals) presenting with primary amenorrhea and lack of secondary sexual characteristics due to primary ovarian failure (indicated by the massive compensatory elevation in FSH).\n- **Definitive Diagnosis:** Turner syndrome is a chromosomal disorder, most commonly **45,X** (or mosaic 45,X/46,XX). The absolute gold standard and definitive diagnostic test is a **Karyotype analysis** (chromosome banding or microarray) to visually confirm the missing or structurally abnormal X chromosome.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Pelvic ultrasound:** Would show streak gonads and a small (infantile) uterus due to lack of estrogen, but this is supportive, not definitive for the genetic diagnosis.\n  - **(B) Brain MRI:** Useful for central causes of amenorrhea (Kallmann, tumors) where FSH would be *low*. This patient has *high* FSH, pointing to the gonads.\n  - **(D) Progesterone challenge:** Would be negative (no bleeding) because she lacks estrogen to build a lining.\n  - **(E) GnRH test:** Used to evaluate precocious puberty or central hypogonadism, not primary ovarian failure.",
