    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has an **imperforate hymen** causing **hematocolpos/hematometra** (trapped menstrual blood in the vagina and uterus).\n- **Pathophysiology of Complication:** Because the menstrual blood has no outflow tract through the vagina, the uterus becomes distended. During active menstruation, the blood is forced to take the path of least resistance: backward through the fallopian tubes and into the peritoneal cavity (retrograde menstruation). \n- **Complication:** Continuous, massive **retrograde menstruation** seeds the pelvis with viable endometrial cells, dramatically increasing the risk of developing severe **endometriosis**, pelvic adhesions, and subsequent infertility if the hymen is not surgically incised promptly.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Endometrial carcinoma:** Associated with unopposed estrogen (e.g., PCOS), not mechanical obstruction.\n  - **(C) CAIS:** A genetic condition, not a complication of an obstruction.\n  - **(D) Asherman syndrome:** Intrauterine adhesions caused by trauma (e.g., aggressive D&C), not imperforate hymen.\n  - **(E) PCOS:** An endocrine disorder, not caused by mechanical obstruction.",
