    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has **Sheehan Syndrome**. \n- **Pathophysiology:** \n  - During pregnancy, the anterior pituitary gland doubles in size (mainly due to lactotroph hypertrophy) to prepare for lactation. However, its blood supply (from the low-pressure hypothalamic-hypophyseal portal venous system) does not increase proportionally. This makes the enlarged pituitary exquisitely sensitive to ischemia.\n  - Severe **postpartum hemorrhage** and hypotension cause sudden severe ischemia and subsequent **infarction/necrosis of the anterior pituitary**.\n- **Clinical Features:** Panhypopituitarism follows.\n  - Lack of Prolactin $\\rightarrow$ **Failure to lactate** (classic first sign).\n  - Lack of LH/FSH $\\rightarrow$ Amenorrhea.\n  - Lack of TSH $\\rightarrow$ Secondary hypothyroidism (fatigue, cold intolerance).\n  - Lack of ACTH $\\rightarrow$ Secondary adrenal insufficiency (loss of pubic hair due to lack of adrenal androgens, weight loss).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Hemorrhagic infarction of adenoma:** This is pituitary apoplexy, a sudden, life-threatening acute headache/collapse in someone with a pre-existing tumor, not specific to postpartum hemorrhage.\n  - **(C) Autoimmune thyroiditis:** Does not explain the failure to lactate or amenorrhea.\n  - **(D) Endometrial scarring:** This is Asherman syndrome. It causes amenorrhea after postpartum D&C, but patients have normal pituitary function (they can lactate and have normal thyroid/adrenal function).\n  - **(E) Hypothalamus:** The hypothalamus has a robust arterial blood supply and is not typically infarcted in Sheehan syndrome.",
