    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has **Asherman Syndrome** (intrauterine synechiae/adhesions).\n- **Diagnostic Logic:**\n  - She has secondary amenorrhea following severe uterine trauma (aggressive D&C + infection).\n  - Biphasic BBT and normal hormones prove her HPG axis and ovaries are working perfectly (she is ovulating and making estrogen/progesterone).\n  - **Negative progesterone challenge:** Despite having normal endogenous estrogen, she does not bleed when progesterone is withdrawn. This proves the problem is an **anatomic outflow obstruction or a destroyed endometrium**.\n- **Pathophysiology:** The aggressive D&C scraped away the regenerative *stratum basalis* layer of the endometrium. The walls of the uterus scarred together (synechiae). There is no endometrium left to proliferate or shed.\n- **Diagnosis/Treatment:** The gold standard for diagnosis and treatment is **Hysteroscopy** to directly visualize and lyse (cut) the scar tissue inside the uterine cavity.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Brain MRI:** Hormones are normal, ruling out central causes.\n  - **(C) Karyotype:** She had regular menses previously, ruling out primary genetic causes.\n  - **(D) GnRH agonist:** Worsens amenorrhea.\n  - **(E) Repeat progesterone:** Will fail again because there is no endometrial tissue to respond to it.",
    "pearl": "**Asherman Syndrome** = Secondary amenorrhea following **uterine trauma (D&C)**. The HPG axis is normal, but the **stratum basalis is destroyed**. Progesterone challenge is negative. Diagnose with **Hysteroscopy**.",
