    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has drug-induced **hyperprolactinemia** causing galactorrhea and secondary amenorrhea.\n- **Mechanism of Prolactin Regulation:** Prolactin is unique among pituitary hormones because it is under tonic, constant *inhibition* by **Dopamine** (released from the hypothalamus via the tuberoinfundibular pathway).\n- **Pharmacology:** Typical and atypical antipsychotics, such as **Risperidone**, haloperidol, and fluphenazine, are strong **Dopamine ($D_2$) receptor antagonists**. By blocking dopamine receptors in the anterior pituitary, the brake on prolactin is removed. Prolactin levels soar, causing galactorrhea. The high prolactin inhibits GnRH, leading to amenorrhea. Risperidone is notoriously the worst offender among atypical antipsychotics for causing hyperprolactinemia.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Lithium:** Used for bipolar disorder, but causes thyroid/renal issues (hypothyroidism, nephrogenic DI), not hyperprolactinemia.\n  - **(C) Valproic acid:** Can cause weight gain and PCOS-like symptoms, but not direct hyperprolactinemia.\n  - **(D) Fluoxetine:** An SSRI; can cause sexual dysfunction but not amenorrhea/galactorrhea.\n  - **(E) Lamotrigine:** Causes Stevens-Johnson syndrome; no effect on prolactin.",
    "pearl": "Antipsychotics (especially **Risperidone** and typicals) block **Dopamine ($D_2$) receptors**. This removes the inhibition on prolactin, causing **hyperprolactinemia, galactorrhea, and amenorrhea**.",
