    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A symptomatic **Prolactinoma** (macroadenoma $>1$ cm) causing mass effect (visual defects, headaches) and amenorrhea.\n- **Treatment Strategy:** Unlike most other pituitary tumors (where surgery is first-line), prolactinomas are uniquely exquisitely sensitive to medical therapy.\n- **Pharmacology:** Because dopamine is the physiologic inhibitor of prolactin, administering a **Dopamine agonist (such as Cabergoline or Bromocriptine)** acts directly on the $D_2$ receptors of the lactotroph adenoma. This not only normalizes prolactin secretion (restoring GnRH pulsatility and menses) but also physically shrinks the tumor size in over 90% of patients, reversing the visual field defects. Surgery (transsphenoidal resection) is reserved only for patients who fail or cannot tolerate medical therapy.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Octreotide:** A somatostatin analog used to treat Growth Hormone-secreting adenomas (Acromegaly) or TSHomas.\n  - **(C) Leuprolide:** Shuts down the HPG axis; would not shrink a prolactinoma.\n  - **(D) Clomiphene:** Used to induce ovulation in PCOS; would not shrink a pituitary tumor.\n  - **(E) Medroxyprogesterone:** Would induce withdrawal bleeding in PCOS, but does not treat a prolactinoma.",
    "pearl": "The first-line treatment for **Prolactinomas** (even large macroadenomas with visual defects) is medical therapy with a **Dopamine agonist (Cabergoline or Bromocriptine)** to shrink the tumor and restore menses.",
