    "stem": "A 26-year-old obese woman presents with secondary amenorrhea, severe facial acne, and excessive terminal hair growth on her chin and chest. Pelvic ultrasound reveals bilaterally enlarged ovaries with multiple small subcapsular follicles (\"string of pearls\" sign). Her physician suspects Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS). In this condition, which of the following best describes the classical abnormality in the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Profound suppression of GnRH pulsatility",
      "B": "Massive overproduction of FSH relative to LH",
      "C": "Increased frequency of GnRH pulses leading to an elevated LH/FSH ratio",
      "D": "Exhaustion of the ovarian follicular reserve",
      "E": "Failure of the anterior pituitary to respond to estrogen positive feedback"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology of PCOS:** The neuroendocrine hallmark of PCOS is an abnormally **rapid frequency of GnRH pulses** from the hypothalamus.\n- **LH/FSH Imbalance:** Rapid GnRH pulses heavily favor the secretion of **LH** over FSH. This leads to the classic **LH:FSH ratio of $>2:1$ or $3:1$**.\n- **Ovarian Consequences:** \n  - The high **LH** constantly stimulates the theca interna cells to produce massive amounts of **androgens** (testosterone/androstenedione), causing hirsutism and acne.\n  - The relatively low **FSH** is insufficient to allow a single dominant follicle to mature fully. The follicles stall in development (creating the multiple cystic follicles seen on ultrasound) and fail to ovulate (amenorrhea).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Suppression of GnRH:** Seen in FHA (anorexia). PCOS has *rapid* GnRH.\n  - **(B) Overproduction of FSH:** The exact opposite occurs; LH is overproduced.\n  - **(D) Exhaustion of reserve:** Seen in menopause/POI, where FSH is sky-high.\n  - **(E) Failure of positive feedback:** The positive feedback mechanism works, but because no single follicle ever matures due to low FSH, estrogen levels never spike sharply enough to trigger the LH surge.",
    "pearl": "In **PCOS**, rapid GnRH pulses drive an **elevated LH/FSH ratio ($>2:1$)**. High LH drives **androgens** (hirsutism), while low FSH causes anovulation (amenorrhea).",
