    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **PCOS Pathophysiology:** Patients with PCOS are profoundly **anovulatory**.\n- **The Endometrial State:** Because they do not ovulate, they never form a corpus luteum, and therefore they **produce no progesterone**. However, their ovarian follicles (and peripheral fat converting androgens to estrone) constantly produce **estrogen**.\n- **Unopposed Estrogen:** The endometrium is subjected to continuous, steady-state estrogen without the stabilizing and differentiating effects of progesterone (a state called **unopposed estrogen**). This causes the endometrium to continuously proliferate and grow (hyperplasia) until it outgrows its blood supply and irregularly sloughs (causing heavy, erratic bleeding).\n- **Clinical Consequence:** This constant proliferative state puts PCOS patients at a significantly increased lifetime risk of developing **endometrial hyperplasia and endometrial carcinoma**.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Atrophic:** Seen in menopause/POI due to *low* estrogen. PCOS has *high* estrogen.\n  - **(D) Secretory phase:** Requires progesterone (which requires ovulation). PCOS patients are anovulatory.\n  - **(E) Fibrotic:** Describes Asherman syndrome.",
    "pearl": "**PCOS** causes anovulation, resulting in a lack of progesterone. The endometrium is subjected to **unopposed estrogen**, causing continuous proliferation and a high risk of **Endometrial Cancer**.",
