    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnostic Logic - Step by Step:**\n  - **History of Hemorrhage:** Might suggest Sheehan or Asherman, but let's look closer.\n  - **Breastfed successfully for 6 months:** This **absolutely rules out Sheehan syndrome**. If the anterior pituitary was infarcted, she would have had no prolactin and could never have initiated lactation.\n  - **Positive Progesterone Challenge:** This means she has endogenous estrogen (her ovaries and pituitary are working) AND her outflow tract is patent. This **absolutely rules out Asherman syndrome** (which would not bleed due to a scarred endometrium).\n- **Conclusion:** If she bleeds with progesterone, she has estrogen. If she has estrogen but is amenorrheic, she is **anovulatory**. The most common cause of anovulation in a reproductive-aged woman with normal body weight/mild overweight is **PCOS** or a mild hypothalamic dysfunction. The hemorrhage is a distractor.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Sheehan:** Ruled out by successful breastfeeding.\n  - **(B) POI:** Ruled out by the positive progesterone challenge (POI patients lack estrogen and won't bleed).\n  - **(D) Asherman:** Ruled out by the positive progesterone challenge (blood can exit, endometrium is intact).",
    "pearl": "A history of successful **breastfeeding** immediately **rules out Sheehan syndrome**. A **positive progesterone challenge** immediately **rules out Asherman syndrome** and primary ovarian failure.",
