    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** Heavy menstrual bleeding and severe pain in a woman in her 40s.\n- **Physical Exam Clue:** The classic pathognomonic physical exam finding for **Adenomyosis** is a **uniformly enlarged, soft, boggy, globular, and tender uterus**.\n- **Pathophysiology:** Adenomyosis is defined as the ectopic presence of endometrial glands and stroma deeply embedded *within* the uterine myometrium. During the menstrual cycle, this trapped ectopic tissue proliferates and bleeds within the muscle wall, causing severe cramping (dysmenorrhea), muscle hypertrophy (globular enlargement), and heavy bleeding.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Endometriosis:** Endometrial tissue *outside* the uterus (ovaries, peritoneum). Uterus size is normal.\n  - **(B) Leiomyoma (Fibroid):** Presents with heavy bleeding, but the uterus is typically **irregularly enlarged, firm, and non-tender** (lumpy/bumpy).\n  - **(D) Endometrial polyp:** Causes bleeding, but the uterus is normal in size and non-tender.\n  - **(E) Endometrial carcinoma:** Usually presents as postmenopausal bleeding; uterus is typically normal size early on.",
