    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has **Leiomyomas (Uterine Fibroids)**. This is the most common benign tumor in females, particularly prevalent in African American women. \n- **Symptoms:** Heavy bleeding (menorrhagia) and \"bulk\" symptoms (pelvic pressure compressing the bladder $\\rightarrow$ frequency, or rectum $\\rightarrow$ constipation). \n- **Exam:** The classic **irregularly enlarged, firm, non-tender** \"lumpy-bumpy\" uterus.\n- **Pathology:** Leiomyomas are benign, monoclonal proliferations of **uterine smooth muscle cells (myometrium)** arranged in distinctive whorled patterns, separated by well-vascularized connective tissue. Their growth is highly estrogen-sensitive (they grow during pregnancy and shrink after menopause).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Endometrial glands:** Describes adenomyosis (if in myometrium) or polyps.\n  - **(C) Trophoblast cells:** Describes a choriocarcinoma or hydatidiform mole (pregnancy related).\n  - **(D) Neuronal/glial tissue:** Describes a hamartoma or teratoma.\n  - **(E) Malignant squamous cells:** Cervical cancer.",
    "pearl": "**Leiomyomas (Fibroids)** are benign tumors of **smooth muscle** (myometrium). Their growth is stimulated by **Estrogen**.",
