    "rationale": "- **The PALM-COEIN System:** Developed to standardize the causes of AUB.\n  - **Structural (PALM):** Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma, Malignancy/hyperplasia.\n  - **Non-Structural (COEIN):** Coagulopathy, Ovulatory dysfunction, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, Not yet classified.\n- **Patient Presentation:** The patient has lifelong menorrhagia, epistaxis, easy bruising, a normal PT, prolonged aPTT, and prolonged bleeding time. This is the classic clinical and laboratory presentation of **von Willebrand Disease (vWD)**, the most common inherited bleeding disorder.\n- **Classification:** Because her heavy uterine bleeding is secondary to an underlying systemic bleeding disorder, it is classified under **C - Coagulopathy (AUB-C)**. Von Willebrand disease is a very common, often overlooked cause of severe menorrhagia in teenagers.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A), (B):** These are structural issues; she has a systemic bleeding problem.\n  - **(D) Ovulatory dysfunction:** Presents with *irregular*, unpredictable periods (like PCOS). Her periods are heavy, but likely regular.\n  - **(E) Endometrial:** Refers to primary defects in local endometrial hemostasis in women with regular cycles and no systemic coagulopathy.",
    "pearl": "Heavy menstrual bleeding starting at menarche, associated with nosebleeds and easy bruising, is highly suspicious for **von Willebrand Disease (AUB-C / Coagulopathy)**.",
