    "topic": "Lecture 3.2: Menstrual Irregularities and the HPO Axis - Set 1",
    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 31-year-old woman presents with intermenstrual bleeding (spotting between her normal periods). She has a normal BMI, regular 28-day cycles, and no pain. Pelvic examination is unremarkable, and the uterus is normal in size. Saline infusion sonohysterography reveals a 2-cm, smoothly contoured, echogenic mass protruding into the endometrial cavity on a vascular stalk. This lesion is most likely a benign, localized overgrowth of which of the following tissues?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Endometrial glands and stroma",
      "B": "Myometrial smooth muscle",
      "C": "Stratified squamous epithelium",
      "D": "Syncytiotrophoblast",
      "E": "Serosal mesothelium"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has an **Endometrial Polyp (AUB-P)**. Polyps are a classic cause of intermenstrual bleeding (spotting between periods) in a woman with otherwise normal, regular ovulatory cycles and a normal-sized uterus.\n- **Pathology:** An endometrial polyp is a benign, localized, hyperplastic overgrowth of **endometrial glands and stroma** that projects into the uterine cavity, often on a vascular pedicle (stalk). They are highly friable and bleed easily, causing the spotting.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Myometrial smooth muscle:** This describes a submucosal leiomyoma (fibroid). While it also protrudes into the cavity and causes bleeding, fibroids are usually firm and cause heavy menstrual bleeding (menorrhagia) and a bulky uterus, rather than just intermenstrual spotting. Polyps are composed of glands/stroma, not muscle.\n  - **(C) Stratified squamous:** Found on the cervix (cervical polyps).\n  - **(D) Syncytiotrophoblast:** Gestational trophoblastic disease (moles).\n  - **(E) Serosal mesothelium:** Outer lining of the uterus.",
    "pearl": "**Intermenstrual bleeding** (spotting between regular periods) in a normal-sized uterus is the classic presentation of an **Endometrial Polyp** (benign overgrowth of endometrial glands and stroma).",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Lecture_3_2__Menstrual_Irregularities_an_12",
        "text": "D iscuss, in even greater detail, the tw o most common causes of anovulatory uterine bleeding (functional hypothalamic amenorrhea and polycystic ovary syndrome) : their pathophysiology, clinical presentations, long term health risks , and t reatments."
      }
    ]
