    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Male Pubertal Milestones:** \n  - The very first physical sign of male puberty (gonadarche) is **testicular enlargement**.\n  - A prepubertal testis has a volume of 1-3 mL. \n  - **Testicular volume $\\ge 4$ mL** (or long axis $>2.5$ cm) marks the onset of puberty (transitioning into Tanner Stage II).\n  - This enlargement is primarily due to the proliferation of seminiferous tubules (Sertoli and germ cells) driven by FSH. Penile lengthening and pubic hair development occur *after* testicular enlargement begins.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Premature adrenarche:** Involves early pubic/axillary hair (DHEA), but does *not* cause testicular enlargement (which requires HPG axis/LH/FSH).\n  - **(C) Delayed puberty:** A 12-year-old with 5 mL testes is progressing completely normally. Delayed puberty in males is defined as no testicular enlargement by age 14.\n  - **(D) Klinefelter syndrome:** Characterized by firm, *small* testes ($<3$ mL) that do not enlarge significantly during puberty due to tubular fibrosis.\n  - **(E) CAIS:** Present as phenotypic females.",
    "pearl": "The first physical sign of puberty in a male is **testicular enlargement (volume $\\ge 4$ mL)**. The first sign in a female is the **breast bud (thelarche)**.",
