    "stem": "An 8-year-old boy is brought to the clinic because of the rapid onset of pubic hair, axillary odor, and facial acne over the past 4 months. He has no significant past medical history. Examination reveals Tanner Stage III pubic hair. His penile length is increased, but his **right testis is 8 mL while his left testis is 2 mL**. An ultrasound of the scrotum is most likely to reveal which of the following?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "A varicocele in the left hemiscrotum",
      "B": "A hydrocele in the right hemiscrotum",
      "C": "A Leydig cell tumor in the right testis",
      "D": "Bilateral testicular microlithiasis",
      "E": "Cryptorchidism of the left testis"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnostic Logic for Precocious Puberty:**\n  - The boy has signs of virilization (pubic hair, acne).\n  - Look at the testes: **Asymmetric testicular enlargement** (Right = 8 mL, Left = 2 mL prepubertal).\n- **The Diagnosis:** The unilateral testicular enlargement rules out central precocious puberty (which causes symmetric, bilateral enlargement via systemic FSH/LH) and adrenal causes like CAH (which cause systemic androgens with bilateral *small* prepubertal testes due to LH suppression). \n- **Leydig Cell Tumor:** A Leydig cell tumor is an autonomous, androgen-secreting tumor of the testis. The tumor itself is palpable and accounts for the physical enlargement of the right testis (8 mL). The massive amounts of testosterone produced virilize the boy. The high testosterone exerts negative feedback on the pituitary, shutting down LH/FSH. Because there is no FSH, the contralateral (left) normal testis remains dormant and prepubertal (2 mL).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Varicocele & (B) Hydrocele:** Do not secrete hormones or cause precocious puberty.\n  - **(D) Microlithiasis:** A radiologic finding associated with germ cell tumors, not androgen-secreting Leydig cell tumors.\n  - **(E) Cryptorchidism:** The left testis is palpable in the scrotum, just small.",
    "pearl": "**Asymmetric testicular enlargement** with signs of precocious puberty strongly points to a **Leydig cell tumor** (Peripheral Precocious Puberty) in the enlarged testis.",
