    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has delayed puberty (no testicular enlargement by age 14).\n- **Constitutional Delay of Growth and Puberty (CDGP):** This is the **most common cause** of delayed puberty in boys. It is a benign, often autosomal dominant condition characterized by a temporary, physiological delay in the reactivation of the HPG axis (\"late bloomers\").\n- **Classic Clues:**\n  1. A strong **family history** of delayed puberty (father grew late).\n  2. The patient's **bone age is delayed** and typically matches his height age and pubertal stage, rather than his chronological age.\n  3. Eventually, the axis *will* wake up spontaneously, and the patient will achieve normal adult height and sexual maturity without any intervention.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Klinefelter & (E) Primary failure:** Present with *hypergonadotropic* hypogonadism (high FSH). CDGP has prepubertal (low) FSH.\n  - **(C) Kallmann syndrome:** Presents similarly with low FSH/LH, but features **anosmia** and will *never* spontaneously resolve.\n  - **(D) Craniopharyngioma:** Associated with headaches, visual field cuts (bitemporal hemianopsia), and pituitary hormone deficiencies.",
    "pearl": "**Constitutional Delay of Growth and Puberty (CDGP)** is a benign variant of normal development (\"late bloomers\"). It features a **delayed bone age** that matches the delayed puberty and a strong **family history**. Reassurance is the primary treatment.",
