    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A classic presentation of **CAIS (46,XY)**.\n- **Key Distinguishing Features:**\n  - **Absent Pubic/Axillary Hair:** Growth of body hair in females requires functional androgen receptors to respond to adrenal androgens (DHEA). In CAIS, the androgen receptors are mutated. Thus, despite high testosterone, there is **no body hair**.\n  - **Normal Breast Development:** The high testosterone produced by the intra-abdominal testes is aromatized to estrogen in the peripheral adipose tissue, driving normal/abundant breast development.\n  - **Absent Uterus:** The testes produce Anti-Mullerian Hormone (AMH), causing the uterus and upper vagina to regress (leaving a blind vaginal pouch).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Turner syndrome:** Poor breast development, presence of a uterus, high FSH.\n  - **(C) Mullerian Agenesis (MRKH):** Similar presentation (absent uterus, normal breasts), but these are 46,XX females with normal ovaries and **normal pubic/axillary hair** (their androgen receptors work perfectly).\n  - **(D) 5$\\alpha$-reductase deficiency:** Present with ambiguous genitalia at birth and *virilize* (deep voice, clitoromegaly) at puberty without breast development.\n  - **(E) Kallmann syndrome:** No breast development, anosmia, low testosterone/estrogen.",
    "pearl": "To differentiate the two causes of primary amenorrhea with an absent uterus:\n**CAIS:** Absent pubic/axillary hair (karyotype 46,XY).\n**Mullerian Agenesis (MRKH):** Normal pubic/axillary hair (karyotype 46,XX).",
