    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has **Kallmann syndrome**.\n- **The Triad:** Delayed puberty (hypogonadotropic hypogonadism), **anosmia** (inability to smell), and associated midline facial defects (e.g., cleft lip/palate).\n- **Pathophysiology:** During embryogenesis, the neurons that synthesize and secrete GnRH originate outside the central nervous system in the **olfactory placode**. They must migrate along the olfactory nerve tract, through the cribriform plate, to reach their final destination in the hypothalamus. In Kallmann syndrome, a genetic mutation (often *KAL1* or *FGFR1*) prevents this migration. The patient is born without olfactory bulbs (causing anosmia) and without hypothalamic GnRH neurons (causing hypogonadotropic hypogonadism).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Autoimmune destruction:** Would cause panhypopituitarism, but does not explain anosmia.\n  - **(C) Androgen receptor mutation:** Causes CAIS.\n  - **(D) Nondisjunction:** Causes Klinefelter (hypergonadotropic, normal smell).\n  - **(E) GNAS mutation:** Causes McCune-Albright (precocious puberty).",
