    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Fertility in Kallmann Syndrome:** Patients with Kallmann syndrome lack hypothalamic GnRH. Consequently, their pituitary produces no FSH or LH, and their testes remain completely prepubertal and dormant.\n- **Testosterone vs. Fertility:** While exogenous testosterone injections successfully virilize the patient (deep voice, beard, muscle mass), exogenous systemic testosterone *cannot* stimulate spermatogenesis. In fact, high systemic testosterone suppresses the HPG axis further. Spermatogenesis requires incredibly high local concentrations of testosterone inside the testis (driven by LH) AND direct stimulation of Sertoli cells (driven by FSH).\n- **The Solution:** To achieve fertility, you must replace the missing brain signals. This is done via a portable pump delivering **pulsatile GnRH**, or by injecting **hCG (acts like LH)** to stimulate Leydig cell testosterone, combined with **recombinant FSH** to stimulate Sertoli cells and sperm production.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Exogenous testosterone:** Virilizes, but does not induce spermatogenesis (it acts as a male contraceptive by inhibiting whatever axis remains).\n  - **(C) Continuous GnRH:** Causes downregulation of the pituitary, worsening the hypogonadism.\n  - **(D) Clomiphene citrate:** An estrogen receptor antagonist that works by blocking negative feedback to *increase* endogenous GnRH. It requires an intact hypothalamus, which this patient lacks.\n  - **(E) ICSI with donor sperm:** He can father his *own* biological children if his testes are stimulated properly.",
    "pearl": "In men with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (e.g., Kallmann), exogenous testosterone provides virilization but **cannot induce spermatogenesis**. Fertility requires **pulsatile GnRH** or **hCG + FSH**.",
