    "rationale": "- **Embryology of Kallmann Syndrome:** GnRH neurons do not originate in the neural tube (central nervous system).\n- **Olfactory Placode:** They originate in the **olfactory placode**, which is a thickening of the **surface ectoderm** located at the rostral end of the embryo. \n- **Migration:** These GnRH neurons must physically crawl out of the placode, follow the tracks of the olfactory and vomeronasal axons across the cribriform plate, and migrate into the forebrain to settle in the hypothalamus. Mutations in *KAL1* (anosmin-1) destroy the axonal tracks, stranding both the olfactory neurons and the GnRH neurons outside the brain.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Endoderm:** Forms gut tube lining and organs (liver, pancreas, lungs).\n  - **(B) Mesoderm:** Forms muscle, bone, connective tissue, kidneys, and gonadal stroma.\n  - **(C) Neural crest:** Forms peripheral nervous system (DRG, autonomic ganglia), melanocytes, adrenal medulla, and facial cartilage, but NOT the olfactory placode.\n  - **(E) Notochord:** Induces the neural plate and becomes the nucleus pulposus of intervertebral discs.",
