    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology of 5$\\alpha$-Reductase Deficiency:** \n  - DHT is a much more potent agonist of the androgen receptor than testosterone. During fetal development, the low baseline levels of testosterone cannot adequately stimulate the external genitalia to masculinize without being converted to DHT. Thus, the infant is born with female/ambiguous genitalia.\n  - **The Pubertal Shift:** At puberty, the testes (driven by the HPG axis) begin to pump out **massive, overwhelming amounts of testosterone**. \n  - Although testosterone has a lower affinity for the androgen receptor than DHT, the sheer overwhelming quantity of testosterone during the pubertal surge is high enough to bind the receptors and drive significant virilization on its own (muscle growth, penile/clitoral enlargement, voice deepening). This phenomenon leads to the classic presentation of a child raised as a girl who \"turns into a boy\" at puberty (Guevedoces).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Upregulation:** Receptors are not upregulated; it's the ligand concentration that spikes.\n  - **(B) Isoenzyme 1:** Isoenzyme 1 is present in the skin/liver, but the massive surge of testosterone is the primary driver of the dramatic virilization.\n  - **(D) Adrenal DHEA:** DHEA is a weak androgen and does not cause profound virilization like testicular testosterone.\n  - **(E) Testicular DHT:** The testes lack the enzyme, they cannot make DHT.",
    "pearl": "In **5$\\alpha$-reductase deficiency (46,XY)**, patients are born female/ambiguous but **virilize at puberty** because the massive pubertal surge of **Testosterone** is high enough to activate androgen receptors without needing conversion to DHT.",
