    "yield_level": "Mid",
    "stem": "A 17-year-old female presents with primary amenorrhea. She has normal secondary sexual characteristics. Pelvic ultrasound confirms a normal uterus and ovaries. Her physician suspects an outflow tract obstruction or an anovulatory state. A progesterone challenge test is performed (medroxyprogesterone acetate is given orally for 10 days and then stopped). Five days after stopping the medication, the patient experiences brisk withdrawal bleeding. What does this positive withdrawal bleed definitively prove?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The patient has primary ovarian failure.",
      "B": "The patient has an anatomic outflow tract obstruction (e.g., imperforate hymen).",
      "C": "The endometrium has been adequately primed with endogenous estrogen, and the outflow tract is patent.",
      "D": "The patient has Kallmann syndrome.",
      "E": "The patient is pregnant."
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **The Progesterone Challenge Test:** Used to assess estrogen status and outflow tract patency in amenorrhea.\n- **Mechanism:** Exogenous progesterone is given to mimic the luteal phase, stabilizing the endometrium. It is then withdrawn, simulating luteolysis. \n- **Positive Test (Bleeding occurs):** In order for bleeding to occur, there must be a lining of endometrial tissue (stratum functionalis) to shed. This lining only grows if the ovaries are producing adequate **endogenous estrogen**. Furthermore, for the blood to exit the body, the **outflow tract (cervix, vagina) must be patent**. \n  - Therefore, a positive test rules out primary ovarian failure, hypothalamic failure, and anatomic obstructions. The diagnosis is typically **anovulation** (e.g., PCOS), where estrogen is present but the patient isn't ovulating to make her own progesterone.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Primary ovarian failure (Turner):** Low estrogen means no lining is built. Progesterone withdrawal will cause **no bleeding**.\n  - **(B) Outflow obstruction:** Even if estrogen built a lining, the blood cannot escape. **No visible bleeding**.\n  - **(D) Kallmann syndrome:** Low estrogen (hypothalamic failure). **No bleeding**.\n  - **(E) Pregnancy:** Progesterone withdrawal will not cause a period if hCG is maintaining a massive endogenous progesterone supply (though pregnancy should be ruled out *before* giving hormones).",
    "pearl": "**Positive Progesterone Challenge (Bleeding):** Patient has adequate **Estrogen** and a **Patent outflow tract** (Diagnosis: Anovulation/PCOS).\n**Negative (No Bleeding):** Patient lacks estrogen (ovarian/hypothalamic failure) OR has an anatomic blockage.",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Lecture_3_1__Puberty_and_Pubertal_Disord_11",
        "text": "Primary Ovarian Insufficiency"
      }
    ]
