    "stem": "A 45-year-old woman is found to have a large, rapidly growing uterine mass. She undergoes a hysterectomy. The pathologist carefully examines the mass to differentiate between a benign leiomyoma and a malignant leiomyosarcoma. Which of the following histologic features is the most critical criterion for diagnosing a leiomyosarcoma?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The presence of a whorled pattern of smooth muscle bundles",
      "B": "The presence of hyaline degeneration",
      "C": "High mitotic index with cellular atypia and tumor necrosis",
      "D": "The presence of dense calcifications within the tumor",
      "E": "The lack of a true fibrous capsule surrounding the mass"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Leiomyoma vs. Leiomyosarcoma:** \n  - Leiomyosarcomas are malignant smooth muscle tumors. They arise *de novo* from the myometrium, NOT from the malignant transformation of a pre-existing benign fibroid.\n- **Histologic Diagnosis:** Because benign fibroids can sometimes grow large or undergo benign degeneration, the pathologist relies on strict microscopic criteria to diagnose cancer. The classic triad required to diagnose a **leiomyosarcoma** includes:\n  1. **High mitotic index** (frequent cell division, e.g., $>10$ mitoses per 10 high-power fields).\n  2. **Severe cellular/nuclear atypia** (pleomorphism).\n  3. **Tumor cell necrosis** (coagulative necrosis of the tumor cells themselves, not just ischemic hyaline degeneration).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Whorled pattern:** The classic benign feature of a leiomyoma.\n  - **(B) Hyaline degeneration:** A very common, completely *benign* degenerative change seen in older fibroids when they outgrow their blood supply and the muscle is replaced by collagen.\n  - **(D) Calcifications:** Classic feature of old, *benign* fibroids, especially in postmenopausal women.\n  - **(E) Lack of capsule:** Benign fibroids do *not* have a true histologic capsule (though they have a pseudocapsule of compressed normal myometrium). This does not indicate malignancy.",
    "pearl": "**Leiomyosarcomas** arise *de novo*. The histologic hallmarks are **necrosis, severe atypia, and a high mitotic rate**.",
