    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The classic presentation of **Adenomyosis**.\n- **Key Discriminators:**\n  - **Symptoms:** Secondary dysmenorrhea (new-onset painful periods in an older woman) and menorrhagia (heavy bleeding).\n  - **Physical Exam:** The pathognomonic finding is a **symmetrically (uniformly) enlarged, soft/boggy, and tender uterus**. \n- **Pathophysiology:** Adenomyosis is the abnormal presence of endometrial glands and stroma growing deep *within* the muscular wall of the uterus (the myometrium). This ectopic tissue swells and bleeds during menstruation, causing severe cramping and a soft, swollen, tender uterus.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Endometriosis:** Ectopic endometrium *outside* the uterus. Presents with severe dysmenorrhea, but the uterus is typically **normal in size** and non-tender (though the adnexa/cul-de-sac may be tender).\n  - **(C) Subserosal Leiomyoma:** Causes an **irregularly enlarged, firm, non-tender** uterus (lumpy-bumpy). Usually causes bulk symptoms, not severe dysmenorrhea.\n  - **(D) Endometrial Hyperplasia:** Causes painless heavy bleeding; uterus is normal size.\n  - **(E) PID:** Presents with acute pelvic pain, fever, cervical motion tenderness (chandelier sign), and purulent discharge.",
    "pearl": "**Adenomyosis** = Heavy, painful periods + **Uniformly enlarged, boggy, tender uterus**.\n**Fibroids** = Heavy periods + **Irregularly enlarged, firm, non-tender uterus**.",
