    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Pathology of Adenomyosis:** Adenomyosis is defined as the downgrowth/invagination of the basal layer of the endometrium into the myometrium (at least $>2.5$ mm deep).\n- **Microscopic Findings:** The pathologist will see islands of benign **endometrial glands and endometrial stroma** completely surrounded by the smooth muscle of the myometrium. \n- **The \"Hypertrophy\":** The presence of this ectopic endometrial tissue induces a reactive, severe hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the surrounding smooth muscle. This muscle hypertrophy is what causes the profound globular enlargement of the uterus seen clinically. The dark cystic spaces are tiny pockets of old menstrual blood trapped within these ectopic glands.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Squamous cells/keratin:** Squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix.\n  - **(C) Monoclonal whorls:** Benign leiomyoma (fibroid).\n  - **(D) Granulomas:** Tuberculous endometritis (rare in the US).\n  - **(E) Trophoblasts:** Choriocarcinoma.",
