    "stem": "A 39-year-old woman with a globally enlarged, boggy uterus and severe dysmenorrhea is diagnosed with adenomyosis via pelvic MRI. She is currently taking combined oral contraceptive pills (COCPs) continuously to suppress her menses and manage her pain. Which layer of the normal uterine lining is the embryologic origin of the ectopic tissue found deep within the myometrium in this condition?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Stratum functionalis",
      "B": "Stratum basalis",
      "C": "Perimetrium",
      "D": "Urogenital sinus",
      "E": "Visceral peritoneum"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Endometrial Anatomy:** The normal endometrium has two main layers:\n  1. **Stratum functionalis:** The superficial layer that proliferates under estrogen and completely sloughs off during menstruation.\n  2. **Stratum basalis:** The deep, regenerative layer that remains intact during menstruation and contains the stem cells that regenerate the functionalis every month.\n- **Pathogenesis of Adenomyosis:** Adenomyosis occurs when the boundary between the endometrium and myometrium breaks down. It is the **stratum basalis** that invaginates and proliferates deep into the myometrium.\n- **Clinical Relevance:** Because the ectopic tissue in adenomyosis is derived primarily from the stratum basalis (which is resistant to hormonal cycling and does not undergo cyclical shedding as aggressively as the functionalis), treatments that try to shed the lining are less effective. The trapped basalis tissue simply swells and bleeds slightly locally, causing intense pain and muscle hypertrophy.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Stratum functionalis:** Sloughs off during menses; it is the tissue that implants externally to cause *endometriosis* (via retrograde menstruation), but it is not the source of adenomyosis.\n  - **(C) Perimetrium & (E) Visceral peritoneum:** The outer serosal lining of the uterus.\n  - **(D) Urogenital sinus:** Forms the lower vagina.",
    "pearl": "**Adenomyosis** arises from the invagination of the **stratum basalis** of the endometrium into the myometrium.",
