    "rationale": "- **Uterine Enlargement in Adenomyosis:** \n  - When the endometrial glands and stroma invade the myometrium, they do not just sit there passively.\n  - The ectopic tissue acts as an irritant and induces a massive **reactive hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the surrounding myometrial smooth muscle cells**.\n  - It is this diffuse, reactive muscle thickening, combined with the tiny pockets of trapped blood and fluid, that makes the uterus uniformly enlarged, boggy (spongy/soft), and heavy.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Fibroid tumors:** Fibroids are monoclonal tumors (Leiomyomas). While they can co-exist with adenomyosis, they cause an *irregular*, firm enlargement, not the uniform, boggy enlargement characteristic of adenomyosis itself.\n  - **(B) Fluid in cavity:** Hematometra (e.g., from an imperforate hymen), which causes a tense, cystic feeling, not a boggy muscular wall.\n  - **(D) Adenocarcinoma:** Endometrial cancer rarely causes massive uterine enlargement until very late stages.\n  - **(E) Abscess formation:** Pyometra/severe PID, which would present with high fever and acute toxicity.",
