    "rationale": "- **Differentiating the Two \"Sister\" Diseases:** Both conditions involve ectopic endometrial tissue and present with severe, painful periods (dysmenorrhea).\n- **Endometriosis (Outside the Uterus):** Tissue implants on the ovaries, uterosacral ligaments, and pelvic peritoneum. \n  - **Exam findings:** The uterus itself is typically **normal in size**. Because implants cause scarring, the uterus may be **fixed and retroverted**. Implants on the ligaments cause **nodularity of the uterosacral ligaments** and tenderness in the posterior fornix. Implants on the ovary form \"chocolate cysts\" (**adnexal mass**).\n- **Adenomyosis (Inside the Uterus):** Tissue implants *within* the uterine muscle wall.\n  - **Exam findings:** The classic, specific finding is a **uniformly enlarged, boggy (soft), tender uterus**. The adnexa and ligaments are typically normal.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A), (B), (D), (E):** These are all classic physical exam findings of **Endometriosis**, resulting from external scarring, adhesions, and endometriomas.",
