    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The classic presentation of **Acute Endometritis**, which is a bacterial infection of the endometrium.\n- **Key Features:** It almost always occurs **postpartum** (or post-abortion/miscarriage). Symptoms include high fever, severe uterine tenderness, and purulent, foul-smelling vaginal discharge (lochia).\n- **Risk Factors:** The most significant risk factors are **Cesarean section**, prolonged rupture of membranes, and prolonged labor. These events introduce normal vaginal flora ascending into the sterile uterine cavity.\n- **Pathogenesis:** The infection is typically polymicrobial (Group B Strep, E. coli, anaerobes like Bacteroides, and Enterococcus).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Chronic endometritis:** Presents insidiously with abnormal bleeding, pelvic pain, and infertility, NOT with acute high fever and toxicity postpartum.\n  - **(C) Endometriosis & (D) Adenomyosis:** Chronic conditions presenting with dysmenorrhea; no fever or foul discharge.\n  - **(E) Retained products:** Can cause delayed bleeding, but the high fever and foul discharge signify that it has become infected (Acute endometritis).",
    "pearl": "**Acute endometritis** is the most common cause of **postpartum fever** ($>24$ hours post-delivery). It presents with fever, **uterine tenderness**, and foul-smelling lochia.",
