    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Pathology of Endometritis:** \n  - **Acute endometritis:** Characterized by the presence of **neutrophils** in the endometrial glands (microabscesses). (Note: Lymphocytes and a few neutrophils are normal in the late secretory/menstrual phase, making diagnosis tricky).\n  - **Chronic endometritis:** Characterized by chronic inflammation of the endometrium. \n- **The Diagnostic Hallmark:** Because lymphocytes are a normal, physiologic component of the endometrium, their presence means nothing. To definitively diagnose **Chronic Endometritis**, the pathologist MUST identify the presence of **plasma cells** in the endometrial stroma. (Plasma cells are never normally found in the endometrium).\n- **Causes:** Chronic endometritis is associated with retained products of conception (after delivery/miscarriage), chronic pelvic inflammatory disease (Chlamydia/Gonorrhea), or the presence of a foreign body (e.g., an **IUD**). It causes abnormal bleeding and infertility.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Neutrophils:** Indicate acute endometritis.\n  - **(B) Eosinophils:** Associated with allergic reactions/parasites, not endometritis.\n  - **(D) Giant cells:** Suggest granulomatous endometritis (e.g., Tuberculosis).\n  - **(E) Lymphocytes:** Normally present in the endometrium; not diagnostic of pathology.",
