    "rationale": "- **Treatment of Acute Postpartum Endometritis:** The infection is highly polymicrobial, requiring broad-spectrum coverage against anaerobes (e.g., *Bacteroides*), Gram-negative aerobes (e.g., *E. coli*), and Gram-positive aerobes (e.g., Group B Strep).\n- **The Gold Standard Regimen:** The classic, highly tested, and most effective first-line intravenous regimen is **Clindamycin + Gentamicin**.\n  - **Clindamycin:** Provides excellent coverage against **strict anaerobes** and Gram-positive cocci.\n  - **Gentamicin:** An aminoglycoside that provides excellent coverage against **aerobic Gram-negative bacilli**.\n  - (Ampicillin is often added if Enterococcus is suspected or if the patient is a GBS carrier).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Ceftriaxone/Azithromycin:** This is the outpatient regimen for uncomplicated Chlamydia/Gonorrhea cervicitis or mild PID, not severe postpartum endometritis (lacks sufficient anaerobic coverage).\n  - **(C) Vancomycin/Fluconazole:** Covers MRSA and yeast; inappropriate for a polymicrobial pelvic infection.\n  - **(D) Metronidazole alone:** Covers anaerobes, but leaves Gram-negatives and Gram-positives untreated.\n  - **(E) Acyclovir:** Antiviral (HSV).",
