    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has **Chronic Endometritis** (confirmed by the presence of plasma cells in the stroma), presenting with abnormal bleeding, pain, and infertility.\n- **Etiology:** The history of an \"incomplete spontaneous abortion managed expectantly\" is the critical clue. The most common cause of chronic endometritis following a pregnancy, miscarriage, or abortion is **retained products of conception (RPOC)**. \n- **Pathogenesis:** A small piece of dead placental or fetal tissue remains stuck in the uterus. It acts as a nidus for chronic, low-grade infection and chronic inflammation (attracting lymphocytes and plasma cells). This chronic inflammation creates a hostile environment for a new embryo, causing infertility. Treatment requires D&C to remove the necrotic tissue and antibiotics.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Chlamydia:** Causes PID (and can cause chronic endometritis), but the strong history of an incomplete abortion points directly to RPOC.\n  - **(C) Submucosal fibroid:** Causes bleeding and infertility, but would *not* show plasma cells on biopsy (it is a benign muscle tumor).\n  - **(D) Hyperplasia:** Characterized by crowded glands, not plasma cell infiltration.\n  - **(E) Autoimmune:** Not a recognized standard cause of chronic endometritis.",
