    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has **Tuberculous Endometritis**, presenting with infertility and amenorrhea (due to endometrial destruction), with a classic history suggestive of prior pulmonary TB (night sweats, cough, weight loss, endemic region).\n- **Pathology:** The hallmark of *Mycobacterium tuberculosis* infection in any tissue is the formation of **caseating (necrotizing) granulomas** with epithelioid macrophages and multinucleated **Langhans giant cells**. \n- **Pathogenesis:** TB classically spreads hematogenously from a primary pulmonary focus to the fallopian tubes (causing tuberculous salpingitis) and then spreads directly into the endometrium. It destroys the endometrial cavity, leading to severe scarring (Asherman-like syndrome) and permanent infertility.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Chlamydia & (B) Gonorrhea:** Cause acute/chronic PID, but they cause a neutrophilic/plasma cell infiltrate, NOT caseating granulomas.\n  - **(D) Actinomyces:** A Gram-positive branching rod uniquely associated with long-standing **IUD use**. It causes sulfur granules and pelvic abscesses, not caseating granulomas.\n  - **(E) Treponema:** Causes syphilis (gummas in tertiary syphilis, but not typical primary uterine granulomas).",
