    "stem": "A 42-year-old woman presents to her gynecologist reporting episodes of spotting between her otherwise normal, regular menstrual periods (intermenstrual bleeding). Her uterus is normal in size and non-tender on bimanual examination. Saline infusion sonohysterography reveals a 1.5 cm echogenic mass projecting into the uterine cavity. The physician performs a hysteroscopic resection. Histologic examination of the mass reveals benign endometrial glands and stroma surrounding a prominent, thick-walled central blood vessel. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Submucosal Leiomyoma",
      "B": "Endometrial Polyp",
      "C": "Adenomyosis",
      "D": "Endometrial Adenocarcinoma",
      "E": "Retained placental tissue"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The classic presentation of an **Endometrial Polyp** is **intermenstrual bleeding** (spotting between periods) in a woman with a normal-sized uterus.\n- **Pathology:** Endometrial polyps are benign, localized, hyperplastic overgrowths of the endometrium that project into the uterine cavity. \n- **Histologic Hallmark:** They are composed of normal-appearing, but disordered, **endometrial glands and stroma** surrounding a characteristic **thick-walled central feeding blood vessel** (the vascular core/stalk). This prominent central vessel makes them highly friable and prone to bleeding (hence the spotting).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Submucosal Leiomyoma:** A tumor of *smooth muscle* (myometrium), not glands/stroma. Usually causes heavy periods (menorrhagia), not just intermenstrual spotting.\n  - **(C) Adenomyosis:** Glands *inside* the myometrium wall, causing an enlarged, boggy uterus.\n  - **(D) Adenocarcinoma:** Presents with postmenopausal bleeding; histology would show severe cytologic atypia and crowded glands without intervening stroma, not a benign stalk.\n  - **(E) Retained placenta:** Usually causes postpartum bleeding and would show chorionic villi/necrotic tissue, not a vascular stalk with normal glands.",
    "pearl": "**Endometrial Polyps** present with intermenstrual spotting. Histologically, they are an overgrowth of benign glands and stroma organized around a **thick-walled central blood vessel**.",
