    "rationale": "- **Pathogenesis of Polyps:** Endometrial polyps are essentially localized areas of focal endometrial hyperplasia. \n- **The Driver:** The endometrium (glands and stroma) is stimulated to grow and proliferate by **estrogen**. Therefore, any state of **unopposed estrogen** (estrogen without the balancing, differentiating effect of progesterone) strongly promotes the development and growth of endometrial polyps. Risk factors include obesity, PCOS, Tamoxifen use, and perimenopause.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Progesterone:** Actually *shrinks* polyps and protects the endometrium by halting proliferation.\n  - **(C) Prolactin & (E) Androgens:** Do not directly stimulate endometrial growth.\n  - **(D) Hypoestrogenism:** Causes the endometrium to become thin and atrophic. (Though polyps *can* present in menopause, their initial growth was driven by prior estrogen exposure).",
    "pearl": "The growth of **Endometrial Polyps** is driven by **Estrogen**. Risk factors include obesity, anovulation, and Tamoxifen.",
