    "stem": "A 38-year-old woman undergoing evaluation for infertility is found to have a 3-cm endometrial polyp situated near the right uterine cornu (near the opening of the fallopian tube). How does this polyp most directly contribute to her inability to conceive?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "By secreting massive amounts of prolactin to inhibit ovulation",
      "B": "By acting as a mechanical obstruction to sperm transport and altering the endometrial microenvironment",
      "C": "By triggering a severe autoimmune attack against the ovaries",
      "D": "By converting all circulating estrogen into inactive estrone",
      "E": "By causing permanent scarring and fibrosis of the entire uterine cavity"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Polyps and Infertility:** Endometrial polyps are a well-recognized cause of infertility and recurrent miscarriage.\n- **Mechanism:** They exert their negative effect primarily through local, mechanical, and inflammatory means.\n  1. **Mechanical Obstruction:** A large polyp (especially near the cornua/ostia) can physically block sperm from entering the fallopian tube, or prevent an embryo from implanting.\n  2. **Hostile Microenvironment:** The presence of a polyp induces a local, chronic inflammatory response in the surrounding endometrium (similar to how a copper IUD works). This inflammation releases cytokines that are toxic to sperm and prevent the proper decidualization required for an embryo to implant.\n- **Treatment:** Removal of the polyp (hysteroscopic polypectomy) often immediately restores fertility.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Prolactin:** Polyps do not secrete pituitary hormones.\n  - **(C) Autoimmune & (D) Aromatase:** Not mechanisms of polyps.\n  - **(E) Scarring:** This is Asherman syndrome (caused by D&C), not a localized polyp.",
    "pearl": "Endometrial polyps can cause **infertility** by acting as a mechanical obstruction (like a natural IUD) and creating a local inflammatory, hostile environment for implantation.",
