    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** Secondary amenorrhea following a traumatic uterine instrumentation (D&C).\n- **Diagnostic Logic:**\n  - **Breastfeeding:** The fact that she breastfed for 6 months **rules out Sheehan syndrome** (pituitary necrosis), because her pituitary successfully produced prolactin.\n  - **Negative Progesterone Challenge:** She was given progesterone, but she did not bleed. This means either she lacks estrogen (so no lining was built) OR the lining is physically destroyed/obstructed. Because her pituitary is fine (she breastfed), her ovaries are likely fine, pointing to a destroyed lining.\n- **Asherman Syndrome:** An aggressive D&C scraped away the deep regenerative layer of the endometrium (the stratum basalis). The anterior and posterior walls of the uterus scarred and fused together (intrauterine synechiae). There is no endometrium left to proliferate or shed, causing amenorrhea and infertility.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Sheehan:** Ruled out by successful lactation.\n  - **(B) POF:** Unlikely in a 27-year-old right after a D&C; the surgical history points directly to Asherman's.\n  - **(D) PCOS & (E) Prolactinoma:** Would typically have a *positive* progesterone challenge (in PCOS) or present with galactorrhea/vision changes (Prolactinoma), and lack the classic D&C history.",
    "pearl": "Secondary amenorrhea after a **D&C (dilation and curettage)** with a **negative progesterone challenge** test is pathognomonic for **Asherman Syndrome** (intrauterine adhesions).",
