    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 31-year-old woman with suspected Asherman syndrome is undergoing evaluation for infertility and amenorrhea. Which of the following specific layers of the uterine wall was severely damaged or completely removed during her previous aggressive dilation and curettage, directly leading to the formation of her intrauterine adhesions?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Stratum functionalis",
      "B": "Stratum basalis",
      "C": "Inner longitudinal myometrium",
      "D": "Outer circular myometrium",
      "E": "Perimetrium"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Endometrial Anatomy:** The endometrium has two layers.\n  1. The **stratum functionalis** is the superficial layer that naturally sloughs off every month during menstruation. Scraping this away does no permanent harm.\n  2. The **stratum basalis** is the deep, permanent layer resting directly on the myometrium. It contains the stem cells that regenerate the functionalis every month.\n- **Pathogenesis of Asherman Syndrome:** If a D&C is too aggressive (especially if the uterus is soft and inflamed, such as postpartum or post-miscarriage), the surgeon's curette scrapes entirely through the functionalis and completely destroys the **stratum basalis**. \n- **The Result:** Without the stem cells of the basalis, the endometrium cannot regenerate. The raw exposed myometrial walls on the front and back of the uterus heal by scarring together, forming thick fibrous bands (synechiae) that obliterate the uterine cavity.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Stratum functionalis:** Supposed to be shed; its removal doesn't cause scarring.\n  - **(C) & (D) Myometrium:** The muscle layer; while it gets exposed, it is the loss of the *basalis* that prevents regeneration.\n  - **(E) Perimetrium:** The outer serosal layer.",
    "pearl": "**Asherman Syndrome** is caused by the iatrogenic destruction of the **Stratum Basalis** (the regenerative stem cell layer of the endometrium).",
