    "stem": "A 29-year-old woman is diagnosed with Asherman syndrome. Her physician plans to surgically lyse (cut) the scar tissue to restore the uterine cavity so she can attempt pregnancy. Which of the following imaging/surgical modalities is the gold standard for both the definitive diagnosis and the simultaneous treatment of Asherman syndrome?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Transvaginal ultrasound",
      "B": "Hysterosalpingography (HSG)",
      "C": "Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) of the pelvis",
      "D": "Hysteroscopy",
      "E": "Diagnostic laparoscopy"
    },
    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "- **Diagnosis and Treatment of Asherman Syndrome:**\n  - **Hysteroscopy:** This involves passing a tiny camera through the cervix directly into the uterine cavity. It is the **gold standard**. It allows the physician to directly visualize the thick, white fibrous bands of scar tissue (synechiae) crisscrossing the cavity. \n  - **Treatment:** Crucially, hysteroscopy allows for immediate \"see-and-treat\" management. The surgeon can pass micro-scissors through the hysteroscope and systematically cut (lyse) the adhesions, restoring the normal shape and volume of the uterine cavity.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Transvaginal ultrasound:** Often appears normal or shows a thin endometrial stripe; cannot definitively diagnose or treat adhesions.\n  - **(B) HSG:** Involves injecting dye into the uterus under X-ray. It *can* diagnose Asherman's (showing multiple irregular filling defects), but it is purely diagnostic; it cannot *treat* the condition.\n  - **(C) MRI:** Too expensive and static; cannot be used for treatment.\n  - **(E) Laparoscopy:** A camera in the *abdomen* looking at the outside of the uterus; Asherman's is a disease on the *inside* of the uterus.",
    "pearl": "The gold standard for both diagnosing and treating **Asherman Syndrome** (intrauterine adhesions) is **Hysteroscopy** (allowing direct visualization and lysis of synechiae).",
