    "stem": "A 31-year-old woman with known Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) presents to her physician to discuss her long-term health risks. She currently experiences only 2 to 3 menstrual periods per year. Her physician strongly advises her to take a medication to induce regular withdrawal bleeding. If she refuses treatment and remains amenorrheic, her state of chronic anovulation will specifically put her at an increased risk for endometrial hyperplasia due to the absence of which of the following protective physiologic events?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The mid-cycle surge of Luteinizing Hormone (LH)",
      "B": "The secretion of inhibin B by the growing follicles",
      "C": "The post-ovulatory production of progesterone by the corpus luteum",
      "D": "The secretion of oxytocin from the posterior pituitary",
      "E": "The local production of prostaglandins in the stratum functionalis"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology of Anovulation (PCOS):** \n  - In a normal cycle, ovulation occurs, creating a corpus luteum. The corpus luteum secretes **progesterone**. \n  - **Progesterone is the great protector of the endometrium.** It stops estrogen-driven proliferation (hyperplasia), downregulates estrogen receptors, and induces the glands to differentiate into secretory glands.\n- **The Defect:** In PCOS, the patient does not ovulate (anovulation). Because she does not ovulate, she never forms a corpus luteum. Therefore, she produces **zero progesterone**. \n  - Meanwhile, her follicles (and peripheral fat) continue to produce constant estrogen. This \"unopposed estrogen\" continuously stimulates the endometrium to grow, leading directly to hyperplasia and cancer.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) LH surge:** The LH surge is missing in PCOS, which *causes* the anovulation. But the direct mechanism protecting the endometrium is the progesterone that *follows* ovulation.\n  - **(B) Inhibin B:** Regulates FSH, doesn't protect the endometrium.\n  - **(D) Oxytocin:** Stimulates uterine contractions during labor.\n  - **(E) Prostaglandins:** Cause menstrual cramping (dysmenorrhea), not hyperplasia prevention.",
    "pearl": "The primary reason PCOS patients get Endometrial Hyperplasia is **Anovulation**, which results in a complete **lack of Progesterone** to protect the lining from unopposed estrogen.",
