    "rationale": "- **Management of Atypical Hyperplasia:** \n  - The standard of care for atypical hyperplasia is a **hysterectomy** (due to the 30-40% risk of concurrent or future cancer).\n  - However, in young women who strongly desire fertility preservation, a trial of medical management is acceptable if they are counseled on the risks.\n- **Medical Treatment:** The goal is to aggressively counteract the unopposed estrogen driving the hyperplasia. This is done by administering **high-dose, continuous Progestins** (either oral Megestrol acetate or a local Levonorgestrel-releasing IUD like Mirena). Progestins powerfully halt cellular proliferation, downregulate estrogen receptors, and force the hyperplastic glands to atrophy, successfully reversing the lesion in many cases.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Clomiphene:** Used to induce ovulation *after* the hyperplasia is cleared, not as the primary treatment to reverse the active precancerous lesion.\n  - **(C) Estradiol:** This is the *cause* of the disease; giving more will cause rapid progression to cancer.\n  - **(D) Paclitaxel/Carboplatin:** Systemic chemotherapy used for advanced/metastatic ovarian or endometrial cancer, not hyperplasia.\n  - **(E) Methotrexate:** Used for ectopic pregnancy.",
