    "stem": "Endometrial hyperplasia is classified into four categories based on glandular complexity and the presence or absence of cytologic atypia. Which of the following histologic features is the single most important predictor of whether a hyperplastic lesion will progress to invasive endometrial adenocarcinoma?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The ratio of glands to stroma",
      "B": "The absolute thickness of the endometrial stripe on ultrasound",
      "C": "The presence of cellular nuclear atypia",
      "D": "The presence of multiple cystic dilations (Swiss cheese pattern)",
      "E": "The presence of scattered lymphocytes in the stroma"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Predicting Malignancy in Hyperplasia:**\n  - *Simple vs. Complex:* Refers to the architecture (crowding of the glands). Complex means the glands are crowded \"back-to-back\" with very little stroma in between.\n  - *Atypia:* Refers to the individual cells lining the glands. Atypia means the cells have large, pleomorphic nuclei, prominent nucleoli, and loss of polarity (they look mutated).\n- **The Most Important Predictor:** The presence of **cytologic atypia** is by far the most critical prognostic factor. \n  - Simple hyperplasia *without* atypia has a $<1\\%$ chance of progressing to cancer.\n  - Complex hyperplasia *without* atypia has a $\\sim 3\\%$ chance.\n  - Complex hyperplasia **WITH atypia** has a nearly **$30\\%$ chance** of progressing to (or already harboring) invasive cancer.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Gland/stroma ratio:** Determines simple vs. complex, which is important, but atypia trumps it for cancer risk.\n  - **(B) Ultrasound thickness:** Tells you to *do* the biopsy, but cannot predict cancer risk like histology can.\n  - **(D) Swiss cheese pattern:** Describes simple hyperplasia without atypia (a very benign, low-risk lesion).\n  - **(E) Lymphocytes:** Irrelevant.",
    "pearl": "In endometrial hyperplasia, the presence of **Cytologic Atypia** is the single most important factor predicting progression to Endometrial Cancer.",
