    "stem": "A 60-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with a chief complaint of spotting blood on her underwear for the past two weeks. She went through menopause 9 years ago. Her BMI is 35 kg/m$^2$. She has a history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes. She has never been pregnant. Which of the following is the most appropriate first step in the evaluation of this patient?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Reassure her that light spotting is common in menopause due to vaginal atrophy",
      "B": "Check her serum FSH and LH levels",
      "C": "Perform an endometrial biopsy or transvaginal ultrasound",
      "D": "Prescribe a 10-day course of medroxyprogesterone to induce a withdrawal bleed",
      "E": "Perform a radical hysterectomy immediately"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has **postmenopausal bleeding**.\n- **The Golden Rule:** Any vaginal bleeding in a postmenopausal woman is **Endometrial Cancer until proven otherwise**. \n- **Risk Profile:** This patient is a walking textbook of risk factors for Type I Endometrial Cancer: Obesity (peripheral aromatization to estrone), Diabetes, Hypertension, and Nulliparity (lifelong lack of protective progesterone).\n- **Next Step:** You must immediately investigate the endometrium. The first step is either a direct **Endometrial Biopsy (EMB)** in the office (which is definitive if positive) OR a **Transvaginal Ultrasound (TVUS)**. If TVUS shows an endometrial stripe $\\le 4$ mm, cancer is highly unlikely (atrophy is more likely). If the stripe is $> 4$ mm, she must get a biopsy.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Reassure:** Malpractice. While vaginal atrophy *is* the most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding overall, you MUST rule out cancer first.\n  - **(B) FSH/LH:** Will be high, just confirming she is menopausal, which you already know.\n  - **(D) Progesterone:** Used for anovulatory bleeding in premenopausal women, not for diagnosing cancer in postmenopausal women.\n  - **(E) Radical hysterectomy:** You must confirm a cancer diagnosis via biopsy before performing major, life-altering surgery.",
    "pearl": "Postmenopausal bleeding is **Endometrial Cancer** until proven otherwise. The first step in management is always a **Transvaginal Ultrasound** (to check lining thickness) or an **Endometrial Biopsy**.",
