    "stem": "A 35-year-old nulliparous woman is diagnosed with Type I endometrial adenocarcinoma. She has a strong family history of colon cancer, with her father and paternal aunt both dying of colon cancer in their 40s. Her physician suspects a genetic syndrome is responsible for her early-onset endometrial cancer. Which of the following genetic defects is most likely responsible for her condition?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Mutation in the BRCA1 gene",
      "B": "Mutation in the PTEN gene (Cowden syndrome)",
      "C": "Mutation in DNA mismatch repair genes (MLH1, MSH2)",
      "D": "Mutation in the APC gene",
      "E": "Mutation in the p53 gene (Li-Fraumeni syndrome)"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** Early-onset endometrial cancer in a patient with a profound family history of early-onset colon cancer.\n- **The Syndrome:** This is classic for **Lynch Syndrome (Hereditary Nonpolyposis Colorectal Cancer, HNPCC)**. \n- **Cancer Risks:** While Lynch syndrome is famous for colon cancer, **Endometrial Cancer is the most common extra-colonic malignancy**, and it is often the very first cancer (\"sentinel cancer\") to appear in women with the syndrome. (Ovarian cancer risk is also increased).\n- **Genetics:** Lynch syndrome is caused by an autosomal dominant germline mutation in **DNA Mismatch Repair (MMR)** genes (most commonly **MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2**). This leads to microsatellite instability (MSI).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) BRCA1:** Causes Breast and Ovarian cancer, not colon/endometrial.\n  - **(B) PTEN (Cowden):** Increases endometrial cancer risk, but causes hamartomas, breast/thyroid cancer, not primarily colon cancer.\n  - **(D) APC:** Causes Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (thousands of colon polyps), but endometrial cancer is not a primary feature.\n  - **(E) p53:** Causes Li-Fraumeni (sarcomas, breast, brain, adrenal cortical tumors).",
    "pearl": "Women with **Lynch Syndrome (DNA mismatch repair mutations)** are at an extremely high risk for both Colorectal cancer and **Endometrial Cancer** (the most common extra-colonic cancer).",
