    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 62-year-old woman is diagnosed with Stage 1A Endometrial Carcinoma (confined to the inner half of the myometrium). She asks her physician about her prognosis and treatment. The physician explains that Type I Endometrial Cancer generally has a very good prognosis because it usually presents early. What is the primary reason that this cancer is typically diagnosed at such an early stage?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "It grows extremely slowly over decades",
      "B": "It is highly sensitive to radiation therapy",
      "C": "It produces an early, alarming clinical symptom that prompts immediate medical evaluation",
      "D": "Routine annual pap smears highly effectively screen for this disease",
      "E": "It secretes a unique tumor marker that is detected on routine blood work"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Prognosis of Type I Endometrial Cancer:** It is the most common gynecologic malignancy in the US, but it generally has the best prognosis.\n- **The Reason:** The cancer grows from the lining of the uterus and bleeds very early in the disease process. Because the patient is usually postmenopausal, *any* vaginal bleeding is highly alarming and abnormal. This **postmenopausal bleeding** terrifies the patient, prompting them to see a doctor immediately. Thus, the cancer is diagnosed early (Stage 1), when a simple total hysterectomy is completely curative.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Slow growth:** While less aggressive than Type II, the early diagnosis is due to symptoms, not just growth rate.\n  - **(B) Radiation:** Not the reason for *early diagnosis*.\n  - **(D) Pap smears:** Pap smears screen for **Cervical** cancer. They are notoriously *terrible* at detecting endometrial cancer (they only catch it $\\sim 50\\%$ of the time, and usually only when advanced).\n  - **(E) Tumor marker:** There is no routine screening blood test (like PSA or CA-125) for endometrial cancer.",
    "pearl": "Type I Endometrial Cancer usually has a good prognosis because it presents early with the highly alarming symptom of **postmenopausal bleeding**. (Note: Pap smears do NOT screen for endometrial cancer).",
