    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** An elderly, *thin*, multiparous woman with postmenopausal bleeding. She has ZERO risk factors for classic estrogen-driven endometrial cancer.\n- **Pathology:** The biopsy shows papillary architecture and **psammoma bodies**. This is classic for **Type II (Serous) Endometrial Carcinoma**.\n- **Pathogenesis of Type II Cancer:** Unlike Type I, Type II cancer is completely **estrogen-independent**. It does *not* arise from hyperplasia. Instead, it arises *de novo* (spontaneously) in an **atrophic (thin, resting) endometrium**. \n- **Prognosis:** It is extremely aggressive, rapidly invading the myometrium and spreading via the fallopian tubes to the peritoneal cavity (much like ovarian cancer).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Unopposed estrogen & (B) Hyperplasia:** Describe Type I (Endometrioid) cancer, which occurs in younger, obese, nulliparous women.\n  - **(D) Mismatch repair:** Lynch syndrome causes Type I (Endometrioid) cancer.\n  - **(E) HPV:** Causes cervical cancer.",
    "pearl": "**Type II (Serous) Endometrial Carcinoma:** Occurs in elderly, thin women. It is **Estrogen-Independent**, arises from **Atrophic** endometrium, has a papillary pattern with **psammoma bodies**, and is highly aggressive.",
