    "rationale": "- **Histopathology of Type II Cancer:** The pathologist is describing the classic features of **Serous Carcinoma** (the most common type of Type II endometrial cancer).\n- **Key Features:**\n  1. **Papillary architecture:** Finger-like projections of tumor cells surrounding a fibrovascular core.\n  2. **Severe atypia:** Highly pleomorphic, ugly cells (due to p53 mutations).\n  3. **Psammoma Bodies:** The \"concentric, laminated, calcified concretions.\" These form when the tips of the papillae outgrow their blood supply, undergo necrosis, and subsequently calcify. \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Clear cell carcinoma:** Another Type II cancer, but characterized by hobnail cells and clear cytoplasm (glycogen), not typically psammoma bodies.\n  - **(C) Type I Endometrioid:** Characterized by crowded, back-to-back *glands* resembling normal endometrium, not papillae/psammoma bodies.\n  - **(D) Choriocarcinoma:** Cytotrophoblasts and syncytiotrophoblasts with massive hemorrhage/necrosis, no papillae.\n  - **(E) Leiomyosarcoma:** Spindle cells (muscle) with necrosis and high mitoses.",
