    "rationale": "- **Clinical Behavior of Type II (Serous) Cancer:** It is considered a systemic disease from the moment of diagnosis.\n- **Pattern of Spread:** Unlike Type I (which tends to stay confined to the uterus for a long time), **Type II Serous Carcinoma behaves exactly like Ovarian Cancer**. The tumor cells readily shed through the fallopian tubes, float in the peritoneal fluid, and implant all over the peritoneal cavity and omentum very early in the disease process.\n- **Surgical Staging:** Because of this aggressive, early peritoneal spread, patients MUST undergo comprehensive surgical staging (including omentectomy and peritoneal washings/biopsies) just like ovarian cancer patients, even if the primary tumor in the uterus appears small. They almost always require systemic chemotherapy post-operatively.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Skip lesions:** Not a defining feature of serous carcinoma.\n  - **(B) Breast metastasis:** Serous carcinoma is a primary uterine cancer.\n  - **(D) Chemo resistance:** They *are* treated with chemotherapy (carboplatin/paclitaxel) precisely because they are systemic; they are not totally resistant.\n  - **(E) beta-hCG:** Secreted by Choriocarcinoma, not serous carcinoma.",
