    "rationale": "- **Subtypes of Type II Cancer:** Type II cancers are estrogen-independent and aggressive. The two main histologic subtypes are Serous and Clear Cell.\n- **Clear Cell Carcinoma:** This tumor is characterized histologically by cells that have **abundant, clear cytoplasm** (due to the massive accumulation of glycogen, which washes out during slide preparation). Another classic feature is the **\"hobnail\" cell**, where the nucleus protrudes into the glandular lumen like the head of a hobnail. It is highly aggressive and carries a poor prognosis.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Endometrioid:** Type I cancer (glandular, estrogen-driven).\n  - **(B) Serous:** Type II cancer, but characterized by papillae and psammoma bodies, not clear cytoplasm/hobnail cells.\n  - **(D) Mucinous:** A variant of Type I cancer; cells contain apical mucin.\n  - **(E) Squamous cell:** Extremely rare in the endometrium (usually cervical).",
