    "topic": "Lecture 4.1: Uterus Pathophysiology and Pathology - Set 2",
    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 58-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with a rapidly enlarging pelvic mass and abnormal vaginal bleeding. She had a pelvic ultrasound two years ago that showed a normal, small uterus. Now, ultrasound reveals a large, 12-cm heterogeneous mass replacing the entire uterus, with central areas of suspected necrosis. She undergoes an urgent hysterectomy. The pathology report confirms Leiomyosarcoma. Which of the following best describes the origin of this aggressive malignancy?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Malignant transformation of a long-standing, benign uterine fibroid",
      "B": "*De novo* malignant proliferation of myometrial smooth muscle cells",
      "C": "Direct invasion of an aggressive endometrial adenocarcinoma into the myometrium",
      "D": "Metastatic spread from a primary gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST)",
      "E": "Malignant transformation of ectopic endometrial tissue within the myometrium"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Pathogenesis of Leiomyosarcoma:** Leiomyosarcomas are highly aggressive malignancies of the smooth muscle (myometrium) of the uterus.\n- **The Crucial Concept:** A very common misconception is that benign fibroids (leiomyomas) turn into cancer. **This is false.** Leiomyosarcomas arise **de novo** (from scratch) directly from the smooth muscle cells of the myometrium. They do NOT represent the malignant degeneration of a pre-existing benign leiomyoma. (The malignant transformation rate of a benign fibroid is near zero).\n- **Clinical Clue:** This is why a new, rapidly enlarging uterine mass in a *postmenopausal* woman (whose uterus should be shrinking) is highly suspicious for a sarcoma and requires surgery, whereas a stable fibroid in a younger woman is benign.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Malignant transformation:** As stated, they arise *de novo*, not from benign fibroids.\n  - **(C) Endometrial invasion:** That would be Endometrial Adenocarcinoma invading the muscle, which looks glandular on histology, not like a smooth muscle sarcoma.\n  - **(D) GIST metastasis:** Primary uterine sarcomas are unrelated to GISTs.\n  - **(E) Ectopic tissue:** Adenomyosis is benign; it rarely turns malignant.",
    "pearl": "**Leiomyosarcomas** of the uterus arise **de novo** from the myometrium. They do **NOT** arise from the malignant transformation of pre-existing benign fibroids.",
