    "stem": "A 55-year-old woman is diagnosed with a high-grade uterine leiomyosarcoma. During staging, a CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis is ordered. Leiomyosarcomas are notorious for early and aggressive metastasis via which of the following primary routes, and to which specific organ?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Lymphatic spread to the pelvic lymph nodes",
      "B": "Direct continuous spread to the cervical stroma",
      "C": "Hematogenous spread to the lungs",
      "D": "Transcoelomic spread to the omentum",
      "E": "Perineural invasion to the sacral plexus"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Metastatic Pattern of Sarcomas:** This is a fundamental rule of oncology: **Carcinomas** (epithelial cancers) primarily spread via **lymphatics** (to lymph nodes). **Sarcomas** (mesenchymal/connective tissue cancers) primarily spread via the **hematogenous** route (through the bloodstream).\n- **Leiomyosarcoma Spread:** Uterine leiomyosarcomas are extremely aggressive. Because they are sarcomas, they invade local blood vessels early and spread hematogenously. \n- **The Target Organ:** The most common site of distant metastasis for a uterine leiomyosarcoma (and most sarcomas) is the **lungs**. A chest CT is mandatory for staging.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Lymphatic spread:** The primary route for Endometrial *Carcinoma* and Cervical *Carcinoma*, not sarcomas.\n  - **(B) Direct spread:** Can occur, but hematogenous distant spread is the defining aggressive feature.\n  - **(D) Transcoelomic (peritoneal) spread:** The classic route for *Ovarian* cancer and *Type II Serous Endometrial* cancer.",
    "pearl": "**Carcinomas** spread via **lymphatics**.\n**Sarcomas** (like Leiomyosarcoma) spread **hematogenously**, most commonly to the **lungs**.",
