    "rationale": "- **Receptor Status in Leiomyosarcoma:** Because leiomyosarcomas arise from the myometrium (a highly hormone-responsive tissue), a subset of these tumors retain expression of **Estrogen Receptors (ER)** and **Progesterone Receptors (PR)**.\n- **Clinical Relevance:** While surgery is the mainstay of treatment, and chemotherapy/radiation are used for advanced disease, patients with advanced or recurrent ER/PR-positive leiomyosarcomas are sometimes treated with **hormonal blockade** (e.g., Aromatase inhibitors like Letrozole, or progestins). \n- **Caveat:** They are far less responsive to hormones than benign leiomyomas or Endometrial Carcinomas, but it provides a targeted therapeutic option with low toxicity for advanced disease.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) HER2/neu:** Targeted in breast and gastric cancer (Trastuzumab).\n  - **(C) Androgen receptors:** Targeted in prostate cancer.\n  - **(D) Prolactin receptors & (E) Glucocorticoid receptors:** Not used in sarcoma management.",
    "pearl": "While highly aggressive and largely treated with surgery/chemo, some uterine **Leiomyosarcomas** express **Estrogen/Progesterone receptors** and may respond mildly to anti-estrogen therapy.",
