    "stem": "A 32-year-old nulliparous woman is undergoing a laparoscopy for severe, chronic pelvic pain and infertility. The surgeon notes multiple small, dark, \"powder-burn\" lesions scattered across the pelvic peritoneum and the uterosacral ligaments. Biopsy of one of these lesions will most likely reveal which of the following?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Granulomatous inflammation with giant cells",
      "B": "Endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterine cavity",
      "C": "Dysplastic squamous epithelial cells",
      "D": "Nests of benign transitional epithelium (Walthard cell rests)",
      "E": "Deposits of amyloid protein"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has classic symptoms of **Endometriosis** (chronic pelvic pain, dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and infertility).\n- **Gross Findings:** The \"powder-burn\" or \"gunshot\" lesions on the peritoneum, uterosacral ligaments, or ovaries are pathognomonic for endometriosis.\n- **Histologic Diagnosis:** Endometriosis is defined precisely as the presence of functional **endometrial glands and endometrial stroma** located *outside* the uterine cavity. \n- **Pathogenesis:** The most widely accepted theory is **retrograde menstruation** (Sampson's theory), where menstrual blood and endometrial tissue flow backward through the fallopian tubes and implant on the pelvic organs. The tissue bleeds cyclically with the menstrual cycle, causing intense inflammation, pain, and scarring (leading to infertility).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Granulomas:** Tuberculous peritonitis/salpingitis.\n  - **(C) Dysplastic squamous cells:** Cervical/Vulvar dysplasia (CIN/VIN).\n  - **(D) Walthard cell rests:** Benign nests of urothelial-like cells found on the fallopian tubes; asymptomatic.\n  - **(E) Amyloid:** Systemic amyloidosis; not a cause of cyclical pelvic pain.",
    "pearl": "**Endometriosis** is defined histologically by the presence of **Endometrial Glands and Stroma** outside the uterus. The classic gross appearance is a **\"powder-burn\"** lesion.",
