    "file": "Lecture_1_1_Anatomy_Pelvis_Perineum_30Q.md",
    "qnum": 1,
    "topic": "Lecture 1.1: Anatomy \u2014 The Pelvic Cavity & The Perineum",
    "yield_level": "Low",
    "stem": "A 34-year-old primigravid woman presents to the clinic 8 weeks after an uncomplicated spontaneous vaginal delivery. She reports involuntary loss of flatus and liquid stool when she experiences the urge to defecate, as well as a constant feeling of pelvic heaviness. Physical examination reveals decreased resting and squeeze anal sphincter tone. An anorectal manometry study demonstrates a widened anorectal angle during attempted continence maneuvers. Which of the following muscles normally maintains the acute anorectal angle required for fecal continence?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Iliococcygeus",
      "B": "Pubococcygeus",
      "C": "Puborectalis",
      "D": "Superficial transverse perineal",
      "E": "Coccygeus"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Pathophysiology:** The **puborectalis** muscle is a distinct, thick muscular sling arising from the posterior aspect of the superior pubic rami on either side of the midline. It passes posteriorly around the anorectal junction, forming a U-shaped muscular collar. \n- **Mechanism of Continence:** Tonic contraction of the puborectalis pulls the anorectal junction anteriorly toward the pubic bone, creating an **acute anorectal angle (~80\u201390 degrees)** between the rectum and the anal canal. This sharp bend acts as a mechanical barrier preventing the passage of stool into the anal canal at rest. During voluntary defecation, the puborectalis relaxes, allowing the anorectal angle to straighten (~130\u2013140 degrees), which permits the downward passage of feces.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Iliococcygeus:** Provides lateral support to the pelvic floor but does not form a sling around the anorectum.\n  - **(B) Pubococcygeus:** Forms the main muscular body of the pelvic floor and supports pelvic viscera, but it is the specialized medial fibers (puborectalis) that kink the anorectum.\n  - **(D) Superficial transverse perineal:** Stabilizes the perineal body but plays no role in the anorectal angle.\n  - **(E) Coccygeus:** Supports the posterior pelvic outlet from the ischial spine to the coccyx; it is not part of the levator ani complex.",
    "pearl": "The **puborectalis sling** (the medial component of the levator ani) is the primary muscle maintaining the **anorectal angle** and gross fecal continence. Relaxation of this muscle straightens the canal to allow defecation.",
    "learning_objectives": [
      {
        "id": "LO_Lecture_1_1__Anatomy___The_Pelvic_Cavity_2",
        "text": "Identify and distinguish the muscular components of the pelvic diaphragm."
      }
    ]
