    "topic": "Lecture 1.1: Anatomy \u2014 The Pelvic Cavity & The Perineum",
    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 42-year-old male undergoes transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP) for benign prostatic hyperplasia. Postoperatively, a 3-way 24-French Foley catheter is placed. In the recovery room, the patient develops sudden excruciating perineal pain and gross hematuria after the balloon is accidentally inflated while the catheter tip is lodged in the membranous urethra. The damaged segment of the urethra passes through which of the following anatomic spaces?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Superficial perineal pouch",
      "B": "Deep perineal pouch",
      "C": "Ischioanal fossa",
      "D": "Retropubic space of Retzius",
      "E": "Rectovesical pouch"
    },
    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Anatomy of the Perineal Pouches:** The **deep perineal pouch** is bounded inferiorly by the **perineal membrane** and superiorly by the inferior fascia of the pelvic diaphragm. \n- **Contents in Males:** In males, the deep perineal pouch contains the **membranous urethra**, the **external urethral sphincter** (voluntary skeletal muscle), the **bulbourethral (Cowper) glands**, the deep transverse perineal muscle, and branches of the internal pudendal neurovasculature. Inadvertent inflation of a Foley balloon in the membranous urethra causes severe trauma within the deep perineal pouch.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Superficial perineal pouch:** Contains the spongy (penile) urethra, erectile tissues (crura, bulb), ischiocavernosus, and bulbospongiosus muscles. Damage here (e.g., straddle injury) causes blood/urine extravasation beneath Buck's/Colles fascia.\n  - **(C) Ischioanal fossa:** The fat-filled lateral space of the anal triangle, common site for perianal abscesses.\n  - **(D) Retropubic space of Retzius:** Extraperitoneal space anterior to the bladder, behind the pubic symphysis.\n  - **(E) Rectovesical pouch:** The peritoneal reflection between the rectum and the bladder in males.",
    "pearl": "The deep perineal pouch contains the **membranous urethra**, **external urethral sphincter**, and **bulbourethral (Cowper) glands** (in males). The superficial pouch contains the **spongy/bulbous urethra** and erectile tissue.",
