    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Anatomy of the Suspensory Ligament:** The suspensory ligament of the ovary (infundibulopelvic ligament) connects the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall. It contains the **ovarian artery** (a direct branch of the abdominal aorta) and the ovarian vein.\n- **Risk of Injury:** As the ovarian vessels descend into the pelvis over the pelvic brim, they cross directly anterior to the **ureter** at the level of the bifurcation of the common iliac artery. Therefore, when clamping the suspensory ligament of the ovary during an oophorectomy, the ureter is highly vulnerable to being caught in the clamp or ligated.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Uterine artery:** Located within the cardinal (transverse cervical) ligament at the base of the broad ligament; the ureter is also at risk here during a hysterectomy (\"water under the bridge\").\n  - **(C) Internal iliac artery:** The ureter crosses the common iliac bifurcation, medial to the internal iliac, but the main internal iliac trunk is deep and lateral to the ovarian vessels.\n  - **(D) Pudendal nerve:** Exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen.\n  - **(E) Obturator nerve:** Travels along the lateral pelvic wall through the obturator canal; vulnerable during pelvic lymph node dissection, not oophorectomy.",
    "pearl": "The **ureter** is at risk of injury in two major gynecologic procedures:\n1. **Oophorectomy:** During ligation of the **ovarian vessels** in the suspensory (infundibulopelvic) ligament.\n2. **Hysterectomy:** During ligation of the **uterine vessels** in the cardinal ligament.",
