    "rationale": "- **Pudendal Nerve Anatomy:** The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) provides sensory innervation to the perineum, labia, and lower vagina, and motor innervation to the external anal and urethral sphincters.\n- **Path of the Nerve:** It exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, hooks around the **sacrospinous ligament** and the **ischial spine**, and then re-enters the perineum through the lesser sciatic foramen to travel in the pudendal (Alcock's) canal.\n- **Clinical Blockade:** Because the nerve intimately loops around the **ischial spine**, the ischial spine serves as the palpable transvaginal bony landmark for a pudendal nerve block. The needle is guided transvaginally, piercing the sacrospinous ligament just medial and posterior to the ischial spine.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Ischial tuberosity:** The weight-bearing part of the pelvis; while the pudendal nerve travels medial to it in Alcock's canal, it is a broad, diffuse structure and not the precise injection target.\n  - **(C) Pubic symphysis:** Anterior midline structure; blocks here would target the ilioinguinal/genitofemoral nerves, not the pudendal.\n  - **(D) Posterior superior iliac spine:** A landmark for the SI joint or lumbar punctures, located high on the posterior pelvis.\n  - **(E) Coccyx:** Forms the posterior boundary of the pelvic outlet but is not the target for a pudendal block.",
    "pearl": "The **ischial spine** is the critical transvaginal bony landmark for administering a **pudendal nerve block** (S2-S4) during labor/delivery.",
