    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "- **Neurovascular Bundles:** The cavernous nerves (parasympathetic fibers from S2-S4) are responsible for penile erection. \n- **Anatomic Course:** These delicate nerves travel within the neurovascular bundles that run posterolaterally along the **prostatic capsule**, sandwiched between the true prostatic capsule and the prostatic fascia (which blends posteriorly with Denonvilliers' fascia).\n- **Surgical Risk:** Because these nerves lie in direct contact with the lateral aspects of the **prostatic capsule**, any surgical excision of the prostate carries a high risk of damaging them, resulting in postoperative erectile dysfunction. \"Nerve-sparing\" prostatectomies attempt to meticulously peel these bundles off the capsule.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Denonvilliers' fascia:** The rectoprostatic fascia separates the prostate from the rectum; while the nerves are near its lateral edges, they course most intimately along the prostatic capsule itself.\n  - **(B) Camper's & (C) Fascia of Camper:** Superficial fatty layer of the anterior abdominal wall.\n  - **(E) Dartos fascia:** Superficial smooth muscle fascia of the scrotum.",
    "pearl": "The **cavernous nerves** (mediating erection) travel in neurovascular bundles posterolaterally on the **prostatic capsule**. Injury during prostatectomy causes severe erectile dysfunction.",
