    "rationale": "- **Pelvic Subperitoneal Spaces:** The subperitoneal pelvic connective tissue is divided into potential spaces that can harbor abscesses or fluid collections.\n- **Paravesical Space:** The space located lateral to the bladder and prostate, bounded laterally by the fascia of the **obturator internus** muscle and the pelvic sidewall, and medially by the bladder/prostate and their fascial condensations. Fluid here is classic for a paravesical/paraprostatic abscess.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Retropubic space (of Retzius):** Located anterior to the bladder, between the bladder and the pubic symphysis.\n  - **(B) Rectovesical pouch:** The lowest point of the peritoneal cavity in males, located posterior to the bladder and anterior to the rectum.\n  - **(C) Ischioanal fossa:** Located in the perineum (inferior to the pelvic diaphragm), lateral to the anal canal.\n  - **(E) Deep perineal pouch:** Located inferior to the pelvic cavity, bounded by the perineal membrane.",
