    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Nerve Identification:** The nerve traveling along the lateral pelvic wall and exiting through the obturator foramen is the **obturator nerve** (L2-L4).\n- **Vulnerability:** The obturator nerve is highly vulnerable to iatrogenic injury during pelvic lymph node dissections (especially for cervical, endometrial, or prostate cancer) as it lies immediately adjacent to the obturator lymph node packets in the obturator fossa.\n- **Neurological Deficit:** The obturator nerve provides motor innervation to the **medial compartment of the thigh** (the adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, gracilis, and pectineus). Therefore, injury results in **weakness of hip adduction**. It also provides sensation to a small patch of skin on the medial aspect of the thigh.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Weakness in hip extension:** Mediated by the inferior gluteal nerve (gluteus maximus).\n  - **(B) Loss of sensation over anterior thigh:** Mediated by the femoral nerve (L2-L4) and lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (L2-L3).\n  - **(D) Incontinence & (E) Perineal anesthesia:** Mediated by the pudendal nerve (S2-S4).",
    "pearl": "The **obturator nerve** runs along the lateral pelvic wall and is commonly injured during **pelvic lymph node dissection**. Injury causes **weakness in thigh adduction** and sensory loss on the medial thigh.",
