    "correct": "D",
    "rationale": "- **Pudendal Neuralgia (Cyclist's Syndrome):** The patient is suffering from pudendal nerve entrapment or compression, classically seen in avid cyclists due to prolonged pressure from a narrow bicycle saddle against the perineum.\n- **Pudendal Nerve Pathway:** \n  1. Arises from S2-S4.\n  2. Exits the pelvic cavity via the **greater sciatic foramen** (inferior to the piriformis muscle).\n  3. Hooks directly around the sacrospinous ligament and the ischial spine.\n  4. Re-enters the perineum by passing through the **lesser sciatic foramen**.\n  5. Travels anteriorly within the fascial pudendal canal (Alcock's canal) on the medial surface of the obturator internus muscle.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Greater sciatic foramen:** The nerve *exits* the pelvis here, but it must pass through the lesser sciatic foramen to *enter the perineum*.\n  - **(B) Obturator canal:** Transmits the obturator nerve/vessels to the medial thigh.\n  - **(C) Deep inguinal ring:** Transmits the spermatic cord and the ilioinguinal nerve (which supplies the *anterior* scrotum, a region spared in this patient).\n  - **(E) Sacral hiatus:** Transmits the filum terminale and S5/coccygeal nerves.",
    "pearl": "The **pudendal nerve** EXITS the pelvis via the **greater sciatic foramen** and ENTERS the perineum via the **lesser sciatic foramen**. It is vulnerable to compression in cyclists, causing perineal pain and numbness.",
