    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient is experiencing **round ligament pain**, a very common, benign cause of sharp groin pain in the second and third trimesters of pregnancy due to rapid uterine expansion stretching the ligament.\n- **Anatomy & Embryology:** The **round ligament of the uterus** originates at the uterine horns, travels retroperitoneally, enters the deep inguinal ring, traverses the inguinal canal, and terminates by blending into the subcutaneous tissue of the **labia majora**.\n- **Derivative:** The round ligament of the uterus (along with the ovarian ligament) is an embryological remnant of the **gubernaculum**, a fibromuscular band that guides the descent of the gonads. In females, the gubernaculum attaches to the developing uterus, splitting into the ovarian ligament (ovary to uterus) and the round ligament (uterus to labia majora). In males, it forms the scrotal ligament securing the testis.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Paramesonephric duct:** Forms the fallopian tubes, uterus, cervix, and upper 1/3 of the vagina.\n  - **(B) Mesonephric duct:** Forms the seminal vesicles, epididymis, ejaculatory duct, and ductus deferens in males (SEED); degenerates in females (Gartner's duct).\n  - **(D) Urogenital sinus:** Forms the lower 2/3 of the vagina, Bartholin glands, prostate, and bulbourethral glands.\n  - **(E) Genital tubercle:** Forms the glans penis and corpus cavernosum in males, and the glans clitoris and vestibular bulbs in females.",
    "pearl": "The female **gubernaculum** becomes the **ovarian ligament** and the **round ligament of the uterus**. Round ligament stretching causes sharp groin pain in pregnancy and it terminates in the **labia majora**.",
