    "rationale": "- **Anatomy of the Ischioanal Fossa:** The patient has an **ischioanal (ischiorectal) abscess**. The ischioanal fossa is a large, wedge-shaped, fat-filled space located lateral to the anal canal and inferior to the pelvic diaphragm. \n- **Lateral Wall Anatomy:** The lateral wall of the ischioanal fossa is formed by the ischial tuberosity and the obturator internus muscle (and its fascia). \n- **Alcock's Canal:** The **pudendal canal (Alcock's canal)** is a fascial split in the obturator internus fascia on the lateral wall of the ischioanal fossa. It transmits the **internal pudendal artery, internal pudendal vein, and the pudendal nerve**. Surgeons draining ischioanal abscesses must avoid deep lateral incisions to prevent catastrophic bleeding or permanent pudendal nerve injury (fecal/urinary incontinence and perineal anesthesia).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Inguinal canal:** Located in the anterior abdominal wall; transmits the spermatic cord or round ligament.\n  - **(C) Hunter's canal:** Located in the medial thigh; transmits the femoral artery/vein and saphenous nerve.\n  - **(D) Dorello's canal:** Located in the skull base; transmits the abducens nerve (CN VI).\n  - **(E) Guyon's canal:** Located in the wrist; transmits the ulnar nerve and artery.",
    "pearl": "**Alcock's canal** (pudendal canal) runs along the lateral wall of the **ischioanal fossa** (within the fascia of the obturator internus). It contains the **pudendal nerve** and **internal pudendal vessels**.",
