    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Rectoprostatic Fascia:** **Denonvilliers' fascia** is the tough, membranous rectoprostatic fascia that separates the posterior aspect of the prostate gland and seminal vesicles from the anterior wall of the rectum.\n- **Clinical Significance:** It serves as a formidable anatomical barrier. Because of the density of Denonvilliers' fascia, prostate cancer extremely rarely invades directly into the rectum. During radical prostatectomy, this fascia must be carefully dissected to separate the prostate from the rectum, avoiding iatrogenic rectal injury.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Buck's fascia:** The deep fascia of the penis, surrounding the corpora cavernosa and corpus spongiosum.\n  - **(B) Colles' fascia:** The superficial perineal fascia, limiting extravasation in straddle injuries.\n  - **(D) Waldeyer's fascia:** The presacral/rectosacral fascia located posterior to the rectum, attaching it to the sacrum.\n  - **(E) Gerota's fascia:** The renal fascia surrounding the kidneys and adrenal glands.",
    "pearl": "**Denonvilliers' fascia** separates the **prostate/seminal vesicles** from the **rectum**. It prevents posterior local spread of prostate cancer and is the critical dissection plane during prostatectomy.",
