    "rationale": "- **Inguinal Canal Anatomy:** The inguinal canal is an oblique passage through the lower anterior abdominal wall.\n- **The Rings:** \n  - The **deep (internal) inguinal ring** is the entrance to the canal. It is a defect or opening in the **transversalis fascia**. This is where the ductus deferens and gonadal vessels enter the canal to form the spermatic cord.\n  - The **superficial (external) inguinal ring** is the exit of the canal. It is a V-shaped opening in the **external oblique aponeurosis**.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) External oblique aponeurosis:** Forms the superficial ring.\n  - **(B) Internal oblique:** Forms the roof of the canal and contributes to the conjoint tendon, but does not form the deep ring.\n  - **(D) Peritoneum:** Lies deep to the transversalis fascia; outpouching of it forms the processus vaginalis.\n  - **(E) Rectus sheath:** Medial to the inguinal canal.",
