    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **Nerve Identification:** The nerve emerging from the superficial inguinal ring alongside the spermatic cord is the **ilioinguinal nerve** (L1).\n- **Anatomy & Innervation:** The ilioinguinal nerve pieces the internal oblique muscle and travels *through* the inguinal canal (but does not enter the deep ring). It exits via the superficial inguinal ring. It provides **sensory innervation** to the medial aspect of the upper thigh and the **anterior surface of the scrotum** (or labia majora in females), as well as the root of the penis (or mons pubis).\n- **Surgical Risk:** It is the most commonly injured nerve during open inguinal hernia repairs and lower quadrant incisions (e.g., appendectomy).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Lateral aspect of the thigh:** Innervated by the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (L2-L3).\n  - **(C) Medial aspect of the thigh:** Innervated by the obturator nerve (L2-L4).\n  - **(D) Perianal region:** Innervated by the pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and inferior rectal nerve.\n  - **(E) Glans penis:** Innervated by the dorsal nerve of the penis (terminal branch of the pudendal nerve).",
    "pearl": "The **ilioinguinal nerve (L1)** exits the **superficial inguinal ring** with the spermatic cord. Injury causes loss of sensation to the **anterior scrotum** / labia majora and medial upper thigh.",
