    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** The patient has **Vulvovaginal Candidiasis** (yeast infection). Classic symptoms include intense pruritus, erythema, and a thick, white, \"cottage-cheese\" discharge. The KOH prep showing **pseudohyphae** is diagnostic for *Candida albicans*.\n- **Pathogenesis:** *Candida* is part of the normal vaginal flora in many women. However, its growth is kept in check by the acidic pH maintained by *Lactobacillus* species. When the patient took broad-spectrum antibiotics (amoxicillin), the normal protective *Lactobacilli* were killed off. This alteration in the microbiome allowed the *Candida* to rapidly overgrow, causing symptomatic inflammation. (Other risk factors include diabetes, pregnancy, and immunosuppression).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Autoimmune destruction:** Describes Lichen Sclerosus.\n  - **(C) Mechanical trauma:** Describes Lichen Simplex Chronicus.\n  - **(D) DNA virus:** Describes HPV infection (causing warts or dysplasia).\n  - **(E) Malignant apocrine cells:** Describes Extramammary Paget Disease.",
    "pearl": "**Vulvovaginal Candidiasis** (pseudohyphae, cottage-cheese discharge) frequently occurs after **antibiotic use**, which kills normal protective flora (*Lactobacillus*) and allows yeast overgrowth.",
