    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Condyloma Acuminatum:** These are benign genital warts characterized by exophytic, cauliflower-like growths on the vulva, perineum, or perianal area. \n- **Pathogenesis:** They are caused by the **\"low-risk\" strains of Human Papillomavirus, specifically types 6 and 11**. \n- **Cancer Risk:** While they are highly contagious and can be a significant cosmetic/psychological burden, HPV 6 and 11 do *not* integrate their DNA into the host genome in a way that causes malignant transformation. Therefore, condyloma acuminata have virtually **zero malignant potential** (unlike the lesions caused by high-risk HPV 16/18).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) HPV 16/18:** High-risk strains that cause high-grade dysplasia (VIN/CIN) and squamous cell carcinoma.\n  - **(C) Poxvirus:** Causes Molluscum contagiosum (umbilicated, dome-shaped papules), not cauliflower warts.\n  - **(D) CMV & (E) EBV:** Not associated with genital warts.",
