    "stem": "A 48-year-old woman with a history of cervical dysplasia presents with a 2-cm, ulcerated, firm mass on her left labium majus. A biopsy confirms invasive squamous cell carcinoma, warty/basaloid subtype. The surgeon plans a radical vulvectomy and lymph node dissection. Which of the following lymph node basins is the primary, initial site of lymphatic drainage for this vulvar malignancy, and therefore the most critical to evaluate for metastasis?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Deep pelvic nodes (iliac)",
      "B": "Para-aortic nodes",
      "C": "Superficial inguinal nodes",
      "D": "Popliteal nodes",
      "E": "Axillary nodes"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Lymphatic Spread of Vulvar Cancer:** Squamous cell carcinoma of the vulva spreads predictably via the lymphatic system before spreading hematogenously.\n- **The Primary Node Basin:** The lymphatic drainage of the vulva (and the lower 1/3 of the vagina) goes directly to the **Superficial Inguinal Lymph Nodes** (located in the groin). \n- **Progression:** From the superficial inguinal nodes, the cancer will spread to the deep inguinal nodes, and then into the pelvis (iliac nodes). Assessing the superficial inguinal nodes (often via a sentinel lymph node biopsy) is the most critical step in staging and determining the prognosis of vulvar cancer.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Deep pelvic (iliac):** The primary drainage for the cervix, uterus, and upper 2/3 of the vagina.\n  - **(B) Para-aortic:** The primary drainage for the ovaries and the fundus of the uterus.\n  - **(D) & (E):** Do not drain the perineum.",
    "pearl": "The primary lymphatic drainage of the **vulva (and lower 1/3 of vagina)** is to the **Superficial Inguinal lymph nodes**. (Cervix/Upper vagina $\\rightarrow$ Iliac nodes; Ovaries $\\rightarrow$ Para-aortic nodes).",
