    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A red, itchy, crusty, \"eczema-like\" lesion on the vulva (or nipple) that fails to respond to topical steroids is the classic presentation of **Paget Disease**.\n- **Histopathology:** The biopsy reveals **Paget cells**: large, atypical cells with abundant **clear, pale cytoplasm** (because they are full of mucin). They are found entirely *within* the epidermis (intraepithelial).\n- **Stains (The Key Discriminator):** Extramammary Paget Disease is a malignant intraepithelial **adenocarcinoma** (glandular origin). Because it is glandular and contains mucin, it stains **PAS positive**, **Cytokeratin 7 (CK7) positive**, and **CEA positive**. (It stains S100 negative).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Melanoma:** Can also present as clear/atypical cells in the epidermis, but melanoma cells are derived from neural crest. Melanoma is **S100 positive** (and Melan-A/HMB-45 positive), but it is **PAS negative** and **Cytokeratin negative**.\n  - **(C) VIN (SCC in situ):** Squamous dysplasia; would not be PAS positive (no mucin) and would not have classic clear Paget cells.\n  - **(D) & (E) Dermatitis/Psoriasis:** Benign inflammatory conditions; would not show malignant clear cells.",
    "pearl": "**Extramammary Paget Disease** of the vulva presents as an itchy, red, crusty lesion. Histologically, it shows intraepithelial clear cells (Paget cells) that are **PAS(+), Keratin(+), and S100(-)**.",
