    "stem": "A 68-year-old woman presents with a 2-cm, ulcerated mass on the right labium majus. A biopsy confirms invasive squamous cell carcinoma, keratinizing type. During surgical staging, which of the following characteristics of her tumor is the single most important prognostic factor determining her long-term survival?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The degree of keratinization (differentiation) of the tumor cells",
      "B": "The size of the primary tumor mass on the vulva",
      "C": "The status of the superficial inguinal lymph nodes",
      "D": "The presence or absence of concurrent human papillomavirus (HPV) infection",
      "E": "The presence of underlying lichen sclerosus"
    },
    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Prognostic Factors in Vulvar Cancer:** For almost all squamous cell carcinomas (vulvar, cervical, head/neck), the most critical factor determining survival is the extent of tumor spread at the time of diagnosis.\n- **The Most Important Factor:** The single most important prognostic indicator in vulvar cancer is **lymph node involvement** (specifically the inguinal lymph nodes). \n  - If the nodes are negative, the 5-year survival rate is very high ($>90\\%$).\n  - If the nodes are positive, the survival rate drops precipitously ($\\sim 50\\%$), and drops even further if deep pelvic nodes are involved.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Differentiation & (B) Tumor size:** These are important prognostic factors, but they are secondary to whether the tumor has escaped the primary site and metastasized to the lymph nodes.\n  - **(D) HPV status:** HPV-related tumors tend to have a slightly *better* prognosis than non-HPV tumors, but lymph node status is far more critical.\n  - **(E) Lichen sclerosus:** Tells you the pathogenesis, not the prognosis of the established cancer.",
    "pearl": "The most critical prognostic factor for **Vulvar Squamous Cell Carcinoma** is the presence and number of **positive inguinal lymph nodes**.",
