    "correct": "B",
    "rationale": "- **Embryology of the Female Genital Tract:**\n  - In a female fetus (XX), the absence of the SRY gene/Testosterone/AMH causes the **Paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts** to develop into the fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper vagina.\n  - The **Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts**, which form the male internal genitalia (epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles), normally regress and disappear in the female.\n- **The Pathology:** Sometimes, a small remnant of the Wolffian duct fails to regress completely. These remnants are located along the lateral walls of the vagina. Over time, they can accumulate fluid and dilate into a **Gartner duct cyst**. \n- **Clinical Presentation:** They are typically asymptomatic, fluid-filled cysts found incidentally on the **lateral vaginal wall**.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Mullerian duct:** Forms the uterus/upper vagina. Anomalies cause septate/bicornuate uterus or vaginal adenosis.\n  - **(C) Urogenital sinus:** Forms the lower vagina and bladder/urethra.\n  - **(D) Ureteric bud:** Forms the ureter/kidney collecting system.\n  - **(E) Gubernaculum:** Forms the ovarian ligament and round ligament of the uterus.",
    "pearl": "A **Gartner duct cyst** is a remnant of the **Wolffian (mesonephric) duct**. It presents as a cyst on the **lateral wall** of the vagina.",
