    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** Vaginal bleeding in an infant or toddler ($<4$ years old) with a polypoid, \"grape-like\" mass protruding from the vagina is the pathognomonic presentation of **Sarcoma Botryoides** (also known as **Embryonal Rhabdomyosarcoma**).\n- **Pathology:** This is a highly malignant sarcoma of primitive **skeletal muscle** cells. \n- **Histology/Staining:** The key morphologic clue is the presence of rhabdomyoblasts (cells with pink cytoplasm and skeletal muscle **cross-striations**). Because it is a muscle tumor, it strongly expresses intermediate filaments specific to muscle tissue, primarily **Desmin** (and Myogenin/MyoD1).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Cytokeratin:** Marker for epithelial cells (Carcinomas). This is a sarcoma.\n  - **(B) S100:** Marker for neural crest cells (Melanoma, Schwannoma).\n  - **(D) CD45:** Leukocyte Common Antigen, marker for Lymphomas.\n  - **(E) Chromogranin:** Marker for neuroendocrine tumors (Carcinoid, Small cell lung cancer).",
    "pearl": "A **grape-like** vaginal mass in a young girl ($<4$ yrs) is **Sarcoma Botryoides** (Embryonal Rhabdomyosarcoma). It is a malignant tumor of primitive skeletal muscle, so it stains positive for **Desmin**.",
