    "rationale": "- **Anatomy and Lymphatic Drainage of the Vagina:** The vagina has a dual embryologic origin, which dictates its lymphatic drainage.\n  - **Upper 2/3 of the vagina:** Derived from the Mullerian ducts. It drains upward, alongside the cervix, directly into the **regional pelvic nodes** (internal and external iliac nodes).\n  - **Lower 1/3 of the vagina:** Derived from the urogenital sinus. It drains downward, alongside the vulva, directly into the **Superficial Inguinal nodes** in the groin.\n- **Application:** Because this patient's tumor is located in the **lower one-third** of the vagina, the initial site of lymphatic spread will be the superficial inguinal nodes.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) External & (C) Internal iliac:** These are the primary drainage sites for the *upper 2/3* of the vagina and the cervix.\n  - **(D) Para-aortic:** Drainage for the ovaries and fundus of the uterus.",
