    "rationale": "- **Epidemiology of Vaginal Tumors:** This is a crucial, highly tested rule of gynecologic oncology.\n- **The Rule:** The vagina is an incredibly rare site for a *primary* cancer. Therefore, if you find a malignant tumor in the vagina (especially in an older woman), **it is almost always a metastasis (secondary spread) from another organ**, rather than a primary vaginal cancer.\n- **Common Sources:** The most common sources of vaginal metastasis are direct extension from the **Cervix** or **Endometrium**, or metastatic spread from the **Ovary, Bladder, or Rectum**.\n- **Applying to the Patient:** Even though she had a hysterectomy (ruling out direct spread from the cervix/uterus), the statistical probability still strongly favors a metastasis (e.g., from an occult ovarian, bladder, or colorectal cancer) over a primary vaginal SCC. \n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Fibroid:** Benign, doesn't present as a fungating, bleeding mass 20 years post-hysterectomy.\n  - **(B) Metastatic cervical cancer:** She had her cervix removed 20 years ago for *benign* disease, so she cannot have cervical cancer.\n  - **(C) Primary vaginal SCC:** Exceedingly rare ($<2\\%$ of GYN cancers); metastasis is always statistically more likely.",
    "pearl": "A malignant tumor found in the vagina is almost always **secondary (metastatic)** from another primary site (e.g., cervix, endometrium, ovary, rectum) rather than a primary vaginal cancer.",
