    "stem": "A 25-year-old woman is diagnosed with Clear Cell Adenocarcinoma of the upper vagina. Her mother confirms that she took a specific medication while pregnant with the patient to \"prevent miscarriage.\" In addition to vaginal clear cell adenocarcinoma, female offspring exposed to this drug *in utero* are at increased risk for which of the following structural reproductive anomalies?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Bicornuate uterus and T-shaped uterine cavity",
      "B": "Complete absence of the uterus and upper vagina (Mullerian agenesis)",
      "C": "Imperforate hymen",
      "D": "Streak ovaries and premature ovarian failure",
      "E": "Severe pelvic endometriosis"
    },
    "correct": "A",
    "rationale": "- **DES Exposure:** The drug in question is **Diethylstilbestrol (DES)**, a synthetic non-steroidal estrogen prescribed in the 1950s-1970s to prevent miscarriage.\n- **The Consequences (DES Daughters):** *In utero* exposure to DES disrupts the normal embryologic development of the Mullerian (paramesonephric) ducts. \n- **The Manifestations:** \n  1. **Malignancy:** It causes Vaginal Adenosis, which can progress to **Clear Cell Adenocarcinoma** of the vagina/cervix.\n  2. **Structural Anomalies:** It severely alters the shape of the uterus. The classic, pathognomonic finding on imaging (HSG) is a **\"T-shaped\" uterine cavity**. They also have a high rate of bicornuate uterus, hypoplastic uterus, and cervical hoods. These anomalies lead to a very high rate of infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and recurrent second-trimester miscarriages (cervical incompetence).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Mullerian agenesis:** The ducts form incorrectly with DES, but they are not completely absent.\n  - **(C) Imperforate hymen:** Failure of the urogenital sinus/hymen to canalize, not related to DES.\n  - **(D) Streak ovaries:** Turner syndrome (45,XO).\n  - **(E) Endometriosis:** Retrograde menstruation; not a congenital structural anomaly from DES.",
    "pearl": "*In utero* exposure to **DES (Diethylstilbestrol)** causes Vaginal Adenosis, **Clear Cell Adenocarcinoma**, and a structurally abnormal, **T-shaped uterus** leading to pregnancy complications.",
